HT_DATA.load("detail", {"4Q7FTjhvZ7Y":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an intimate, embedded look at late-90s web history and the spirit of building something important under pressure.","q":{"x":"Code Rush, which is an awesome documentary for anyone interested in the history of the Web","i":20596499,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":22816350,"author":"e15ctr0n","month":"2020-04","story":"Mitchell Baker Named CEO of Mozilla","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Captures the excitement of the Mozilla open-sourcing era","quote":"The documentary Code Rush is required viewing, IMHO, to capture some of the excitement of that era.","text":"> Does anyone can tell anything positive about her? She's the reason Mozilla exists today as an independent, non-profit organization. On March 31, 1998 Netscape allowed the Mozilla code base to be open sourced under the Mozilla Project. [1][2] This was the result of an intensive year-long effort to (1) remove proprietary source code that couldn't be open-sourced, (2) release the source code under a brand new license called the Mozilla Public License. This license was written principally by Mitchell Baker whose formal education is in law, with input from ESR, Linus Torvalds, VA Research, etc. [3] On July 14, 2003 AOL (which had acquired Netscape earlier) closed down the Netscape division and laid off almost everyone there, including Mitchell Baker. That very day Mitch Kapor [4] stepped in with funding and advice to help Baker and a core team of total 10 employees set up the Mozilla Foundation in an office in Mountain View that is today occupied by the Khan Academy. [5] Wikipedia [6] has a good summary of her career. The documentary Code Rush is required viewing, IMHO, to capture some of the excitement of that era. [7][8] Bonus points for spotting JWZ. :-) [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] [LINK] [8] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20596499,"author":"jwatt","month":"2019-08","story":"Contributing to the Mozilla code base","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome documentary for anyone interested in web history","quote":"Code Rush, which is an awesome documentary for anyone interested in the history of the Web","text":"It was Netscape in 1999 he got flown out. Documented in Code Rush, which is an awesome documentary for anyone interested in the history of the Web: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49371264,"author":"st_goliath","month":"2026-08","story":"Why Microsoft Entertainment Pack had a sticker announcing that it had Tetris?","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested for those with fond memories of Netscape","quote":"if you have fond memories about Netscape, you might enjoy \"Project Code Rush","text":"Given the context and your reference to Quake, I assume by \"the older days\" you mean the 90s, PCs and early Windows? If you weren't previously aware of \"The Old New Thing\" blog, Raymond Chen has been sharing stories about Windows 95 development for decades at this point. So many in fact, he published a book under the same name (the book is essentially a best-of collection from the blog in paper form). The os2museum[1] blog is also a gold mine. Tim Paterson at one point tried to start blogging, sharing a few anecdotes about the early days of DOS[2] Speaking of books, you might enjoy \"Showstopper!\" about the development of NT, even tough it is a bit shallow on the technical side. For the technical aspects, you might want to get it together with \"Inside Windows NT\" (first edition) by Helen Custer. You might also enjoy: \"Another Boring Topic\" [3] YouTube channel, covering (among other things) early Windows development in excruciating detail and \"Old Computers Sucked\" [4], actually trying to run the software on era appropriate hardware. \"Cathode Ray Dude\" is all over the place but often also covers hardware and software of roughly that era[5]. A sibling comment already mentioned JWZs blog, if you have fond memories about Netscape, you might enjoy \"Project Code Rush\" [6]. Btw. Netscape Navigator 3.0 has just turned 30. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40825832,"author":"wenbin","month":"2024-06","story":"The story, as best I can remember, of the origin of Mosaic and Netscape [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Commenter plans to re-watch it","quote":"And re-watch this also","text":"gonna watch it over the weekend :) And re-watch this also - Project Code Rush - The Beginnings of Netscape / Mozilla Documentary [VIDEO]"},{"id":37279573,"author":"akling","month":"2023-08","story":"Firefox 1.0 New York Times Ad","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary following Netscape folks during the Mozilla open-sourcing rush","quote":"There’s a fun documentary that follows some folks inside Netscape during the rush to make Mozilla open source","text":"There’s a fun documentary that follows some folks inside Netscape during the rush to make Mozilla open source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32801709,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour look inside Netscape as it open sources its browser","quote":"a one-hour glimpse into Netscape Communications in 1998, just as they open source their browser","text":"Code Rush, a one-hour glimpse into Netscape Communications in 1998, just as they open source their browser: [VIDEO] I get very tired whenever I watch it for some reason."},{"id":25849889,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2021-01","story":"Kids find a security flaw in Linux Mint by mashing keys","stance":"rec","why":"Explains 1990s Netscape culture behind the author's views","quote":"You can watch this if you have an hour.","text":"It has something to do with the 1990's dot-com culture, like the original Netscape was somehow more pure than what came after, and this causes him to view modern inheritors like YC with a jaundiced eye. You can watch this if you have an hour. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23452034,"author":"Jugurtha","month":"2020-06","story":"Cloudflare TV","stance":"rec","why":"Embedded documentary following Netscape people in 1998","quote":"an embedded documentary following people at Netscape in 1998","text":"Not parent, but I thought it were a TV stream that filmed Cloudflare teams solving problems. Similar to the documentary[1] \"Aardvark'd: 12 Weeks with Geeks\" where they show the life of interns developing an actual product at Fog Creek, the first paying customer, bug fixes, etc. I appreciated that one, as I did \"Code Rush\" [3], an embedded documentary following people at Netscape in 1998, even shows the moment Jamie Zawinski bringing the source code and uploading it, or moments where they needed Apple to greenlight using proprietary code, trying to reach Steve Jobs, and ending up implementing it. Another one I liked was \"Downloaded\", a documentary about Napster with the main people (Sean Fanning, Sean Parker, Ron Conway, etc.)[4] PS: Since you're here, I was unable to log to my CloudFlare account for more than a week, reset my password an everything, but always couldn't log in until a couple of days ago. I would have wanted to see a video on the background of the issue and how it was resolved : ) [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":23476139,"author":"purerandomness","month":"2020-06","story":"Ask HN: Career and life advice for a 30yo","stance":"rec","why":"Conveys the spirit of doing something important at Netscape","quote":"I watched the Netscape documentary \"Project Code Rush\" [2] with Jamie Zawinski and loved the spirit and the feeling of doing something important.","text":"There's a wide community of people feeling just like you, assembling in \"Maker communities\", as they call it. Good entry points are the IndieHackers podcast, and conferences like MicroConf (watch their conference talk recordings here, especially Patrick McKenzie's aka patio11 [0]) The basic idea is to create a sustainable job you love for yourself, the people that depend on you, and many others. The antithesis of \"Our incredible journey\", aka VC sellout. Once you've done this, you can do whatever you want. Build a game company. Pursue academia [1]. Join the demoscene, tour the world, join demo party competitions and write sick 64k intros. Commit to open source and help the Haiku project. Hang around at your local hacker space and help create an independend mesh-based ISP. I know exactly what you're talking about. I watched the Netscape documentary \"Project Code Rush\" [2] with Jamie Zawinski and loved the spirit and the feeling of doing something important. It feels like it's in such a stark contrast to many people's reality, pushing JIRA tickets in a toxic AdTech startup. However, I think it's an illusion. There never was a \"golden age\", and people simply forget that bullshit jobs always existed, you just don't see domcumentaries of them, nobody would watch them. For me, what helped me was realizing that the world, or the \"industry\" doesn't owe me anything, and I'm completely on my own, and if I want to do something meaningful, well, I'll have to create my own sustainable job"},{"id":22707433,"author":"ragle","month":"2020-03","story":"Yelp to stop auto-creating fundraisers after outrage from business owners","stance":"rec","why":"Fun documentary about Netscape engineers","quote":"Fun documentary","text":"> and worked on a few important pieces of software before becoming a bar owner instead. Fun documentary: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21320525,"author":"lancebeet","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: How was life for a regular dev during the dot com burst?","stance":"rec","why":"Glimpse of Netscape culture during open sourcing and AOL acquisition","quote":"I really recommend the 2000 documentary Code Rush","text":"I really recommend the 2000 documentary Code Rush[1] about Netscape's open sourcing of the browser and acquisition by AOL. It doesn't give any major insights into the industry, but does give a glimpse of what the culture was like back then. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20469679,"author":"mtmail","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Books to recommend about early tech history (late 90s)?","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour documentary suggested among early tech history resources","quote":"Project Code Rush\", a 1h documentary","text":"\"The PayPal Wars\". Also see [LINK] \"Founders at Work: Stories of Startups' Early Days\" \"Project Code Rush\", a 1h documentary ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":18524776,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-11","story":"AOL acquires Netscape in $4.2B deal (1998)","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on Netscape's last year and Mozilla source release","quote":"There's a good Netscape documentary filmed during their last year leading up to the acquisition.","text":"There's a good Netscape documentary filmed during their last year leading up to the acquisition. When they were working to get the Mozilla source code released. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48198837,"author":"MrDOS","month":"2026-05","story":"The lasting influence of Netscape Time","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes a scene about a wrong FTP hostname","quote":"Spoiler: he had the wrong hostname.","text":"[VIDEO] > This is bad. > What's that? > Um, well, so I connected to the, uh... The machine that- that controls the FTP push, is like, not answering. Spoiler: he had the wrong hostname. > Mm... oh. Duh!"},{"id":38834941,"author":"neverrroot","month":"2024-01","story":"Netscape 1.x","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as the birth-hour of Mozilla reference","quote":"","text":"Thank you for replying, your work shaped me into what I came to be. I was a backend developer at the end of the 90’s, sadly not for Netscape Server, it was mostly C++ CGI. Have you been around during this time period (birth hour of Mozilla)? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33800332,"author":"aliqot","month":"2022-11","story":"Blue Peter discovers the Internet (1995)","stance":"reference","why":"Describes documentary on Netscape engineers racing to release Mozilla","quote":"Code Rush is a documentary following the lives of a group of Netscape engineers in Silicon Valley.","text":"[VIDEO] Project Code Rush Code Rush is a documentary following the lives of a group of Netscape engineers in Silicon Valley. It covers Netscape's last year as an independent company, from their announcement of the Mozilla open source project until their acquisition by AOL. It particularly focuses on the last minute rush to make the Mozilla source code ready for release by the deadline of March 31 1998, and the impact on the engineers' lives and families as they attempt to save the company from ruin."},{"id":32214121,"author":"tauwauwau","month":"2022-07","story":"Tech booms fuelled liveability busts in Silicon Valley and Bengaluru","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of late-90s hacker look","quote":"","text":"Richard Stallman still looks like one to me :), I know its from late Internet era, but see following [VIDEO]"},{"id":15750171,"author":"greymeister","month":"2017-11","story":"FCC plan would give Internet providers power to choose the sites customers see","stance":"reference","why":"jwz statement from Code Rush relevant to the news","quote":"News like this makes me think back to this[0] statement by jwz","text":"News like this makes me think back to this[0] statement by jwz from the Netscape/Mozilla documentary \"Code Rush\" [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10525359,"author":"jd3","month":"2015-11","story":"Y Combinator and Paul Graham are bad for the world (Part 1)","stance":"reference","why":"Related piece by jwz on VCs and youth","quote":"","text":"Fascinating read. Thank you for this. Reminds me of a piece Jamie Zawinski did a few years ago[0][1] regarding a similar issue relating to VC's and \"your one and only youth.\" [2] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PHe0bXAIuk0",2],["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["ip641WmY4pA",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["ouvi-fwrfIY",1],["PhNUjg9X4g8",1],["GKYCA3UsmrU",1],["OKY6BGcx37k",1],["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["4Z2uzEM0ugY",1],["d1EB1zsxW0k",1],["24R8JObNNQ4",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["FjHJ7FmV0M4",1],["b9FC9fAlfQE",1],["3nwwKbM_vJc",1]]},"FACK2knC08E":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it to counter overpopulation alarm with data on falling birth rates.","q":{"x":"I can highly recommend this lecture by the late Hans Rosling","i":15745430,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":21457482,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2019-11","story":"World Scientists’ Warning of a Climate Emergency","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows what family planning looks like in practice in Bangladesh","quote":"He shows how family planning looks like in practice in countries like Bangladesh","text":"The issue here is mostly with the countries in extreme poverty. Countries with average fertility rate of 3 and higher. Women in countries with the highest fertility index often do not control when they get pregnant. Having 6 and more children is rarely effect of conscious family planning. It's effect of lack of education, lack of access to contraception, forced sex in marriage etc. Sometimes having many children is an insurance policy in case some children will die before they reach productive age. We really should be addressing this type of issues underlying uncontrolled population growth. People should be able to choose when they want to have children, how many children they want, they should be educated on how to provide for their children, and certainly people should not be afraid that some part of their offspring will die. It has nothing to do with penalizing having children like they did in China. I really recommend, \"DON'T PANIC — Hans Rosling showing the facts about population\". He shows how family planning looks like in practice in countries like Bangladesh, so that westerners can imagine what are we talking about: [VIDEO] Dealing with global warming is going bad and we should panic about that, but humanity is making great progress on controlling population growth. There is really no issue with you having 3 children. Western population will be declining anyway as fertility rate in western countries is mostly below 2. Just please, support political, systematical chang"},{"id":21051417,"author":"Isinlor","month":"2019-09","story":"Greta Thunberg: 'How dare you? You have stolen my dreams and my childhood'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows population will stabilize and people are misinformed about world stats","quote":"I really recommend","text":"Because there is no world overpopulation. People talk about overpopulation since ancient times. [LINK] Currently standard of living are growing almost all over the world and human population is predicted to pick and stabilize at around 11 billion. I really recommend \"DON'T PANIC — Hans Rosling showing the facts about population\" [VIDEO] What's also interesting in his video is the fact that people tend to be very misinformed about state of the world affairs. E.g. when asked questions about certain statistics, they do worse than random. And the better educated you are the more misinformed you may be."},{"id":15745430,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2017-11","story":"India's Indira canteen: The best meal you can buy for 13 cents","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains falling birth rates via child survival and development","quote":"I can highly recommend this lecture by the late Hans Rosling","text":"Until the modern era, the global birth rate was fairly constant at about six per woman. The population growth we've experienced over the last century is mostly to do with child survival. Before 1900, the child mortality rate in India was over 50%. If you need children to care for you in old age and expect most of your children to die, you're likely to have a lot of them. When child survival improved, attitudes changed. Parents no longer want three or four children to work on the family farm, they want one or two children to become doctors or engineers. Economic development and the availability of reliable contraception have made that aspiration practical. If you're interested in the topic, I can highly recommend this lecture by the late Hans Rosling: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14551434,"author":"lentil_soup","month":"2017-06","story":"The Old Are Eating the Young","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rosling shows global birth rate near replacement and population stabilizing","quote":"I really recommend his videos if you haven't seen them.","text":"This video seems relevant: [VIDEO] According to Hans Rosling we've reached \"peak child\", meaning we're almost at 2 children per woman globally. This means population will continue to grow for a few years as globally there are more children than adults but will stabilize after that around the ~11 billion mark. And as long as we stay at that \"peak child\" number we won't go any further. I really recommend his videos if you haven't seen them."},{"id":28055708,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-08","story":"This Can’t Go On","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long BBC presentation on population, really interesting","quote":"It's an hour long; really interesting.","text":"Hans Rosling's presentation \"Don't Panic\" (produced by the BBC) is pretty good: * [VIDEO] It's an hour long; really interesting."},{"id":24384607,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-09","story":"The Lancet: World population projections by 2100","stance":"rec","why":"Good hour-long presentation on population facts","quote":"There's a good (though hour-long) presentation by the late Hans Rosling","text":"There's a good (though hour-long) presentation by the late Hans Rosling call \"DON'T PANIC — Hans Rosling showing the facts about population\": * [VIDEO] \"Don’t Panic – The Facts About Population\": * [LINK]"},{"id":22889130,"author":"brigandish","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: Who Is Firing?","stance":"rec","why":"Rosling walks through the stats on health and development","quote":"I always enjoy Hans Rosling going through the stats, if you’ve time it’s well worth it","text":"Since economic growth tends to go hand in hand with better access to health services and greater social freedom, it does. I always enjoy Hans Rosling going through the stats, if you’ve time it’s well worth it [VIDEO]"},{"id":19899598,"author":"Carlee","month":"2019-05","story":"CO2 in the atmosphere just exceeded 415ppm for the first time in human history","stance":"rec","why":"Statistics show wealthier people have fewer children","quote":"You should check out Hans Rosling's lectures","text":"You should check out Hans Rosling's lectures [1]. Statistics points towards the fact that as people become more wealthy, they have less children. It should stagnate around 11 million people by most models. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19718231,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-04","story":"The global population pyramid","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why population will peak at 11 billion","quote":"There's a good presentation by Hans Rosling about why population will peak at 11B","text":"There's a good presentation by Hans Rosling about why population will peak at 11B (stick with until 26m): * [VIDEO] Further on he explains why we may not have to worry about resource use given growing prosperity: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":16345653,"author":"lugg","month":"2018-02","story":"When will the world reach 'peak child'?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers Africa population predictions and misconceptions or dated knowledge","quote":"this is the one specifically covering Africa / population predictions and the misconceptions / dated knowledge.","text":"He has a lot of good videos out there but this is the one specifically covering Africa / population predictions and the misconceptions / dated knowledge. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13639648,"author":"tomjen3","month":"2017-02","story":"Who are you calling Malthusian?","stance":"rec","why":"Reassures that population fears are overblown","quote":"In that case don't worry","text":"In that case don't worry: [VIDEO] Also consider that the carrying capacity of the world isn't fixed, but depends on how good our technology is (with a potential theoretical upper limit). Well tech is improved by people working on and inventing better technology, right? So you get better technology with more people - and with that you can have more people working on better technology. The biggest mistake, and the one all Malthusians made, is to forget that humans are, on average, net produces not net consumers."},{"id":27591295,"author":"rocknor","month":"2021-06","story":"Australia opposes Great Barrier Reef ′in danger′ listing","stance":"reference","why":"One of several links showing birth rates are in decline","quote":"","text":"> if the 1 billion \"rich people\" drop their emissions down to below average then the majority of the problem will still be here. True. But the \"majority\" is not as big as you think. > there will be 1 billion less wealthy people Just wrong. There are lots of ways to reduce emissions without any effect in wealth. Eating less meat is one example. > for no particular net gain. Very wrong, there are huge gains: [LINK] > I advocate the radical approach of encouraging people to have less children. I knew you would say this, I was just waiting for it. Global birth rates have been in freefall for decades now, it's tragic that people don't know about it. You'd assume that supposedly \"better\" education in western countries would make people more aware, but western governments are only concerned with maintaining global western hegemony which requires falsely accusing the rest of the world and keeping their citizens ignorant. Also, you can't force people to have less children, that's inhumane. I suggest you go through the following links. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":25894366,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2021-01","story":"Ghost cities and abandoned areas with a declining population","stance":"reference","why":"Rosling disputed the claim of a 20 billion plateau","quote":"Hans Rosling famously[1] didn't think so.","text":"> and the plateau is calculated at 20 billion. According to who? Hans Rosling famously[1] didn't think so. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24949999,"author":"drichel","month":"2020-10","story":"Overpopulation one of the ten greatest threats to humanity","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the commenter's side against overpopulation alarm","quote":"I'm with Hans Rosling on this topic","text":"Result of a roundtable discussion wuth concerned citizens? Not convinced, I'm with Hans Rosling on this topic [VIDEO]"},{"id":21651280,"author":"lkramer","month":"2019-11","story":"US births decline for fourth year in a row, CDC says","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that birthrates are declining worldwide","quote":"the birthrate across the world is declining.","text":"This is not really true. Except for some parts of Africa, the birthrate across the world is declining. The worldwide average is 2.4. Likely the world's population will start decline in actual terms some time in the 2nd half of the 21st century: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15891605,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2017-12","story":"Recycling Chaos in U.S. As China Bans 'Foreign Waste'","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence that population growth is a solved problem","quote":"Population growth is a solved problem.","text":"The UN forecast that the global population will reach an absolute maximum of about 11 billion by 2100. Some forecasts are even more conservative, suggesting a peak of 9 billion by 2050. The global birth rate has peaked and is now in overall decline, with most of the developed world being well below the replacement rate. Most of the population growth we'll see over the next century is simply due to more people living longer lives. The fertility rate in China is massively below replacement at 1.57 births per woman. India is barely above replacement at 2.4. The fertility rate in India today is lower than the fertility rate was in the US in 1970. The global fertility rate has halved in 50 years. Even in the poorest countries in sub-Saharan Africa, the fertility rate is declining. The fertility rate in Rwanda has more than halved from 8.4 in 1980 to 3.8 today. As far as I can ascertain, Niger is the country on earth with a persistently high fertility rate. Population growth is a solved problem. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"l9ZqXlHl65g":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it, with its follow-up, as an exposé of fake likes and bot activity, and as a warning before spending on Facebook ads.","q":{"x":"before you waste any money, watch these two videos","i":8381761,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":14181295,"author":"lingben","month":"2017-04","story":"A Facebook bot purge clobbered USA Today","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes fraud and bots on Facebook.","quote":"for a fantastic expose of how facebook is riddled with fraud and bots","text":"for a fantastic expose of how facebook is riddled with fraud and bots, check out veritasium's two videos: facebook fraud [LINK] the problem with facebook [VIDEO]"},{"id":9442364,"author":"lingben","month":"2015-04","story":"The Bot Bubble: Click Farms Have Inflated Social Media Currency","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent videos explaining implications for Facebook's business model.","quote":"these two excellent videos from veritasium corroborate this article","text":"for those interested in this topic these two excellent videos from veritasium corroborate this article and go beyond to explain the stark implications for facebook's business model: facebook fraud: [LINK] the problem with facebook: [VIDEO] > From January 2013 to February 2014, a global team of researchers from the Max Planck Institute for Software Systems, Microsoft’s and AT&T’s research labs, as well as Boston and Northeastern Universities, conducted an experiment designed to determine just how often advertising campaigns resulted in likes from fake profiles. The researchers ran ten Facebook advertising campaigns, and when they analyzed the likes resulting from those campaigns, they found that 1,867 of the 2,767 likes—or about 67 percent—appeared to be illegitimate. After being informed of these suspicions, Facebook corroborated much of the team’s work by erasing 1,730 of the likes. Sympathetic researchers from a study run by the online marketing website Search Engine Journal have suggested that targeted Facebook advertisements can yield suspicious likes at a rate above 50 percent. In the fall of 2014, Professor Emiliano De Cristofaro of the University College of London presented research which found that even a page explicitly labeled as fake gained followers—the vast majority presumably bots. > The bot buildup can even affect companies that aren’t advertising with Facebook, but are just passively hoping their pages gain real fans. In 2014, Harvard University’s Facebook fan"},{"id":8381761,"author":"lingben","month":"2014-09","story":"With New Ad Platform, Facebook Opens Gates to Its Vault of User Data","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Watch before wasting money on Facebook ads.","quote":"before you waste any money, watch these two videos","text":"facebook ads are junk and have been proven to be so before you waste any money, watch these two videos: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7212372,"author":"lingben","month":"2014-02","story":"Facebook Fraud [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lays bare structural issues with Facebook's business model.","quote":"Well worth the watch.","text":"If you haven't yet watched Derek's earlier video about \"the problem with facebook\", you should: [VIDEO] because in it he lays bare several fundamental structural issues about facebook which 99% people don't realize. Well worth the watch. Unless fb changes drastically, it will die a surprisingly fast death."},{"id":12440532,"author":"vacri","month":"2016-09","story":"How we unnecessarily burned 20k with Facebook Ads","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis showing Facebook ad metrics are unreliable.","quote":"Definitely have a watch if you're considering spending on Facebook.","text":"Derek Muller burned money on Facebook ads, and did an analysis showing that their metrics are flat-out horseshit. Definitely have a watch if you're considering spending on Facebook. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10025857,"author":"lingben","month":"2015-08","story":"Why Facebook’s video theft problem can’t last","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct introduction to Facebook's hollow foundation.","quote":"Here are two videos that succinctly introduce the reality of facebook's mirage","text":"facebook as a platform and business is hollow, but very few realize it. this is similar to Enron, Madoff, the financial crisis, Groupon, etc. history has shown that 99.99999% of people totally miss these business models that are built on a MASSIVELY fraudulent or hollow foundation. When the music stops thousands and thousands of gallons of pixels will be spilt trying to explain how nobody saw their collapse coming and why they are not to blame. Here are two videos that succinctly introduce the reality of facebook's mirage: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9201935,"author":"Veedrac","month":"2015-03","story":"Reasons not to use Facebook","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting take on content distribution from Veritasium.","quote":"I found them pretty interesting listening","text":"Since this seems to just be a braindump, I might as well add Veritasium's comments on the matter: [VIDEO] [LINK] I found them pretty interesting listening from a content-distribution and -consumption side of things, rather than from a social-network perspective."},{"id":7638445,"author":"vacri","month":"2014-04","story":"Facebook earnings gain as ad sales surge 82%; CFO is stepping down","stance":"rec","why":"Breaks down fraudulent paid views from developing countries.","quote":"It's worth checking out his videos if you want more detail","text":"Veritasium on youtube has a couple of videos breaking down the numbers - not only do you have to pay to get views now, but the views delivered above and beyond what you usually get are fraudulent. He showed the usage of his page beforehand, with users clustered in Western countries, generally 20-40% of whom interacted with his page in some way. After paying for more views, they were all from developing countries, a profile of users he'd never seen before with very little (0-1%) engagement with his page. It's worth checking out his videos if you want more detail on the patterns he found. It's pretty clear that it's fraudulent. [VIDEO] (explores problems he sees with FB as a marketing platform) [LINK] (massive influx of unengaged developing-world pageviews)"},{"id":7451941,"author":"vacri","month":"2014-03","story":"The Free Marketing Gravy Train Is Over on Facebook","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Facebook withholding posts unless you pay.","quote":"Veritasium has an interesting related video","text":"Ambiguous headline: 'over' meaning 'finished'? Or 'over' meaning 'located'? Veritasium has an interesting related video explaining some of the Facebook shennanigans where they withhold your posts from a large amount of your subscribers if you're popular, unless you pay them money: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7452203,"author":"lingben","month":"2014-03","story":"The Free Marketing Gravy Train Is Over on Facebook","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Facebook's model is self-destructive.","quote":"in case you haven't watched these videos from Veritasium yet","text":"in case you haven't watched these videos from Veritasium yet: [VIDEO] [LINK] both videos show why facebook's model is inherently self-destructive and incoherent and will inevitably implode"},{"id":16740285,"author":"majewsky","month":"2018-04","story":"Mark Zuckerberg on Facebook’s hardest year, and what comes next","stance":"reference","why":"Derek Muller describes Facebook feed manipulation observations.","quote":"","text":"Facebook manipulates the feed to show only those posts which guarantee maximum user engagement. When a new post is made, the post is initially shown only to a small subset of fans. It will be shown to more people only when they engage with your post or if you cough up money to promote the post. Here's science journalist Derek Muller (of Veritasium) describing his observations on this issue in detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7547365,"author":"sparkie","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: Idea Sunday","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Facebook ads do not directly correspond to ROI.","quote":"","text":"I mean, we're practically all advertisers in some way or another - every time we share a promotion with our friends, or even just discuss a new product we're interested in. Most of the time we're not advertising for the sake of profit, but just sharing interests with like-minded people. Social media outlets attempt to tap into our general ignorance of advertising profitability - and they act as the middle-man between companies wishing to advertise, and the people who're sharing with friends - but facebook take all of the profit by inserting themselves between these relationships, and the users who are sharing don't notice they done a large part of the advertising work, for free. Facebook can obviously provide some usefulness, because it knows where you are, what your interests are, and your recent activities - so it can target you for specific promotions. A recent video by Vertitasium[1] showed how this isn't without problems though, and does not directly correspond to ROI for advertisers. (Likewise with many advertising models, there's always people trying to game them to earn a quick buck). Facebook provide the \"advertising seed\", by predicting who is likely to share. I see a different potential model that could arise with the gaining popularity of cryptocurrencies and electronic transactions though - one where advertisers only pay when it results in a direct ROI, by tracing electronic signatures through a public, distributed ledger. If a company wants to promote a product,"},{"id":7219170,"author":"j_s","month":"2014-02","story":"Facebook Puts a Downer on Upworthy","stance":"reference","why":"Says we are all advertisers; Facebook filters for profit.","quote":"","text":"This has been called The Problem with Facebook ('Facebook uses its filtering power to make money [...] we are all advertisers') by the guy currently on the frontpage for his 'Facebook Fraud' video ( [LINK] ). The Problem With Facebook [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oVfHeWTKjag",3],["pYXy-A4siMw",1],["ctS8FzqcRug",1],["ZeecOKBus3Q",1],["nnl6nY8YKHs",1],["L5pUA3LsEaw",1],["BickMFHAZR0",1]]},"OSGv2VnC0go":{"wy":"HN treats it as a must-watch classic for writing Pythonic code, though somewhat dated for modern Python.","q":{"x":"the excellent talk \"Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python\".","i":25890731,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25890731,"author":"jka","month":"2021-01","story":"Software engineering topics I changed my mind on","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk on simple, clean, thoughtful code with broad applicability.","quote":"the excellent talk \"Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python\".","text":"Your mention of simple and clean code makes me think of the excellent talk \"Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python\". Although it relates to Python programming, if the ideas and principles (and thoughtfulness and pace) from it could be applied to much more written code, then (I think) we as an industry and all our users would be in a better place. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15625058,"author":"bhoeting","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: Which programmer do you look up to?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hettinger talk on writing idiomatic Python.","quote":"I'm currently watching one of his videos [1] and it's amazing.","text":"I've never heard of Raymond Hettinger before but I'm currently watching one of his videos [1] and it's amazing. Thanks for sharing your list, I'll definitely look up some more of these people. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13354164,"author":"clusmore","month":"2017-01","story":"Origins of Python's “Functional” Features (2009)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Idiomatic Python talk relevant to comprehensions versus filter.","quote":"Must watch","text":"Must watch: [VIDEO] I believe that the list comprehension will be faster than filter, but as always, any time you replace readable code with unreadable code for performance reasons, you damn well better time it."},{"id":31569509,"author":"mumblemumble","month":"2022-05","story":"Python-patterns – A collection of design patterns/idioms in Python","stance":"rec","why":"Generic tips for writing pleasant Python code.","quote":"some classic talks from Raymond Hettinger.","text":"For an alternative perspective, allow me to present some classic talks from Raymond Hettinger. First, some generic tips on how to write Python code that's pleasant to work with: [VIDEO] And then this one digs more specifically into how not to write Python like it's Java, starting at 12:40 (with talking about why leading up to that): [LINK]"},{"id":29154691,"author":"greggyb","month":"2021-11","story":"Your users will do what you make easy","stance":"rec","why":"Example-heavy insight into Pythonic style.","quote":"they are example-heavy and give a good insight to a very Pythonic mind.","text":"These are a few videos which present Pythonic style. They do not form a proper curriculum, but they are example-heavy and give a good insight to a very Pythonic mind. 1. Beyond PEP 8 [LINK] 2. Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python [VIDEO]"},{"id":27268487,"author":"kzrdude","month":"2021-05","story":"Writing Pythonic Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Classic 2013 Hettinger talk, though somewhat dated for modern Python.","quote":"Raymond Hettinger's talks are great.","text":"Raymond Hettinger's talks are great. I know it's not a succint blog post, but they are out there are watching a video is very approachable. Maybe [LINK] (Dataclasses! We could cheekily say Python gets better at something Rust does - dataclasses makes Python better at records.) And [LINK] This talk (2013): [VIDEO] Unfortunately this video is now a bit out of touch with modern Python. Another (2013) classic: [LINK] it is very well known, and it has the very memorable advice: what's a class that only has one method? That should be a function!"},{"id":8667901,"author":"shackenberg","month":"2014-11","story":"Python idioms I wish I'd learned earlier","stance":"rec","why":"Hettinger's PyCon talk on iterators, dicts, named tuples and idiomatic Python.","quote":"Topics include: 'looping' with iterators to avoid creating new lists, dictionaries, named tuples and more","text":"If you were underwhelmed by this blog post have a look at: Transforming code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python by Raymond Hettinger at PyCon 2013 [LINK] and [VIDEO] Topics include: 'looping' with iterators to avoid creating new lists, dictionaries, named tuples and more"},{"id":8009063,"author":"msvalkon","month":"2014-07","story":"Anti-Patterns in Python Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Avoiding pitfalls and using nice language features","quote":"Great talk on avoiding some of the common pitfalls new python developers step in.","text":"Check out Raymond Hettingers Transforming Code into Beautiful, Idiomatic Python talk on youtube [1]. Great talk on avoiding some of the common pitfalls new python developers step in. Exposes some nice language features. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7367953,"author":"kriro","month":"2014-03","story":"Python Language Features and Tricks","stance":"rec","why":"Great Python talk with enjoyable style","quote":"Halfway through and this one seems great so far","text":"Great suggestion. Just searched for him on youtube and a couple of excellent looking talks popped up. Halfway through and this one seems great so far: [VIDEO] Really enjoy his style as well :)"},{"id":29719098,"author":"greggyb","month":"2021-12","story":"A Python Guide for the Ages","stance":"reference","why":"Hettinger traces for-else to Knuth and goto, suggests naming it nobreak.","quote":"Link to the talk","text":"Raymond Hettinger has a good section on this, tracing it back Knuth's discourse on structured programming in the face of 'goto'. He suggests calling the keyword 'nobreak', rather than 'else'. Inside any loop are a conditional and a jump. In pseudocode: if not <loop end condition> then <do loop things> <jump to top of loop> else //loop is done <do things in the case that the loop has terminated naturally> If we 'break' in the body of the loop for some reason, we will never hit the 'else' in this chunk of code. As Mr. Hettinger explains, this is obvious to anyone reading Knuth or coming from a 'goto' style of control flow. This is not an insult, but an observation. (Un)Fortunately, structured programming is the absolute norm now, and we learn looping constructs directly, rather than learning 'goto' and then building to looping constructs. Especially in a language with rich iteration protocols, such as Python, it is very much unapparent that the looping constructs are fancy wrappers around 'goto'. Link to the talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17170561,"author":"jimmytucson","month":"2018-05","story":"Python’s For - Else","stance":"reference","why":"Hettinger explains why for-else should have been called nobreak.","quote":"He says if he had a time machine he could tell Guido in the future we’re all using structured programming","text":"Here’s why (Hettinger): > In 1991, “else” was the obvious choice because of the traditional way compilers implemented while-loops: pastebin.com/tY35CTJ4 That is, “if I haven’t finished the loop, GOTO the body.” He says if he had a time machine he could tell Guido in the future we’re all using structured programming so no one will find “else” intuitive; call it “nobreak” instead: [VIDEO] Guido says if he had a time machine he would not have included the feature at all: [LINK] This whole thing was Knuth’s idea, not Guido’s: [LINK]"},{"id":11877101,"author":"smt88","month":"2016-06","story":"jQuery 3.0 Released","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"1) There's certainly not \"one way\" to iterate in Python[1], and they're not even consistently named (xrange is the iterator version of range, but izip is the iterator version of zip). 2) You can use Python in the browser[2][3][4 sort of]. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK] 3. [LINK] 4. [LINK]"},{"id":8051415,"author":"eddyg","month":"2014-07","story":"The optional “else” in Python loops","stance":"reference","why":"Explains loop else clause","quote":"I came to the comments to post the same thing[1]","text":"Glad you posted this. I came to the comments to post the same thing[1] because thinking of it as \"notfound\" makes remembering (and comprehending it) easier. :) [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["Pzl1B7nB9Kc",1],["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["RjfbvDXpFls",1],["2AXv49dDQJw",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["Jib2AmRb_rk",1],["u404SLJj7ig",1],["LZgyVadkgmI",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["EWX5B6cD4_4",1],["GDzBE6vz5r0",1],["F8sZRBdmqc0",1],["P76Vbsk_3J0",1],["5MgBikgcWnY",1],["pyX8kQ-JzHI",1],["rcwngbUrZNg",1]]},"gSPNRu4ZPvE":{"wy":"HN users repeatedly offer it as the best introduction to agroforestry as a climate and food solution.","q":{"x":"Ever since watching the video on syntropic agriculture which I bumped into by reading a comment here in HN","i":23796146,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23796146,"author":"reginaldo","month":"2020-07","story":"Investors say agroforestry is climate friendly and also profitable","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Syntropic agriculture works at small and large scales, improves marginal land","quote":"Ever since watching the video on syntropic agriculture which I bumped into by reading a comment here in HN","text":"Ever since watching the video on syntropic agriculture which I bumped into by reading a comment here in HN [1], I've been doing some research on the matter. The practice seems promising. It seems to work at both small and large scales, so it enables small farms, which are very hard to defend as a business in the US. It also has a component of improving the value of land, by making marginal (sandy) terrain into productive agricultural land. The other interesting fact is that the productivity per acre for the individual cultivars when planted together is higher than if they were the only thing being planted. I can see those things combining to drastically increase products. The part of the video that was the most interesting was the farmer saying \"this works. If I don't sell, I don't live, and I do sell\" (not an exact quote). Intriguing indeed. [1] [LINK] / [VIDEO]"},{"id":22170315,"author":"alisson","month":"2020-01","story":"Deep Decarbonization: A Realistic Way Forward on Climate Change","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Syntropic agriculture and its carbon storage in soil","quote":"I think for agriculture the way to go is syntropic agriculture","text":"I think for agriculture the way to go is syntropic agriculture: [VIDEO] I've seen people implementing this very successfully, I've been doing it myself for around 4 years now, and its very impressive how much carbon the soil stores after a few years on this practice."},{"id":37389698,"author":"myshpa","month":"2023-09","story":"Farms that create habitat key to food security and biodiversity","stance":"rec","why":"Gotsch reforested degraded pasture without external inputs","quote":"I'd also recommend Syntropic Agriculture, an agroforestry method pioneered by Ernst Gotsch.","text":"Very good movie. I'd also recommend Syntropic Agriculture, an agroforestry method pioneered by Ernst Gotsch. He personally reforested 500 hectares of degraded pastures and doesn't use any external resources such as pesticides and fertilizers. Instead, he employs methods like maximizing photosynthesis and selective pruning. As a result, his land now stands as one of the most fertile and biodiverse regions within the Atlantic rainforest, achieving even higher yields than traditional farms. As syntropic farmers often say, they grow the soil to grow the food. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] Another example would be the Natural Farming method pioneered by Masanobu Fukuoka. He also practices farming without the use of pesticides and herbicides, and his yields also surpass those of traditional farms. [LINK]"},{"id":29781577,"author":"beckman466","month":"2022-01","story":"Healthy soil is key to feeding the world","stance":"rec","why":"Ernst Gotsch and syntropic agroforestry","quote":"this video about Ernst Götsch and his 'syntropic' agroforestry is very exciting","text":"wish i could upvote this a hundred times. i love the work of Million Belay with The Alliance for Food Sovereignty in Africa (AFSA) ( [LINK] ) as well as with the Stockholm Resilience Agency. this video of his and others like it really drove home the important core reasons to focus on soil health: [LINK] ( [LINK] ) also i think this video about Ernst Götsch and his 'syntropic' agroforestry is very exciting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23631196,"author":"alisson","month":"2020-06","story":"What vertical farming and ag startups don't understand about agriculture","stance":"rec","why":"Big farmers building machines adapted to syntropic agriculture","quote":"There's an interesting video about it showing some big farmers here trying to build machines better adapted to this kind of agriculture","text":"Around 6 years ago I quit my job as a developer to dive into agriculture. I learned about syntropic agriculture systems and felt in love with it because: - You are able to work with space and time in a way to maximize yield (not 1 crop yield, but but multi crop) - It focus on being biodiverse - It builds forests So in this systems you will see rows of trees intercalated with rows of beans, corn, soy anything \"weedy\" or grasses... Harvest this small plants for many years, after a few years you harvest fruits, and after 2 decades you harvest the wood and start over. All with extensive pruning. This way you end up with better soil each time without machines or fertilizers (sure you can speed even more the process with them), its a type of agriculture focused on nature's processes instead of inputs. There's an interesting video about it showing some big farmers here trying to build machines better adapted to this kind of agriculture, this is the biggest bottleneck to scale because right now most machines are very focused on monocultures: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18630970,"author":"alisson","month":"2018-12","story":"Can We Grow More Food on Less Land?","stance":"rec","why":"Big producers adopting agroforestry to scale it","quote":"There's a great method called Agroforestry that uses forest floors to produce more food","text":"There's a great method called Agroforestry that uses forest floors to produce more food: Quick overview of a farmer's land that uses this method: [LINK] he harvest around 3x more cocoa per hectare than traditional methods and its one of the most valuable in the world right now. Big producers joining to make it more scalable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16521776,"author":"alisson","month":"2018-03","story":"Dirt Poor: Have Fruits and Vegetables Become Less Nutritious?","stance":"rec","why":"Building soil and edible forests instead of monoculture","quote":"We should be focusing into building soil and edible forest to harvest multiple floors","text":"We should be focusing into building soil and edible forest to harvest multiple floors instead of just that usual monoculture one: [VIDEO] If we look closely forest's soil usually gets better and better, I see some people replicate this and seen some 'agroforest' with more then 5 years old and its stunning how productive there lands are, how diverse and how the soil just get better without adding external stuff like fertilizer, its a process type of agriculture instead of an input one."},{"id":13951273,"author":"alisson","month":"2017-03","story":"UN experts denounce ‘myth’ that pesticides are necessary to feed the world","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes Ernst Gotsch's agroforestry work","quote":"here's a good video that summarize his work","text":"Sure! When I started learning about agroforestry I looked for english books on the subject but didn't find good ones, Gaia's Garden by Toby Hemenway have really good info you could use. But in portuguese I've so a few good PDFs here, I guess that's because of the work of Ernst Gotsch here in Brazil, here's a good video that summarize his work: [VIDEO] An agroforestry look like this: [LINK] Take very special attention to the ground on the video I linked above. So the main thing is the soil, the work will basically be always improving it by pilling up organic matter, from regular pruning and your organic waste too, the school must have good source of organic waste, also the city gardens/grass pruning are a good place to look for organic matter you could use on this project. Also, the more you plant together and at the same time the better. On the video you can see Bananas and Eucalyptus side by side, people think that Eucalyptus doesnt let anything grows near it, etc... but then I saw a lot of things fruiting just a few centimeters from Eucalyptus, because of the regular pruning. I'll send you an email so we can continue to talk about this."},{"id":28714240,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-10","story":"Treating beef like coal would make a big dent in greenhouse-gas emissions","stance":"reference","why":"Ernst Gotsch as alternative regenerative agriculture example","quote":"that and regenerative agriculture, agro-forestry, etc. Ernst Gotsch","text":"this is a half-baked ‘solution‘ that just makes eating meat a luxury. a thing only for the rich. it’ll be like crimes that are punished by a fine - they just become the ‘fee’ that allows the propertied class to do whatever they want. beef is deadly for humanity because of destructive water and land usage (Vice had a great video showing the Amazon deforestation due to beef production). edit: my point is that the propertied class will lower wages and raise commodity prices to be able to pay to keep eating an unsustainable amount of beef. instead we should restrict or ban industrial animal agriculture, while investing in educational material to help society transition away from beef. that and regenerative agriculture, agro-forestry, etc. Ernst Gotsch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28714528,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-10","story":"Treating beef like coal would make a big dent in greenhouse-gas emissions","stance":"reference","why":"Ernst Gotsch as alternative regenerative agriculture example","quote":"that and regenerative agriculture, agro-forestry, etc. Ernst Gotsch","text":"the point is that the propertied class will lower wages and raise commodity prices to pay to keep eating an unsustainable amount of beef. instead we should restrict or ban industrial animal agriculture, while investing in educational material to help society transition away from beef. that and regenerative agriculture, agro-forestry, etc. Ernst Gotsch: [VIDEO] i don’t want to give my children a dead planet either."},{"id":29013757,"author":"beckman466","month":"2021-10","story":"Stripe is kick-starting market for carbon removal","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Stripe Inc. is signing up to pay for carbon-removal technologies that haven’t been invented yet. > The payments company has formed a partnership with Deep Science Ventures, a London investment firm that specializes in building technology companies from the ground up. DSV will recruit scientists to develop ways to remove carbon dioxide from the atmosphere. If they come up with viable concepts, Stripe will be their first customer. why not focus on implementing already existing ecologically sustainable inventions and strategies? what i read here: rent-seeking tech founders aim to funnel profits they collected from the taxing of economic activity [1] towards a privately-funded lottery of hoped-for future tech. the techno-fix is dangerous because it 1) ignores existing systemic dysfunction: \"The current political economy is based on a false idea of “immaterial scarcity.” It believes that an exaggerated set of intellectual property monopolies – for [trade secrets,] copyrights, trademarks and patents – should restrain the sharing of scientific, social and economic innovations. Hence the system discourages human cooperation, excludes many people from benefiting from innovation and slows the collective learning of humanity. In an age of grave global challenges, the political economy keeps many practical alternatives sequestered behind private firewalls or unfunded if they cannot generate adequate profits.\" [2] and 2) it covertly wields the lone genius myth [3], that supposedly the S"},{"id":20649637,"author":"alisson","month":"2019-08","story":"To rein in global warming, healthy forests and sustainable diets are key, U.N","stance":"reference","why":"Shows people working machines together with forests","quote":"we are still figuring out a way of working with this stuff together with forests","text":"For industrialized I mean canned, over packaged, processed and refined stuff, also produces from vary far away on our daily diet, not against it but we do over consume this stuff. (Obs: thats what I see around me) But also, mono-crops are another issue which comes from industrialization too. I'm not against machines and robots, but we are still figuring out a way of working with this stuff together with forests: [VIDEO] Here farms acreage is increasing, more mono-crops and less forests, all with the help of industrialization, crops from other side of the world and endemic fruits, nuts disappearing and because of that loosing habitats. At least eucalyptus is winning, even though native wood is better in so many ways, but the iron industry here don't buy the charcoal if not from mono-crops of eucalyptus."}],"r":[["UcN7SGGoCNI",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["G0R09YzyuCI",1],["o0VrZPBskpg",1],["sI1C9DyIi_8",1],["5WPB2u8EzL8",1],["qPb_0JZ6-Rc",1],["d_zt3kGW1NM",1],["h6fcK_fRYaI",1],["_2khAmMTAjI",1]]},"9QMGAtxUlAc":{"wy":"HN treats it as a classic, must-watch talk for any discussion of company values, and especially for its takedown of Amazon's leadership principles.","q":{"x":"No discussion of company values would be complete without a link to Bryan Cantrill’s classic talk,","i":30374551,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30374551,"author":"nonfamous","month":"2022-02","story":"What are your company's anti-values?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic talk essential to any discussion of company values.","quote":"No discussion of company values would be complete without a link to Bryan Cantrill’s classic talk,","text":"No discussion of company values would be complete without a link to Bryan Cantrill’s classic talk, “Principles of Technology Leadership”. [VIDEO] Uber once had a stated company value of “Always be hustling”. Really."},{"id":19160579,"author":"warp_factor","month":"2019-02","story":"Anti-Application video for YC120","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cantrill's talk on company principles from an engineer with integrity.","quote":"His talk on companies principles is a must-watch","text":"I love Bryan Cantrill for being that classy bad-ass engineer with so much integrity. His talk on companies principles is a must-watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16304930,"author":"kbenson","month":"2018-02","story":"Talks I have given","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cantrill's most personal talk, uplifting, funny and important.","quote":"this is an extremely good talk[1].","text":"Before we get to the list: if you only watch one talk of mine, please watch Principles of Technology Leadership (slides) presented at Monktoberfest 2017. This is the only talk that I have asked family and friends to watch, as it represents my truest self — or what I aspire that self to be, anyway. Let me just say, this is an extremely good talk[1]. Very uplifting and depressing at different times. It goes through serious turns, and very funny periods. Ultimately though, the message is very important and worth hearing. I highly recommend it. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30203812,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2022-02","story":"Amazon Pip Horror Story","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of why Amazon's leadership principles are not real principles.","quote":"Brian Cantrill (of 0x1de computers) gives a great breakdown of how sacrosanct Amazon's leadership principles are","text":"Yeah, vibes with my experience working there. Toxicity beyond pale. Good riddens, they contact me every week to try to get me back. Fuck off Amazon, you have no integrity, you abuse your employees from white collar to blue collar. You literally fired people who emailed Jeff Bezos to report abuses, after you told them to, when the NY Times \"crying at desk\" expose came out. Bezos instilled this shite culture into Amazon. And his midlet pawns carried out his half-baked decrees like those fucking wet noodle \"Leadership Principles\" which make no actual sense and aren't principles at all in any sense of the word. Brian Cantrill (of 0x1de computers) gives a great breakdown of how sacrosanct Amazon's leadership principles are: [VIDEO] Principles are things like Integrity, Honesty, Respect -- not Amazon's fuckwit bulletpoints like \"Be right a lot\" or \"Frugality.\" Technologically, AWS is built like a fucking house of cards with spit, tape, and glue. There is no real cohesion or organization, just a bunch of teams doing their own practices and gluing their rando codebase into the Yellow Console. Sorry, but just making the frontend look unified doesn't undo what the backend spaghetti is... That's why the outages of late will continue. It's all bubbling up from the absolute toxicity in lack of basic humanism at the core."},{"id":27701512,"author":"dev_snd","month":"2021-07","story":"Amazon announce two new leadership principles","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk arguing Amazon's leadership principles are unhelpful; Amazon part near minute 26.","quote":"I recommend watching the whole talk","text":"There's a great talk by Brian Cantrill about Leadership principles [0] which also includes a discussion of the Amazon Leadership principles. He argues that Amazon's leadership principles actually aren't helpful and don't make much sense and I tend to agree. [0] [VIDEO] (The part about Amazon starts around minute 26, but I recommend watching the whole talk)"},{"id":21519006,"author":"Fellshard","month":"2019-11","story":"No Shenanigans","stance":"rec","why":"Lambastes Amazon and Uber core principles as vague and easily ignored.","quote":"He gives Amazon a lambasting over their core principles, and Uber even moreso.","text":"This subject and the utter vagueness of the values presented here require me to bring up Bryan Cantrill's talk, 'Principles of Technology Leadership'.[1] He gives Amazon a lambasting over their core principles, and Uber even moreso. It's both funny and sobering to examine how what seems like a useful and valuable foundation ends up being flimsy and easy to ignore when made vague enough. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19235736,"author":"jka","month":"2019-02","story":"Key Values – Find Engineering Teams That Share Your Values","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on principles versus values, relevant to a values-matching site.","quote":"It seems to resonate pretty closely with what you're building","text":"Is the Key:Values team (nice choice of name by the way!) familiar with Bryan Cantrill's talk on 'Principles of Technology Leadership'? [VIDEO] It seems to resonate pretty closely with what you're building, so if it helps align or educate anyone (candidates, recruiters, or the team) that'd be neat. Edit: FWIW, the talk uses the term 'principle' to indicate what your site calls a 'value'; in practice I've seen tech companies talk about their 'core values' and so I think yours is very valid."},{"id":18379787,"author":"faitswulff","month":"2018-11","story":"Quitting Amazon 5 months after I started","stance":"rec","why":"Funny takedown of Amazon's leadership principles.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has a sometimes hilarious takedown of Amazon's","text":"> Supposedly, every employee should be guided by the leadership principles during their day-to-day routine. The principles actually make a lot of sense, when used appropriately. As time went, I discovered that the most common application of the principles was to creatively find a leadership principle that best supports the situation. Bryan Cantrill has a sometimes hilarious takedown of Amazon's \"leadership principles\" here, which definitely corroborates this blog post: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16686903,"author":"z3t4","month":"2018-03","story":"Arizona Government Suspends Uber's Self Driving Cars from Roads","stance":"rec","why":"Bryan Cantrill talk on technology leadership principles, related to the Uber thread.","quote":"Watch the whole video, it's good!","text":"Related talk by Bryan Cantrill (Principles of Technology Leadership) Watch the whole video, it's good! [VIDEO]"},{"id":42952958,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2025-02","story":"Oracle justified its JavaScript trademark with Node.js–now it wants that ignored","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker cites own talk among works on moral decay in tech.","quote":"I will continue to speak my truth about the moral decay in tech","text":"I am glad to see that many people are being correcting you, but just to put a sharp point on it: I am using that metaphor to condemn Ellison, not to condone or accept him. Unfortunately, since that talk in 2011, what was an aberration (albeit a broadly unspoken one) has since -- tragically -- become the norm. I refuse to accept this, however: I will continue to speak my truth about the moral decay in tech, and why we must hold ourselves to a higher standard.[0][1][2][3][4] And I obviously agree with your indignation that poor corporate behavior should not be the status quo! [0] Principles of Technology Leadership , [VIDEO] (2017) [1] Andreessen's Corollary: Ethical Dilemmas in Software Engineering , [LINK] (2019) [2] (Anti-)application Video for YC120 , [LINK] (2019) [3] Coming Of Age , [LINK] (2022) [4] Intelligence is not Enough , [LINK] (2023)"},{"id":37105342,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2023-08","story":"How a startup loses its spark","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker points to own talk as statement of his beliefs on principles.","quote":"I think I would point you instead to my beliefs about principles[0],","text":"I think I would point you instead to my beliefs about principles[0], values[1], and (because you are young), the promise and peril of youth[2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":28513660,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2021-09","story":"I refuse to let Amazon define Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the distinction between principles and values.","quote":"There is an important distinction to be made here between principles and values","text":"The problem is that they are bromides and claptrap, not principles. There is an important distinction to be made here between principles and values, which they are not making in part because Amazon themselves do not understand the difference.[0] Having a community define its values is important[1], but this is emphatically not the way to do it -- and these are not it besides. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":27699758,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-07","story":"Amazon announce two new leadership principles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the argument that these are values, not principles.","quote":"","text":"Tim O'Reilly's blog post (before this announcement, which likely comes on back of Bezos' final annual letter to shareholders [0]) contrasting Ingram's and Amazon's approach towards the marketplaces they operate in sheds light on why the inclusion of these principles is substantial [1]. Leadership principles, though in tension with each other (and so, some argue they really shouldn't be classified as \"principles\" but rather \"values\" [2]), permeates the culture of performance and team building at Amazon; and that makes me think this is not only a welcome but potentially a company-defining change. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26441739,"author":"bcantrill","month":"2021-03","story":"Oxide cofounder: I pay everyone in my company the same salary – $180,250","stance":"reference","why":"Author cites own talk on organizational values.","quote":"Mistaken or crooked organizational values can be more damaging than absent or implicit ones.","text":"For whatever it's worth, the only (real) difference between the blog entry and the op-ed that Business Insider asked to run is this closing paragraph that I added at their suggestion: Some of the specifics of our model may or may not be a fit — we appreciate that uniform compensation is an unusual luxury, and we know that transparency of compensation can be a painful change to effect in an organization that has no history of it (or worse, has secretly been rewarding similar work vastly inequitably). But we do believe — fervently — that compensation reflects a company's values, and that it's absolutely essential that companies elucidate those values. If your company has no formal values, there's a tremendous opportunity to define them. This opportunity is not without peril, however, as my Monktoberfest 2017 talk on \"Principles of Technology Leadership\"[0] describes: Mistaken or crooked organizational values can be more damaging than absent or implicit ones. Don't define your values in terms of aspirations, aphorisms, slogans, or (because it apparently has to be said!) memes. Rather, they should be an honest expression of the shared values that bind the organization. Once your true values are elucidated, reevaluate your compensation in light of them. This isn't easy to do (especially because it will be a foregone conclusion that any reexamination will not result in the reduction of anyone's compensation), but your employees — current and future — will thank you for it. [0] [VID"},{"id":19161466,"author":"eurticket","month":"2019-02","story":"Anti-Application video for YC120","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as an example of Cantrill's talks on tone and messaging.","quote":"Bryan's talks[0] usually involve tone and messaging","text":"Misdirected, not over the top, it's very appropriate especially when it was a sponsored project and they were getting called out as if Ben was their employee and they were cosigning whatever Ben's views were. Regardless how you feel on gendered pronouns, if you're writing documentation regarding all users it seems like a no brainier to actually involve all users in the language, something which Bryan puts great significance into. Bryan's talks[0] usually involve tone and messaging (as well as being triggered and raged) as key points. But back to the drama issue. The problem about this whole thing isn't about the pronouns really, it was the actions that Ben took even after it was explained and the hard stance Joyent felt it needed to take on the issue in the aftermath. Ben, if we're giving him the benefit of the doubt (because he isn't a native English speaker) to word something 'wrong', is a simple fix, who cares. - The real part of this whole mess was because he tried reversing a commit that was made by the project lead after it was worded to include more users. Ben was the main agitator in this almost 6 year old mess regardless of his amount of contributions. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["coFIEH3vXPw",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["YKuRkGkf5HU",1],["5Vt8zqhHe_c",1],["fzBRYsiyxjI",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["5P5Mk_IggE0",1],["ooNzRrHA9VY",1],["aKLntZcp27M",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["u404SLJj7ig",1],["LZgyVadkgmI",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["PpG0z-npFIY",1],["Nnuq9PXbywA",1]]},"fHNmRkzxHWs":{"wy":"A perennial recommendation for anyone learning performance optimization, cited whenever hash tables, cache behavior or STL containers come up.","q":{"x":"I'm also a big fan of \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\" by Chandler Carruth at CppCon 2014.","i":39579432,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39579432,"author":"slavik81","month":"2024-03","story":"Ask HN: How can I learn about performance optimization?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter is a big fan of this performance talk","quote":"I'm also a big fan of \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\" by Chandler Carruth at CppCon 2014.","text":"Strongly agree. That's perhaps less true for the software I work on these days (lapack), but I've seen that so many times over my career. I'm also a big fan of \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\" by Chandler Carruth at CppCon 2014. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8705962,"author":"amaks","month":"2014-12","story":"Std::string half of all allocations in the Chrome browser process","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers inefficient standard library containers like unordered_map on modern CPUs","quote":"See the great talk on CppCon 2014 about those issues","text":"There are many things in the standard C++ library which are named incorrectly (std::string), awkwardly (std::unordered_map) and and implemented inefficiently from the perspective of modern CPUs (same std::unordered_map, which uses linked list for the underlying hash table buckets). See the great talk on CppCon 2014 about those issues - [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24364139,"author":"alexhutcheson","month":"2020-09","story":"On modern hardware the min-max heap beats a binary heap","stance":"rec","why":"Efficiency with algorithms and performance with data structures","quote":"you'd probably really enjoy Chandler Carruth's \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\" talk from CppCon 2014","text":"If you haven't already seen it, you'd probably really enjoy Chandler Carruth's \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\" talk from CppCon 2014: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14634281,"author":"adrianN","month":"2017-06","story":"Diving into the world of hash tables","stance":"rec","why":"Nice talk on carefulness with dictionaries in performance-sensitive code","quote":"See for example this nice talk by Chandler Carruth","text":"Yes, if you write performance sensitive code you have to be very careful with dictionaries. See for example this nice talk by Chandler Carruth [VIDEO]"},{"id":11721276,"author":"humanrebar","month":"2016-05","story":"Why I don't spend time with Modern C++ anymore","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among talks on C++ efficiency and performance","quote":"","text":"Here's a few: - \"Tuning C++: Benchmarks, and CPUs, and Compilers! Oh My!\" [LINK] - \"Understanding Compiler Optimization\" [LINK] - \"Optimizing the Emergent Structures of C++\" [LINK] - \"Efficiency with Algorithms\" [VIDEO] The first one is really good if you've never benchmarked anything in a Linux environment. Or if you otherwise want to learn how to investigate how C++ gets turned into machine code."},{"id":11802177,"author":"amaks","month":"2016-05","story":"C++ for Games: Performance, Allocations and Data Locality","stance":"rec","why":"Explains which data structures are slow on modern CPUs","quote":"some data structures are just not fast on modern CPUs, so they have to be chosen wisely. Here is good talk","text":"STL is not inherently slow per se, some data structures are just not fast on modern CPUs, so they have to be chosen wisely. Here is good talk: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":10018701,"author":"sanderjd","month":"2015-08","story":"The Case for Controlled Side Effects","stance":"rec","why":"Talk that made the commenter rethink linked versus contiguous data structures","quote":"I've just watched a couple cppcon talks ([0], [1]) and I'm feeling a bit drunk on the kool-aid.","text":"Also, those allocations imply linked, rather than contiguous, data structures, which (often? always?) imply poor cache interaction, which is a major source of inefficiency. Would love some more perspectives on this, as I've just watched a couple cppcon talks ([0], [1]) and I'm feeling a bit drunk on the kool-aid. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":8979237,"author":"yoklov","month":"2015-02","story":"The Care and Feeding of C++'s Dragons (2013) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"A great and actionable CppCon performance talk for most developers","quote":"Chandler Carruth's CPPCon talk [0] this year was also great","text":"Chandler Carruth's CPPCon talk [0] this year was also great (surprisingly, as I had a fairly low opinion of most of the others). It wasn't better than Mike Acton's [1], but I suspect that it was more actionable for most devs. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":26436215,"author":"alexhutcheson","month":"2021-03","story":"Drop millions of allocations by using a linked list (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Supports point that scattered allocations cause cache misses","quote":"See Chandler Carruth’s talk from CppCon 2014","text":"But then your objects are scattered all over the address space, which means that objects you’re accessing in sequence are unlikely to be in the same cache line, so you pay a cache miss on every single access. Having (maybe?) more efficient malloc/free in exchange for dramatically increasing the cost of accessing your data is not a good trade-off for most access patterns. See Chandler Carruth’s talk from CppCon 2014: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21082491,"author":"inetknght","month":"2019-09","story":"Designing far memory data structures: think outside the box","stance":"reference","why":"Presents a table of approximate round-trip times for data layers","quote":"In the talk he presented a table with (approximately) round-trip-times of various data layers.","text":"This talk seems to me to follow a similar line of thinking to the one I saw presented by Chandler Carruth at the 2014 C++ conference [0]. In the talk he presented a table with (approximately) round-trip-times of various data layers. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17914610,"author":"chillee","month":"2018-09","story":"Benchmarking C++ Sets","stance":"reference","why":"Shows STL unordered_map is slower than it needs to be","quote":"C++ STL unordered_map is so much slower than it needs to be","text":"Somewhat interestingly, C++ STL unordered_map is so much slower than it needs to be: [VIDEO] Using one of the policy-based data structures instantly gets you about an order magnitude of speedup (for accesses)."},{"id":14633907,"author":"tjalfi","month":"2017-06","story":"Diving into the world of hash tables","stance":"reference","why":"Covers unordered_map performance and endorses alternatives","quote":"Chandler Carruth talks about unordered_map performance[1] in his CppCon 2014 talk","text":"Chandler Carruth talks about unordered_map performance[1] in his CppCon 2014 talk \"Efficiency with Algorithms, Performance with Data Structures\". He endorses most of the other alternatives you mentioned. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11799932,"author":"santaclaus","month":"2016-05","story":"C++ for Games: Performance, Allocations and Data Locality","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses why unordered_map is forced into chaining","quote":"This comes up in one of Chandler Carruth's talks [1].","text":"I think C++'s built in unordered_map has to use chaining due to restrictions on iterators mandated for standard library containers, which is a drag. You've still got to roll your own open addressed container. :( This comes up in one of Chandler Carruth's talks [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11805346,"author":"adrianN","month":"2016-05","story":"Searching 20 GB/sec: Systems Engineering Before Algorithms (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Chandler on writing good code that can be profiled","quote":"See for example what Chandler has to say about writing good code","text":"Uniformly shitty code is hard to profile. See for example what Chandler has to say about writing good code [VIDEO]"},{"id":10268672,"author":"sanderjd","month":"2015-09","story":"C++ vs. OCaml: Ray tracer comparison (2005)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on STL data structures and why unordered_map uses buckets","quote":"","text":"I'm pretty curious about this: have memory access patterns and cache awareness become more of a big deal in the past decade or so, or has it only recently reached my awareness? I studied CS in the early 2000s and while I was aware of various trade-offs between contiguous and linked data structures, it's only in the last few years that the importance of cache (and non-\"system\" languages' widespread flouting of its importance) has really bubbled up in my consciousness. It's certainly likely that I just didn't take the right kinds of classes, or that I did but just missed or failed to really internalize this, but I'm curious if something else is going on. Has the bottleneck increasingly become memory rather than CPU? Have we more thoroughly exhausted other areas of optimization? I was thinking about this while watching a talk[0] given by someone from Google about data structures in the STL, which decried that the standardized specification for `std::unordered_map` more or less forces an implementation to use buckets instead of the more cache-friendly technique of probing. I'm sure that the people who standardized it were very competent, which makes me think that, at least at the time, buckets were thought to be the better implementation. [0]: [VIDEO] Edit: Added video link."}],"r":[["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["4AfRAVcThyA",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["PorfLSr3DDI",1],["lkgszkPnV8g",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["AchISJUKfH4",1],["eR34r7HOU14",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["rX0ItVEVjHc",1]]},"s86-Z-CbaHA":{"wy":"Recommended as an accurate, entertaining on-ramp to set theory and infinity, worth pausing and rewatching.","q":{"x":"This vsauce video is actually a really accurate (and entertaining!) introduction to set theory","i":28439875,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28439875,"author":"Aicy","month":"2021-09","story":"Intermediate Algebra","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accurate and entertaining introduction to set theory and ordinals","quote":"This vsauce video is actually a really accurate (and entertaining!) introduction to set theory","text":"Learn set theory! Specifically ordinals (just one formal defintion of a number) This vsauce video is actually a really accurate (and entertaining!) introduction to set theory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40797838,"author":"djvdq","month":"2024-06","story":"Banach–Tarski Paradox","stance":"rec","why":"Nice explanation of the Banach-Tarski paradox","quote":"years ago I watched this video from Vsauce about this very topic, and IIRC it was explained quite nicely","text":"I don't know if this will explain your questions, but years ago I watched this video from Vsauce about this very topic, and IIRC it was explained quite nicely: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34484499,"author":"josephcsible","month":"2023-01","story":"The Point of the Banach Tarski Theorem","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes the sphere as infinitely thin filaments","quote":"It's an infinite number of infinitely thin filaments from the center of the sphere going outward in every direction.","text":"[VIDEO] nicely visualizes it. It's an infinite number of infinitely thin filaments from the center of the sphere going outward in every direction."},{"id":33697480,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2022-11","story":"The Simple Geometry Behind Brownie Bake Offs and Equal Areas","stance":"rec","why":"Banach-Tarski explained with some technical details, not too many","quote":"If you like that, I recommend to watch the video by Vsause","text":"Note that the OP is about cuts that are nice, cutting polygons into smaller polygons, that could be done using a scissor. The article you linked is about using magical cuts that be non measurable, that look like magical interlocking 2D sponges. They are somewhat like the figures in the gif, but they actually more weird. It's somewhat similar to the Banach–Tarski Paradox. If you like that, I recommend to watch the video by Vsause [VIDEO] that explains the Banach–Tarski Paradox with some technical details, but not too many."},{"id":31135510,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2022-04","story":"I stopped working on black hole information loss","stance":"rec","why":"Half-hour Banach-Tarski explainer worth rewatching","quote":"you may need to pause them and rewatch them a few times to understand the details.","text":"I agree that most videos in YouTube simplify the problem too much or are just wrong. My recommendations anyway: * Mathologer: \" Ramanujan: Making sense of 1+2+3+... = -1/12 and Co. \" [LINK] * Vsauce: \" The Banach–Tarski Paradox \" [VIDEO] Each one is like half an hour long, but you may need to pause them and rewatch them a few times to understand the details."},{"id":23732052,"author":"shezi","month":"2020-07","story":"Sphere Eversion","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how far visual explanation of Banach-Tarski can go","quote":"Have a look at how vsauce explains Banach-Tarsky [1]","text":"I have done differential topology and geometry for a time in University. There is a lot of visualisation going on in every lecture every day, even if it is not computer visualization. Everything in difftop is drawn at some point, just so you can get an understanding of what's happening. In fact, I would say that topology and subfields are formalisations of these beautiful and silly visual ideas. One problem, however is that visualizations are by necessity simplifications, even in pure 2d cases like this one. That's not a sphere, that's a tessellation of one. Everything on a screen is differential and \"smooth\" (or actually, everything is discrete and thus not differentiable), but some things are symbols for \"not differentiable here\". Even these break down in pathological cases, singularities and such. This doesn't make the visualisation any less useful, but IMHO it can in most cases not be \"the\" reasoning, only a guide to reasoning. Besides,as the article points out, there are many non-constructive proofs that don't have any visuals. Have a look at how vsauce explains Banach-Tarsky [1] or any of 3blue1brown's excellent videos to see the edges of what's possible with visualisations. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13623711,"author":"espeed","month":"2017-02","story":"The Essence of Linear Algebra [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Mind-blowing math video among a list of STEM channels","quote":"(mindblown)","text":"Other good STEM video channels/series: Dave Ackley's series \"Hyperspace Academy\", \"Robust First Computing\", and \"Artificial Life\" are well animated: [LINK] Vsause's video on \"The Banach–Tarski Paradox\": [VIDEO] (mindblown) XylyXylyX channel's series on \"What is a Tensor?\" and \"What is a Manifold?\": [LINK] Socratica's series on \"Abstract Algebra\": [LINK] Mathologer's channel on all-things math: [LINK] Ben Garside's series \"Vector Spaces\": [LINK] PatrickJMT's channel on calculus, game theory...(too many to list): [LINK] Numenta's ( [LINK] ) series \"HTM School\" on the Hierarchical temporal memory ( [LINK] ) model for ANNs: [LINK] Dan Shiffman's series \"The Nature of Code: Simulating Natural Systems with Processing\": [LINK]"},{"id":40802127,"author":"nayuki","month":"2024-06","story":"The Point of the Banach Tarski Theorem (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Explainer video on the paradox","quote":"(explainer video)","text":"Vsauce: The Banach–Tarski Paradox (24m13s) [2015-08-01]: [VIDEO] (explainer video) Matematikrevyen: Matematikrevyen 2011: Banach-Tarski (3m42s) [2011-12-30]: [LINK] (music video)"},{"id":34483353,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2023-01","story":"The Point of the Banach Tarski Theorem","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I discovered the Banach-Tarski theorem via Vsauce[0]. It was interesting but I couldn't get the significance of it.","text":"I'm glad to have found this post. I discovered the Banach-Tarski theorem via Vsauce[0]. It was interesting but I couldn't get the significance of it. It either didn't seem like an unexpected result or too esoteric to appreciate. There's phrasing in the post that could be misunderstood (later clarified) but can leave unclarity from assumed understanding of the earlier description. > In R3, given a solid ball B of radius R, it is possible to partition B into finitely many pieces such that those pieces can be reassembled to form two solid balls B1 and B2 each of radius R. \"finitely many pieces\" could be confusing because though we may be talking about 6 'pieces' those pieces have an uncountable infinity of radial line slices. It's also missing the rigid motion part which is key. The part that didn't seem surprising (and is considered trivial) was in handling uncountable infinities of things. The 'number' of points on a line [0, 1) is the same as the number on a line [0, 2) so I wouldn't be surprised to map points from [0, 1) to [0, 2) filling the latter without 'gaps'. Similarly for areas. But what the theorem is saying is that this kind of mapping doesn't work in R1 nor R2 but does work in R3 (with rigid motions). The part that makes B-T surprising is that the extra volume/ball can be constructed with rigid motions of those uncountably infinite sets. This is where it seems beyond me to appreciate: that one or two balls have the same uncountably number of radial line slices is c"},{"id":31799943,"author":"yakubin","month":"2022-06","story":"Why does science news suck so much?","stance":"reference","why":"Long-form videos like this one perform well","quote":"one of, if not the most popular Vsauce video is the one about the Banach-Tarski paradox[6], which is almost 30 minutes long.","text":"> The medium of YouTube is not conducive to lengthy expositions I've actually found a couple channels on YT specialising in lengthy reviews/essays, which I find very good. Off the top of my head: 1. Whitelight[1] - game reviews/critiques/analyses. Particularly worthy of note are the Assassin's Creed Unity[2] (1.5h), Batman Arkham City[3] (3h 10min) and Watch Dogs[4] (1h 15min) reviews. 2. MauLer[5] - critiques of mostly Star Wars, but sometimes also other mainstream films. The ongoing series of TFA critiques has 4 parts so far (there is going to be at least 6 total), each part taking anywhere between 2 and 4 hours. I also feel that I'm forgetting some, which I don't watch regularly, but periodically am reminded about their existence and then after a long break spend several days watching, to then forget about them again. But the channels I listed aren't fringe. Quite the contrary, they're quite popular. I think it's also interesting that one of, if not the most popular Vsauce video is the one about the Banach-Tarski paradox[6], which is almost 30 minutes long. His other videos also show this trend, where the long-form ones seem to get more views in general. And those are just essays/reviews/etc. There is a whole genre of podcasts on youtube dedicated to 3-4h in-depth interviews. Everybody knows a whole bunch of them, therefore I don't even need to list any examples here to support that. So it seems that YouTube is a pretty good place for long-form in-depth exploration of what"},{"id":13844170,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-03","story":"Cantor diagonalisation","stance":"reference","why":"Counterintuitive logic of infinities","quote":"it's so counterintuitive that it can seem like evidence that something in the logic of infinities must be wrong.","text":"The logic embedded in Supertasks and the Banach-Tarski Paradox has always seemed strange. It's not flawed, but it's so counterintuitive that it can seem like evidence that something in the logic of infinities must be wrong. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9992433,"author":"ihm","month":"2015-08","story":"The Banach–Tarski Paradox [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Briefly shows paradoxical decomposition of the free group","quote":"which is flashed briefly in the video here[0] but sadly not discussed at length.","text":"I actually think it's very possible to have an \"intuitive understanding\" of Banach Tarski (I would say I have one, but perhaps we disagree on what is mean by such an understanding). My \"intuitive understanding\" of this comes via an intuitive understanding of a paradoxical decomposition of the free group and its Cayley graph, which is flashed briefly in the video here[0] but sadly not discussed at length. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gM95HHI4gLk",1],["L3LMbpZIKhQ",1],["aHx-XUA6f9g",1],["3Y2weLDiUWw",1],["7ZGCPap7GkY",1],["YyIQKBzIuBY",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["XeJSXfXep4M",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["xnez6tloNSQ",1],["jcKRGpMiVTw",1],["zUyH3XhpLTo",1],["kRlhlCWplqk",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["R2paSGQRwvo",1],["LmlAYnFF_s8",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["EK32jo7i5LQ",1]]},"zjkBMFhNj_g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the dense, approachable starting point for understanding LLMs and often cites it for specific claims about scaling and training.","q":{"x":"Start with his short intro to LLMs, only 1 hour long but is dense with valuable stuff","i":40687717,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40687717,"author":"mindwok","month":"2024-06","story":"Why has LLM progress seemingly stalled around the GPT-4 level? Or has it?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short, dense one-hour intro to LLMs.","quote":"Start with his short intro to LLMs, only 1 hour long but is dense with valuable stuff","text":"All of Andrej Karpathy's stuff on YouTube is absolutely top tier. He's a rare example of a brilliant contributor in his field who is an equally brilliant educator. Start with his short intro to LLMs, only 1 hour long but is dense with valuable stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39505819,"author":"FlyingAvatar","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Do LLMs get \"better\" with more processing power and or time per request?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers System 1 vs System 2 thinking in LLMs.","quote":"I highly recommended watching Andrej Karpathy's Intro to LLMs talk","text":"Short answer is No. I highly recommended watching Andrej Karpathy's Intro to LLMs talk, particularly the section on System 1 vs System 2 thinking. Long story short, what you are describing, using more processing to prepare a better response, is something that is an area of interest, but is not currently part of ChatGPT (or any other LLM that I am aware of). See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38407783,"author":"shrimpx","month":"2023-11","story":"Generative AI for Beginners","stance":"strong_rec","why":"High-level LLM overview with extensive discussion of attack vectors.","quote":"an amazing high-level overview of LLMs, including extensive discussion about attack vectors","text":"Andrej Karpathy's \"Zero to Hero\" series on YouTube is the ultimate guide to building LLMs. Extremely information-dense but as complete as it gets: [LINK] Also, an amazing high-level overview of LLMs, including extensive discussion about attack vectors, that he published a couple days ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48679374,"author":"philipkglass","month":"2026-06","story":"The Unbearable Cheapness of Open Weight Models","stance":"rec","why":"Longer, more technical introduction to what LLM weights are.","quote":"Longer and slightly more technical, \"Intro to Large Language Models\" by Andrej Karpathy","text":"The weights are just numbers. I don't what technical background you have in other areas of computing, but I think that this is a good, short introduction that doesn't assume too much: [LINK] To quote part of it, Training a model can be thought of as tuning the dials on a really big machine. The way that a language model behaves is entirely determined by these many different continuous values, usually called parameters or weights. Longer and slightly more technical, \"Intro to Large Language Models\" by Andrej Karpathy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39909829,"author":"sk11001","month":"2024-04","story":"Ask HN: Why has ChatGPT a bias towards correctness?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the pretraining and tuning stages of LLM training.","quote":"Look into the training process -","text":"Look into the training process - [VIDEO] The goal of the initial pretraining phase is to make it good at predicting the next word. The rest of the training process is aimed at making it (1) helpful and (2) as correct as possible. I think some people oversimplify things by calling LLMs \"next token predictors\" and they leave out the tuning towards helpfulness and correctness."},{"id":40159206,"author":"runjake","month":"2024-04","story":"Ask HN: 16 yo Nephew, in E. Africa, wants to train an LLM with on disk Wikipedia","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable one-hour intro to LLMs.","quote":"Karpathy has an approachable communication style.","text":"In addition to the other great suggestions, point him to Karpathy's YouTube channel[1]. Karpathy has an approachable communication style. Here's his \"1 hour intro to LLMs\" video: [VIDEO] 1. [LINK]"},{"id":40188612,"author":"tikkun","month":"2024-04","story":"Ask HN: AI Training for Executive Level","stance":"rec","why":"Practitioner-led LLM intro, better than university offerings.","quote":"I suspect these videos would be higher quality than most offerings from universities or business schools","text":"I suspect these videos would be higher quality than most offerings from universities or business schools (given that it's a fast moving field, and most of the true experts are practitioners, and so if they're teaching, they're not teaching actual classes, at most they're doing youtube videos or interviews): - [LINK] (Jeremy Howard Hacker's Guide to LLMs) - [VIDEO] (Karpathy Intro to LLMs)"},{"id":38653228,"author":"jpdus","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: AI/ML papers to catch up with current state of AI?","stance":"rec","why":"Dense one-hour busy person's intro to LLMs.","quote":"the recent 1h \"busy persons intro\" by Andrei Karpathy is great + dense as well","text":"Hey, imho best overall technical intro to LLMs (I guess that´s your main interest as you mentioned qlora + llama) is by Simon Willis [1]. Additionally or if you prefer videos, the recent 1h \"busy persons intro\" by Andrei Karpathy is great + dense as well [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] EDIT: Maybe I misunderstood as you asked about papers, not general intros. I don´t think that reading papers is the best way to \"catch up\" as the pace is rapid and knowledge very decentralized. I can confirm what Andrej recently wrote on X [3]: \"Unknown to many people, a growing amount of alpha is now outside of Arxiv, sources include but are not limited to: - [LINK] - HN - that niche Discord server - anime profile picture anons on X - reddit\" [3] [LINK]"},{"id":38653719,"author":"kozikow","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: AI/ML papers to catch up with current state of AI?","stance":"rec","why":"Karpathy content suited for listening away from the computer.","quote":"Anything from Karpathy on youtube","text":"My view is to focus on doing stuff. That's what I did. Pick up some task you want the model to do, try finetuning llama, playing with APIs from OpenAI, etc. Googling and asking GPT along the way. Foundational model training got so expensive that unless you can get hired by \"owns nuclear power plant of GPUs\" you are not going to get any \"research\" done. And as the area got white-hot those companies have more available talent than hardware nowadays. So just getting into the practitioner area is the best way to get productive with those models. And you improve as a practitioner by practicing, not by reading papers. If you're at the computer, your time is best spent writing code and interacting with those models in my opinion. If you cannot (e.g. commute) I listen to some stuff (e.g. [VIDEO] - Anything from Karpathy on youtube, or [LINK] channel)."},{"id":45429754,"author":"sipofwater","month":"2025-09","story":"The Intuition Behind How Large Language Models Work, Part I","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a resource on how LLMs work.","quote":"","text":"* \"[1hr Talk] Intro to Large Language Models\" by Andrej Karpathy: [VIDEO] from [LINK] * Andrej Karpathy: [LINK]"},{"id":44320812,"author":"whilenot-dev","month":"2025-06","story":"Andrej Karpathy: Software in the era of AI [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier talk compared with Karpathy's newer presentation.","quote":"I watched Karpathy's Intro to Large Language Models [0] not so long ago","text":"I watched Karpathy's Intro to Large Language Models [0] not so long ago and must say that I'm a bit confused by this presentation, and it's a bit unclear to me what it adds. 1,5 years ago he saw all the tool uses in agent systems as the future of LLMs, which seemed reasonable to me. There was (and maybe still is) potential for a lot of business cases to be explored, but every system is defined by its boundaries nonetheless. We still don't know all the challenges we face at that boundaries, whether these could be modelled into a virtual space, handled by software, and therefor also potentially AI and businesses. Now it all just seems to be analogies and what role LLMs could play in our modern landscape. We should treat LLMs as encapsulated systems of their own ...but sometimes an LLM becomes the operating system, sometimes it's the CPU, sometimes it's the mainframe from the 60s with time-sharing, a big fab complex, or even outright electricity itself? He's showing an iOS app, which seems to be, sorry for the dismissive tone, an example for a better looking counter. This demo app was in a presentable state for a demo after a day, and it took him a week to implement Googles OAuth2 stuff. Is that somehow exciting? What was that? The only way I could interpret this is that it just shows a big divide we're currently in. LLMs are a final API product for some, but an unoptimized generative software-model with sophisticated-but-opaque algorithms for others. Both are utterly in need fo"},{"id":41572490,"author":"ofou","month":"2024-09","story":"TexTube: Chat with any YouTube video transcript in ChatGPT fast","stance":"reference","why":"Used as an example video to test transcript chat tool.","quote":"","text":"It might be a preview or something because I have YT premium and doesn't show up that anywhere. Can you share a video that works for that? Like this one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41224380,"author":"jejeyyy77","month":"2024-08","story":"OpenDevin: An Open Platform for AI Software Developers as Generalist Agents","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for scaling improvement graphs.","quote":"","text":"Actually the improvement graphs are still scaling exponentially with training/compute being the bottleneck. So there isn't yet any evidence of diminishing returns. source: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38530602,"author":"verdverm","month":"2023-12","story":"Tree of Attacks: Jailbreaking Black-Box LLMs Automatically","stance":"reference","why":"Shows gibberish prompt suffix jailbreaks and base64 encoding tricks.","quote":"I watched the latest Karpathy video, where he shows that you can create a prompt suffix that looks like jibberish","text":"I watched the latest Karpathy video, where he shows that you can create a prompt suffix that looks like jibberish, using an iterative approach. It looks like this technique will be impossible to defend against, because it discovers for an arbitrary string that causes the jailbreak. Looks like this paper is building on the token level attack from that work. The base64 encoding technique was amusing as well [VIDEO]"},{"id":38390952,"author":"JacobJeppesen","month":"2023-11","story":"Sam Altman's ouster was precipitated by letter to board about AI breakthrough","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy discusses combining reinforcement learning with LLMs.","quote":"Andrej Karpathy mentions it in his new talk (~38 minutes in)","text":"Seems like they have made progress in combining reinforcement learning and LLMs. Andrej Karpathy mentions it in his new talk (~38 minutes in) [1], and Ilya Sutskever talks about it in a lecture at MIT (~29 minutes in) [2]. Finding a proper reward function to train LLMs in a reinforcement learning setup, and using it to train a model to solve math problems in a similar fashion to how AlphaGo used self-play to learn Go, would indeed be a tremendous breakthrough. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":38398549,"author":"a_wild_dandan","month":"2023-11","story":"About That OpenAI \"Breakthrough\"","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses System Two approaches toward the end of the talk.","quote":"Serendipitously, Karpathy broadly discussed these two issues yesterday !","text":"Current architectures have a lot of juice left across several axes, using extraordinarily accurate scaling laws. We're nowhere close to a wall. The industry has been focusing hard on 'System Two' approaches to augment our largely 'System One'-style models, where optimal decision policies make Q-learning a natural approach. The recent unlock here might be related to using the neural nets' general intelligence/flexibility to better broadly estimate their own states/rewards/actions (like humans). [EDIT: To be clear, the Q-learning stuff is my own speculation, whereas the 'System Two' stuff is well known.] Serendipitously, Karpathy broadly discussed these two issues yesterday ! Toward the lecture's end: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38402589,"author":"huijzer","month":"2023-11","story":"Training for one trillion parameter model backed by Intel and US govt has begun","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the view that scaling reliably improves accuracy.","quote":"Karpathy in his recent video [1] agrees","text":"Karpathy in his recent video [1] agrees, but at this point scaling is a very reliable way to better accuracy. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38403594,"author":"scriptsmith","month":"2023-11","story":"Training for one trillion parameter model backed by Intel and US govt has begun","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to show Karpathy says bigger models improve without algorithmic progress.","quote":"it's a very nice bonus, but we can sort of get more powerful models for free","text":"Seems like he actually disagrees here: If you train a bigger model on more text, we have a lot of confidence that the next-word prediction task will improve. So algorithmic progress is not necessary, it's a very nice bonus, but we can sort of get more powerful models for free, because we can just get a bigger computer, which we can say with some confidence we're going to get, and just train a bigger model for longer, and we are very confident we are going to get a better result. [VIDEO] (23:43)"},{"id":38420488,"author":"jstanley","month":"2023-11","story":"MonadGPT – What would have happened if ChatGPT was invented in the 17th century?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the 10TB of web text used in pre-training.","quote":"In Andrej Karpathy's intro to LLMs[1], he says pre-training uses about 10TB of text from the web.","text":"Are you sure? In Andrej Karpathy's intro to LLMs[1], he says pre-training uses about 10TB of text from the web. It's hard to believe that a comparable amount of text had been created, even across all languages, in the entire history of humanity up to the 17th century. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38473771,"author":"bayindirh","month":"2023-11","story":"Let's Not Flip Sides on IP Maximalism Because of AI","stance":"reference","why":"Video used as background for discussing LLMs as lossy compression.","quote":"","text":"Well, I was discussing Large Language Models (LLMs) as a technology, and esp, via this video [0] with a friend, just now. He made a striking remark. LLMs compress the information they ingest in a variably-lossy way, and when you query them, they rebuild a representation from whole or part of this compressed data in a statistical manner. As a result they're a lossy storage medium. Not unlike some music formats which store audio data in a lossy way. You know, this data can be restored with mathematical and statistical wizardry. You don't get everything you put in, but 97%-99% out of it, and companies and RIAA went bonkers for years, because even if it's not an exact reproduction, it was a reproduction enough, and this is a copyright violation. If an LLM can reproduce what I have written, or coded with 97%-99% accuracy without any license information, and I licensed this thing with less than permissive licenses, and sue the maker of that LLM, what will happen? - Will it be a copyright infringement? - Will it be fair use? It's the first if you look at it fairly, but it'll be probably be the latter, because money, fame and other corporate points will be at stake, otherwise. [0]: [VIDEO] Edit: I forgot to add the video. :)"}],"r":[["QgbLb6QCK88",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["bZQun8Y4L2A",1],["jkrNMKz9pWU",1],["qsEjV8DdSbs",1]]},"2AXv49dDQJw":{"wy":"Praised as the clearest visual explanation of transmission lines, impedance and signal propagation, with several commenters saying it made it all click.","q":{"x":"by far the best explanations of electricity that I've encountered in any format","i":46269031,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46269031,"author":"andrewflnr","month":"2025-12","story":"Science Communications on YouTube","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains open circuits and impedance mismatch clearly","quote":"by far the best explanations of electricity that I've encountered in any format","text":"I want to hijack this comment to plug AlphaPhoenix ( [LINK] ), who is responsible for by far the best explanations of electricity that I've encountered in any format. For instance, this one that clarifies what happens when you try to send electricity on an open circuit, and (IIRC this is the right one) what impedance mismatch is actually about: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42841064,"author":"brunohaid","month":"2025-01","story":"Oliver Heaviside and the theory of transmission lines (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Makes controlled impedance and signal propagation click visually","quote":"the moment when it finally clicked was watching this excellent AlphaPhoenix video","text":"Excellent post! Controlled impedance took me a long time to wrap my head around when starting PCB design, the moment when it finally clicked was watching this excellent AlphaPhoenix video [VIDEO] asking and practically demonstrating the simple question: When you flip on a switch, to turn on anything, send data, morse something etc, how does the circuit know how much current the load at the other end needs? Spoiler: Given that information can't travel faster than light, the simple answer is: it doesn't. So it just guesses and adjusts, which you don't want as it gives you exactly the ringing etc Heaviside identified. The video is a nice complement, as it perfectly visualizes the issues at play. It's a pretty wild bit of understanding to have, even in simple situations like flipping on a light switch."},{"id":40750269,"author":"simcop2387","month":"2024-06","story":"Wireless power transfer system achieves 270-kilowatt charge","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed measurements of how electrical waves propagate and impedance matching","quote":"it's probably the most detailed and easy to understand demonstration and explanation of all of it that I've ever seen.","text":"This spurred on some other videos from another youtuber that goes by AlphaPhoenix, he went way into all the details on it including measuring the complete flow of the wave of the electrical power and how it all works. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] (on his second channel, has a lot of \"behind the scenes\" of how it was done) It goes into a lot of depth of how the wave propagates, including measuring it at effectively \"a billion fps\" (he goes over how he does this) along the wire and how impedance matching works, etc. It's about 2.5 hours of video IIRC but it's probably the most detailed and easy to understand demonstration and explanation of all of it that I've ever seen."},{"id":40779600,"author":"spaceywilly","month":"2024-06","story":"Why does current flow the opposite way from the electrons?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizes voltage rippling through branches of a circuit","quote":"This YouTube video is probably one of the most amazing things I have ever seen","text":"As a matter of fact, yes! This YouTube video is probably one of the most amazing things I have ever seen, he measured the change in voltage traveling along a Y circuit, where one end is open and one is closed. You can see the voltage “ripple” through both branches of the circuit, reflect of the ends of the branches, and eventually settle into steady state. There are limits to this way of thinking (for example, water can’t travel in the space between “tubes”, but electricity can), but it is a very good way to approximate it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47053946,"author":"spaceywilly","month":"2026-02","story":"Physicists Make Electrons Flow Like Water","stance":"rec","why":"High-speed scope shows charge flowing down circuit branches like water","quote":"This guy has a lot of great videos using water channels to explain electricity.","text":"Well, the water analogy actually holds up quite well if you consider the charge field moving, not the electrons themselves. This guy has a lot of great videos using water channels to explain electricity. It is fascinating how under a high speed scope, you can see the electrical Charge “flow” like water down each branch of a circuit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43149982,"author":"kzrdude","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how electricity propagates along a conductor","quote":"This video he made about it is very good, and worth watching","text":"AlphaPhoenix's measurements, experiments and visualisations really help! They show some things that are normally not even taught and even electrical engineers will appreciate to see this. This video he made about it is very good, and worth watching if you wonder about how electricity propagates from one end of a conductor to the other. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43346876,"author":"hnuser123456","month":"2025-03","story":"How far neuroscience is from understanding brains (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows electrical waves behaving like water in pipes","quote":"Turns out \"water in a pipe\" is actually surprisingly accurate","text":"Turns out \"water in a pipe\" is actually surprisingly accurate, just that the \"waves\" are sloshing around at half the speed of light. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41558247,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment showing signal timing on long wire is complicated","quote":"the truth turned out to be quite complicated","text":"well, the truth turned out to be quite complicated; see also [LINK] and [VIDEO] . the result is more complicated than just a single time delay, and the details of the experimental setup (like the wire diameter! and load impedance relative to transmission-line pulse impedance) matter a lot. in alphaphoenix's first experiment, putting 5 volts into a kilometer of wire, he got 0.2 volts across his resistor immediately, then after about 1.6μs, a jump up to about 2 volts, and then a gradual rise to 1.7 volts, some overshoot peaking after another 1.6μs, and then settling back down to the 1.7-volt level (derek's results in the linked video, which incidentally links to one of the two i linked above, were quantitatively different but qualitatively similar) the really unintuitive thing about this i think is that people think of electrical energy as flowing inside wires, when actually almost all of it flows around the wires, as veritasium explained quite ably. this is something people doing high-speed pcb layout have to deal with a lot in order to avoid emi problems as i understand it, derek has a ph.d. in physics, or actually in physics education research [LINK] . that doesn't mean he knows everything about physics, but generally my experience with people in ph.d. programs is that they're good at listening to counterarguments and admitting when they're wrong, and also seeking out experts before publishing (i think the veritasium video does contain a minor error in that it says electrons"},{"id":41300625,"author":"schiffern","month":"2024-08","story":"Basic Mechanisms In Fire Control Computers (1953) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how electricity chooses a path in a circuit","quote":"How electricity flows through analog circuits and chooses the right path is another fascinating subject.","text":"The neat part is that it's almost instantaneous, but not quite. How electricity flows through analog circuits and chooses the right path is another fascinating subject. Seeing how it actually works \"in action\" seems to glimpse some insights into ultra-fast computing paradigms: not just computing with analog circuits, but also structuring computation like circuits. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["EK32jo7i5LQ",1],["aKLntZcp27M",1],["T-TwcmT6Rcw",1],["RjfbvDXpFls",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["Jib2AmRb_rk",1],["zcqZHYo7ONs",1],["LtlrITxB5qg",1]]},"5eW6Eagr9XA":{"wy":"Frequently recommended in threads on learning, the 10,000 hours rule and deliberate practice as a clearer account of how expertise develops.","q":{"x":"Veritasium has a really good video on the subject [0], with a much more compelling explanation for what it takes to develop expertise.","i":42301813,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42301813,"author":"lolinder","month":"2024-12","story":"Learn perfect pitch in 15 years","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what it takes to develop expertise beyond hours","quote":"Veritasium has a really good video on the subject [0], with a much more compelling explanation for what it takes to develop expertise.","text":"It's absolutely pseudoscience. 10k hours doing what ? And why would we expect to see an approximately equal amount of time to achieve expertise across so many different human endeavors? Veritasium has a really good video on the subject [0], with a much more compelling explanation for what it takes to develop expertise. He comes up with 4 things, but the two that stood out the most to me are that the field needs to be deterministic—the more randomness is involved the less expertise is possible—and that the learner needs consistent and accurate feedback on what they get right and wrong. Absent those factors no number of hours will do you any good. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36581047,"author":"angarg12","month":"2023-07","story":"Learning needs to be effortful to be effective","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite talk on deliberate practice","quote":"My favourite talk in the subject is this one from Veritasium","text":"Deliberate practice is a well studied phenomenon. My favourite talk in the subject is this one from Veritasium [VIDEO]"},{"id":33122328,"author":"Zensynthium","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: 10k hours rule to master anything. Could I switch career when I am 43?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Becoming an expert at any age","quote":"Also check out the video here, it's worth a watch","text":"Derek from Veritasium had a pretty good video on becoming an expert in anything and I didn't see age as a blocking factor. I'd say go for it! Also check out the video here, it's worth a watch [VIDEO]"},{"id":39538867,"author":"swframe2","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Slow thinkers, how do you compensate for your lack of quick-wittedness?","stance":"rec","why":"Video on becoming an expert","quote":"The Veritasium youtube channel has a few interesting videos related to this topic","text":"The Veritasium youtube channel has a few interesting videos related to this topic: This one is on IQ: [LINK] Pay attention to the stats on how IQ correlates to success (near the end). This one is about becoming an expert: [VIDEO] This one is about someone who just worked harder than everyone else: [LINK]"},{"id":35094580,"author":"somenameforme","month":"2023-03","story":"How to Start a Rocket Engine","stance":"rec","why":"Veritasium's later high-production video","quote":"Another amazing YouTuber is Veritasium.","text":"I'd also add this standard. Another amazing YouTuber is Veritasium. He went from [1] to [2]. The production quality is more than slightly improved, but the \"spirit\" remains identical. And I think that spirit is what drives success or failure. The production quality is just icing. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33119766,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: 10k hours rule to master anything. Could I switch career when I am 43?","stance":"rec","why":"Four ingredients for becoming an expert, with timestamps","quote":"you need 4 things to become an expert.","text":"The youtuber Veritasium recently did a video on the 10,000 hour expert. Summary: you need 4 things to become an expert. From comment with timestamps: 5:00 Repeated attempts with feedback. 6:47 Valid environment. 11:23 Timely feedback. 13:50 Don't get to comfortable. Video link: [VIDEO] Comment link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48461667,"author":"NalNezumi","month":"2026-06","story":"The better the autopilot the worse the pilot","stance":"reference","why":"Source on how expertise is acquired","quote":"","text":"There's been many studies around the concept \"how do you get expertise? In what field can you actually develop expertise?\" such as Naturalistic Decision Making [1] [2]. The wisdom from there can be applied to the question of automation. If your task is for example, to pilot a preset route with stable condition and very low surprise, you will fall for the \"getting too comfortable\" trap and we tend to start to get lazy(or efficient) and offload the mental effort and skills atrophy. A common workaround to this is to have regular training(deliberate practice) that introduce the \"tricky\" situation to keep the skill up. Problem ofc arises when people don't keep this up. This can be seen in the diagnostic performance differences between junior and senior doctors, not always in the favor to the senior [3]. If you add a layer of automation but the insight gathered by working on that layer is great (and falls off) then deliberate practice start to become a requirement [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":46030689,"author":"dahart","month":"2025-11","story":"210 IQ Is Not Enough","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Weschler’s definition of intelligence fits almost all functional adults, and most animals on the planet for that matter, and it doesn’t really back up your claim above about extreme intelligence. You’ve conflated a lot of things together here. There’s no evidence of any concepts that average adults can’t understand given time and interest. Ability to solve Fermat’s last theorem is function of interest and grit and time and context and education and whole slew of things not directly related to intelligence. Your example actually demonstrates my point quite nicely: there are a lot of people who understand Wiles’ proof even though they didn’t come up with it. You and I could too, if we wanted; the barrier is willingness to devote the large amount of time and energy required, not intelligence. > ‘massive outlier’ is a matter of perspective, but the amount of competition their best time is >1% faster than It seems like you understood my point about standard deviations and ranking versus absolute values, but you’re insisting on looking at rankings still? Why? This is exactly the common mistake I was referring to. Yes, Usain Bolt is the fastest sprinter in the world, which puts him at a little over 6 standard deviations above the norm, if I used my lookup table correctly. He’s faster than around eight billion people. But does that mean he’s eight billion times faster than the average person, or a hundred times faster, or even ten times faster than the average? Well, standard deviati"},{"id":45261595,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2025-09","story":"Ask HN: Generalists, when do you say \"I know enough\" about any particular topic?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">The idea is generalists know a lot about everything and when to pass it off to a subject matter expert. [VIDEO] [LINK] It's all about history repeating; or better yet understanding when it's something new and often forcing that. They say 10,000 hours to become an expert, but that's brute forced learning. When you actually learn how to learn, what universities USED TO teach and have since stopped teaching, you can do it much quicker. People who know how to learn can become an expert in under 1000 hours. >In 2025, with everything in tech changing by the minute, I’m realizing I need to set boundaries about how deep I go on any particular topic. But I’m unsure how. Particularly if I don’t want to get left behind as things continue to evolve. that's the wrong way to approach it; or perhaps definition of depth? Depth to me is about a subject that cant be understood until certain other learning milestones have been learnt. Which is perhaps a depth, but also something you can force as something new. A new pattern that will as above be part of the learning. It's fine to go deep, so long as you're seeing a new pattern you cant predict. The limit isnt about depth, it's about seeing something new."},{"id":44355476,"author":"NalNezumi","month":"2025-06","story":"Why don't Americans trust experts? Just ask a paranormal investigator","stance":"reference","why":"Simplified Klein and Kahneman on valid expert intuition","quote":"here's a simplified version of this by Veritasium","text":"Not just a problem in US but I'd say one of the issue is that we have expert inflation, and not all expertise are equally valid. We have some insight from Decision theory. Klein and Kahneman have both identified required properties for valuable expert intuition (or knowledge, tacit or not) to be available to aquire. (here's a simplified version of this by Veritasium [1]) Most people would see through the conartist claiming \"I'm a roulette expert. I've done roulette for 10 years, I have 10 years of roulette expertise, you should listen to me when it comes to what number you should bet on a roulette!\" Yet we all fall for other form of expert where their problem domain follow similar structures that makes expert knowledge acquisition either hard or impossible. But every expert with a PhD want to claim their expertise to be as valid as the actual valid expertise. Combine that with internet, short form (tweet etc) communication, we tend to dumb down the conversation using \"expert\" to cover all the domains, to the fraud-experts advantage. Certain fields, you should probably trust the expert almost blindly (Emergency Care, firefighters) but others, maybe not (clinical psychologist, policy maker, economists). Problem is that if we just bundle them all up, people will end up not trusting either. \"bundling up\" is a recurring tactic by conartists of all kind (subprime mortgage anyone?) If we would \"rate\" different field on their expertise validity (or scientific validity etc) easily und"},{"id":35390781,"author":"afry1","month":"2023-03","story":"Brains Speed Up Perception by Guessing What’s Next (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Expertise comes from recognizing patterns","quote":"We acquire expertise by recognizing the patterns in what we do.","text":"The 2:00 timestamp in this Veritassium video on expertise goes into exactly that phenomenon. We acquire expertise by recognizing the patterns in what we do. But once you take the patterns away, a lot of that \"expertise\" disappears too: [VIDEO] Jazz piano vs classical is a perfect example of this: same instrument, two paradigms with totally different idioms."},{"id":34700476,"author":"ninjagoo","month":"2023-02","story":"Meta to Ask Many Managers to Become Individual Contributors or Leave","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There might be fundamental incompatibilities here because of orthogonal goals - if you're an ambitious person in a corporate hierarchy won't one (or maybe even primary) goal be to climb the ladder? And if you're a founder won't your goal be to 'grow the product or service'? Now one way to climb the ladder may be to grow the product or service, but in a larger org the ability to influence a product or service in a major way might be a bit limited, with decision-making at the business level being controlled by upper echelons. So the ladder-climbing process would select for different skills and temperament than founding/running a company. Essentially, in a corporate hierarchy, we're asking people with ladder-climbing skills to become business people as they reach the top, and not everyone can make that transition successfully. In fact, I'd go so far as to say that very few can actually make that transition successfully. Human brains learn through feedback [1]. To become a good business person, you need to run a business and get feedback just like a real founder/CEO, including business failures. Your question essentially becomes - how can a CEO structure incentives so that managers at all levels become good business people? It's a great question, to which there is no simple answer that I know of. Would appreciate any pointers to potential solutions. One way could be to treat each team at a certain level (say no more than 50 people) as a business of its own. It might not be the mo"},{"id":32877443,"author":"lathiat","month":"2022-09","story":"Multitasking, something we copied from computers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sounds a bit like “System 1/System 2” thinking. can definitely drive and listen to a podcast when you’re good at it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32357278,"author":"manholio","month":"2022-08","story":"Against Discipline","stance":"reference","why":"Expertise from deliberate practice","quote":"","text":"\"Vision\" is what happens in your brain after you grind 10,000 hours on a skill that is improvable: [VIDEO] At that point, you no longer have to grind, because your brain has hardwired decision paths that are automatic and produce correct results for extremely complex problems like \"is this character likeable and understandable by the reader\", and you can effortlessly add a few high value paragraphs to their backstory. But it's foolhardy to think that level of \"flow\" can be achieved out of thin air, without writing hundreds of characters before them, re-reading them, getting feedback from other readers, studying other briliant authors with a eye for their technique etc."}],"r":[["4Wrc4fHSCpw",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1],["nmgFG7PUHfo",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["FkKPsLxgpuY",1],["BCiZc0n6COY",1],["JmwbYl6f11c",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["wbSwFU6tY1c",1],["GiYO1TObNz8",1],["qEV9qoup2mQ",1]]},"KtQ9nt2ZeGM":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as an up-to-date, fact-focused case for solar and batteries that readers can cite on land use and costs.","q":{"x":"Alec Watson’s recent, and excellent, Technology Connections YouTube piece on renewable energy.","i":47154920,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48399847,"author":"id34","month":"2026-06","story":"In a first, wind and solar generated more power than gas globally in April 2026","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nearly hour-long renewables talk with little climate politics.","quote":"I recommend this video from YouTuber Technology Connections","text":"I recommend this video from YouTuber Technology Connections: [VIDEO] I appreciate his ability to talk renewables for almost an hour and barely mention climate change. This video has a bit of a twist ending, but he gives you a solid out before he becomes political if you're showing it to someone who won't be receptive to that messaging."},{"id":48622598,"author":"ak217","month":"2026-06","story":"Fossil Fuels Are 40% of Freight Shipping Tonnage, but Half Its Fuel Use","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains EVs unlock energy portability.","quote":"Highly recommend everyone watch this video","text":"Highly recommend everyone watch this video [VIDEO] Briefly, the most important reason an EV is better because it unlocks energy portability . You gain the flexibility to source your energy in many more ways than with a gas car. Oil energy is about as optimized as it's ever going to get. With electricity, we're just getting started."},{"id":47627948,"author":"anon7000","month":"2026-04","story":"Solar and batteries can power the world","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic video showing solar on corn land exceeds US demand.","quote":"the technology connections video on this was fantastic.","text":"Yeah, the technology connections video on this was fantastic. If one was to cover that land in solar, you’d produce far more than the current energy demands of the US. Relying on an energy source which requires constant, continuous resource extraction is fucking stupid when we can spend resources up front and get reliable energy (solar + battery) for decades with minimal operating cost & maintenance. And then we’ll have a recycling loop to minimize future resource extraction. If you want to debate that, spend some time with this video first: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47711721,"author":"simoncion","month":"2026-04","story":"Maine Is About to Become the First State to Ban Major New Data Centers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Relevant discussion on corn land versus solar.","quote":"the entire video is very, very well worth watching.","text":"> in effect you've basically hamstrung green sources of energy around the country by being very smart for your own state. > The question is, should you be allowed to this. \"...you've basically hamstrung green sources of energy\"? Well, after we stop growing corn to feed exclusively to cars and start using solar panels deployed on that land to harvest electricity for cars and houses and everything else that runs on electricity [0], if we're still short on power we can have the discussion you're itching to have. [0] The immediately relevant discussion starts here < [VIDEO] > and runs through to about 38:29, but the entire video is very, very well worth watching."},{"id":47123098,"author":"mapontosevenths","month":"2026-02","story":"The Modern Toyota That Lost 65% of Its Value in One Year","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Spends about 15 minutes on batteries and annual cycles.","quote":"Actually watch the video or move on.","text":"> And no, there's no mention of batteries there; it's literally a straw man fight, showing their applicability to daily solar power generation cycles while almost completely ignoring their applicability to annual cycles. Why is it every single time someone in this thread speaks up they are just plain wrong? Here is a direct link to the part about batteries. He talks about them for about 15 minutes which is something like a quarter of the video. There is even a chapter mark to take you to that part. He also mentions them half a dozen more times throughout the video and warns in the beginning that people like you will chime in with misinformation without watching the video. You proved him right. [VIDEO] > applicability to annual cycles. He talks about that too. I'm not going to bother linking. Actually watch the video or move on. > Just take a weekly chart of the actual energy output of the panels for the year, and calculate the price relative to the worst week I don't have to. The United States government did and even considering the cost of storage, it's still cheaper than all the alternatives. Has been for years now. See my earlier comments for links. Private investors have done the same math, and that's why almost all new electricity generation being built is solar. It's the basically free money. Nobody with a brain can legitimately think that digging goop out of the desert, doing expensive processing to it, shipping it to the other side of the earth, and literally lighting"},{"id":47154920,"author":"Wistar","month":"2026-02","story":"Following 35% growth, solar has passed hydro on US grid","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent recent piece on renewable energy.","quote":"Alec Watson’s recent, and excellent, Technology Connections YouTube piece on renewable energy.","text":"Related: Alec Watson’s recent, and excellent, Technology Connections YouTube piece on renewable energy. “You are being misled about renewable energy technology” [VIDEO]"},{"id":49052653,"author":"otterley","month":"2026-07","story":"Producing ammonia and fertiliser using wind power in Morris, Minnesota","stance":"rec","why":"Exhaustive detail on economics of solar and wind versus fossil fuels.","quote":"Technology Connections goes into this in exhaustive detail","text":"Solar and wind energy have been more economical than fossil fuel on a cost per unit energy basis for quite some time. The reasons we're still so tied to fossil fuel are largely political, not economic. Technology Connections goes into this in exhaustive detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47868330,"author":"MetaWhirledPeas","month":"2026-04","story":"3.4M Solar Panels","stance":"rec","why":"Addresses relatives' concerns about solar farms and land.","quote":"I shared it; they probably didn't watch it but it sure was pertinent.","text":"I was shocked (pun) to hear how my relatives were each reacting to solar energy. One was rural and was concerned about nearby land getting turned into a solar farm. Another was concerned about farmland being edged out in favor of solar. And a third spent some time in emergency response on a solar farm and was off-put by their vastness and the electrical danger while traversing through them. Coincidentally this video emerged within a day of my conversation with the three of them. I shared it; they probably didn't watch it but it sure was pertinent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47520987,"author":"phyzome","month":"2026-03","story":"A Eulogy for Vim","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks cobalt misinformation about batteries.","quote":"Technology Connections did a great video that goes into this","text":"The cobalt thing is apparently misinformation. You've been misled. Technology Connections did a great video that goes into this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46878004,"author":"Rebelgecko","month":"2026-02","story":"FERC: Renewables made up 88% of new US power generating capacity to Nov 2025","stance":"rec","why":"Long video on solar cost and land use versus corn.","quote":"you might enjoy the recent video about renewables from Technology Connections","text":"If you like (VERY) long form videos, you might enjoy the recent video about renewables from Technology Connections: [VIDEO] Some of the interesting take ways for me, as someone who was already pretty hung ho about solar and batteries: * Solar has gotten so cheap that it's still profitable in the shady northlands * The amount of land it would take to run the US primarily on solar is \"a lot\" in absolute terms. But it pales in comparison to the amount of land we currently use to grow corn for feeding gas cars."},{"id":46906886,"author":"goda90","month":"2026-02","story":"Cubans rendered powerless as outages persist and tensions with US escalate","stance":"rec","why":"Long rant on humanity's reliance on single-use fuels.","quote":"A YouTuber known for talking about dishwashers and Christmas lights recently put out a long rant","text":"This really illustrates how important it is to switch to renewable energy. I know it's not an easy task for impoverished communities to get the startup capital to install solar+batteries, especially one in such a politically tumultuous position, but that really is a path to stability for so many people around the world. A YouTuber known for talking about dishwashers and Christmas lights recently put out a long rant about how ridiculous it is that humanity still leans so much on single use fuels: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46936142,"author":"danaris","month":"2026-02","story":"Banning lead in gas worked. The proof is in our hair","stance":"rec","why":"Up-to-date information on renewables and batteries.","quote":"Here, I'll give you a relevant video I watched recently to start with","text":"It was, several years ago. That's so much not the case now that renewables + batteries were by far the largest source of new generation in the US (yes, the US, with Trump actively trying to destroy them) last year. Look up some of the new information coming out about them recently. Here, I'll give you a relevant video I watched recently to start with: [VIDEO] (channel is Technology Connections)"},{"id":48881456,"author":"runarberg","month":"2026-07","story":"AI Boosts Research Careers but Flattens Scientific Discovery","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This may seem so blatantly obvious to us that it need not be mentioned, but to a lot of people I bet it is not obvious at al, and in fact may even be counter-obvious. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48649919,"author":"fennecbutt","month":"2026-06","story":"Canada is looking to build up to 10 new nuclear reactors over the next 15 years","stance":"reference","why":"Technology Connections covered these calculations months ago.","quote":"technology connections did this exact topic and these exact calculations (and much more)","text":"How convenient for Musk that technology connections did this exact topic and these exact calculations (and much more) as a topic months and months ago: [VIDEO] Why up Musk? Is he your primary source of news and ideas?"},{"id":48085988,"author":"starkparker","month":"2026-05","story":"Louis Rossmann tells 3D printer maker Bambu Lab to 'Go (Bleep) yourself'","stance":"reference","why":"Example of favoring facts over emotion in advocacy.","quote":"Even amongst YouTubers, you can favor facts over emotions (without discarding emotion!)","text":"Favoring emotion over facts while advocating for a cause is still a sugar high of advocacy, regardless of the cause's righteousness. A short burst quickly forgotten, with a chance of discrediting what you're trying to advocate if the facts aren't right. Even amongst YouTubers, you can favor facts over emotions (without discarding emotion!) and be a more effective advocate who arms others with both motivation and useful, effective knowledge.[1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47904688,"author":"svpk","month":"2026-04","story":"3.4M Solar Panels","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp where solar would cover 180% of US grid usage.","quote":"Timestamp of where he says that","text":"That's incorrect per the video. It wouldn't cover 80% of the current US grid it would cover 80% more! It would be 180% of total US grid usage. Timestamp of where he says that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47912151,"author":"subscribed","month":"2026-04","story":"New study compares growing corn for energy to solar production. It's no contest","stance":"reference","why":"Covers costs and benefits of corn ethanol versus solar electricity.","quote":"I already mentioned the video that covers this specific relationship and details","text":"Haha, but you did that already: > How much CO2 is released producing, shipping, and installing the solar panels? Or maybe you wanted someone else list all the steps. I was simply helping you with the part you missed: > GP is right: you gotta consider all the variables The fact you seemingly missed the fact that growing and processing corn keeps emitting GHG (not only co2), and that producing, transporting and installing the solar panels costs *once*. I already mentioned the video that covers this specific relationship and details, the actual costs and benefits of growing corn for ethanol vs generating electricity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46883446,"author":"worldsayshi","month":"2026-02","story":"Data centers in space makes no sense","stance":"reference","why":"Details on solar versus ethanol corn land use.","quote":"More details here","text":"> There is also the need for real estate in areas with good sun exposure that also have sufficient fresh water supply for cleaning. Solar panels are 20x more efficient than growing corn for ethanol. Swap out some of those 30 million acres of ethanol corn fields (in the US) and you'll have more energy than you need. More details here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46904880,"author":"antiframe","month":"2026-02","story":"CIA suddenly stops publishing, removes archives of The World Factbook","stance":"reference","why":"Source for converting ethanol corn land to solar.","quote":"","text":"I think the lament is the rise of the \"facts are the enemy\" stance is a step towards dystopia. I recently learned that if we converted all the land we use to grow corn for ethanol (not food) into solar farms the US would produce 84% more energy than it currently produces (from all sources) [1]. Of course that's a huge undertaking, but we're not even talking about it because pesky things like facts are swept aside in lieu of punchy counters like: panels are expensive (they're not), we don't have the land (we do), what about the batteries (solved problem with today's--let along tomorrow's tech), the corn best doesn't get enough sun (it does), etc. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46931757,"author":"mdf","month":"2026-02","story":"Total Surface Area Required to Fuel the World with Solar (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes numbers on land used for ethanol versus solar.","quote":"The most recent Technology Connections video[1] quoted some numbers on this.","text":"Yes, and the corn-based ethanol here is used for \"feeding cars\" that have combustion engines, i.e. it's already used exactly for energy production. The most recent Technology Connections video[1] quoted some numbers on this. All this land dedicated to disposable energy production could be dedicated to renewable energy production instead. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["c12cYAUTXXs",1],["08mwXICY4JM",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["T6X2uwlQGQM",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["MWsGBRdK2N0",1],["9yPjoh9YJMk",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["EUWVVTY63hc",1]]},"S0qjK3TWZE8":{"wy":"The canonical real-world example in game theory discussions of manipulating a prisoner's dilemma.","q":{"x":"This is a classic IMO.","i":12441162,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":44036402,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2025-05","story":"Game theory illustrated by an animated cartoon game","stance":"strong_rec","why":"The amazing Golden Balls moment.","quote":"No conversation on game theory is complete until someone brings up Golden Balls","text":"No conversation on game theory is complete until someone brings up Golden Balls, and in particular this amazing moment (warning: terrible audio quality). [VIDEO]"},{"id":12441162,"author":"hcarvalhoalves","month":"2016-09","story":"What is the Nash Equilibrium and why does it matter?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Player manipulates the game to defeat the Nash equilibrium.","quote":"This is a classic IMO.","text":"If you find this article interesting, Coursera's \"Competitive Strategy\" [1] is a pretty good introduction on the topic of game theory and how it can be used as a decision-making framework for business, I've found. As a case study, I recommend watching this episode of \"Golden Balls\" [2], which shows how a player can manipulate a game outcome and defeat the natural Nash equilibria. This is a classic IMO. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8441679,"author":"surreal","month":"2014-10","story":"Rock Paper Scissors Programming Competition","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A clever strategy removing the opponent's incentive to defect.","quote":"This is the most clever I've seen","text":"This is the most clever I've seen: [VIDEO] He ensures his opponent has nothing to gain from defecting... (Rules etc until 2m 30s, then the game)"},{"id":21660885,"author":"satori99","month":"2019-11","story":"Paradoxical Geniuses: “Let us burn the ships” (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"A great demonstration of a prisoners dilemma in action.","quote":"There is great demonstration of this in action on a British game show","text":"There is great demonstration of this in action on a British game show with the players being in a prisoners dilemma situation; [VIDEO]"},{"id":10894757,"author":"J-dawg","month":"2016-01","story":"The pirate game","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining two-player game show example.","quote":"is a very entertaining example of a two-player zero-sum game","text":"This clip [1], from the UK game show \"Golden Balls\", is a very entertaining example of a two-player zero-sum game EDIT: As explained in the replies, this is not actually a zero-sum game. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33966821,"author":"int0x2e","month":"2022-12","story":"SBF Arrested by Bahamian Authorities","stance":"reference","why":"A show that made the prisoner's dilemma into television.","quote":"it did work flawlessly in a show called \"Split or Steal","text":"This is almost accurate. You can slightly increase your chances if you say \"I will defect, but will take care of your family and give you half my income forever. If you also defect, we will both go to prison and you will be worse off\", then don't actually defect - which means you both cooperate! It won't always work, but it did work flawlessly in a show called \"Split or Steal\", which basically made the prisoner's dilemma a TV show: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29560283,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2021-12","story":"Plans you're not supposed to talk about","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This reminds me of CEO-speak. A CEO cannot speak their true mind or their true assessment of a situation. They can't say \"wow we're in trouble here\" because then people will jump ship and then the company will be even more in trouble (and the CEO would get sued for breaching Fiduciary Duty) What a CEO says is merely wishful thinking for a goal they want people to follow. They can never speak their true plan. (And of course, it's all just game theory, a more elaborate version of [VIDEO] )"},{"id":24833019,"author":"nosuchthing","month":"2020-10","story":"A Brief Introduction to the Basics of Game Theory","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This paper is funny if you actually understand game theory because you can see how the author either chose to withhold key applicable information for applied game theory or inadequately researched famous game theory principles and developments. This is a much easier and more realistic introduction to game theory principles: [LINK] Game theory is often explained as a math problem involving humans and a specific scenario that requires a choice, from humans. The scenarios are fun thought experiments and about as useful as the famous trolley problem [1][2] In reality humans have many more variables affecting decisions than the pure rational equations can clearly define. Often the undefined variables are within the context of communication theory and reputation or auditing systems. In the case of the famous prisoners dilemma, the real solution is establishing a secret out of band communication channel prior to the dilemma, alongside a known reputation and retaliation penalty for abusing the mutual trust.[3] [4] The famous \"tragedy of the commons\" rational resource optimization game is often cited as justification for machiavellian exploitation, yet humans being social creatures are subject to reputations, and have sophisticated communication, cooperation, and retaliation abilities. [4] Elinor Ostrom's \"Rules, games, and common-pool resources\" and Robert Axelrod's work \"The Evolution of Cooperation\" both explain game theory in the context of human scale realities. Of particular int"},{"id":10718046,"author":"Aljik","month":"2015-12","story":"Philosophy of science books every computer scientist should read","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"If you really ended up thinking like that I think that is more reflective of your personality/mind than Yudkowsky's writings. Like, I don't mean this to be insulting, but normal people don't end up paranoid and anxious and worrying about what levels people are playing at and thinking that super intelligent beings are the nature of the world, after reading LW. Normal people say \"Huh thats cool\" and it makes for fun dinner table talks and at best comes up in real life if you are a contestant on a game show: [VIDEO] Yudkowsky isn't some devious mastermind trying to brainwash people into thinking wrong so he can. So he can what? Even if he was a devious mastermind who created a polemic work so as to get people to think wrong... what would his goal be?"},{"id":10070608,"author":"Natsu","month":"2015-08","story":"Why Greece and the EU aren't playing games","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an irrational choice guaranteeing a better outcome.","quote":"take this infamous episode of split or steal","text":"This says we can't use game theory to analyze this situation, but the only argument given in support is that humans aren't rational. \"Irrational\" (or otherwise emotional) strategies are by no means outside of the analysis of game theory. It's well known that by \"irrationally\" eliminating one of your choices, you can push your opponent into different options. For example, take this infamous episode of split or steal: [VIDEO] One side makes a choice to go with perhaps the most \"irrational\" choice. But if you think about it for a bit, it's obvious that they end up guaranteeing the better choice for both parties by being less greedy than they might be. Anyone interested in the topic of rationality as it pertains to game theory might also like reading this: [LINK]"},{"id":9042709,"author":"nosuchthing","month":"2015-02","story":"Game Theory Calls Cooperation Into Question","stance":"reference","why":"Real game show where players could communicate.","quote":"A real game show [1] put two people in a position of the prisoners dilemma and they were free to communicate.","text":"Thought experiments are not set in stone, and even if they were modifications could me made. A real game show [1] put two people in a position of the prisoners dilemma and they were free to communicate. [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8566393,"author":"tonglil","month":"2014-11","story":"If you use a Mac or Android, e-commerce sites may be charging you more","stance":"reference","why":"Both parties get the same or nothing.","quote":"This type of thinking reminds me of the show Golden Balls","text":"This type of thinking reminds me of the show Golden Balls: two parties get the same thing or no one gets anything at all. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4400376,"author":"emmelaich","month":"2012-08","story":"The Emerging Revolution in Game Theory","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests a solution to the Prisoner's Dilemma.","quote":"The relevant vid link within","text":"Sort of related in that it pertains to Prisoner's Dilemma, and suggested to some that there is a 'solution' to PD: [LINK] The relevant vid link within: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4027025,"author":"neilk","month":"2012-05","story":"Iterated prisoner's dilemma contains evolutionary opponent dominating strategies","stance":"reference","why":"Famous Golden Balls episode demonstrating the idea.","quote":"Could we say that this famous episode of Golden Balls demonstrates a simple application the idea?","text":"Could we say that this famous episode of Golden Balls demonstrates a simple application the idea? Quote: \"You're about to walk away with my money because you're an idiot.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nqFVOL7mLd4",1],["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["M8F5hXqS-Ac",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["lyu7v7nWzfo",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["7PoajCqNKpg",1],["o4-YnLpLgtk",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1]]},"jEbRVNxL44c":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best explanation of how Zipline's whole system works and as an example of drones doing real, useful work.","q":{"x":"this video by Real Engineering that does a great job of explaining how the whole thing works","i":23448100,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":37581634,"author":"zx10rse","month":"2023-09","story":"FAA Authorizes Zipline to Deliver Commercial Packages Using Drones","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Commenter's favorite company, sees it as the way forward for delivery.","quote":"One of my favorite companies since I heard about them","text":"One of my favorite companies since I heard about them - [VIDEO] I personally believe this is the way forward, like in a star wars movie full of a plethora of \"dumb\" robots who have a specific work task, and are almost self sufficient. Like you have a robot that fix delivery drones, a robot that assembles them, and etc. Drones will be also much better at avoiding obstacles, and they will be much more efficient than cars. I am not really sure why there isn't more competition in this market. I believe they can build an autonomous delivery hub, and factory. I wish them all the best."},{"id":37580990,"author":"gniv","month":"2023-09","story":"FAA Authorizes Zipline to Deliver Commercial Packages Using Drones","stance":"rec","why":"Gives more info on Zipline's Rwanda deliveries, less rushed.","quote":"This video has a bit more info and is less rushed","text":"They’ve been doing those deliveries in Rwanda for 6 years! Very impressive. This video has a bit more info and is less rushed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31147012,"author":"SPBS","month":"2022-04","story":"Drones have transformed blood delivery in Rwanda","stance":"rec","why":"Details fixed-wing design, battery-GPS unit, rail launch, parachute drop and wire landing.","quote":"The drone is not a quadcopter, they are too inefficient. Mini plane design.","text":"For those blocked by a paywall, Real Engineering did a video on it [ [VIDEO] ]. The most interesting aspects to me were: - The drone is not a quadcopter, they are too inefficient. Mini plane design. - GPS device is directly attached to the swappable battery unit to ensure it is always connected to a satellite, eliminating satellite connection delays when the drone powers up. - It is launched into a sky by a powered rail. It doesn't need to land to deliver the goods, the items get parachuted down. No infrastructure needed on the receiving end, the item just drops from the sky. - For landing the drone flies past an aircraft arresting wire while hooking onto it which decelerates it and causes it to swing down (suspended above the ground). Easy to retrieve."},{"id":28070186,"author":"RheingoldRiver","month":"2021-08","story":"The slow collapse of Amazon’s drone delivery dream","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the neat wire-hook landing technology of Zipline drones.","quote":"flyzipline is so cool!","text":"flyzipline is so cool! I saw an AMA they did on reddit a while ago and their tech for the thing landing where it hooks onto the the wire is really neat! [VIDEO]"},{"id":28071975,"author":"matkoniecz","month":"2021-08","story":"The slow collapse of Amazon’s drone delivery dream","stance":"rec","why":"Amazing look at drone delivery of medical supplies in Rwanda.","quote":"with amazing (delivery of medical supplies in Rwanda)","text":"> Even if they're literally worse at everything than the alternatives, cost more, are less reliable, etc. Drones may be better for very quick delivery or where road network is very poor. See [LINK] with amazing [VIDEO] (delivery of medical supplies in Rwanda) and mentions ship-to-ship transport."},{"id":28262943,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2021-08","story":"Police helicopter's 100mph chase with 'super sophisticated' drone near Tucson","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Zipline's assembly-line prep for life-and-death drone deliveries in Rwanda.","quote":"Really cool tech, the way they have a sort of assembly line for getting ready-to-fly parts on the package in the space of minutes","text":"I was also going to mention zipline in Rwanda, last video I saw was two years ago but it looks like they’re doing well enough to be hiring interns in San Fran. Really cool tech, the way they have a sort of assembly line for getting ready-to-fly parts on the package in the space of minutes - these are life and death deliveries out here! [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23448100,"author":"ViralBShah","month":"2020-06","story":"Zipline: Drone delivery of medical supplies","stance":"rec","why":"Explains well how the whole Zipline system works.","quote":"this video by Real Engineering that does a great job of explaining how the whole thing works","text":"I learnt about Zipline in a talk at JuliaCon by Tucker McClure who spoke about how they use Julia for designing and tuning their aircraft. Had the great fortune of spending an evening at the conference to learn more about the company itself, how it got started and their use of Julia. Recommend the 2019 JuliaCon video on how Zipline uses Julia for vehicle simulation: [LINK] And this video by Real Engineering that does a great job of explaining how the whole thing works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21020998,"author":"Fabricio20","month":"2019-09","story":"Google Wing Launching US Drone Deliveries with FedEx, Walgreens","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Zipline's catapult launch and parachute delivery system in Rwanda.","quote":"Real Engineering [1] and Wendover Productions [2] have really nice videos about their system currently deployed in Rwanda.","text":"The catapult launches the drones into the air, which then parachute the package to the location. Real Engineering [1] and Wendover Productions [2] have really nice videos about their system currently deployed in Rwanda. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":20622605,"author":"1023bytes","month":"2019-08","story":"Swiss Post Suspends Drone Delivery Service After Second Crash","stance":"rec","why":"Shows Zipline's drone delivery in Rwanda as an interesting engineering solution.","quote":"one of the most interesting solutions an engineering perspective too","text":"Delivery drones are definitely unnecessary in densely populated areas. It's cool and futuristic, but it's not economical at all, it's very unsafe and also loud. However it's a wonderful thing for rural places with poor infrastructure. For example Zipline in Rwanda, one of the most interesting solutions an engineering perspective too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38591245,"author":"rodent_rodeo","month":"2023-12","story":"The U.S. Government's Most Powerful Spying Tool Is Fighting for Its Life","stance":"reference","why":"Notes Zipline's quick-launch drones, wondering about military use.","quote":"from the moment I saw zipline I thought their quick launching and distance was interesting","text":"If you watch the video, at the end they show a road runner. I was bracing myself for the poor little guy to get blasted to atoms. Spoiler: road runner survived. I work in the opposite field of weapons but from the moment I saw zipline I thought their quick launching and distance was interesting. Maybe this is already how drones are launched in places like Ukraine, I don't know. I do understand that the road runner is probably much faster and able to intercept jets. [VIDEO] I'm not really looking forward to drone warfare, tbh."},{"id":28070677,"author":"rudian","month":"2021-08","story":"The slow collapse of Amazon’s drone delivery dream","stance":"reference","why":"Shows launchable planes delivering medical supplies across Ghana.","quote":"","text":"There's an American company[1] in Ghana sending medical supplies around the country with small launchable planes.[2] 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25563328,"author":"victorthehuman","month":"2020-12","story":"FAA issuing new rules to allow drones to fly over people and at night","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fixed-wing drone delivery working in rural Rwanda.","quote":"A good example of that is this drone delivery service in Rwanda","text":"If we're talking about fixed wing drones in rural environments then it does makes sense. A good example of that is this drone delivery service in Rwanda [VIDEO]"},{"id":23446985,"author":"cinntaile","month":"2020-06","story":"Zipline: Drone delivery of medical supplies","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Zipline's drone launches and lands in more detail.","quote":"The website includes a video [0] that explains how it works in more detail too.","text":"The website includes a video [0] that explains how it works in more detail too. It's quite interesting how they evade issues with landing and lifting the plane, lifting the plane by shooting it off a rail seems like a common approach but landing it by catching it using a small hook at the tail of the plane isn't that common I presume. On their website it says \"Gas combustion vehicles break down, get stuck in traffic jams that prevent urgent response, and put human drivers at risk behind the wheel, particularly when the route is rough and treacherous. Zipline’s drones are battery powered and fly quickly and directly to their destinations, leaving ground vehicles behind.\" That sounds like an unfair comparison, a more accurate comparison would have been to gas powered drones. Is it still the case for gas powered drones that they're more unreliable than electrical drones? It's probably because of the weight of a gas powered engine in such a small drone no? [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bnoUBfLxZz0",1],["vWkgEddb4-A",1],["mSgXWpvQEHE",1],["19zm1Fn0S9M",1],["_DTpQ4Kk2wA",1],["EH6vE97qIP4",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["OhnjMdzGusc",1],["sj8Sg8qnjOg",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["g2tMcMQqSbA",1],["LPqtfC70QTU",1]]},"vgT4Y30DkaA":{"wy":"HN commenters use it to point people to Threads, which they call the most harrowing depiction of nuclear war and say should be mandatory viewing.","q":{"x":"should be mandatory viewing for all people in positions of power","i":28686901,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38091886,"author":"willprice89","month":"2023-10","story":"What a third world war would mean for investors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conveys how miserable post-nuclear life would be.","quote":"does the best job hammering home just how miserable and hopeless life will be","text":"Of all post-nuclear-apocalypse stories I've seen or read, I think the movie Threads (1984) ( [VIDEO] ) does the best job hammering home just how miserable and hopeless life will be for everyone except a tiny fraction of lucky and privileged survivors. Maybe those are the people who will still be worrying about investments after WW3..."},{"id":34448330,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2023-01","story":"Nuclear explosion impact on humans indoors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fictional nuclear attack in the UK seen through ordinary lives.","quote":"You should watch Threads (1984), the most messed up film ever made","text":"You should watch Threads (1984), the most messed up film ever made, produced in part by the BBC [VIDEO] (trailer only) It's about the lives of average people before, during and after a fictional nuclear attack in the UK."},{"id":28686901,"author":"bb101","month":"2021-09","story":"Hypersonic HAWC missile flies, but details are kept hidden","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the horror that follows the bombing.","quote":"should be mandatory viewing for all people in positions of power","text":"And our reality would segue into the storyline of Threads. The 1984 BBC film should be mandatory viewing for all people in positions of power. The bombing is only the start of the horror. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40627546,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2024-06","story":"British Nuclear Warning System","stance":"rec","why":"Trailer is harrowing through its audio alone.","quote":"Even the trailer is harrowing with audio alone","text":"Threads (1984) [LINK] [LINK] Even the trailer is harrowing with audio alone: [VIDEO] The part where the audio seamlessly transitions from screams into screeching wind, wails, and explosions was chillingly well done in the most dramatic way. If you like Threads, check out Children of Men, which is also disturbingly realistic and also set in UK."},{"id":21585969,"author":"davidwtbuxton","month":"2019-11","story":"Beria and the Beautiful Game","stance":"rec","why":"Bleak depiction of British society after a nuclear attack.","quote":"incredibly bleak depiction of British society in the aftermath of a nuclear attack","text":"Also Threads, from the same era, incredibly bleak depiction of British society in the aftermath of a nuclear attack. [VIDEO] (For those who haven't seen it, it is grim, but gripping.)"}],"r":[["rvkEpstd9os",1],["T6X2uwlQGQM",1],["XetplHcM7aQ",1],["z6yL0_sDnX0",1],["V-vFtiDYiIw",1],["gdK4y6O-llE",1],["nO5vjmDFZaI",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1]]},"ob_GX50Za6c":{"wy":"HN uses it as the source of the 'bicycle for the mind' quote, which commenters cite in discussions of computing, programming and LLMs.","q":{"x":"Immediately made me think of Steve Jobs and this great clip","i":35345174,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46316974,"author":"joshstrange","month":"2025-12","story":"Apples, Trees, and Quasimodes","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs quote the commenter loves","quote":"one of my favorite since it resonates so clearly with me","text":"> the Bay Area tradition that treated computers not as office appliances but as tools for thought, instruments of liberation Missed a great chance to use the \"bicycle for our minds\" Steve Jobs quote (one of my favorite since it resonates so clearly with me) [VIDEO]"},{"id":3079638,"author":"tuxguy","month":"2011-10","story":"26-year old iconoclast Steve Jobs' address to the Academy of Achievement in 1982","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jobs on computers as the bicycle for our minds","quote":"Just saw another brilliant Jobs quote, circa 1991","text":"Just saw another brilliant Jobs quote, circa 1991, via my svlug friend Rick Moen. I think one of the things that really separate us from the higher primates is that we are tool builders. I read a study that measured the efficiency of locomotion for various species on the planet. The condor used the least energy to move a kilometre,and humans came in with a rather unimpressive showing about a third of the way down the list. It wasn't too proud of a showing for the crown of creation. So, that didn't look so good -- but somebody at _Scientific American_ had the insight to test the efficiency of locomotion for a man on a bicycle, and a man on a bicycle -- a human on a bicycle -- blew the condor away, completely off the top of the charts. And that's what a computer is to me. A computer is the most remarkable tool that we've ever come up with, and it's the equivalent of a bicycle for our minds. -- Steve Jobs, 1991 [VIDEO]"},{"id":35345174,"author":"nntwozz","month":"2023-03","story":"Bicycle","stance":"rec","why":"Great clip of Jobs's bicycle for the mind","quote":"Immediately made me think of Steve Jobs and this great clip","text":"Immediately made me think of Steve Jobs and this great clip: \"Computers are like a bicycle for our minds.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":9037984,"author":"tim333","month":"2015-02","story":"Apple's New Market","stance":"rec","why":"Cool Jobs bicycle for the mind quote","quote":"The Steve Jobs quote mentioned about bicycles for the mind is cool","text":"The Steve Jobs quote mentioned about bicycles for the mind is cool [VIDEO] . I know it's old but I hadn't seen it before. The rest of the article is kind of intellectual mush starting from saying \"litigated against\" in the first sentence when he hasn't and continuing in a similar vein."},{"id":1818483,"author":"kqr2","month":"2010-10","story":"Being Steve Jobs's Boss","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs explains computers as bicycles for the mind","quote":"Interview with Steve Jobs where he talks about the computer as a bicycle for the mind","text":"Interview with Steve Jobs where he talks about the computer as a bicycle for the mind: [VIDEO] To put it in context, he starts off by talking about the efficiency of animal locomotion. In the animal kingdom, the condor is one of the most efficient. It uses the least amount of energy to travel a kilometer. Human walking is rather inefficient in comparison. However, with a bicycle, a man can blow away a condor."},{"id":43753159,"author":"scrlk","month":"2025-04","story":"LLM-powered tools amplify developer capabilities rather than replacing them","stance":"reference","why":"LLMs as the next bicycles for the mind","quote":"I feel like LLMs are just the next step on the Jobs analogy of \"computers are bicycles for the mind","text":"> The developers who thrive in this new environment won’t be those who fear or resist AI tools, but those who master them—who understand both their extraordinary potential and their very real limitations. They’ll recognise that the goal isn’t to remove humans from the equation but to enhance what humans can accomplish. I feel like LLMs are just the next step on the Jobs analogy of \"computers are bicycles for the mind\" [0]. And if these tools are powerful bicycles available to everyone, what happens competitively? It reminds me of a Substack post I read recently: > If everyone has AI, then competitively no one has AI, because that means you are what drives the differences. What happens if you and LeBron start juicing? Do you both get as strong? Can you inject your way to Steph’s jumpshot? What’s the differentiator? This answer is inescapable in any contested domain. The unconventionally gifted will always be ascendant, and any device that’s available to everyone manifests in pronounced power laws in their favor. The strong get stronger. The fast get faster. Disproportionately so. [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":39521863,"author":"mnky9800n","month":"2024-02","story":"Nvidia CEO predicts the death of coding","stance":"reference","why":"Computer as bicycle for the mind supports argument","quote":"Huang only argues the same as Steve Jobs, a computer is a bicycle for the mind","text":"This is a really ridiculous take that is created from sound bites. What he's says is not that coding will no longer exist, but that the purpose of computing is not coding but to solve interesting problems. We will only find interesting problems by having deep domain knowledge. Huang only argues the same as Steve Jobs, a computer is a bicycle for the mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36747797,"author":"0wis","month":"2023-07","story":"Electric Bike, Stupid Love of My Life","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the bicycles for the mind quote","quote":"that’s where the Steve Job’s quote « Computers are bicycles for the mind » comes from","text":"Remember that bicycle are the most efficient way to move a human, that’s where the Steve Job’s quote « Computers are bicycles for the mind » comes from [1]. This efficiency never stops to amaze me. And an electric bicycle makes it way more convenient while still being quite efficient. I share the love of electric bikes with the author and I am sure that huge progresses in UX and efficiency are it to come in the field. Would love to work on it one day ! (But hardware is hard…) I am happy to see the subject on HN frontpage. If anyone know any quirky projects on the subject, I would love to know more ! [1] : [VIDEO]"},{"id":11745849,"author":"sampo","month":"2016-05","story":"Automation Should Be Like Iron Man, Not Ultron","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs quote on computers as mind bicycles","quote":"","text":"> In 1968 Douglas Engelbart used the wonderfully space aged term \"brain amplification\" And Steve Jobs in 1990, \"And that’s what a computer is to me ... it’s the equivalent of a bicycle for our minds.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":11777428,"author":"aub3bhat","month":"2016-05","story":"Coding without Google","stance":"reference","why":"Programming as bicycles for the mind","quote":"or to put as Steve Jobs did bicycles for the mind.","text":"I think I view programming as mental abstractions in action, or to put as Steve Jobs did bicycles for the mind. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2924921,"author":"acak","month":"2011-08","story":"Steve Jobs' Best Quotes","stance":"reference","why":"Bicycle analogy for computers and the mind","quote":"The computer is to the human mind, what the bicycle is to our ability to travel.","text":"The bicycle analogy to [VIDEO] The computer is to the human mind, what the bicycle is to our ability to travel."}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["baFaEvBywGc",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1],["cxrkC-pMH_s",1],["sycgL3Qg_Ak",1],["modXC5IWTJI",1],["CnMRePtHMZY",1],["si9iqF5uTFk",1],["3TFz1xqp3us",1],["xcwJt4bcnXs",1]]},"GVsUOuSjvcg":{"wy":"Treated as the accessible reference on analog computing for neural nets, especially the Mythic AI segment.","q":{"x":"Veritasium explains it really well in general here (and demos the device)","i":42188403,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":30583948,"author":"iKlsR","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: What concepts have you never truly understood?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made neural networks click when other resources failed","quote":"suddenly enough a Vertiasium video pops up and about everything just slots into place","text":"Agreed. I've never really gotten into neural networks, most of the times I start but get distracted or bored/lost and suddenly enough a Vertiasium video pops up and about everything just slots into place, stuff you've glossed over or heard before just make sense. [VIDEO] . Start at minute 4:00."},{"id":42188403,"author":"sushid","month":"2024-11","story":"The Analog Thing: Analog Computing for the Future","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of analog computers plus device demo","quote":"Veritasium explains it really well in general here (and demos the device)","text":"I think they're saying analog computers could be the future of computing. Veritasium explains it really well in general here (and demos the device) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41183384,"author":"sholladay","month":"2024-08","story":"What Is Analog Computing?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how analog works, uses, pros and cons, history, future","quote":"Veritasium has a good video about analog computers.","text":"Veritasium has a good video about analog computers. It covers how they work, what they are used for, pros and cons compared to digital, what they look like today, a bit of the history and their possible future. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35315582,"author":"JieJie","month":"2023-03","story":"Richard Stallman's thoughts on ChatGPT, AI and their impact on humanity","stance":"rec","why":"Shows shrinking models to fit analog chips for self-contained devices","quote":"I found this Veritasium video interesting, about a company that is paring down models so they fit on analog chips","text":"I found this Veritasium video interesting, about a company that is paring down models so they fit on analog chips, so the chips can be used in self-contained devices. It also appears that quantum computers may be even better for matrix math that transformers rely on. If all these hardware advances pan out... [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31591803,"author":"beambot","month":"2022-06","story":"Physicists are building neural networks out of vibrations, voltages and lasers","stance":"rec","why":"Covers analog neuromorphic architectures and low-power promise","quote":"Great Veritasium video on YouTube about analog neuromorphic architectures and their promise for lower-power operations","text":"Great Veritasium video on YouTube about analog neuromorphic architectures and their promise for lower-power operations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31661910,"author":"woojoo666","month":"2022-06","story":"Photonic Chip Performs Image Recognition at the Speed of Light","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of analog AI research","quote":"Veritasium has two great videos on it","text":"It's a big area of research in AI. Veritasium has two great videos on it: [VIDEO] [LINK] I found a hackernews discussion for the second one: [LINK]"},{"id":30590394,"author":"cultofmetatron","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: What is your take on carbon emissions of Machine Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Explains analog hardware for matrix operations better than commenter could","quote":"here's two youtube videos that explain is much better than I could.","text":"basicly, we can do things like matrix operations and differntial equations using specialized hardware that performs computation over analog signals rather than buidlign discrete systems. the end result is that we dont' need anywhere near the level of compute to do the job as its done using an analog of the physical process. here's two youtube videos that explain is much better than I could. [LINK] [VIDEO] suffice to say, your average house fly exhibits behavior that absolutely dominates our best drones and with a fraction of the circuitry."},{"id":44400111,"author":"btilly","month":"2025-06","story":"A New Kind of Computer (April 2025)","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier coverage of analog approximations of neural nets","quote":"This paradigm for computing was already covered three years ago by Veratasium in","text":"This paradigm for computing was already covered three years ago by Veratasium in [VIDEO] . Maybe not the specific photonic system that they are describing. Which I'm sure has some significant improvements over what existed then. But the idea of using analog approximations of existing neural net AI models, to allow us to run AI models far more cheaply, with far less energy. Whether or not this system is the one that wins out, I'm very sure that AI run on an analog system will have a very important role to play in the future. It will allow technologies like guiding autonomous robots with AI models running on hardware inside of the robot."},{"id":40837363,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2024-06","story":"An Analog Network of Resistors Promises Machine Learning Without a Processor","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on Mythic AI using NAND flash as analog compute","quote":"Veritasium did a video on analog computers, which included a segment on Mythic AI","text":"A couple years ago Veritasium did a video on analog computers, which included a segment on Mythic AI that uses NAND flash cells kind of \"undervolted\" as an analog computer to run neural networks. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":39537356,"author":"loudmax","month":"2024-02","story":"The Era of 1-bit LLMs: ternary parameters for cost-effective computing","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Mythic AI using flash memory for machine learning","quote":"They visit Texas company Mythic AI to discuss how they use flash memory for machine learning.","text":"The Veritasium Youtube channel did a video about this about a year ago: [VIDEO] They visit Texas company Mythic AI to discuss how they use flash memory for machine learning. There's a California company named Syntiant doing something similar."},{"id":36166357,"author":"mcdonje","month":"2023-06","story":"An open source, educational, low-cost modern analog computer","stance":"reference","why":"Prior Veritasium coverage of analog computing","quote":"Veritasium did a couple of videos on analog computing a year ago.","text":"That's really neat. Veritasium did a couple of videos on analog computing a year ago. I'm glad there are people lowering the barrier to entry. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36166536,"author":"shagie","month":"2023-06","story":"An open source, educational, low-cost modern analog computer","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped link to the analog neural net chip segment","quote":"","text":"[LINK] is an analog chip for doing neural nets. [VIDEO] (this is a time stamp'ed link for a video mentioned elsewhere in the comments)."},{"id":36168418,"author":"depingus","month":"2023-06","story":"An open source, educational, low-cost modern analog computer","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Mythic analog chip performance figures","quote":"The Mythic analog chip [2] is pumping 25 TOPS at 3W.","text":"Actually, I have a Pixel 7 with the Tensor G2. I can't find the tera operations per second on it, but Google has said that its [1] 60% more powerful than their Tensor Coral (4 TOPS at 2W of power). That puts the G2 at about 6.4 TOPS. The Mythic analog chip [2] is pumping 25 TOPS at 3W. For comparison, modern (digital) GPUs are doing 25-100 TOPS at 50W-100W...while costing >$1000. So, its a big jump. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36171977,"author":"code_witch_sam","month":"2023-06","story":"Falcon 40B LLM (which beats Llama) now Apache 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Mythic analog inference approach","quote":"analog chips like what MythicAI is developing seem like the next obvious leap forward for deploying inferences broadly.","text":"analog chips like what MythicAI is developing seem like the next obvious leap forward for deploying inferences broadly. ASIC/FPGA wouldn't be much different than a GPU. ASIC seems like a brittle solution [VIDEO]"},{"id":35382513,"author":"gopalv","month":"2023-03","story":"The Zombie Comeback of Analog Computing","stance":"reference","why":"Featured Mythic AI, the company in the article","quote":"The company (Mythic AI) in the article was featured on a Veritasium video[1] about analog computing.","text":"The company (Mythic AI) in the article was featured on a Veritasium video[1] about analog computing. The interesting part for analog is obviously going to be wear and repeatability, because the digital layer auto-corrects the errors until it becomes damaged and unusable clearly, while analog will slide off somewhat gracefully but you need a way to detect it happening multiple layers later. AI is somewhat tolerant of errors since, the cumulative error across a lot of biases can approach zero if the errors are random. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33482743,"author":"WorkerBee28474","month":"2022-11","story":"Quantum winter is coming","stance":"reference","why":"Shows startup analog computing products near the end","quote":"they show some startup products near the end.","text":"Fire control computers, like on a navy ship, were faster than digital computers of the day. YouTube has a number of videos on them. An opamp performs multiplication faster than a digital computer (speed of light vs a few cycles). It's not super useful on its own, but it does fit the criteria. In Veritasium's video 2/2 on analog computers [0] they show some startup products near the end. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31662310,"author":"dinosaurdynasty","month":"2022-06","story":"Embodiment is indispensable for AGI","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that analog coprocessors exist today","quote":"which is a thing these days","text":"Considering evolution optimized for biological fitness and not intelligence, it would be incredibly surprising if it wasn't possible to do better, especially using a vastly different architecture. You could also build an AGI to run on a digital CPU but interact with a simulation that used analog coprocessors (which is a thing these days [VIDEO] ). You can also include quantum coprocessors (which already exist in their infancy) for various things (probably only useful for quantum simulations at this point though). Also considering recent ML work, an AGI is more likely going to run on something similar to a GPU than a CPU."},{"id":30926826,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2022-04","story":"Λ-2D: Exploration of Drawing as Programming Language, Ideas from Lambda Calculus","stance":"reference","why":"One of many links in rambling toolkit list","quote":"Neural networks / analog stuff","text":"Warning: Bit of a 'Hitch hikers guide to the galaxy\"/conway game of life yak shave for a reply. Nice start to providing a simple way to visually interact/experiment/manipulate different multiple Neural network concepts by loading/transforming any traditional code (working or not) & reshuffling/tesselating the structual representation of the code to generate a turing tape path via color execution paths. Switching to pi calculus could provide the theory to support coroutine/coprossing/ threading\" multiple interacting tape paths/color combinations/path diffs/scaled recusion to perform assembly language instructions. Raster graphics without the yak ( [LINK] ) Feed back between tape & \"traditional code\" as neural network layer(s)! Flexiable code (via tessilations) without changing the actual imported source code! So, complete svg (tape interactions) web page as code source on how to interact with web page, ( [LINK] ) what's displayed/played, documentation & usage manual included within code structure! Unicode permits implied piping rich kern font without all the tech fluff. Unicode fails because allows infinate multics and is there for k&r incomplete. But, this approach is way more dimensional/colorful than unicode/tech stuff. ?? can one's code unintentially provide a wordle problem & solution in one package ?? ( [LINK] ) ?? turing complete fourier transforms [LINK] toolkit concepts: Neural networks / analog stuff : [VIDEO] perceptrons: [VIDEO] spreadsheets (: [LINK] (turing sprea"},{"id":30644506,"author":"yazaddaruvala","month":"2022-03","story":"U.S. eliminates human controls requirement for fully automated vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of improving compute hardware","quote":"","text":"[LINK] This is a rather intuitive mathematical proof that neural nets can emulate all functions in many dimensions. Knowing integrals (Calc 102) would help. With this information it’s either that self-driving is eventually possible or human brains are not mathematically representable (i.e. something akin to a soul). Meanwhile, the data collection/storage is getting better (all Teslas are continually testing FSD and reporting failure cases, synthetic generation of “good and unique” perfectly labeled data[0] is only a year or two “old”). Sensors/cameras are improving (and have a lot more room to improve). The compute hardware is getting better and cheaper (analog computers[1], TSMC 2nm and beyond, Apple/Google/Teslas neural cores, etc). Additionally, given Waymo is already in production without humans in PHX it’s arguable that we already have “autonomous vehicles”. We now just need them to improve slightly and for Waymo or similar to test in the top 25 cities in the US. Which is mostly a matter of capital and time not technology limits. Maybe weather will be an issue for a few more years/decades - but even if Waymo only operated in Summer, I’d be happy to reduce my Lyft costs - and with enough data and maybe better sensors the neural nets will be able to handle weather too. Especially if the car just goes slow. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30530824,"author":"bklaasen","month":"2022-03","story":"We're Building Computers Wrong [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The tech is great, but the (presumably most compelling) use cases this guy can come up with are automated surveillance and efficiencies in junk food production.","text":"[VIDEO] Start here to hear the deplorable and utterly uninspiring uses the guy comes up with for analogue chips, then rewind to the start. The tech is great, but the (presumably most compelling) use cases this guy can come up with are automated surveillance and efficiencies in junk food production."}],"r":[["IgF3OX8nT0w",2],["d8RRE2rDw4k",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["HUEvRyemKSg",1],["QtxVdC7pBQM",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1]]},"-Ht4qiDRZE8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the clearest account of human computation and games with a purpose, straight from the inventor.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching the TED talk by Luis von Ahn, creater of CAPTCHA and reCAPTCHA.","i":7600445,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7600445,"author":"vowelless","month":"2014-04","story":"Google's Street View computer vision can beat reCAPTCHA with 99% accuracy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk by the creator of CAPTCHA on human computation.","quote":"I highly recommend watching the TED talk by Luis von Ahn, creater of CAPTCHA and reCAPTCHA.","text":"I highly recommend watching the TED talk by Luis von Ahn, creater of CAPTCHA and reCAPTCHA. He calls this \"human computation\". He is also the founder of Duolingo. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3502776,"author":"drucken","month":"2012-01","story":"Breaking CAPTCHA with automated humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inventor of CAPTCHA explains how reCAPTCHA digitizes documents.","quote":"It is very informative and entertaining. I highly recommend it","text":"The interesting thing about these human-based attempts to defeat individual CAPTCHA-\"protected\" services is that if they are actually reCAPTCHA or variants, they perform a strong socially useful function! This is because reCAPTCHA solving is linked to the digitization of paper documents. There was recently a TEDTalk by the inventor of the CAPTCHA about this topic. It is very informative and entertaining. I highly recommend it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30873560,"author":"elviejo","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Could a game trick users into doing work?","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on games with a purpose.","quote":"His Ted talk is a good starting point.","text":"Yes this is called Games with a Purpose. It is Luis Von Ahn thesis. Luis created: recaptcha, to help digitilize books by harnessing millions of captchas. The ESP game to help tag images for search purposes. Both sold to Google And later founded Duolingo, to democratize language learning. His Ted talk is a good starting point. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3774042,"author":"pittsburgh","month":"2012-03","story":"Google Now Using ReCAPTCHA To Decode Street View Addresses ","stance":"rec","why":"Luis von Ahn defends reCAPTCHA as a clever way to digitize books.","quote":"it's hard to hate ReCaptcha after hearing Luis von Ahn talk about and defend his invention","text":"As a developer, I agree that captchas can be a drag on users, and I think we should put serious thought into whether or not their UX-cost is justified. Personally, I try to avoid using them on sites I build. That said, as a user, it's hard to hate ReCaptcha after hearing Luis von Ahn talk about and defend his invention: [VIDEO] It's a clever way to digitize books (and now street signs) while keeping out spambots, and when I combine that knowledge with my affinity for Luis von Ahn as a person I find myself less annoyed every time I have to prove I'm not a robot."}],"r":[["FV0DXNB94NE",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["Nbj1AR_aAcE",1],["dljNabciEGg",1],["Qi1Yry33TQE",1]]},"7J7X7aZvMXQ":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as a clear, well-animated explanation of memory, especially for visual learners.","q":{"x":"It is both detailed enough and clear thanks to the simplified visuals.","i":35418798,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35418798,"author":"superkuh","month":"2023-04","story":"What Is an MCR or Multiplexer Combined Ranks DIMM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear visualization of DDR multiplexing.","quote":"It is both detailed enough and clear thanks to the simplified visuals.","text":"I hate to be the guy recommending youtube videos but if you want to understand how DDR5 multiplexing works and this article doesn't do it for you check out the amazing visualization \"How does Computer Memory Work?\" [VIDEO] . A lot of it is applicable to all DDR but it specifically covers DDR multiplexing starting around 9 minutes in. It is both detailed enough and clear thanks to the simplified visuals."},{"id":33961051,"author":"qwertox","month":"2022-12","story":"SK Hynix Reveals DDR5 MCR DIMM, Up to DDR5-8000 Speeds for HPC","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Really good content explaining memory timings with animation.","quote":"the content was really good and is worth watching if you have some time on your hands.","text":"Yesterday I got presented an ad disguised as an educational YouTube video, but the content was really good and is worth watching if you have some time on your hands. \"How does Computer Memory Work?\" [0] Uploaded 1 month ago, 35 minutes playing time. It even goes into explaining what the timings mean while showing an animation for it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37749855,"author":"Cort3z","month":"2023-10","story":"UltraRAM","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent explanation of how memory works.","quote":"Branch Education has an excellent video on this named \"How does computer memory work?\"[1].","text":"DDR ram is refreshed every 64ms (varies by DDR generation and specific chips). Branch Education has an excellent video on this named \"How does computer memory work?\"[1]. It would still take an exceedingly long time to reach a trillion, but it's still pretty frequent. [1]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":35448240,"author":"myself248","month":"2023-04","story":"The microcode and hardware in the 8086 processor that perform string operations","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable video with well-used animations.","quote":"a video I just watched on the topic, which I really enjoyed.","text":"That's great, and dovetails perfectly into a video I just watched on the topic, which I really enjoyed. It's a little flashy with the animations at first, but getting into the meat of things, they're used well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34334644,"author":"superasn","month":"2023-01","story":"What Happens When a CPU Starts","stance":"rec","why":"Great for visual learners.","quote":"it's a great way to understand it if you're a visual learner","text":"I found this video a long time ago about this and it's a great way to understand it if you're a visual learner [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["IHZwWFHWa-w",1],["eMlx5fFNoYc",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["FTKP0Y9MVus",1],["6ihrGNGesfI",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["HVv_IQKlafQ",1],["HPqGaIMVuLs",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["BBwepkVurCI",1],["k1luKAS_Xcg",1]]},"9zinZmE3Ogk":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear, charismatic starting point for understanding Python concurrency and the GIL.","q":{"x":"the best learning material for me on this was this lecture","i":36917555,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36917555,"author":"culebron21","month":"2023-07","story":"How to use a Python multiprocessing module","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best learning material on Python concurrency for this commenter","quote":"the best learning material for me on this was this lecture","text":"I've just left the question open, but the best learning material for me on this was this lecture. Other points down the list, I had to figure out just by trial and error. Raymond Hettinger, Keynote on Concurrency, PyBay 2017 [VIDEO]"},{"id":17472824,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What talks or videos do you suggest to watch on a long flight?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favourite talk by charismatic Python core dev","quote":"I love them, the guy is charismatic, very smart and his Python knowledge constantly impresses me.","text":"I've started watching Raymond Hettinger's talks lately. He's a core dev on Python. I love them, the guy is charismatic, very smart and his Python knowledge constantly impresses me. [LINK] My favourite: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28690800,"author":"fastball","month":"2021-09","story":"The GIL and its effects on Python multithreading","stance":"rec","why":"Lighter, more practical talk on the GIL","quote":"I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Raymond Hettinger at PyBay 2017","text":"For anyone looking for something on the same topic but a little bit lighter / more practical, I really enjoyed this talk[1] by Raymond Hettinger at PyBay 2017, where he discussed the GIL as well. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19951926,"author":"xkgt","month":"2019-05","story":"Has the Python GIL Been Slain? Subinterpreters in Python 3.8","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for understanding the GIL and concurrency","quote":"will be a great place to start if you are new to this","text":"It is because only one thread at a time holds the lock in order to avoid race conditions. The keynote[1] by Raymond Hettinger from PyBay '17 will be a great place to start if you are new to this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26179355,"author":"targafarian","month":"2021-02","story":"Python Concurrency: The Tricky Bits","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for point that removing the GIL still needs locks","quote":"As Raymond Hettinger likes to point out, even if you throw away the GIL, you will have to implement locks on shared resources","text":"To say concurrency is broken in Python because of the GIL seems to be reiterated adage without actual wisdom behind it.[1] Are you writing an IO-bound task? Then asyncio[2] (even if just viewed as an interface: e.g., you can make it even more performant by using uvloop[3]) is a great way to achieve performant concurrency. As Raymond Hettinger likes to point out, even if you throw away the GIL, you will have to implement locks on shared resources (unless you're writing embarrassingly parallel codes--in which case either you can invoke them separately or use the multiprocessing module to great effect, or see how I do it easily below), and by the time you get all the locks implemented in your GIL-less world (locks which are error-prone), you've given up the performance you thought you'd get due to throwing away the GIL. Are you writing a CPU-bound task? I've only encountered this in scientific computing, as I'm a scientist, and for this (as another commenter mentions) I either use numpy[4] (strictly within a numpy call, the GIL is not held) or numba[5] (by default the entirety of your numba-fied functions do not hold the GIL). Therefore, I can either use Python threads or asyncio or Numba's built-in parallelism[6,7] (summary: some numpy routines for working with arrays of values are auto-parallized if called within a numba-fied function, and you can explicitly parallelize your own loops via `for i in numba.prange(N):`; in any case, then decorate the function with `@numba.njit(pa"},{"id":17858554,"author":"dangom","month":"2018-08","story":"JupyterCon: I don't like Notebooks [slides]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a clear shell-based Python teaching style","quote":"they are so much clearer and better structured","text":"Notebooks may be suitable for scratching things, but not much else. The problems noted by the OP are very serious, specially in the scientific world, where many do not have proper software engineering skills. The real issue is that newcomers do not know better, and so they don't realize the damage they're inflicting upon themselves and others before it's too late (irreproducible code, hidden states, dep. management, etc). Even the fast.ai library, which is a wonder, has broken notebooks. For those who try to follow the course at home, trying to run the notebooks is frustrating, as things are out of order and so errors pop up all the time. Jeremy is a wonderful teacher, but compare following a Fast.ai course video, which uses notebooks, to following a python video from, e.g., Raymond Hettinger [2], which uses sphinx and a shell. While the documentation style and ugly shell don't look nearly as cool, they are so much clearer and better structured. Notebooks become popular because they fill one gap that was left uncovered. As the scientific community moves away from Matlab into Python and R, reading code - pushed, amongst others, by the popularity of Github - becomes a day-to-day activity. Matlab scripts were easily explorable because users would load them, set breakpoints here and there, and look at results interactively - exactly what notebooks aim to provide. The difference is that what used to be breakpoints now become cells, comments now turn into Markdown and figures are i"},{"id":17301988,"author":"devxpy","month":"2018-06","story":"Why Threads Are a Bad Idea (1995) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Further explanation of why locks are only cooperative","quote":"locks don't lock anything! They are just a flag","text":"Threads are not hard. In fact, threads are extremely easy to implement. However, real Threading code is just incredibly difficult to reason, just by looking at it. This makes it easy for you to introduce race conditions without even knowing that there is one! There is also the fact that locks don't lock anything! They are just a flag, that a any code may choose to ignore. They are a not an enforcing tool, just a cooperative one. (More here: [VIDEO] ) P.S. I created a library, that makes it easier to write safer multiprocessing code [LINK]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1],["5ESJH1NLMLs",1]]},"G0Cp7DrvNLQ":{"wy":"Commenters in climate and impact-event threads send people to Carlson's Rogan appearances for his account of ancient cataclysms.","q":{"x":"he gives an enthralling account of the climate events at the time.","i":22511522,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":19800010,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2019-05","story":"Questions About Food and Climate Change, Answered","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Three-hour discussion giving perspective on climate and the earth.","quote":"watch all of the podcasts with Joe Rogan, Randall Carlson and Graham Hancock.","text":"For those that would like to hear a mind blowing discussion about climate (and the earth in general), watch all of the podcasts with Joe Rogan, Randall Carlson and Graham Hancock. They are three hours each and I promise you will learn many things that give perspective on the repeat arguments and confusion about the climate. There are many other podcasts with these gentlemen, but here are three to start with. Joe Rogan and Randall Carlson #606 (3 hours) [1] Joe Rogan, Graham Hancock and Randall Carlson #725 [2] Joe Rogan, Randall Carlson #1284 [3] [1] - [VIDEO] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK]"},{"id":18459050,"author":"noetic_techy","month":"2018-11","story":"Crater under Greenland points to climate-altering impact in the time of humans","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Randall Carlson's appearances on climate and impact theory.","quote":"Go listen to Randall's Joe Rogan appearances, they will blow your mind","text":"Looks like the maverick geologist Randall Carlson was right. The evidence was pretty damn good, but many in the mainstream geology had a hard time accepting it for some dumb reason. Just goes to show that real scientists should never bow to consensus opinions. The outliers always advance the fields. Go listen to Randall's Joe Rogan appearances, they will blow your mind: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] The Younger Dryas Impact Hypothesis: [LINK] The controversy: [LINK]"},{"id":22511522,"author":"eloff","month":"2020-03","story":"Evidence of cosmic impact that caused destruction of one of earliest settlements","stance":"rec","why":"Enthralling account of climate events and impact at end of last glacial period.","quote":"he gives an enthralling account of the climate events at the time.","text":"I remember hearing on the Joe Rogan podcast[1] about a theory of an impact at the end of the last glacial period, resulting in a short-term reversal of the warming trend known as the Younger Dryas Cooling Event. There was quite a bit of evidence in the form of meltglass and other things accumulating, but the theory was very much not accepted by mainstream science at the time. From the article it sounds like this idea is getting more support. Randall Carlson is pretty out there with his one massive flood theory as opposed to many smaller events. Nonetheless he's not an idiot by any means and he gives an enthralling account of the climate events at the time. Also it sounds like a much more credible explanation for the sudden extinction of large mammals (70% of species in North America.) Only Africa was largely unaffected. Including 100 million mammoths dying off which was previously attributed to human over-hunting - just the logistics of which with spears and primitive bows boggles the mind. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20698347,"author":"fluxby","month":"2019-08","story":"Arctic permafrost is thawing fast","stance":"rec","why":"Randall Carlson on a Rogan podcast.","quote":"I highly recommend getting to know the work of Randall Carlson on the subject.","text":"I highly recommend getting to know the work of Randall Carlson on the subject. Joe Rogan had him on podcast [1] few times. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["06e-PwhmSq8",1],["-f-CFQxaUY4",1],["sWN13pKVp9s",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["aDejwCGdUV8",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["xc8epam4NyY",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1]]},"KJe9H6qS82I":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever Game of Life or cellular automata come up, as the best visual example of a mesmerizing continuous-domain alternative.","q":{"x":"Must see evolution of GOL","i":22862632,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22862632,"author":"sandworm101","month":"2020-04","story":"Game of Life, Simulated in the Game of Life (2012) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"SmoothLife, continuous Game of Life with floating point values","quote":"Must see evolution of GOL","text":"Must see evolution of GOL: \"SmoothLife is a family of rules created by Stephan Rafler. It was designed as a continuous version of Conway's Game of Life - using floating point values instead of integers.\" [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":29961492,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2022-01","story":"29-year-old Conway conjecture settled","stance":"rec","why":"Best video of SmoothLife","quote":"best video of SmoothLife","text":"Toffoli[0] & Margolus’[1] Cellular Automata Machines (1987) is the CA bible, I guess. Andrew Adamatsky edited the encyclopedic Cellular Automata (2018). Ed Fredkin's[2] stuff on reversible CA is fascinating. Gerard Vichniac[3] wrote some great papers in the 80s, e.g. Simulating Physics with Cellular Automata (1984). Kenichi Morita[4] papers. Rudy Rucker[5] has written a lot of stuff on 1 and 2D CA, and worked as a CA programmer. Etc. There are a lot of papers. The very common finite element method of solving differential equations and PDEs is basically the same thing as CA - see e.g. Anderson's Computational Fluid Dynamics . Attractors and approximations for lattice dynamical systems by Shengfan Zhou, 2002. 1954's Studies of Nonlinear Problems by Fermi, Ulam, Pasta, featuring a 1D CA. [LINK] Noether's theorem comes up frequently - that every symmetry of the system corresponds to a conservation law.[6] Smooth Life is Stephan Rafler's very cool version of CGOL with every discrete aspect turned into a continuous one.[7] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] [6] [LINK] [7] best video of SmoothLife [VIDEO] [LINK] This slideshow by Rafler contains the best, most complete explanation of it I've found: [LINK] Shadertoy version [LINK]"},{"id":17340382,"author":"kjeetgill","month":"2018-06","story":"Show HN: Yagol, a Game of Life sandbox built with React.js","stance":"rec","why":"Mesmerizing continuous-domain version of Game of Life","quote":"Check out the video[0]","text":"If you're mesmerized by the \"Game of Life\" like I am your going to enjoy the Continuously Domain versions like smooth life. Check out the video[0], and an implementation in python[1] on HN. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":7686014,"author":"TazeTSchnitzel","month":"2014-05","story":"Show HN: HTML5 Game of Life in 24-bit RGB","stance":"rec","why":"SmoothLife as a more interesting take on Game of Life","quote":"I find SmoothLife more interesting","text":"24 parallel games combined in a way indecipherable to normal human eyes isn't terribly interesting to me. I find SmoothLife more interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30563475,"author":"muth02446","month":"2022-03","story":"Lenia – Mathematical Life Forms (2018) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tim Hutton's SmoothLife video","quote":"Tim Hutton's video","text":"Also: [LINK] featuring the seminal paper by Stephan Rafler and Tim Hutton's video [VIDEO] [LINK] featuring a more recent variant"},{"id":27483263,"author":"bioplastic","month":"2021-06","story":"ALiEn – a GPU-accelerated artificial life simulation program","stance":"reference","why":"Video of SmoothLifeL generalizing Game of Life to continuous domain","quote":"Reminds me of SmoothLife.","text":"Reminds me of SmoothLife. Generalization of Conway's \"Game of Life\" to a continuous domain - SmoothLife (Stephan Rafler) [LINK] Video of SmoothLifeL: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27034928,"author":"wolfium3","month":"2021-05","story":"Self-Reproducing Cellular Automata","stance":"reference","why":"Example of continuous cellular automata","quote":"For those interested, there are also","text":"(not directly related to the article) The cellular automata in the article is a discrete one. For those interested, there are also \"continuous\" automata [VIDEO] ."},{"id":17004116,"author":"cma","month":"2018-05","story":"Alan Turing’s chemistry hypothesis turned into a desalination filter","stance":"reference","why":"Smooth life using Turing-style reaction-diffusion equations","quote":"which uses turing style reaction-diffusion equations","text":"If you are familiar with Conway's game of life, there is also \"smooth life\" which uses turing style reaction-diffusion equations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4642714,"author":"tim_hutton","month":"2012-10","story":"Conway's Game of Life, using floating point values instead of integers","stance":"reference","why":"SmoothLife video with technical details and source code","quote":"Technical details on the YouTube page","text":"Original post: [LINK] Technical details on the YouTube page: [VIDEO] (Two lots of source code available: Stephan's and mine) Other discussions about this: Reddit: [LINK] Metafilter: [LINK]"},{"id":4647797,"author":"hughlomas","month":"2012-10","story":"Conway's Game of Life, using floating point values instead of integers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"do not seem to replicate the output of the video","text":"Good job. Any idea why using the parameters specified in the video description of [VIDEO] do not seem to replicate the output of the video? [LINK] I tried the same parameters in the version downloadable at [LINK] and it did not seem to produce similar output to the video either."}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["ab-r0capbzk",1]]},"1wAaI_6b9JE":{"wy":"Recommended as an enjoyable, accessible talk for building intuition about higher dimensions.","q":{"x":"I love Matt Parker's take on the fourth dimension","i":16527898,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":32195957,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2022-07","story":"Strange new phase of matter acts like it has two time dimensions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visualizing a 4D cube plus mathematical party tricks","quote":"The entire video in mesmerizing","text":"For anybody whom might be interested in \"what a 4D cube might look like...\" [VIDEO] The entire video in mesmerizing -- one of the coolest party tricks I've learned is the double-twisted morbius strip dissection."},{"id":16527898,"author":"IngoBlechschmid","month":"2018-03","story":"Counterintuitive Properties of High Dimensional Space","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enjoyable hour-long general audience talk on the fourth dimension","quote":"I love Matt Parker's take on the fourth dimension","text":"I love Matt Parker's take on the fourth dimension: see for instance this one-hour long but very enjoyable talk for general audiences at the Royal Institution: [VIDEO] (Things to See and Hear in the Fourth Dimension) If you know German, then you might like this talk as well: [LINK] (The curious world of four-dimensional geometry)"},{"id":14473929,"author":"jameshart","month":"2017-06","story":"4D Toys: a box of four-dimensional toys","stance":"rec","why":"Matt Parker lecture on how extra dimensions let you go around things","quote":"the whole thing is worth watching","text":"I found this Matt Parker lecture provided some good tools for getting your head around the fact that extra dimensions really do let you go around things, not through: [VIDEO] (the whole thing is worth watching, but the bit about higher dimensional visualization starts there)"}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["smStEPSRKBs",1],["VdqhHKjepiE",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["eviSykqSUUw",1],["O45LaFsaqMA",1],["pUF5esTscZI",1]]},"4oAB83Z1ydE":{"wy":"Shared as an emotionally powerful example of great advertising, especially after Steve Jobs died.","q":{"x":"I must admit that after watching it just now I teared up a bit.","i":3078230,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3078230,"author":"revscat","month":"2011-10","story":"Steve Jobs has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moving commercial shared after Jobs's death.","quote":"I must admit that after watching it just now I teared up a bit.","text":"This is a link to the \"Think Different\" commercial from Apple. I must admit that after watching it just now I teared up a bit. [VIDEO] What a life."},{"id":2164246,"author":"wallflower","month":"2011-02","story":"'1984': As Good as It Gets","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Emotional favorite spot that reassured the faithful.","quote":"My eyeballs just sweated.","text":"> Ken Segall did the brilliant “Here’s to the Crazy Ones” spot for the ’96 Super Bowl, which remains one of my favorite spots. It reassured the faithful that good things were coming. [VIDEO] My eyeballs just sweated."},{"id":4615186,"author":"alid","month":"2012-10","story":"Ask HN: I'm Talking to a High School Class about Entrepreneurship ","stance":"rec","why":"Inspired intro for a class talk on entrepreneurship.","quote":"the Apple 'Think Different' ad would make an inspired intro","text":"What a great opportunity! If you can show a video, the Apple 'Think Different' ad would make an inspired intro: [VIDEO] A fun activity could be to get them into groups, and get them to look at a certain product or issue through an 'Entrepreneurial Lens' - How can we make this better? How could we reach customers? You could have them fill out a lean canvas with their ideas :)"},{"id":308858,"author":"alaskamiller","month":"2008-09","story":"New Microsoft \"I'm a PC\" Ads (sans Seinfeld)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the benchmark ad campaign Microsoft fails to match.","quote":"What made Apple's ads work was that they didn't bother talking to you about computers.","text":"The fundamental mistake for these ads is simply that computers are a tool. Nothing more, nothing less. Microsoft wants to associate themselves as the tool of choice behind these supposedly famous and important people and their work but quite frankly that's bullshit. It's trying to capture the essence of Apple's Think Different ad campaign ( [VIDEO] ) but fails miserably. What made Apple's ads work was that they didn't bother talking to you about computers. They know it doesn't matter what computer you use, you just need to know Apple. Saving the environment, broadcasting yourself, campaigning for candidates, being a movie star, teaching children in Africa, all of that could be accomplished with either a Mac computer, Windows computer, or a Linux computer. Other than telling you that these people are PC it doesn't do much in helping you understand why Microsoft is even relevant in any of the situations presented. At least in the current ads Apple explains why their product is better based on features, simplicity, security, and functionality. Microsoft's ad just made me feel like it was empty and soulless, reinforcing what I have always associated with the Micro$oft brand. PS: offtopic, Discovery Channel's I Love commercial is wonderful: [LINK]"},{"id":66250,"author":"ALee","month":"2007-10","story":"Quitting job","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We've all read it, and probably seen it","text":"We've all read it, and probably seen it [VIDEO] , but for those quitting their jobs and changing the world... Here's to the crazy ones. The misfits. The rebels. The troublemakers. The round pegs in the square holes. The ones who see things differently. They're not fond of rules. And they have no respect for the status quo. You can quote them, disagree with them, glorify or vilify them. About the only thing you can't do is ignore them. Because they change things. They push the human race forward. And while some see them as the crazy ones, We see genius. Because the people who are crazy enough to think they can change the world, Are the ones who do."}],"r":[["ybcvlxivscw",1],["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["KcfFJ6pNEZk",1],["ZV-AFnCkRLY",1]]},"lY8Ol2n4o4A":{"wy":"Recommended as an eye-opening, fast introduction to what Blender can do and a model of a productive workflow.","q":{"x":"This video of his really opened my eyes to what Blender can do","i":23989973,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":33274699,"author":"slavik81","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: Recommend me a Blender course","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favourite Blender guide for productive workflow","quote":"My favourite guides of his are not in the Lazy Tutorials playlist, though.","text":"Ian Hubert's Lazy Tutorials would be an excellent supplementary series once you know some of the basics. They're not a general guide to Blender, but they're great resources. He has his own style of working and he's shockingly productive with it. Lazy Tutorials: [LINK] My favourite guides of his are not in the Lazy Tutorials playlist, though. Perhaps they were not lazy enough: Blender Motion Tracking: Room Transformation - [VIDEO] Wild Tricks for Greenscreen in Blender - [LINK]"},{"id":23989973,"author":"nicholasjon","month":"2020-07","story":"Microsoft Joins the Blender Development Fund","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening entry point into what Blender can do","quote":"This video of his really opened my eyes to what Blender can do","text":"Came here to say this. This video of his really opened my eyes to what Blender can do ( [VIDEO] ) and makes a cool entry point into that world."},{"id":23989845,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-07","story":"Microsoft Joins the Blender Development Fund","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how powerful Blender has become","quote":"The tutorials Ian Hubert does really demonstrates how powerful the product has become","text":"I just started paying attention to Blender again after some years. The tutorials Ian Hubert does really demonstrates how powerful the product has become - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23378428,"author":"aidos","month":"2020-06","story":"SpaceshipGenerator: A Blender script to procedurally generate 3D spaceships","stance":"rec","why":"Fast demo of transforming footage with Blender motion tracking","quote":"shows how he turns a video in a farm house into a dystopian scene using Blender's motion tracking","text":"I'd also like to point people to Ian Hubert's youtube videos on some of the things he does in Blender. This short clip (he races through his stuff) shows how he turns a video in a farm house into a dystopian scene using Blender's motion tracking. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["E1AxVXt2Gv4",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1],["495nCzxM9PI",1],["V74AxCqOTvg",1]]},"mOtKD7ml0NU":{"wy":"HN points to it as a good introduction to Idris 2 and a striking demonstration of what dependent types enable.","q":{"x":"this looks like a good introduction","i":20391990,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28659571,"author":"sourceless","month":"2021-09","story":"What will programming look like in 2020? (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Powerful types restrict implementations so the compiler can guess the program","quote":"when your type system is powerful enough you can restrict the set of possible implementations","text":"I don't know, some of that is at least starting to happen with Idris 2, though not in the exact way predicted: [VIDEO] (Type Driven Development with Idris 2) Short summary of linked video: when your type system is powerful enough you can restrict the set of possible implementations to the point that the compiler can make a decent guess as to what the program should be to satisfy the type signature."},{"id":27626805,"author":"still_grokking","month":"2021-06","story":"Fun and dystopia with AI-based code generation using GPT-J-6B","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Say hello to Idris! :-)","text":"You don't need the tests. You need only the types. And especially you don't need \"artificial dumbness\", which does not understand code, and therefore won't produce correct results, but just an advanced programming language. Say hello to Idris! :-) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20378302,"author":"pezo1919","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite non-generic or semi-obscure programming language?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Idris lang seems awesome with the atom plugin.","text":"Idris lang seems awesome with the atom plugin. :) Did not use it yet but I hope it will get more attention. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20391990,"author":"resoluteteeth","month":"2019-07","story":"Idris 2 programming language announced","stance":"rec","why":"A good introduction to Idris 2 and how it differs","quote":"this looks like a good introduction","text":"Until yesterday it was officially called \"Blodwen\" a \"prototype successor to Idris\", so I assume the fact that it's now officially being called a \"pre-alpha implementation of Idris 2\" means it's somewhat closer to being ready, but is there some specific announcement about the exact significance of it being officially called Idris 2 now? Edit: Never mind, I found the official announcement on the Idris mailing list: [LINK] He says \"No doubt I'll say more about this over the next few weeks,\" so it sounds like he's planning on making a more detailed announcement later. Edit 2: Also, as someone who hasn't been following Blodwen or how it's different from Idris 1, this looks like a good introduction: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18705024,"author":"huntie","month":"2018-12","story":"Dependent Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Idris examples of dependent types, including an impressive matrix example","quote":"I thought the matrix example was really impressive.","text":"Dependent types are types which depend on values. As an example, think of the cons procedure: (List A, A) -> List A. It takes a List of A's and an A and returns a List of A's. With dependent types you can write this as (List n A, A) -> List n+1 A. This tells us that cons takes a List of A's with length n and an A returns a List of A's with length n+1. Edwin Brady shows off some examples in Idris here[1]. I thought the matrix example was really impressive. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42504990,"author":"dietr1ch","month":"2024-12","story":"What would it take to add refinement types to Rust?","stance":"reference","why":"Dependent types make types first-class","quote":"Dependent types seem weird, but they help making types first-class","text":"Refinements embed a predicate that elements of the new type must pass. def Nat = Int: (|x| -> x >= 0) Dependent types allow types to be computed from functions (and depend on arguments, otherwise it seems they become just weird constants), def Five(as_type: String) -> NumericType(as_type): match as_type: \"string\" => \"five\" \"int\" => 5 \"float\" => 5.0f \"double\" => 5.0d _ => panic() // Unnecessary if you refine `as_type` from a String to an enum or a fixed set of strings. Dependent types seem weird, but they help making types first-class ( [VIDEO] ) and gaining types like `Array<T, N>` that allow ensuring things are the right length, and define append/extend properly."},{"id":42016171,"author":"jbjohns","month":"2024-11","story":"Hazel: A live functional programming environment featuring typed holes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"that was the first place I saw this style of development.","text":"Interesting. I didn't notice any reference to Idris [1] but that was the first place I saw this style of development. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21105059,"author":"T-R","month":"2019-09","story":"Modern SAT solvers: fast, neat and underused","stance":"reference","why":"Live code suggestion using an SMT solver in Idris successor","quote":"Edwin Brady gave a presentation on using an SMT solver to do live code suggestions/implementation inference in the successor to Idris","text":"SAT solvers are starting to be used as tooling for functional languages - Liquid Haskell uses one for refinement types[1], and Djinn uses one for suggesting functions given a type[2]. Similarly to Djinn, Edwin Brady gave a presentation on using an SMT solver to do live code suggestions/implementation inference in the successor to Idris[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20327204,"author":"rossng","month":"2019-07","story":"What I Want from a Type System (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of type-powered IDE features in Idris","quote":"There are some pretty awesome IDE ideas in Haskell-ish languages like Idris [1], too.","text":"Hoogle is pretty nice, and few other languages had an equivalent until quite recently. I'm hopeful that cool type-powered IDE features will start to arrive once haskell-ide-engine is a bit more stable. It's getting there! There are some pretty awesome IDE ideas in Haskell-ish languages like Idris [1], too. There is some movement towards dependent types in Haskell, so it might get similar features one day. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19476834,"author":"nathcd","month":"2019-03","story":"Compiling Knowledge into Probabilistic Models","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Idris 2 writing a whole function from a type","quote":"shows it writing a whole function when given a function type","text":"> In the general-purpose programming context, imagine if you could give examples of a program output (domain data) along with a skeleton of a program (source file with incomplete parts) and ask a system to fill in the holes. This part reminds me of some of capabilities of the Idris compiler [1]. In an Idris program you can leave \"holes\" to stand in for incomplete parts of a program [2], and the compiler can infer various bits of code from types and holes. In a demo of the in-progress Idris 2 compiler [3], Edwin Brady refers to it as a \"lab assistant\" and shows it writing a whole function when given a function type. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19080776,"author":"Jeff_Brown","month":"2019-02","story":"Reltron: a GUI for exploring relational databases","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I often wish I was using Idris[1], which pretty much reads your mind and writes what you meant.","text":"I agree about its availability. I think it's far more useful than you seem to, though. Reducing the problem space is huge. It's why macro programming (ala Lisp) is so dangerous. It's why I left Haskell for Python, and why I often wish I was using Idris[1], which pretty much reads your mind and writes what you meant. It's true of coding generally, but SQL programming especially, that there's a conceptually obvious, small space of reasonable programs, but because we're writing in text, we're free to roam over an enormous superspace of (occasionally) wrong or (usually) meaningless programs. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["X36ye-1x_HQ",1]]},"ngpsJKQR_ZE":{"wy":"Libertarian-minded commenters post it in surveillance, government and constitution threads as a red-pill argument that political authority is illusory.","q":{"x":"Here's all you need to know about the Constitution","i":10232944,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":13983705,"author":"kakapupu","month":"2017-03","story":"Anger as US internet privacy law scrapped","stance":"rec","why":"Offered as a red pill on government and rule.","quote":"","text":"> I do not understand. I thought that in the current climate, where people are becoming increasingly aware and concerned about privacy, that such laws would be expanded in scope. It's very simple. The government is not working towards our interests, but their own. That applies to every government everywhere, all throughout history, because all governments are populated by psychopaths. They don't want you to have any privacy at all. Instead, they want to monitor everything you say and do, because it helps them maintain their rule over you. Red Pill: [VIDEO] Oh and by the way, this account will be shadowbanned, because HN is controlled by psychopaths too, and anti-government (=pro-sanity) posters are censored out. One more thing. You're being told about a \"privacy law\" being scrapped, because they want to advance \"the chilling effect\". They want everyone to be afraid of speaking out, as the budding police state engulfs them. You could look into moving to some less scary country."},{"id":10232944,"author":"HiYaBarbie","month":"2015-09","story":"America’s Fragile Constitution","stance":"rec","why":"Presented as all you need to know about the Constitution.","quote":"Here's all you need to know about the Constitution","text":"In reality, it's not that complicated. Here's all you need to know about the Constitution: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10111044,"author":"sillygeese","month":"2015-08","story":"Stocks Off Sharply as Market Upheaval Grows","stance":"rec","why":"Argues government rests on extortion and should not exist.","quote":"We need the government out of existence","text":"Yeah, speaking the truth tends to get you downvoted. People are silly and emotional. But it's nice to see another independent thinker here. I've noticed you before. Don't pay much attention to the political circus though, the false choice between \"Demopublicans\". That's all smoke and mirrors to make you think you have a say in how you're \"governed\" (i.e. coerced ). > The current mess of the US economy is due to the choices of many politicians, in congress and the presidency, from both parties. It's much deeper than that. The dollar was detached from the gold standard in 1971, and that was the beginning of \"the real end\". Before that, the establishment of the Federal Reserve in 1913 set the scene for all the economic trouble to come, and so on. Check out: [LINK] > This is why we need the government out of the economy, and let it work. You're on the right track, but take it further. We need the government out of existence : [VIDEO] .. it is based on extortion, after all. What maintains governments is the belief in political authority: [LINK] > Hell if it weren't for government the bad banks would have failed in 2008-- and we would have had a real recovery afterwards. That's certainly true."},{"id":6964083,"author":"anoncowherd","month":"2013-12","story":"Edward Snowden, after months of NSA revelations, says his mission’s accomplished","stance":"rec","why":"Argues belief in political authority is illusory.","quote":"","text":"The only way to \"solve\" the problem is to spread awareness of it. Political authority is the idea that a small group of people \"has the right\" to impose their will on everyone else. In reality though, they obviously do not. Everyone has the exact same rights (and they don't include coercing other people), and you can't delegate rights you do not have. But the belief in political authority is the root cause of practically all problems in today's world. Once the \"Authority Religion\" unravels, so does mankind's enslavement: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10234400,"author":"HiYaBarbie","month":"2015-09","story":"Hacking Team, Computer Vulnerabilities, and the NSA","stance":"reference","why":"Additional clarification on freedom and law.","quote":"","text":"I can't tell what you're trying to convey. You may just be trolling, of course. \"The rule of law\" sounds good and all, but you have to differentiate between laws that are only detrimental to us, and laws that would actually correspond to morals: 1) Don't aggress against others, 2) Don't violate others' property rights.. and those are the only two laws you need. Now we've got like hundreds of thousands of laws, and they're mostly just meant to benefit the ruling class (and their cronies) at our expense. The \"If You Were King\" video clearly shows there is no such thing as a \"good ruler\". That applies to governments too (because politicians are our modern-day rulers). But in case you really need it, here's some additional clarification: [VIDEO] You talk about \"freedom\", but do you know what it means?"},{"id":9561030,"author":"sillygoose","month":"2015-05","story":"Self-Driving Trucks Are Going to Hit Us Like a Human-Driven Truck","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, we can agree that utility is non-linear. What we haven't established yet is: \"so what?\" :) > I think it's a fair question, but it seems hypocritical to ask immediately after asking: \"why would anyone else have the right to decide how their property is allocated or used\". Why would it be hypocritical? I'm not advocating for someone else using your property, nor for helping some through harming others. > However, every study I've seen done on this issue has shown that unconditional cash transfers to impoverished people tends to be used reasonably well[1] Didn't we already conclude that evidence is really quite meaningless in this discussion? It's not difficult to find statistics and articles to support the idea of redistribution. It is difficult, however, to explain why anyone's money should be \"redistributed\" away from him, considering it's his property. If a rich person's kid has a shitload of Lego bricks, should some of them be taken away from him to be redistributed to poor people's kids? > Certainly there will be some people who choose not to use redistributed income well, just as there are some people who have lots of capital who choose not to use their income well. What does it mean to use one's income well? Who decides if my use of my income is acceptable? Why would anyone be in a position to decide that for me ? If I want to buy my seventh Ford Fiesta because it will bring me immense joy, can you claim I'm not using my capital or income well ? > This one you'll h"},{"id":7635628,"author":"whatevsbro","month":"2014-04","story":"Popcorn Time Devs Drop Like Flies But No One Will Talk","stance":"reference","why":"Argues no ruler can be good.","quote":"","text":">> we should not have rulers > Explain \"should not\"? It’s a violation of our rights, for starters. Second, it doesn’t benefit society at large. A ruler’s rule only benefits himself, and his cronies. And finally, as the “If You Were King” video explained, you can’t be a “good ruler” - there’s no such thing, because whatever good you’d like to accomplish, is financed by the immorality of robbing people for taxes. Besides, whatever you wanted to accomplish would require people doing something to that end, and they’d either do it voluntarily (and your rule would be unnecessary), or you’d have to force them and then you’re just being a thug. > Life's unfair. Rule of law applies in some sense - if I put my savings in a bank (UK), the government guarantees them even in the event of the bank going bankrupt. That’s irrelevant, and not even a net benefit to society, again, because those guarantees cause people not to care which bank they put their money in. Without such guarantees, people would only use trustworthy banks, and banks would have to show trustworthiness through their actions . Besides, any deposit guarantee by the government is paid from money that’s been forcefully confiscated from everyone. Oh, and there’s a limit to how many deposit guarantees they can actually carry out without the economy imploding from inflation etc. > Why not apply the law more carefully and thoroughly instead of throwing it away? Again, no rulers doesn’t mean no rules. You’re thinking of laws in a "},{"id":7329063,"author":"whatevsbro","month":"2014-03","story":" Schneier Born Yesterday?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'll try to keep this brief, in case you won't respond again. And yeah, this is getting too taxing on my time too :p > For one thing, I might want to control the laptop you paid for, so it's clearly leaving something important out. Blindingly obviously, \"value you want to control\" only applies when you've produced or legitimately acquired that value, through a voluntary exchange for example. But I think this idea covers all property, regardless of whether it's a house or a book you wrote. There's no point in bickering about \"what if you don't want to control the value you've produced? -is it property then?\" .. etc, what you want to do with value under your control is up to you. As for an apple in your hand, yes, of course it depends on the context, but it's pointless to confound the issue like that. The point was that in general , anything that's already in your possession is automatically considered your property, and there's no need for a central state to \"enforce\" that - it's just our intuitive understanding of property. > Reasonable levels of taxation, determined democratically and enforced fairly, don't violate any of these. There is no reasonable level of extortion, and it's not like we get to determine it democratically anyway. But how exactly do you think a portion of our property could, in fact, be the government's property instead? -Because \"we\" (hah!) have \"democratically determined\" a certain level of taxation? Does taxation mean some of your property magically tu"},{"id":7467589,"author":"whatevsbro","month":"2014-03","story":"Obama to Call for End to N.S.A.’s Bulk Data Collection","stance":"reference","why":"Argues rights cannot be granted to some and not others.","quote":"","text":"> The other alternative is if the Supreme court rules spying is unconstitutional but then they are just ignored by the Executive branch Is there any doubt that mass surveillance is \"unconstitutional\"? Do you think the people organizing the surveillance (ie. establishing the police state) give a fuck? > for now there is still constitutionally protected rights and limited government Constitutionally protected rights, such as Bradley Manning's habeas corpus, perhaps? Limited government? -Oh please. The takeaway here is that strangely enough, a piece of parchment written 200+ years ago cannot magically prevent tyranny in 2014. (Much like the very same parchment cannot magically grant some people rights that others don't have: [VIDEO] )"}]},"4QXZ2RzN_Oo":{"wy":"Recommended whenever rocket engine cycles or turbopumps come up, as a clear introduction from a trusted explainer.","q":{"x":"Scott Manley has a fantastic video explaining all of this","i":19155304,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19155304,"author":"mabbo","month":"2019-02","story":"The Tech Behind SpaceX’s New Engine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains rocket engine plumbing.","quote":"Scott Manley has a fantastic video explaining all of this","text":"Scott Manley has a fantastic video explaining all of this: [VIDEO] In terms of efficiency, it's worth mentioning that even tiny gains have big impacts because of the rocket equation. Having a 1% boost the entire time the rocket is running can have an enormous impact in the overall velocity, or reduce the overall amount of fuel needed for the same targets."},{"id":21397405,"author":"paranoidrobot","month":"2019-10","story":"Pro (noun.), abbreviation of Professional: born 1798, died 2019, aged 221","stance":"rec","why":"Good intro to how rocket engines and turbopumps work.","quote":"Scott Manley gives a good intro to the way rocket engines work","text":"If you're interested, Scott Manley gives a good intro to the way rocket engines work, and in particular turbo pumps[1] at ~3:30. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20816454,"author":"JshWright","month":"2019-08","story":"150 Meter SpaceX Starhopper Test [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Great resource for learning about rocket engines.","quote":"Scott Manley is a great resource","text":"Calling Raptor \"really complex\" still manages to undersell it a bit, in my opinion. It's pushing the state of the art in rocket engine design pretty significantly. It's the first full flow closed cycle engine to fly. For folks who are interested in learning more about rocket engines, Scott Manley is a great resource: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["j3mhkYbznBk",1],["4jEz03Z8azc",1],["zFhYJRqz_xk",1],["2tdiKTSdE9Y",1],["HaEPXoXVf2k",1],["eg3_kUaYFJA",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["hT8GZlBBv5k",1],["hzyFZcuHmeI",1],["SFBBjynBpSw",1],["NAsM30MAHLg",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["AqeJzItldSQ",1],["kUfF2MTnqAw",1],["QEIUdMiColU",1]]},"5MdSE-N0bxs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear argument for why deep and cheap learning succeeds, often alongside the associated paper.","q":{"x":"physical data tends to have symmetries, and these symmetries make descriptions of the data very compressible into relatively small neural circuits","i":17050814,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17050814,"author":"tedsanders","month":"2018-05","story":"Why does deep and cheap learning work so well?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Physical data has symmetries, making it compressible into small neural circuits.","quote":"physical data tends to have symmetries, and these symmetries make descriptions of the data very compressible into relatively small neural circuits","text":"Here's a related 2016 talk by Max Tegmark (second author) on connections between deep learning and physics: [VIDEO] The gist of it is that physical data tends to have symmetries, and these symmetries make descriptions of the data very compressible into relatively small neural circuits. Random data does not have this property, and cannot be learned easily. Super fascinating."},{"id":13406749,"author":"jonmc12","month":"2017-01","story":"Game Theory Reveals the Future of Deep Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Clear argument for physics-based constraints in deep learning.","quote":"the argument for using physics models for constraints in a DL system was a more clear argument","text":"I think the argument for using physics models for constraints in a DL system was a more clear argument [VIDEO]"},{"id":12468523,"author":"adityab","month":"2016-09","story":"Link Between Deep Neural Networks and the Nature of the Universe","stance":"rec","why":"Tegmark's talk linking physics and deep learning.","quote":"You should watch Max Tegmark's talk \"Connections between Physics and Deep Learning\" if this interests you [1].","text":"You should watch Max Tegmark's talk \"Connections between Physics and Deep Learning\" if this interests you [1]. Additionally, a paper that has everyone excited about deep connections between the mathematical analysis of physical systems and the hierarchical feature learning paradigm speaks of the connection in terms of the Renormalization Group [2]. Regardless of it's practical utility, the philosophical implications do tickle the intellect. On a dreamy note, I wonder if it would be possible to draw Category-theoretic parallels between some physical theories and statistical learning theory. There is so much to learn, and I am trying my best to teach myself (on the side) the mathematics that they don't teach in my CS grad school. So much to learn, such little time. :) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":48421544,"author":"krackers","month":"2026-06","story":"How LLMs Work","stance":"reference","why":"Tegmark on why deep cheap learning works so well.","quote":"See Tegmark's \"why does deep cheap learning work so well","text":"See Tegmark's \"why does deep cheap learning work so well\" (well not so cheap anymore...) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15080650,"author":"mikhailfranco","month":"2017-08","story":"Deep Learning and Quantum Entanglement [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among references on how deep learning maps onto physics.","quote":"","text":"My random stream-of-consciousness reference reactions ... Tegmark & Lin's discussion of how deep learning maps onto the physical world: Why does deep and cheap learning work so well? [1][2] The work of Aerts et al. more than 10 years ago, including how vector space models of human categorization show QM structure [3]. Lucien Hardy's exquisite teasing out of the difference between classical and quantum probability: Quantum Theory From Five Reasonable Axioms [4] It turns out that the innovation, power and strangeness of QM is related to separating physical processes into: 1. Linear continuous unitary reversible evolution relying on complex amplitudes (wavefunction propagation). 2. Non-linear discontinuous irreversible 'collapse' or 'measurement' or 'entanglement' or 'correlated branching', with probabilities based on the Born Rule (real values from the square of wave function amplitudes). Neural nets also decompose a problem into successive alternating layers of reversible continuous functions and discrete irreversible categorical decisions (e.g. softmax/sigmoid logistic classifiers). An even more obscure tangent is that most of the financial market is now constructed from 'options', which track continuous values with a continuous payoff, but offer a choice of execution to collapse the option and create a real financial result, e.g. see N.N.Taleb's dicussion of optionality [5]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":12579830,"author":"clickok","month":"2016-09","story":"iGAN – Deep learning software that generates images with a few brushstrokes","stance":"reference","why":"Tegmark talk on why neural nets model long-range information well.","quote":"According to a talk by Max Tegmark[0] (and its associated paper[1])","text":"According to a talk by Max Tegmark[0] (and its associated paper[1]), neural nets (particularly LSTMs) might be inherently better at this sort of thing due to the way they model mutual information. Markov models are best suited to situations where an observation k-steps in the past gives exponentially less information about the present[2] (decaying according to something like λ^k for 0 <= λ < 1). Intuitively, the amount of context imparted by a word or phrase decays somewhat more slowly. That is, if I know the previous five words, I can make a good prediction about the next one, and likely the next one, and slightly less likely the one after that, whereas in a Markovian setting my confidence in my predictions should decay much more quickly. So in answer to the grandparent, such a thing should be reasonably straightforward to build if it doesn't exist already, and it may offer improvements over a similar model based on Markov chains. --- 0. [VIDEO] 1. [LINK] 2. Why is this? Lin & Tegmark offer details in the paper, but it comes from the fact that the singular values of the transition matrix are all less than or equal to one (an aperiodic & ergodic transition matrix has only one singular value equal to one), and so the other singular vectors fall away exponentially quickly, with the exponent's base being their corresponding singular value."}],"r":[["5guXaWwQpe4",1]]},"8BIcAZxFfrc":{"wy":"HN commenters call it the definitive, thorough answer to whether vaccines cause autism and recommend it as an entertaining deep dive.","q":{"x":"His documentary on Andrew Wakefield has become my definitive answer to people who ask me if the MMR vaccine causes autism","i":38752136,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38752136,"author":"faeriechangling","month":"2023-12","story":"Hbomberguy didn't want to make that 4-hour plagiarism video","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Definitive documentary on MMR and autism.","quote":"His documentary on Andrew Wakefield has become my definitive answer to people who ask me if the MMR vaccine causes autism","text":"hbomberguy rocks. His documentary on Andrew Wakefield has become my definitive answer to people who ask me if the MMR vaccine causes autism, a question I have been asked directly and indirectly for decades. Despite the fact I'd been asked this for decades, and despite me having much time to research this topic in that time, his 2 hour documentary released two years ago blew everything I had ever seen anybody say on the subject out of the water. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45999734,"author":"precommunicator","month":"2025-11","story":"RFK Jr.'s loathesome edits: CDC website now falsely links vaccines and autism","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive showing how absurd vaccine-autism claims are.","quote":"I highly recommend this deep dive, with some entertainment value","text":"I highly recommend this deep dive, with some entertainment value: Vaccines and Autism: A Measured Response [1]. It shows how absurd those claims are [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27889749,"author":"marticode","month":"2021-07","story":"Time to assume that health research is fraudulent until proven otherwise?","stance":"rec","why":"Thorough, humorous review of Wakefield's study flaws.","quote":"This video does a good (and humorous) job of reviewing everything that was wrong with him and his study","text":"Wakefield got off easy. This video does a good (and humorous) job of reviewing everything that was wrong with him and his study and it's a dense... 1h40m [VIDEO]"},{"id":37661955,"author":"thicknavyrain","month":"2023-09","story":"Common infections can spark psychiatric illnesses in children","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing the vaccine-autism claim was charlatanism.","quote":"was pure charlatanism, bad science","text":"The early research in support of this conclusion (despite the fact no subsequent research has born it out) was pure charlatanism, bad science and the theory is rightly derided because those who continue to propagate it do so out of naked self interest and financial gain: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30386350,"author":"pas","month":"2022-02","story":"Covid testing firm ‘selling swabs carrying customers’ DNA’ to third parties","stance":"reference","why":"Details unethical conduct in Wakefield's study.","quote":"The children were young (mean age 6) and some had severe autism, so they had to be sedated, held down by adults, etc.","text":"He conducted research for money. This involved ~7 children spending 1 week in a hospital. The children were young (mean age 6) and some had severe autism, so they had to be sedated, held down by adults, etc. His \"informed consent\" form does not describe the risks. [VIDEO] (Where was the IRB during all this? Good question.)"},{"id":28078931,"author":"scotty79","month":"2021-08","story":"94% of English adults have Covid antibodies, ONS data shows","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why UK antivax faded after Wakefield exposure.","quote":"At least that's what's postulated here","text":"They had Wakefield creating antivaxxers and then they had a journalist that got to the bottom of his BS. This expose was highly publicised so people got through into their heads that antivaxx is BS. Wakefield then ran away to US and Brits lived happily ever after ... till Brexit. At least that's what's postulated here [VIDEO]"},{"id":27434391,"author":"bdsa","month":"2021-06","story":"Fastly Outage","stance":"reference","why":"Debunking of Wakefield's MMR fraud.","quote":"Andrew Wakefield's MMR fraud, which was perhaps debunked more effectively in the UK than the US.","text":"I have seen the argument made that one of the reasons for high vaccine confidence in the UK is as a result of Andrew Wakefield's MMR fraud, which was perhaps debunked more effectively in the UK than the US. [VIDEO]"}]},"D7Sd8A6_fYU":{"wy":"Recommended in C versus C++ and embedded threads as the expert view on adoption and persuasion.","q":{"x":"The mentioned cppcon presentation by Dan Saks is awesome. Highly recommend watching even if you are not interested in C or C++","i":23205586,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23205586,"author":"drummer","month":"2020-05","story":"Modern C++ gamedev: thoughts and misconceptions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome talk worth watching regardless of interest.","quote":"The mentioned cppcon presentation by Dan Saks is awesome. Highly recommend watching even if you are not interested in C or C++","text":"The mentioned cppcon presentation by Dan Saks is awesome. Highly recommend watching even if you are not interested in C or C++ : [VIDEO]"},{"id":16496387,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"Should you Rust in embedded yet?","stance":"rec","why":"Renowned expert on C++ in embedded.","quote":"So, I will let one of the renowned experts speak instead.","text":"So, I will let one of the renowned experts speak instead. CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++” [VIDEO] Embedded Development with Dan Saks [LINK] Regarding Java vs C++, yes the enterprise world has adopted Java, however as someone doing consulting across Java, .NET and C++, I am really seeing it coming back since ANSI C++ has picked up steam again. I see it in projects related to IoT, AI, VR, big data,.... They are all polyglot projects with C++ plus something else, not C plus something else."},{"id":16279913,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-02","story":"C++ and the Culture of Complexity (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Expert view on C++ in embedded.","quote":"If you prefer to listen to someone actually relevant in the C++ embedded community, here is what Dan Saks has to say about it.","text":"I mean the embedded systems where Assembly and C89 still rule, and there is very hard to change, because the problem of adopting anything else is cultural. Basically, while there are projects being done in C++, and many companies are finally migrating from C to C++, the large majority is sticking with C. If you prefer to listen to someone actually relevant in the C++ embedded community, here is what Dan Saks has to say about it. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":45437659,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2025-10","story":"Defer: Resource cleanup in C with GCCs magic","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes Dan Saks's advice on persuading C programmers.","quote":"If you're arguing, you're losing.","text":"> But C++ still can't be used in many places that C is Unless we are speaking about PICs or similar old school 8 and 16 bit CPUs, with compilers like those from MIKROE, there is hardly a platform left were the vendor compiler isn't C and C++ (even if it doesn't go beyond C++11). And if it must be deployed as freestanding, there are still enough improvements to take advantage of. In the end it boils down to human factor in most cases, however as Dan Saks puts \"If you're arguing, you're losing.\", taken from CppCon 2016: “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++” [VIDEO]"},{"id":30181483,"author":"phendrenad2","month":"2022-02","story":"The Problem with C (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of talks proselytizing C programmers to C++.","quote":"talks promoting proselytizing to C programmers and bringing them to C++","text":"I wonder if the reason for there being no C language conference is: It's generally accepted that C is \"dead\" or should become dead. In fact at C++ conferences you will see many anti-C talks: * [LINK] And also talks promoting proselytizing to C programmers and bringing them to C++: * [VIDEO] And of course, now, Rust is supposed to supplant C."},{"id":27074147,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-05","story":"Modern C and What We Can Learn from It [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on culture gap between C and C++.","quote":"It is a culture problem, something that only will change with mentality reboot.","text":"It is a culture problem, something that only will change with mentality reboot. \"CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++”\" [VIDEO] \"Writing better embedded Software - Dan Saks - Keynote Meeting Embedded 2018\" [LINK] \"Embedded & C++ - Meeting 2017 Keynote\" [LINK] In a way, the same kind of subculture in C++ that jumps in cries of horror if you happen to use std::vector/array/string with bounds checking enabled."},{"id":26926784,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2021-04","story":"Improve type generic programming","stance":"reference","why":"Example of talks on selling C++ to C communities.","quote":"a common theme in C++ conferences how to succeed selling C++ to those communities","text":"Because it is a culture pretty much anti-C++, to the point that it is a common theme in C++ conferences how to succeed selling C++ to those communities, for example, \"CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++” [VIDEO]"},{"id":18281358,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-10","story":"Rust can be difficult to learn and frustrating, but it's also very exciting","stance":"reference","why":"Expert viewpoint on resistance to modern languages in embedded.","quote":"for another point of view of someone more relevant than my insignificant self","text":"Sure it is for political reasons when even C99 is seen as too modern, and many are unwilling to let go of C89 and Assembly even if the OEM toolchain supports modern C. And for another point of view of someone more relevant than my insignificant self, CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++” [VIDEO] \"Embedded Development with Dan Saks\" [LINK] Thankfully there are industry certification standards like AUTOSAR, which now require C++, so those devs can choose to keep grasping to C and switch industry or learn more modern languages."},{"id":18064025,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-09","story":"What can C++ do for embedded systems developers? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Dan Saks on C++ for embedded.","quote":"You can ask Dan Saks about it.","text":"You can ask Dan Saks about it. CppCon 2016: Dan Saks “extern c: Talking to C Programmers about C++” [VIDEO] Embedded Development with Dan Saks [LINK] When I started coding in C++ (Turbo C++), 640 KB were just fine, I could even make use of a full TUI framework (Turbo Vision). Hence my point of view about PIC class processors with single digit KB, being eventually the only target not possible to use with C++, unless one wants to suffer a bit with linker scripts."},{"id":16489235,"author":"Inityx","month":"2018-03","story":"Should you Rust in embedded yet?","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses difficulty persuading C programmers to use C++.","quote":"Talking to long-time C programmers about C++ is actually surprisingly difficult","text":"C is king because the industry is currently dominated by people who have been doing this since before C++ was a thing. Additionally, most of these people are primarily electrical engineers, and don't have as strong of a background in computer science. They've been using C for decades and it does everything they want, why would they take the time to learn the boundless complexity introduced by a language that offers them (what they perceive to be) very little? Talking to long-time C programmers about C++ is actually surprisingly difficult: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15162017,"author":"rjzzleep","month":"2017-09","story":"Comparing C and C++ usage and performance with a real world project","stance":"reference","why":"Dan Saks on C++ adoption among C programmers.","quote":"the biggest mistakes C++ developers did to kill the adoption of C++ for C programmers","text":"according to dan saks, who apparently to some people is famous c++ is faster than c (well in the test setup he describes below) [LINK] Language Design Implementation Relative Performance either any inline 1 (fastest) C++ polystate non-inline 1.56 x fastest C++ bundled non-inline 1.65 x fastest C polystate non-inline 1.70 x fastest C bundled non-inline 1.79 x fastest C++ unbundled non-inline 1.82 x fastest C unbundled non-inline 1.95 x fastest He furthermore argued that the biggest mistakes C++ developers did to kill the adoption of C++ for C programmers was to diverge from the previous line of \"C++ is a better C\" to \"if you're using C++ as a better C you're doing it wrong\" [VIDEO] (I have no skin in the game, I was just curious to see if it's worth looking at rust for embedded when I came across that talk)"}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["XkDEzfpdcSg",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["lgivCGdmFrw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"DC8tO16xdrY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the most exhaustive, well-sourced rebuttal of JFK conspiracy theories, often when new assassination claims surface.","q":{"x":"there's an exhaustive two part series by Sean Munger that sets out the many interlocking lines of evidence that Oswald acted alone.","i":38274612,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37460183,"author":"a2tech","month":"2023-09","story":"New JFK Assassination Revelation Could Upend the Long-Held “Lone Gunman” Theory","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Extensive, well-sourced case that Oswald acted alone.","quote":"It's all very logical and straightforward.","text":"I used to be passingly interested in the conspiracy part of the JFK assassination until I watched this EXTENSIVE in length and sourced video on the assassination: [VIDEO] It's all very logical and straightforward."},{"id":39172006,"author":"pgreenwood","month":"2024-01","story":"How the FBI targeted Camus, and then Sartre after the JFK assassination (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Good summary of evidence that Oswald acted alone.","quote":"A good summary of the evidence that Oswald acted alone can be found here","text":"The HSCA conclusions were largely based on the now discredited acoustic analysis of the police channel dicabelt recording. If it weren't for that the findings would have largely supported that of the Warren Commission. [LINK] A good summary of the evidence that Oswald acted alone can be found here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38274612,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2023-11","story":"Review: 'JFK: What the Doctors Saw' eyewitnesses contradict Warren Commission","stance":"rec","why":"Two-part series laying out evidence Oswald acted alone.","quote":"there's an exhaustive two part series by Sean Munger that sets out the many interlocking lines of evidence that Oswald acted alone.","text":"This new stuff aside, there's an exhaustive two part series by Sean Munger that sets out the many interlocking lines of evidence that Oswald acted alone. I am pretty convinced at this point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37464151,"author":"sireat","month":"2023-09","story":"New JFK Assassination Revelation Could Upend the Long-Held “Lone Gunman” Theory","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating video, though the bank robber analogy is questioned.","quote":"Fascinating video and indeed pretty straightforward.","text":"Fascinating video and indeed pretty straightforward. However, the bank robber analogy starting at 26mins [VIDEO] does not sit quite right. You can't just stick with one theory and keep fitting other facts to that theory forever - there is going to be a breaking point. To keep going with that particular bank robber story - Jake. In the example barring other facts, it is 99.9% probable that Jake indeed is the bank robber. What if instead of finding $9k out of $10k, police only find $5k out of $100k on Jake? What if not only talking to his girlfriend about robbing the bank he also talked about robbing the bank at the bar the previous week? What if there was an amateur theatre troupe at that bar that night? What if one of those actors was also an accomplished make up artist and high speed driver? Okay, the story keeps getting sillier. Even with these new facts the prosecution would still win. Let's add a new fact security cameras at a bar also show Jake, with similar timestamps to the bank's cameras. There are 3 more patrons that are willing to swear it was Jake. An old lady reports that she saw a car drive up at a high speed to Jake's car and leave a package. At that point we have a problem with being so sure on Jake being the guilty one. There is some reasonable doubt now. My point is you can never be 100% sure. All you can do is adjust priors. With JFK as it currently stands we might say 95% chance that Oswald was the lone gunman and 80% that he acted completely alone. We can "},{"id":37461077,"author":"lostNFound","month":"2023-09","story":"New JFK Assassination Revelation Could Upend the Long-Held “Lone Gunman” Theory","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the flawed acoustic recording behind the HSCA conclusion.","quote":"Essentially the conclusion of the HSCA was, as you say, literally entirely based on that recording.","text":"Another commenter linked a video which I’ll re-link[0]. Essentially the conclusion of the HSCA was, as you say, literally entirely based on that recording. If the recording hadn’t been there, the HSCA actually was on the verge of concluding the same as Warren. (In other words, if the recording evidence wasn’t there they would’ve concluded Oswald lone). The recording was, indeed, later found to not even be from the time of the shots but from an entirely different point in time, after police already noticed Kennedy shot. In that very recording, police officers say things in reaction to the shooting. After those voices, the “shots” from which the conclusion was drawn appear. Painfully obviously, that doesn’t make any sense. Also the recording was in an entirely different point in space too, as it wasn’t even from a bike in the motorcade, as the HSCA thought but it from a different 3-wheeled-motorcycle stationed where Kennedy would’ve been arriving. Drawing conclusions from that recording about the Kennedy shooting is probably less reliable than drawing them from the Live Aid concert version recording of Bohemian Rhapsody by Queen. In summary, after adjusting for that lone error in HSCA, Warren and HSCA concluded the same. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_x0eyNkNpL0",1],["2vuMzGhc1cg",1],["DleXA5ADG78",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1],["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["Uwuv05aZ6ug",1]]},"Qct6LKbneKQ":{"wy":"Recommended as a candid, in-depth look at hard UX redesign work and the limits of MIDI for notation.","q":{"x":"the whole video is worth watching as well","i":40222082,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":36700691,"author":"l__l","month":"2023-07","story":"MuseScore 4.1 is now available","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome video on the design of MuseScore 4.","quote":"he put out an awesome vid on the design of MS4 at the beginning of the year","text":"If anyone is yet to see it, Tantacrul (on youtube) is now deeply involved with MuseScore (UX lead or similar?); he put out an awesome vid on the design of MS4 at the beginning of the year: [VIDEO] His video on how Sibelius' UX is a pile of shit remains one of my favourite vids on yt: [LINK]"},{"id":40218762,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-05","story":"Printing Music with CSS Grid","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of MuseScore 4 engraving improvements and layout challenges.","quote":"his overview of MuseScore 4.0's engraving improvements [1] - in particular this second video, concerning mainly layout","text":"That's very much it. You could also make an analogy to the arts of typography and typesetting. It's of the same importance to a composer or musician that the notation is legible and beautiful, as it is to a graphic designer or reader that type and layout are legible and beautiful. If the GP is interested in the topic, there are great videos by Tantacrul on his experience redesigning the notation font for MuseScore [0] and his overview of MuseScore 4.0's engraving improvements [1] - in particular this second video, concerning mainly layout, shows just how far this CSS approach would have to go to have a truly legible, reflowable layout in the face of beams/slurs/ties. (I don't want to take away from anyone on the Sibelius team, they're likely even better experts on the subject than Tantacrul is, but as far as I know, they didn't make long-form Youtube videos about the topic! He also likely got the job of leading the MuseScore redesign thanks to his previous videos that critiqued and reimagined the interfaces of music composition software... including older versions of Sibelius [2] and MuseScore [3]) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":40222082,"author":"asddubs","month":"2024-05","story":"Printing Music with CSS Grid","stance":"rec","why":"Engraving segment explains notation layout; whole video worth watching for UI folks.","quote":"the whole video is worth watching as well","text":"Maybe the segment on engraving of Tantacruls Musescore 4 video is a good explanation of some of the things involved: [VIDEO] the whole video is worth watching as well (and there are several videos on the channel focusing on the UI of notation apps, worth watching for anyone who does UI but isn't interested in music production as well)"},{"id":34074040,"author":"btown","month":"2022-12","story":"In praise of MIDI, tech’s hidden gift to humanity","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating perspective on MIDI's limits for notation software.","quote":"The recent video on MuseScore 4 has a fascinating (though simplified) perspective on how limiting MIDI is","text":"The recent video on MuseScore 4 has a fascinating (though simplified) perspective on how limiting MIDI is: [VIDEO] MIDI's issues in a notation context are twofold: non-standardization of controls for e.g. choosing articulations for string instruments, and an inability to communicate future information if known to the MIDI producer - say, for instance, knowledge that this Note On event is the beginning of a phrase that will last for 2 measures, as known to notation software. There's a real way in which MIDI solved for communicating trigger events over the wire, but was not extensible enough to allow for further backwards-compatible standardization that would let it communicate music over the wire. By contrast, the web evolved from communicating hypertext to sending entire application environments and executable code over the same protocols. The web was born later, to be sure, but in an age where an Arduino can execute JavaScript, I wish we were looking to much more extensible modes of communication as inspiration for what can be done with interoperable musical devices."},{"id":49043769,"author":"rdedev","month":"2026-07","story":"Nothing works and everyone is euphoric","stance":"reference","why":"Hour-long account of redesigning MuseScore UX, used as an anecdote.","quote":"has an hour long video on how he redesigned the interface and UX of the software","text":"> The solution is to gut your product org, replace management with leading engineers, and hire some of your most fanatic users. They don't need to do anything other than give their opinions, and use the product every day. Put them in a room with your top engineers, and the software will mysteriously get better. This will just lead to massive selection bias. If all you care about are power users go for it. Otherwise you end up with a complicated system that is going to put off new users. You can even have different power users who likes different parts of the software. Now it's totally possible to end up with extensive subsystems that don't really gel with each other My anecdote for this is the the recentish redesign of Musescore. Tantacrul, the ux designer/product manager for Musescore has an hour long video on how he redesigned the interface and UX of the software. He is also a musician so you can call him a power users if you want but that was not the users he had in mind when redesigning the UX [VIDEO]"},{"id":40551086,"author":"bsder","month":"2024-06","story":"Debian KDE: Right Linux distribution for professional digital painting in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how hard fixing and evolving MuseScore was.","quote":"Musescore has a nice video about how hard fixing it was","text":"> Why do we get the free magic of people making great backward compatible code with other projects and so painfully not with this one? And I explained what the difference is. These projects are old and difficult . Most open source projects are neither. There are very similar problems with open source domain-specific tools, for example (Musescore, GIMP, FreeCAD, KiCad before CERN decided to fund it, etc.). Musescore has a nice video about how hard fixing it was and how hard it was to get to Version 3 and then trying to get it to Version 4 and how lucky they sometimes had to get: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dKx1wnXClcI",2],["XehNK7UpZsc",1],["7JUs4c99-mo",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["UQuIVsNzqDk",1],["ML4tw_UzqZE",1],["0bcZb-SsnrA",1],["IV3dnLzthDA",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["qbKGw8MQ0i8",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1]]},"ZQFzMfHIxng":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an enlightening explanation of atomics, caches and memory ordering from a strong presenter.","q":{"x":"Watching this was enlightening to say the least (and the presenter is very good)","i":15770071,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15770071,"author":"speps","month":"2017-11","story":"Designing a Lock-free, Wait-free Hash Map","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enlightening talk by a very good presenter on caches and atomics","quote":"Watching this was enlightening to say the least (and the presenter is very good)","text":"No, from what I understand, you can't even do it in software anyway due to how the caches work between cores inside the CPU. Watching this was enlightening to say the least (and the presenter is very good): [VIDEO]"},{"id":44953726,"author":"inetknght","month":"2025-08","story":"Without the futex, it's futile","stance":"rec","why":"Informative talk on memory ordering and atomics","quote":"I found Fedora Pikus's cppcon 2017 presentation [1] to be informative","text":"tl;dr: In a multi-threaded context, memory reads and writes can be reordered by hardware. It gets more complicated with shared cache. Imagine that you have core 1 writing to some address at (nearly) the same time that core 2 reads from that. Does core 2 read the old or the new? Especially if they don't share the same cache -- core 1 might \"write\" to a given address, but it only gets written to core 1's cache and then \"scheduled\" to be written out to main memory. Meanwhile, later core 2 tries to read that address, it's not in its cache, so it pulls from main memory before core 1's cache has flushed. As far as core 2 is concerned, the write happened after it read from the address even though physically the write finished in core 1 before core 2's read instruction might have even started. A memory barrier tells the hardware to ensure that reads-before is also \"happens-before\" (or after) a given writen to the same address. It's often (but not always) a cache and memory synchronization across cores. I found Fedora Pikus's cppcon 2017 presentation [1] to be informative, and Michael Wong's 2015 presentation [0] filled in some of the gaps. C++, being a generic language for many hardware implementations, provides a lot more detailed concepts for memory ordering [2], which is important for hardwares that have more granularity in barrier types that what most people are used to with x86-derived memory models. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":32938922,"author":"chrsig","month":"2022-09","story":"Lockfree Algorithms (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"One of several great Pikus talks on lock-free programming","quote":"Fedor Pikus has given a number of great talks on the subject at cppcon over the years","text":"Fedor Pikus has given a number of great talks on the subject at cppcon over the years some links, in no particular order: CppCon 2017: Fedor Pikus “C++ atomics, from basic to advanced. What do they really do? [VIDEO] CppCon 2016: Fedor Pikus “The speed of concurrency (is lock-free faster?)\" [LINK] CppCon 2015: Fedor Pikus PART 1 “Live Lock-Free or Deadlock (Practical Lock-free Programming)\" [LINK] CppCon 2015: Fedor Pikus PART 2 “Live Lock-Free or Deadlock (Practical Lock-free Programming)” [LINK] CppCon 2017: Fedor Pikus “Read, Copy, Update, then what? RCU for non-kernel programmers” [LINK] Tony Van Eerd has also given some talks on lock free queues, I'll leave it as an exercise to the reader to search youtube :)"}],"r":[["xbBdIG--b58",1],["ARYP83yNAWk",1],["2EWejmkKlxs",1],["B90_SNNbcoU",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["bY2FlayomlE",1],["92w5doU68D8",1]]},"rEf35NtlBLg":{"wy":"Recommended as a captivating look at how a professional crew calmly handles an in-flight engine problem.","q":{"x":"If you really want to see something captivating, watch this clip of a Swiss A340 crew handling an overheating engine in-flight.","i":18081430,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18081430,"author":"joezydeco","month":"2018-09","story":"Abstract of the NTSB Report on Air Canada flight 759's taxiway overflight at SFO [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Swiss A340 crew handles an overheating engine, very well made","quote":"If you really want to see something captivating, watch this clip of a Swiss A340 crew handling an overheating engine in-flight.","text":"PilotsEyeTV is a really really neat thing. They sell entire box sets of whole flights. It's pretty cool that the European airlines allow this amount of camera work in the cockpit: [LINK] If you really want to see something captivating, watch this clip of a Swiss A340 crew handling an overheating engine in-flight. It's incredibly well done. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16875285,"author":"jballanc","month":"2018-04","story":"Southwest 1380: think about the flight attendants","stance":"rec","why":"Cockpit view of an engine failure in a commercial airliner","quote":"Here's an amazing video of what happens in the cockpit during an engine failure in a commercial airliner","text":"Here's an amazing video of what happens in the cockpit during an engine failure in a commercial airliner: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8738511,"author":"joezydeco","month":"2014-12","story":"The pilots of Instagram: violating rules of the air","stance":"rec","why":"Swiss crew handling an A340 engine problem in flight, filmed in cockpit","quote":"This one of a Swiss Air crew handling an A340 engine problem in-flight is a great watch as well","text":"There's a company called PilotsEYE.tv that sells DVDs of exactly this. It seems to be mostly European airlines participating, but they have some serious aircraft filmed in operation. They load the cockpit up with GoPros. Not sure but there might also be someone in the jump seat catching snapshots of the console displays as well. This one of a Lufthansa A380's final approach and landing from FRA to SFO is a really nice one: [LINK] This one of a Swiss Air crew handling an A340 engine problem in-flight is a great watch as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29291374,"author":"bellyfullofbac","month":"2021-11","story":"Malaysia Airlines flight MH370 entered a holding pattern for 22 minutes","stance":"reference","why":"Shows pilots pressing buttons to enter a holding pattern","quote":"","text":"If going by the book, the pilot probably would have to ask for clearance (although maybe not if they're over international waters?), but it really is a button press (or two of them): [VIDEO]"},{"id":13539021,"author":"sschueller","month":"2017-02","story":"GitLab Database Incident – Live Report","stance":"reference","why":"Swiss flight emergency landing with a film crew in the cockpit","quote":"There is a neat video[1] where a Swiss flight has to make an emergency landing","text":"There is a neat video[1] where a Swiss flight has to make an emergency landing and just happens to have a film crew in the cockpit. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xKu2de0yCJI",1],["5SaFTm2bcac",1]]},"6c_LeIXrzAk":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it for its historical context on tuning systems and for how instruments influence what music gets composed.","q":{"x":"lots of historical context about tuning systems, but also raising the issue of timbre of specific instruments","i":23346742,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23346742,"author":"viraptor","month":"2020-05","story":"Guitar Decomposed: 5. Mutating the Third","stance":"rec","why":"Tuning systems history and instrument timbre","quote":"lots of historical context about tuning systems, but also raising the issue of timbre of specific instruments","text":"If you're into that, check out Adam Neely's channel. There's been a few videos on that and related topics: [VIDEO] (Spoiler: lots of historical context about tuning systems, but also raising the issue of timbre of specific instruments)"},{"id":17929873,"author":"mafuyu","month":"2018-09","story":"We know music is pleasurable, the question is why?","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Neely channel on why music is pleasurable","quote":"I recommend checking out Adam Neely's channel, where he touches on some similar concepts","text":"I recommend checking out Adam Neely's channel, where he touches on some similar concepts: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":16766320,"author":"abakker","month":"2018-04","story":"Where I started might not be useful to you","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Neely on how instruments shape what we compose","quote":"Adam Neely discusses this very well here","text":"Re #2, I'm not a programmer, but as a writer I find the same thing to be true. I work better with a pen and legal pad than a keyboard. I've got this feeling that we have neural pathways built for typing and, subconsciously, we favor phrases, words, and sentences that are easy to type. I know I even have a tic where if I pause to think I'll insert extraneous commas. It's kind of like how musicians tend to improvise around licks and scales that they have practiced a lot. There's something to thinking about the idea of a song on paper that is more creative than unintentionally falling back on chords/forms that are easy to play. (Adam Neely discusses this very well here: [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["q58dLWjRTBE",1],["7Hlb8YX2-W8",1],["_fQtxKmgJC8",1]]},"7wC42HgLA4k":{"wy":"Linked in electoral-system discussions as a quick, clear argument against the electoral college.","q":{"x":"you can effectively become president if you can get the right 22% of the USA to vote for you.","i":12675598,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":25030940,"author":"nanny","month":"2020-11","story":"Looking for Voter Fraud (In Old Elections) with Data Visualization","stance":"rec","why":"Debunks the claim that the electoral college protects small states","quote":"Watch some of these videos from CGP Grey for more info","text":">The electoral college is a form of protection for the naturally less densely populated agrarian states against the densely populated urban ones. This is commonly repeated, but it's BS. What \"protection\" does the electoral college give to Kansas, Wyoming, the Dakotas, Idaho, or Montana? Presidential candidates don't pay any attention to any of those \"less densely populated agrarian states\". In addition, dense states like NJ, NY, CT, and Mass don't get attention from candidates. California doesn't get any attention either, so what gives? The states that get the attention are the swing states with the highest number of electoral votes, simply because the electoral college incentivizes trying to swing a small number of voters in such states. Watch some of these videos from CGP Grey for more info: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12675598,"author":"cyphar","month":"2016-10","story":"Could Twitter Be Better Off as a Nonprofit?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how a candidate can win with 22 percent of votes","quote":"you can effectively become president if you can get the right 22% of the USA to vote for you.","text":"> It's been around for a couple of hundred years so it can't be that terrible. Heh. If only everything that is old was good. The all-or-nothing electoral college system: 1. Removes power from all people, because they vote for electors not for the actual candidates. 2. Removes representation of people in both swing and predetermined states, because they don't get represented by their elector (all electors have to vote for the same party, not according to the fraction of their state that voted for party X). However, in a weird twist, not all states require that electors vote for the candidate the state told them to vote for -- meaning that they have insane amounts of power. 3. The way electors are assigned actually means that small states have more voting power than large states -- meaning that you can effectively become president if you can get the right 22% of the USA to vote for you. Oh, and please note that the electoral college system came about because the founding fathers thought you (the commonman) was too dumb to understand how voting should work. So they gave the power of voting to electors. Here's some videos on the topic: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":10191499,"author":"Someone1234","month":"2015-09","story":"What if states earned their Electoral College votes based on turnout?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the electoral college is a bad system","quote":"Some reasons why you shouldn't anymore (CGP Grey)","text":"Some reasons why you shouldn't anymore (CGP Grey): [LINK] [VIDEO] Even in the UK, where the concept originates, a lot of people view it as a bad idea. The issue right now is that nobody can agree what to change it to, since people gravitate towards the voting system that helps them in the next election cycle (and the big two want it to remain a two-party system)."},{"id":21868607,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2019-12","story":"ToTok, an Emirati messaging app, is the latest escalation of a digital arms race","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a candidate can win with only 22 percent of the popular vote","quote":"it talks about how it's possible to win the electoral college with only 22% of the popular vote","text":"But the system (regardless of if you call it \"points\" or \"an actual vote\") means that one candidate could get 78% of the popular vote and still loose as discussed in [VIDEO] (it talks about how it's possible to win the electoral college with only 22% of the popular vote). I don't think that can be called a fair \"actual vote\"."},{"id":19779418,"author":"astura","month":"2019-04","story":"The Looming End of Payless ShoeSource","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument against the electoral college","quote":"","text":">If presidential elections were just driven by popular vote candidates would only campaign in the top population centers. Entire regions of the country would be ignored. This is definitely 100% false; this goes against the mathematical population distribution of the United States. The top 100 biggest cities in the United States combined only make up less than 20% of the population. But even if it were true, it's not any different than the current status quo where candidates simply only campaign in \"swing states,\" ignoring the vast majority of the rest of the Unites States. In fact, it would be better, because more Americans live in population centers than they do swing states. [VIDEO] The other problems: Many people who live in solidly blue or solidly red states don't even bother voting for president - they know their vote doesn't matter. I know this because I've literally heard people say it. This it not even considering electoral college also entirely ignores 4 million Americans because they live in territories, not states."},{"id":11929139,"author":"smnscu","month":"2016-06","story":"Startups and immigration","stance":"reference","why":"Bonus video on broken electoral system","quote":"bonus video","text":"That indeed is the problem, not the system itself. (if you want to continue with the cheap sarcasm) I see this pattern so many times in the US, blaming parties involved instead of the original wrongdoers (e.g. \"Facebook and Google are so evil\", yet the NSA and USG are out of the argument completely). That even a fairly educated crowd such as the one on HN falls for these cheap tactics is a great proof that in fact this technique works great for manipulating masses of people. (bonus example: the broken bipartisan and electoral college systems – all the talk is about how horrible Hillary and Trump are, instead of maybe taking steps to fixing the system itself | bonus video [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["2FMBSblpcrc",1],["yNzQ_sLGIuA",1],["RCRTgtpC-Go",1],["mOJlg8g8_yw",1],["vyenmLqJQjs",1],["YuIIjLr6vUA",1],["elvOZm0d4H0",1]]},"F8sZRBdmqc0":{"wy":"Recommended in cache-algorithm threads as a fun, informative walk through caching strategies and ARC.","q":{"x":"Bryan Cantrill does a pretty good survey of caching strategies in the intro","i":26673880,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38855977,"author":"bminor13","month":"2024-01","story":"Sieve is simpler than LRU","stance":"rec","why":"Fun talk on adaptive LRU/MFU cache","quote":"is a fun watch on the topic.","text":"> it seemed natural to have a set of generations for items that have only been seen once, and then another generation for things that have been more active Have you looked at ARC? It sounds similar - it is a cache split between LRU and MFU areas, where the split point changes dynamically depending on the workload. [VIDEO] is a fun watch on the topic."},{"id":36442096,"author":"anotherhue","month":"2023-06","story":"FIFO-Reinsertion is better than LRU [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the ARC replacement cache","quote":"Might I also recommend","text":"Might I also recommend: Bryan Cantrill on ARC: A Self-Tuning, Low Overhead Replacement Cache [PWL SF] 10/2017 [VIDEO]"},{"id":26673880,"author":"greggyb","month":"2021-04","story":"The Multi-Generational LRU","stance":"rec","why":"Good survey of caching strategies in the intro","quote":"Bryan Cantrill does a pretty good survey of caching strategies in the intro","text":"Bryan Cantrill does a pretty good survey of caching strategies in the intro to this Papers We Love talk on the ARC in ZFS. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20488890,"author":"mapgrep","month":"2019-07","story":"ZFS on Linux still has annoying issues with ARC size","stance":"reference","why":"Linked alongside paper on ARC autotuning","quote":"","text":"The author calls ARC autotuning “opaque” which surprised me given a well regarded paper has been published on it: [LINK] [VIDEO] ...that said the author has been writing on the ARC for more than 10 years judging from his blog links so perhaps that paper did not answer his questions."}],"r":[["D7GDTZ45TRw",1],["GDzBE6vz5r0",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["P76Vbsk_3J0",1]]},"HVv_IQKlafQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as a quick, non-expert primer whenever Gaussian splatting comes up.","q":{"x":"I really enjoyed this succinct YouTube video on the topic, for those interested in a primer","i":38578059,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46673508,"author":"KerrickStaley","month":"2026-01","story":"Gaussian Splatting – A$AP Rocky Helicopter Music Video","stance":"rec","why":"Two-minute introduction to Gaussian splatting.","quote":"This 2-minute video is a great intro to the topic","text":"This 2-minute video is a great intro to the topic [VIDEO] I think this tech has become \"production-ready\" recently due to a combination of research progress (the seminal paper was published in 2023 [LINK] ) and improvements to differentiable programming libraries (e.g. PyTorch) and GPU hardware."},{"id":38578059,"author":"spsesk117","month":"2023-12","story":"Gaussian Explosion","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct primer for non-experts.","quote":"I really enjoyed this succinct YouTube video on the topic, for those interested in a primer","text":"I am a complete layman in the world of complex computer graphics programming and research, but I have an interest in it as someone who enjoys playing computer games. I really enjoyed this succinct YouTube video on the topic, for those interested in a primer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37880591,"author":"qwertox","month":"2023-10","story":"Gsplat: CUDA accelerated rasterization of gaussians with Python bindings","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Gaussian splatting matters for graphics.","quote":"Both are worth watching","text":"This week I saw a YouTube video which left me impressed regarding Gaussian Splatting. [LINK] I got it recommended because I watched the following video: [VIDEO] Both are worth watching if one didn't know it exists."},{"id":37913970,"author":"willis936","month":"2023-10","story":"4D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Dynamic Scene Rendering","stance":"reference","why":"Concise explanation of splatting.","quote":"which concisely explains splatting","text":"Youtube recommended this video to me, which concisely explains splatting. Information necessary for trade is there but the trade is left to the viewer. The key drawback that isn't highlighted is that you need a physical space to be a close approximation of what you want to render. So if you want to make a few counter strike maps based off of your workplace (not recommended) then this would be a good technology, but if you want to make an open world on an alien planet you're likely better off with traditional rendering. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40230597,"author":"ghostly_s","month":"2024-05","story":"Show HN: Maps and Splats – Mashup of 3D tile maps with Gaussian Splats","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this video did not help at all","text":"> [1] 2 min intro to 3D Gaussian splatting: [VIDEO] I don't know what the hell a \"gaussian splat\" is and I have to say this video did not help at all."}],"r":[["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["HPqGaIMVuLs",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["ncHmEUmJZf4",1]]},"JdxkVQy7QLM":{"wy":"Brought up in threads about chord progressions and musical copyright as the funny canonical version of the idea.","q":{"x":"There was a relevant and entertaining stand-up bit about this","i":19407696,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":38265887,"author":"aidenn0","month":"2023-11","story":"Google Sues Men Who Weaponized DMCA Notices to Crush Competition","stance":"rec","why":"Comedic presentation of the same chord progression idea, preferred over alternatives.","quote":"I like Rob P's presentation[2] of the same idea more.","text":"I like the live version of that better[1], and while the Axis of Awesome wins in the shear number of songs, I like Rob P's presentation[2] of the same idea more. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19407696,"author":"naravara","month":"2019-03","story":"How did classical music in movies and TV become synonymous with villainy?","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining stand-up bit on how familiar music feels derivative.","quote":"There was a relevant and entertaining stand-up bit about this","text":"There was a relevant and entertaining stand-up bit about this: [VIDEO] This might partly be why people love the music they heard as teens first. I hear a lot of stuff now that just seems like riffs on stuff I’ve heard before. I don’t have the nostalgia of first being exposed to it in my formative years to carry me through."},{"id":4102454,"author":"lotharbot","month":"2012-06","story":"I analyzed the chords to 1300 popular songs for patterns. This is what I found.","stance":"rec","why":"Rant on how many songs share Pachelbel's Canon chord sequence.","quote":"Rob Paravorian on how so many songs have the same chord sequence as","text":"Related: [VIDEO] - Rob Paravorian on how so many songs have the same chord sequence as Pachelbel's Canon in D"},{"id":16671091,"author":"saurik","month":"2018-03","story":"Appeals Court Says It's Okay to Copyright an Entire Style of Music","stance":"reference","why":"Comedic take on Pachelbel's Canon chord progression appearing in many songs.","quote":"it is a \"style of joke\" done by others (and a far as I'm concerned, often better), such as Rob Paravonian.","text":"Which is itself meta-interesting, as it is a \"style of joke\" done by others (and a far as I'm concerned, often better), such as Rob Paravonian. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14668644,"author":"iheartmemcache","month":"2017-06","story":"Generating music with expressive timing and dynamics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"It's sort of convincingly improvisatory.\" I'm not sure if it was the dynamics specifically, but it was clear to me that A was human. Within 30 seconds I was so sure, I hit pause and loaded the answer to see if I was right (I was, and I'm likely the worst pianist on these forums and only a casual fan of music that falls into the 'classical' genre.) Here's[0] a fabulous physics paper that analyses the 16th notes by a studio drummer widely considered one of the best in his field. IIRC, the paper mentions he couldn't record with a click because it'd throw him off. That being said, the quality of the recording didn't suffer (his 2nd take of the track was more than good enough for the rest of the musicians to record against). So his own 'internal metronome' was more than good enough. The interesting thing wasn't that his syncopation was incompatible with a click track, but rather the skew which evolved throughout each phrase had a mathematical model that fit well against it. The study compared his recording against a corpus of user submissions of the same track and all of these drummers universally followed a similar set of dynamics. So presumably all humans (or at least, all western drummers who elected to submit their recordings) have that same skew intrinsically. It's interesting whether it's a byproduct of culture (like an accent) or a feature intrinsic to humans. In fact, that itself would be an interesting study -- compare the patterns of a traditionally schooled western j"},{"id":13160438,"author":"woliveirajr","month":"2016-12","story":"“BRAAAM”: The Sound That Invaded the Hollywood Soundtrack","stance":"reference","why":"Pachelbel Canon cited as something you can't unhear once noticed.","quote":"","text":"One problem is that you can't stop hearing it, after you know. Like Pachelbel Canon [1]. Like orange and blue scenes and posters [2] [3]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":7127724,"author":"evanb","month":"2014-01","story":"One Man Has Written Virtually Every Major Pop Song Of The Last 20 Years.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"That axis of awesome video reminds me of","text":"That axis of awesome video reminds me of [VIDEO] by Rob Paravonian. Also, the article reminds me of a parody video about the prolific nature of Bob Dylan: [LINK]"},{"id":18906248,"author":"saurik","month":"2019-01","story":"Analyzing the chords of 1300 popular songs for patterns","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I have to leave the obligatory (Rob Paravonian's Pachelbel's Rant).","text":"Which I guess means I have to leave the obligatory [VIDEO] (Rob Paravonian's Pachelbel's Rant). :("},{"id":18907961,"author":"pier25","month":"2019-01","story":"Analyzing the chords of 1300 popular songs for patterns","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes, after all I-IV-V is the most common chord progression. It would have been nice if the author also had analyzed chord progressions instead of single chords. Who knows how many Pachelbel's progressions he would have found! :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":10305000,"author":"ArekDymalski","month":"2015-09","story":"A melody written by a crowd","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"campfire song like","text":"It seems that the structure is already defined as standard pop/rock verse,verse,chorus,verse etc. And I guess it will result in people gravitating towards popular chords progression and melody. So at the end it won't be noise but a perfect Kate Perry/Shakira/Nickelback/campfire song like [VIDEO]"}]},"PGv5BqNL164":{"wy":"Mentioned alongside The Cuckoo's Egg as the screen version of an early computer-security detective story.","q":{"x":"Then check out the old Nova documentary about the story","i":39844506,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39844506,"author":"sys32768","month":"2024-03","story":"Cliff Stoll, the mad scientist who wrote the book on how to hunt hackers (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Old Nova documentary of the Cuckoo's Egg story.","quote":"Then check out the old Nova documentary about the story","text":"Definitely read his book The Cuckoo's Egg. Terrific story and prose. Then check out the old Nova documentary about the story: The KGB, The Computer, and Me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35244135,"author":"ndsipa_pomu","month":"2023-03","story":"How a Berkeley Eccentric Beat the Russians – Then Made Useless, Wondrous Objects","stance":"rec","why":"1990 documentary featuring Cliff Stoll.","quote":"It's amazing to see how little he's changed.","text":"Haven't seen anyone mention the 1990 documentary: [VIDEO] It's amazing to see how little he's changed."},{"id":27687389,"author":"cartoonworld","month":"2021-06","story":"A foreign seller has hijacked my Amazon Klein bottle listing","stance":"rec","why":"Television adaptation of Cliff Stoll's story.","quote":"A legend!","text":"There was also a television adaptation, \"The KGB, the Computer, and Me\" produced by PBS Nova in like 1992, starring Clifford Stoll as Cliff Stoll! Here is a bootlegged youtube link--it's the best I've got. [VIDEO] A legend!"},{"id":32717271,"author":"zoenolan","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What book have you re-read 3x or more?","stance":"reference","why":"NOVA episode based on the book.","quote":"NOVA made an episode based on the book [2]","text":"The Cuckoo's Egg by Clifford Stoll [1] Pulling a small thread of trivial inconsistent accounting value to in the end exposing an international spy ring. I grew up in the cold war, so I have always been interested in what went on. First recommended by a lecturer at Uni. The book reads like a techno-thriller but happened. Full of technical detail, I learnt a lot about UNIX system when I first read the book. I would still say the book offers a great primer on computer security concepts and the logical processes people use to understand a systems. Always good to be reminded of that from time to time. NOVA made an episode based on the book [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32601397,"author":"meatsock","month":"2022-08","story":"Cybersecurity in 1989: Looking back at “The Cuckoo's Egg” (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Re-enactment of the Cuckoo's Egg story.","quote":"it is hard not to mention the fine WGBH produced re-enactment [1] version of this story","text":"it is hard not to mention the fine WGBH produced re-enactment [1] version of this story any time this book is mentioned. there's also a german film from the POV of the other side of cliff's story [2] [1] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["oYp5XuGYqqY",1],["TMbl_ofF3AM",1],["y7HrM-fk_Rc",1]]},"SKtQgFBRUvQ":{"wy":"HN lists it among good resources on building text editors and as a recommended lunch-and-learn talk.","q":{"x":"So far I've found and enjoyed reading/watching the resources below","i":12832111,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16777511,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2018-04","story":"Immutable.rs: immutable data structures for Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Xi editor's immutable text structure and Rust's suitability","quote":"the author lays out the details here","text":"We had a talk at RustConf previously about the Xi editor; it uses an immutable data structure to handle text. It's a video but the author lays out the details here: [VIDEO] They make an interesting argument about how Rust makes it easier and nicer to write these kinds of structures. It's still an area of exploration in Rust generally though; I wouldn't say immutable data structures are idiomatic, but that's also just because we haven't had a ton of easy ones to use, so nobody uses them. So we'll see!"},{"id":15159748,"author":"kartD","month":"2017-09","story":"Ask HN: Recommended (recent) videos for a Lunch and Learn","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among recommended lunch-and-learn videos","quote":"RustConf 2016 - A Modern Editor Built in Rust by Raph Levien","text":"Little drunk so bear with me :) Rich Hickey: Clojure concurrency[ [LINK] ] Keynote - Rust: confident, productive systems programming by Aaron Turon [ [LINK] ] RustConf 2016 - A Modern Editor Built in Rust by Raph Levien [ [VIDEO] ] Microservices at Netflix Scale: Principles, Tradeoffs & Lessons Learned[ [LINK] ] What I Wish I Had Known Before Scaling Uber to 1000 Services[ [LINK] ] Dynamic Code Optimization and the NVIDIA Denver Processor [ [LINK] ] * if you can get anything on Apple's Ax series that's even better"},{"id":12698724,"author":"MildlySerious","month":"2016-10","story":"Lime Text – Open Source Sublime Text alternative (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about the Xi editor","quote":"There's a talk about it, too, if interested","text":"There's a talk about it, too, if interested: [VIDEO] I'm hoping to find the time to get more comfortable with Rust and try to contribute at some point"},{"id":12832111,"author":"bonquesha99","month":"2016-10","story":"Buttery Smooth Emacs","stance":"rec","why":"Resource on building text editors","quote":"So far I've found and enjoyed reading/watching the resources below","text":"What are some good resources to learn more about the inner workings of text editors or building one from scratch? So far I've found and enjoyed reading/watching the resources below and would love to learn more. * The Craft of Text Editing: [LINK] * Writing a Text Editor From Scratch: [LINK] * A Modern Text Editor Built in Rust: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["UGttmR2DTY8",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["BKP1I7IocYU",1],["kb-m2fasdDY",1]]},"TshYfYIxR9E":{"wy":"HN points to it for a clear explanation of photon sieves versus Fresnel lenses and for showing what interference patterns actually look like.","q":{"x":"It shows how the interference patterns actually look.","i":31557291,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31557291,"author":"eterevsky","month":"2022-05","story":"Chaos: The real problem with quantum mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what interference patterns actually look like","quote":"It shows how the interference patterns actually look.","text":"Regarding waves and interference, have a look at this video: [VIDEO] . It shows how the interference patterns actually look. Yes, the wave is 3D and the interference patterns can be kind of like checker board. We are usually considering a 2D example because it is simpler to visualize and analize. > how do we know that entanglement is the result of 'spooky action at a distance' and not the result of creating two entangled particles? A very high-level explanation is that we know this because quantum computers work (even though right now we can't build them big enough to be useful) and it can't be explained by any classical effects."},{"id":31151250,"author":"avian","month":"2022-04","story":"Photon Sieve","stance":"rec","why":"Photon sieve made with home-made lithography setup","quote":"The Huygens Optics channel has a fascinating video abut a photon sieve created using a home-made litography setup","text":"The Huygens Optics channel has a fascinating video abut a photon sieve created using a home-made litography setup: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24525672,"author":"Learning2-2","month":"2020-09","story":"Engineers produce a fisheye lens that’s completely flat","stance":"rec","why":"Explains difference between Fresnel lens and photon sieve","quote":"Here is a video explaining the difference between a Fresnel lens and a photon sieve","text":"Here is a video explaining the difference between a Fresnel lens and a photon sieve: Photon sieves used to visualise optical wave front propagation [VIDEO]"},{"id":33207439,"author":"stuntkite","month":"2022-10","story":"New salt-grain sized micro camera takes images on par with a full size camera’s","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They are talking about the photon seive micro lenses that make up the whole lens. Each micro lens is the size of a virus. The \"camera\" is the size of a grain of salt. I don't think this is intentional, but this feels like trigger bait for people dealing with a manic psychotic state. Like there were other things to compare it to other than an HIV virus. I'm sure it's exactly the same size, but you know, might as well say \"the same size as a large Coronavirus wrapped in 5G Monkeypox\". Also, > The researchers believe their micro camera could be dusted on a surface, making anything a camera. (Perhaps not the most comforting thought, as Nerdist points out.) I think this is really interesting technology. I was suspecting something similar would arrive after learning about making optical tensors that you print in 3D space and learning more about \"photon sieves\". This is a weird part of our future. There are really interesting new on a chip photonics methods to generate targetable full spectrum light out of a single laser light source. 2025 we'll see some really really crazy light processing products. - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"}],"r":[["xP5-iIeKXE8",1]]},"wvJaKQ94lBY":{"wy":"Linked whenever mixed Python and C++ debugging came up as a neat PTVS feature.","q":{"x":"if you want to get a feel for an advanced feature, try","i":10000741,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10000741,"author":"smortaz","month":"2015-08","story":"Why Write Python in Visual Studio?","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates an advanced PTVS feature","quote":"if you want to get a feel for an advanced feature, try","text":"If you want to get a feel for what PTVS is like, you can watch these short videos: [LINK] 10k foot view web page: [LINK] if you want to get a feel for an advanced feature, try: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6776069,"author":"smortaz","month":"2013-11","story":"Debugging Python Like a Boss","stance":"rec","why":"Shows PTVS mixed-mode Python/C++ debugging","quote":"try PTVS which has nice features like mixed-mode Python/C++ debugging","text":"very nice! plug: if you happen to be on windows, try PTVS which has nice features like mixed-mode Python/C++ debugging as well cross debugging from Visual Studio <-> linux & MacOS. (it's a free plug-in). [VIDEO]"},{"id":5961191,"author":"smortaz","month":"2013-06","story":"Learn Python in minutes","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates debugging Python and C++ together","quote":"debugging Python & C++ together","text":"awesome tutorial. if you need a free Python IDE (based on VS) to try the exercises in, we just released PTVS 2.0 Beta: [LINK] . for you gurus, it's got a cool new feature: debugging Python & C++ together: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7111093,"author":"smortaz","month":"2014-01","story":"Why I'm Betting On Julia","stance":"reference","why":"Shows PTVS Python/C++ debugging support","quote":"if you do you use Python & require Python/C++ debugging, PTVS now supports it","text":"mini ASK HN: would there be any interest in supporting Julia in Visual Studio? (as a free/oss plugin). i lead the Python Tools for Visual Studio project at msft and would be curious if there is interest. as a side note, if you do you use Python & require Python/C++ debugging, PTVS now supports it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5978051,"author":"bru","month":"2013-07","story":"Python Tools for Visual Studio","stance":"reference","why":"Mixed-language Python and C++ debugging overview","quote":"","text":">[...] you're a hardcore PhD who wants mixed-language Python and C++ debugging [1] [...] It is mentioned. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5947667,"author":"smortaz","month":"2013-06","story":"Visual Studio 2013, ALM, and DevOps","stance":"reference","why":"Shows debugging CPython and C++ together","quote":"Has a neat feature to debug CPython & C++ together","text":"In case you're a Python dev, VS2013 now includes support. info at [LINK] . Has a neat feature to debug CPython & C++ together: [VIDEO]"},{"id":5953278,"author":"3JPLW","month":"2013-06","story":"Python Tools for Visual Studio 2.0 Beta","stance":"reference","why":"Documents mixed-mode debugging introduced in the beta","quote":"Beta introduces mixed Python and C/C++ debugging.","text":"Two highlights from the release notes: - Starting with VS2013, we’re happy to announce that PTVS is “in the box”. This means when you install Visual Studio, there is a an existing “Python” download link which will install PTVS for you. - Mixed-mode debugging! Alpha introduced remote debugging on Linux and MacOS. Beta introduces mixed Python and C/C++ debugging. Please refer to the documentation or this video: [VIDEO]"}]},"UyFF4cpMVag":{"wy":"HN cites it as the one dramatic real-world proof that a launch escape system works.","q":{"x":"here is a neat video of a launch escape system working as intended during Soyuz T-10-1","i":4709412,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":4399273,"author":"jlgreco","month":"2012-08","story":"How We Nearly Lost the Discovery Shuttle","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Soyuz T-10-1 escape tower pulling crew away from exploding rocket.","quote":"The video of T-10-1 is stunning","text":"The video of T-10-1 is stunning: [VIDEO] That the people on that rocket escaped with \"bruises\" is amazing."},{"id":4709412,"author":"jlgreco","month":"2012-10","story":"Mercury-Redstone 1 - The four inch flight","stance":"rec","why":"Only real use of a launch escape system, Soyuz T-10-1.","quote":"here is a neat video of a launch escape system working as intended during Soyuz T-10-1","text":"For those interested, here is a neat video of a launch escape system working as intended during Soyuz T-10-1: [VIDEO] It is the only time a launch escape system has actually been used."},{"id":17010745,"author":"dvdkhlng","month":"2018-05","story":"NASA advisers say SpaceX rocket technology could put lives at risk","stance":"reference","why":"Real emergency use of the Soyuz escape system.","quote":"Video of Soyuz launch escape system used in a real emergency","text":"Related video: Video of Soyuz launch escape system used in a real emergency: [VIDEO] This is probably how SpaceX imagines astronauts would be saved in case of a fueling problem."},{"id":3261943,"author":"kens","month":"2011-11","story":"Is SpaceX changing the rocket equation?","stance":"reference","why":"Soyuz explosion where the escape system saved the crew.","quote":"There's a video of the Soyuz explosion mentioned above at that shows the escape system saving the crew.","text":"A few random comments: There's a video of the Soyuz explosion mentioned above at [VIDEO] that shows the escape system saving the crew. The use of solid rocket boosters for human space flight (as was planned for Ares I) is hugely controversial because they can't just be shut down in the event of a problem, as regular engines can, making survivability more difficult. Personally, I think NASA is overly risk-adverse, to the point that nothing gets accomplished (see Constellation / Ares I). An interesting article on this is [LINK] On the topic of aircraft turbine failures, there's the interesting case of a DC-10 engine that disintegrated in flight due to a pilot experiment, killing a passenger. For details see: [LINK] Finally, if you're interested in SpaceX, NASA budgeting, and so forth, I recommend [LINK]"}],"r":[["_YysNM2JoFo",1]]},"V6VP-2aIcSc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a concrete, worked example of the case against heavy OOP rather than abstract argument.","q":{"x":"I recommend the whole series","i":31866740,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31866740,"author":"codemonkey-zeta","month":"2022-06","story":"Goodbye, shitty Car extends Vehicle object-orientation tutorial (2011)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rewrites a nontrivial OOP program procedurally, saving code and complexity","quote":"I recommend the whole series","text":"For anyone unfamiliar, I recommend the whole series, but it also culminates in the presenter rewriting an entire nontrivial OOP program into a procedural one and saving whopping amounts of code/complexity. The big example helps to show this isn't just abstract rambling against OOP. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13133833,"author":"lj3","month":"2016-12","story":"Talks that changed the way I think about programming","stance":"rec","why":"Brian Will's critique of OOP with a large example","quote":"There are three talks, actually.","text":"There are three talks, actually. Here are the other two. Object-Oriented Programming is Embarrassing: 4 Short Examples[0] Object-Oriented Programming is Garbage: 3800 SLOC example[1] [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14598968,"author":"barking","month":"2017-06","story":"How to name things: the hardest problem in programming (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Rewrites an OO program in procedural style, cutting function count","quote":"he rewrites an OO program in procedural style","text":"Brian Will, the guy behind the 'why OOP is bad' youtube video brings this into his argument. In a follow up video to that video he rewrites an OO program in procedural style. In doing so he reduced the number of methods/functions from 321 to 113, 54 of which were contained inside other functions as they were only accessed by the containing function. The total number of lines of code remained about the same. That's over 200 fewer meaningful names to try to come up with in a short program. Plus the fact that functions that do bigger tasks are easier to name in the first place, imo. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14221170,"author":"hasenj","month":"2017-04","story":"The Clean Architecture (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This kind of thinking bothers me, but I'm not sure how to put my finger on what exactly it is that bothers me, or how to put it into words. The thing with these abstract architecture principles is they are not always practical if you try to be puristic about enforcing them. I get where these ideals come from. I've seen novice programmers take a stab at writing mildly complicated apps, and the code is a nightmare to read because everything is jumbled up together. This is bad and we can all agree on that. But I've also seen projects where everything is split into a tiny little function or object that don't seem to be doing anything meaningful. Presumably these projects are following the principles of \"single responsibility\" and \"loose coupling\", but it's so loose that it's hard to put the pieces together in your mind and nearly impossible to follow the flow of the program. I consider generic rules of the form: \"<X> related objects should not be doing <Y> related stuff\" to be harmful. (An example of such \"bad rules\" can be found in the article under the heading \"Use Cases\") Instead I prefer practical rules that make the code easy to read, understand, and updated, without making it any more complicated that it needs to be. * If a piece of logic can be contained in a function, let it be contained in one function without splitting it across 10 different objects and factories and coordinators. Even if that function is slightly long, there's no need to split it apart just because it'"}],"r":[["cfyWvJdsDRI",1],["816VLQNdPMM",1]]},"W88Sact1kws":{"wy":"HN cites it as a compelling example of unforeseen ecosystem effects and rewilding.","q":{"x":"probably one of the most fascinating videos on YouTube","i":38777101,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38777101,"author":"sakopov","month":"2023-12","story":"Colorado wolf reintroduction to move forward","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how reintroduced wolves reshaped Yellowstone's rivers.","quote":"probably one of the most fascinating videos on YouTube","text":"Seems most fitting than ever to post this. How wolves change rivers is probably one of the most fascinating videos on YouTube [1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44702532,"author":"thangalin","month":"2025-07","story":"Return of wolves to Yellowstone has led to a surge in aspen trees","stance":"rec","why":"Wolves triggered a cascade transforming Yellowstone's wildlife and rivers.","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] \"Embark on a journey to Yellowstone, where a few wolves did not just roam, but rewrote the rules of an entire ecosystem. Discover how these majestic predators triggered a cascade of life, transforming not only the park's wildlife but its very rivers and landscapes. It's a story of how nature's architects can reshape our world in ways we never imagined.\""},{"id":40085051,"author":"mandmandam","month":"2024-04","story":"Long Reviled as 'Ugly,' Sea Lampreys Get Some Respect (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of unforeseen ecosystem effects of species.","quote":"","text":"> Why would the years of life on earth (or evolution) matter for species alive today being of \"ecological importance\" or not? It matters because we're not that smart . A species deemed \"inessential\" to ecological importance might very well turn out to be critical for unforeseen, even unforeseeable reasons. Not just to the species itself, which you seem to discount despite their ancient pedigree, but even to us through second order effects. The amount of time a species has been around is a strong clue that they're fulfilling a role in the ecosystem. Nature doesn't really do 'superfluous'; everything connects. Not in a hippy dippy way, but in the realest way, and in ways beyond our comprehension. The classic examples when I was growing up of 'nature being wasteful' - the appendix and 'junk DNA' - have now been revised... Because we learned more. No one knew how wolves change rivers [0] until it was studied. In hindsight it's obvious and makes perfect sense, but we didn't deem wolves \"essential\" when we killed them all. 0 - [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["YXehBx0Yc_w",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1]]},"qJZ1Ez28C-A":{"wy":"HN commenters call it eye-opening and deeper than typical layman explanations, linking it in double-slit and mechanics threads.","q":{"x":"this veritasium video is quite eye opening","i":43381112,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43381112,"author":"DanielVZ","month":"2025-03","story":"Did the Particle Go Through the Two Slits, or Did the Wave Function?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening, deeper than typical layman explanations","quote":"this veritasium video is quite eye opening","text":"I really don’t understand the topic much but this veritasium video is quite eye opening and goes into further depth than any layman explanation I’ve ever seen in the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44281972,"author":"Balgair","month":"2025-06","story":"Infinite Grid of Resistors","stance":"rec","why":"Great video with an excellent light-paths demo","quote":"Veritasium had a great video similar to this on the paths that light takes.","text":"Aside: Veritasium had a great video similar to this on the paths that light takes. I'll link to the part where they do the best physics demo I have ever seen : [VIDEO]"},{"id":43386255,"author":"beyondCritics","month":"2025-03","story":"Did the Particle Go Through the Two Slits, or Did the Wave Function?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows quantum waves are real and tangible","quote":"This veritasium video demonstrates, that quantum waves are very real and tangible.","text":"[VIDEO] I had learned about the double slit experiment in school, but in my mind it was something of a theoretical construct. This veritasium video demonstrates, that quantum waves are very real and tangible. This is how physics should be."},{"id":45725853,"author":"aeonik","month":"2025-10","story":"Structure and Interpretation of Classical Mechanics (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"History of action and the origin of the idea","quote":"This Veritassium Video goes back further in time and talks about Action, and the Genesis of this idea.","text":"You know about the Ultraviolet catastrophe? Physics Explained Ultraviolet Catastrophe: [LINK] This Veritassium Video goes back further in time and talks about Action, and the Genesis of this idea. [VIDEO] Chemistorian, The history of Atomic Theory. [LINK]"},{"id":44774148,"author":"jimbosis","month":"2025-08","story":"Double-slit experiment holds up when stripped to its quantum essentials","stance":"reference","why":"Experiment showing light taking all paths","quote":"There's another Veritasium video that has a neat experiment ostensibly showing light","text":"There's another Veritasium video that has a neat experiment ostensibly showing light (both lamp and laser sources) \"taking all paths\" (or words to that effect--I don't really know what I'm seeing or what I'm talking about!) It starts around 25 minutes in. [VIDEO] EDIT: Whoops. The YouTube video linked by naasking in this comment: [LINK] touches on the Veritasum video I linked to and goes to some length to explain that it is NOT proving the light is taking \"all possible paths.\" He also brings up and links to a video on the \"Looking Glass Universe\" channel in which the hostess recreates the Vertiasium experiment and gives a differing interpretation. (Some commenters there have objections to the experimental setup. Oh boy, I may be down a rabbit hole here.)"}],"r":[["2JM2oImb9Qg",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["FkKPsLxgpuY",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1]]},"5eBT6OSr1TI":{"wy":"Used as a running joke or satirical shorthand whenever the Daily Mail is cited or criticized.","q":{"x":"there is a good (and humorous) summary of the paper made into a song using only their headlines","i":3096600,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":26891921,"author":"jason0597","month":"2021-04","story":"Daily Mail owner sues Google over search results","stance":"rec","why":"Satirical song still accurate about Daily Mail","quote":"What an amazing video.","text":">Relevant: [VIDEO] Ahhh this takes me back. What an amazing video. Made 11 years ago and it's just as accurate as it was back then. Honestly? Fuck the Daily Mail. Also The S*n, Daily Express, etc."},{"id":3096600,"author":"simonsarris","month":"2011-10","story":"Google+ traffic grew by 480% in one month so press reports a 60% decline.","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous song summarizing the Daily Mail through its headlines","quote":"there is a good (and humorous) summary of the paper made into a song using only their headlines","text":"I hate to post trite things on HN but if you haven't heard of the Daily Mail there is a good (and humorous) summary of the paper made into a song using only their headlines: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27917279,"author":"mavhc","month":"2021-07","story":" Facebook took our election ad money then bulk-deleted our pages","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You can pick between multiple headlines: \"Hurrah for the Blackshirts\", \"At last a gay gene has been found so we can abort gay babies\", and then there's [VIDEO]"},{"id":22082917,"author":"thaumasiotes","month":"2020-01","story":"The Boring Company’s Las Vegas tunnel is nearly 50% done","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> undermined by the fact that other channels will report on this stuff, but not the BBC. If you think the willingness of a news organization to publish something depends on how newsworthy it is, I guess all I can do is respond by quoting the Daily Mail Song: Pop stars take drugs Teen boys wear hoods Sports stars have sex Bears shit in woods ( [VIDEO] ) But that's still English news. I guess I can quote something else: > the more lurid, titillating, sensational, or nationalistic, the better. Even the distinguished Xinhua News Service is not immune from this sort of thing. I couldn't help but chuckle when the podcast host Scott Peters worried whether or not the American press corps' obsession with non-stories like Kim Kardashian and Bruce/Catelynn Jenner would give it [Xinhua] a strategic advantage in the contest to control global media narratives. My guess is Mr. Peters doesn't read Xinhua too often. If I had few dollars [for] every time Xinhua placed a photo collage like \"Beautiful Female Soldiers From All Over the World,\" \"Singer Valen Hsu Poses For Fashion Shots,\" \"Sleeping Babies With Their Cutest Pets,\" on their front page... ( [LINK] ) Newsworthiness is something that can force news organizations to begrudgingly give up space they would rather have allocated to -- going back to the song -- their photo feature on schoolgirl skirt styles. If you're watching the news because you're hoping to learn something, you're making a blatant category error; that's not what the news i"},{"id":13988428,"author":"lkrubner","month":"2017-03","story":"UK triggers the official Brexit process in a letter to EU","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But, but, but, the Daily Mail says Brexit is good! It's absolutely true if it's written in the Daily Mail! I'm sure you've heard this song already, but it is worth a listen again to remember how Britain got here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1495691,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2010-07","story":"* cures (or causes) cancer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those of you not familiar with the type of articles printed by the Daily Mail, here's Dan and Dan's Daily Mail Song: [VIDEO] (Scaremongering cancer headlines near the end here - [VIDEO] )"},{"id":27324536,"author":"ReflectedImage","month":"2021-05","story":"'Unique fingerprints' in Covid-19 could only have arisen from a laboratory","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"In the UK it is widely known to be utter trash. Here's a song about it written in 2010: [VIDEO] Song's punchline: \"It's absolutely true because I read about it in the daily mail /s\""},{"id":26891679,"author":"arp242","month":"2021-04","story":"Daily Mail owner sues Google over search results","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Like what? I don't see anything that's not covered by other outlets. I do see stuff like \"Can you THINK yourself thin? A hypnotist to the stars says you can - and reveals the five-step technique to try if you want to shed the 'lockdown stone'\"[1] Taken from the front page right now. The article is even worse than the title suggests. Relevant: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":18877116,"author":"CM30","month":"2019-01","story":"People older than 65 share the most fake news, a new study finds","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fox isn't the first, nor is it the only one, especially outside of the US. The UK tabloids have operated a lot like that for pretty much their entire existence, peddling misinformation and moral panics like they're going out of fashion. Hell, the Daily Mail even had a song written about it cause of that. [VIDEO] It's also pretty prevalent on the radio, and has a fair few historical precedents from before journalism saw credibility as an important thing to keep in mind (yellow journalism et all)."},{"id":6824192,"author":"rjknight","month":"2013-11","story":"To my friends in the Node community","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm surprised by the disproportionate level of angst from the people who are upset about the use of 'they' in place of 'he'. I can see the point of eliminating both and using 'it' instead, but some of the comments on that Github issue sounded like the kind of 'political correctness gone mad!' drivel that one[1] reads in the Daily Mail[2], for goodness' sake. [1] A gender-neutral pronoun! [2] If you're fortunate enough to not know what the Daily Mail is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4954160,"author":"summerdown2","month":"2012-12","story":"The 2nd Amendment should protect the Internet, not your AK47","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Unfortunately the Daily Mail isnt really a news source. Try: [LINK] > In 2010/11, firearms were involved in 11,227 recorded offences in England and Wales, the seventh consecutive annual fall and a decrease of 13 per cent compared with the previous year (when 12,976 offences were recorded). Also: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v3pYRn5j7oI",1],["DkGMY63FF3Q",1]]},"9vRtA7STvH4":{"wy":"HN cites it in energy debates as an illustration of the long-term thermal limits of exponential energy consumption growth.","q":{"x":"You might enjoy Sabine Hossenfelder's video exploring this","i":40567535,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40567535,"author":"alxmng","month":"2024-06","story":"Fusion tech finds geothermal energy application","stance":"rec","why":"Explores waste heat problem and possible planetary chimney solution.","quote":"You might enjoy Sabine Hossenfelder's video exploring this","text":"You might enjoy Sabine Hossenfelder's video exploring this \"I recently learned that waste heat will boil the oceans in about 400 years\": [VIDEO] It turns out we can probably solve this by building planetary chimneys 5km tall that move heat to the outer atmosphere."},{"id":38851451,"author":"nostromo","month":"2024-01","story":"What it will take to unleash the potential of geothermal power? (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Commentary on extrapolated energy growth causing ocean-boiling waste heat.","quote":"commentary from Sabrine Hossenfelder, highly recommended","text":"It’s based on extrapolation of current trends, so I doubt it’ll actually happen. Besides, we’d all die before it got to that. But here are some sources that describe it: [VIDEO] (commentary from Sabrine Hossenfelder, highly recommended) [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":39562493,"author":"audunw","month":"2024-03","story":"The lithium-ion battery may not be the best bet for EVs","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates waste heat limits if energy consumption keeps growing.","quote":"If we continue increasing energy consumption at current rates and cover it with nuclear power the ocean will boil in 400 years","text":"No matter which energy source you use, increasing energy use will eventually cause problems. With nuclear power, eventually you'll add so much waste heat added to the atmosphere that it will be worse than greenhouse gases: If we continue increasing energy consumption at current rates and cover it with nuclear power the ocean will boil in 400 years: [VIDEO] Obviously that's not meant to be a serious scenario. But it's illustrative. I don't see the problem of lifting everyone up while using less energy. We just gotta be smart about the solutions we make, rather than just brute-forcing it by using lots of energy. Like: I prefer to bike to work. That makes my life better. I also prefer living in a walkable/bikeable town. If I'm forced to drive a car the energy associated getting to work will be very high. If I can use an e-bike it's a tiny fraction, and I get to exercise without wasting time in a gym."},{"id":37383601,"author":"wcoenen","month":"2023-09","story":"Tidal Energy Is Not Renewable","stance":"reference","why":"Sabine Hossenfelder on boiling oceans from energy growth in 400 years.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder recently made a video where she talked about a similar timescale, i.e. boiling oceans in 400 years","text":"I'm not sure the author realized this, but they're actually making a statement about how crazy exponential growth is. Not about the sustainability of tidal power. This becomes obvious once you look closer at what that 2% growth rate (as assumed in the post) implies. Our global energy consumption in 2008 was estimated to be 474 exajoules. The total energy received by the earth from the sun during a year is about 5 million exajoules, a fraction of which reaches the surface. 5 million is much more than 474. But at a seemingly modest 2% per year growth rate (as it was between 1980 and 2006), our energy consumption will match those 5 million exajoules in less than 500 years! Think about that: if energy consumption growth continues at the current pace, then in 500 years we'll either be using ALL solar energy received by the earth (leaving none for the biosphere), or we'll have figured out some magic technology to produce 5 million exajoules of energy per year. Assuming the magic technology, where are we going to get rid of all that extra heat? It would effectively be like having a second sun on earth, cooking us in place. edit: I copied the numbers above from a post I wrote in 2010, so it may be a bit out of date. But Sabine Hossenfelder recently made a video where she talked about a similar timescale, i.e. boiling oceans in 400 years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36240227,"author":"audunw","month":"2023-06","story":"the US solar market is projected to triple in size by 2028","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how waste heat from energy use could heat the planet.","quote":"Sabine Hossenfelder has a video covering the topic","text":"I think we should start talking about the possible climate impact of waste heat from nuclear thermal power plants too. I'm definitely pro-nuclear, wherever it makes sense, in the short term. As long as it's never at the expensive of building renewables too. But many nuclear proponents talk as if nuclear is the end-goal for power generation. That we should just replace everything with nuclear. But if we do, it's going to get harder to bring the Earths climate back to equilibrium, since a world on 100% nuclear power would add a lot of heat to planet. Not as bad as greenhouse gases, but significant enough to make reversal harder. Sabine Hossenfelder has a video covering the topic: [VIDEO] I think focusing on solar and energy storage will always be a good bet. It will probably be the absolute dominant way to get energy in 100 years, along with geothermal in colder climates. We're just one generational improvement from that being inevitable, and those improvements are arguably already out of the lab and in trial production stage. Given how important solar+storage will be, the amount of R&D flowing into it will be staggering in the coming decades. When we've completed the transition to sustainable solutions (which right now is requiring a lot of mining and energy intensive metal processing, which will mostly be recycled in the future) and the population growth flattens and then reverses, I'd hope that the amount of energy needed for industry will fall dramatically. Edit: I'm also h"}]},"CcZdwX4noCE":{"wy":"The go-to explainer whenever content filters wrongly flag innocent words, well made and short.","q":{"x":"He talks about how it's impossible to implement with all edge-cases, easy to circumvent","i":23132266,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23132266,"author":"thepete2","month":"2020-05","story":"Scunthorpe Sans – a self-censoring font","stance":"rec","why":"Why profanity filtering has edge cases and is easy to circumvent","quote":"He talks about how it's impossible to implement with all edge-cases, easy to circumvent","text":"There is a video [0] about this censoring-\"problem\" by Youtuber Tom Scott. He talks about how it's impossible to implement with all edge-cases, easy to circumvent and thus usually not a good idea. Fittingly, the video is filmed in Peniston. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14043262,"author":"colemannugent","month":"2017-04","story":"Scunthorpe Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on why web filters fail","quote":"It's well done, like the rest of his content.","text":"Tom Scott did a video on this: Why Web Filters Don't Work: Penistone and the Scunthorpe Problem - [VIDEO] It's well done, like the rest of his content."},{"id":49319070,"author":"varun_ch","month":"2026-08","story":"Guiding Ships with Moire Patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott video that feels eerie in the LLM era","quote":"kinda eerie to watch now with today","text":"With Tom Scott in particular, there’s a number of his videos that are kinda eerie to watch now with today’s LLM context eg. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26459679,"author":"Crosseye_Jack","month":"2021-03","story":"Tweeting “Memphis” autolocks your Twitter account","stance":"reference","why":"The Scunthorpe problem applied to Twitter moderation","quote":"Penistone and Scunthorpe and now Memphis.","text":"Penistone and Scunthorpe and now Memphis. [VIDEO] As much as I dislike Twitter (I only really use it for work reasons) they are in a no win situation. They either moderate what people post with a flawed system that will never be 100% or be hounded by the \"Why won't anyone think of the children\" group."}]},"DZTIbHIsIYw":{"wy":"HN brings it up in dating app threads as a real-world illustration of how hard Tinder is for men.","q":{"x":"I loved the Youtube video where a woman tries to impersonate a man and get a date with women on a dating app.","i":24535008,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":34534727,"author":"Wojtkie","month":"2023-01","story":"Sexual loneliness: A neglected public health problem?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Might be an interesting one to show to your wife.","text":"I found this video a while back that highlights what you're talking about.[0] Might be an interesting one to show to your wife. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24535008,"author":"Fjolsvith","month":"2020-09","story":"Dating Apps Destroyed Dating","stance":"rec","why":"Woman experiences how hard dating apps are for men.","quote":"I loved the Youtube video where a woman tries to impersonate a man and get a date with women on a dating app.","text":"I loved the Youtube video where a woman tries to impersonate a man and get a date with women on a dating app. She cannot believe how difficult it is for men: [VIDEO] At about 11 minutes in, the woman says, \"Guys don't do this on dating apps. Just don't stop replying. They don't do that. Like, they don't 'ghost'. And, its weird that women do that so often.\""},{"id":32723255,"author":"pizza234","month":"2022-09","story":"The toll of dating app burnout","stance":"reference","why":"Real-world Tinder experiment contradicting message-count research.","quote":"Any research/analysis on the topic pales compared to reality.","text":"> Thus, if you're an average-looking man, your odds of getting messages are actually greater than they are for an average-looking woman. Any research/analysis on the topic pales compared to reality. Here's what happens in real world (\"Tinder Experiment: 18 Yr Old Woman Tries Being A Man!!!\"): [VIDEO] . It's a larger view compared to the number of messages, but the principle still applies."},{"id":25201526,"author":"carabiner","month":"2020-11","story":"A 4-month online dating experiment using 10 fictional singletons (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"2020 experiment of a woman using a man's Tinder profile.","quote":"A 2020 version: 18 Yr Old Woman Tries Being A Man on Tinder","text":"[2012] A 2020 version: 18 Yr Old Woman Tries Being A Man on Tinder [VIDEO] She tries to get as many matches and dates as possible using the male host's pictures. Spoiler: after a week of paltry messages and matches, she says she's \"struggling\" and \"feeling down, even though it's not my damn profile. I thought this would be easy. I don't want to do this again. I hate this.\""}],"r":[["f5SE47Xjx2Q",1]]},"R9c-_neaxeU":{"wy":"HN cites it as a fun, tangible way to teach machine learning, including to kids.","q":{"x":"an amazing way to teach kids about this.","i":34530572,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":46549382,"author":"Tachyooon","month":"2026-01","story":"Learning to Play Tic-Tac-Toe with Jax","stance":"rec","why":"Kids run a matchbox AI player for tic-tac-toe.","quote":"An \"AI player\" made of matchboxes, run by schoolkids, is a fantastically fun idea.","text":"The first time I heard of it was in this Matt Parker video where he helped do this with a school-sized group of kids [0]. An \"AI player\" made of matchboxes, run by schoolkids, is a fantastically fun idea. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34517910,"author":"jimktrains2","month":"2023-01","story":"A Matchbox Game-Learning Machine (1991) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Matchbox machine that learns to play tic-tac-toe.","quote":"Stand up maths did an episode on a similar","text":"Stand up maths did an episode on a similar \"machine\" called MENACE (Machine Educable Noughts And Crosses Engine) that learns to play tick tac toe. [VIDEO] The video description has additional links to resources: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":34530572,"author":"Tachyooon","month":"2023-01","story":"A Matchbox Game-Learning Machine (1991) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Physical matchbox learning machine that teaches kids about AI.","quote":"an amazing way to teach kids about this.","text":"Seems like this is what Matt Parker built together with some other people a few years ago: [VIDEO] . It's incredibly inefficient compared to running it on a computer, but an amazing way to teach kids about this."},{"id":44670381,"author":"jerf","month":"2025-07","story":"Solving a Childhood Mystery: How BASIC Games Learned to Win","stance":"reference","why":"Physical MENACE built for a museum display.","quote":"Matt Parker did a video about a physical implementation of MENACE","text":"Matt Parker did a video about a physical implementation of MENACE as part of a museum display for a day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17322682,"author":"notEvenOnce","month":"2018-06","story":"On Intelligence in Cells: The Case for Whole Cell Biology (2009) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"MENACE as an analogy for adaptive cells.","quote":"Consider MENACE (Machine Educable Noughts And Crosses Engine).","text":"The concept fits. Consider MENACE (Machine Educable Noughts And Crosses Engine). [VIDEO] If cells operate in a similar fashion, where adaptation to externalities encodes itself into a durable surface of arbitrary pockets, then we have a recipe for inert systems exhibiting variable degrees of behavior, which is capable of evolving over time. This doesn't solve for sentience, but it does solve for expressive adaptation to fit into complex, hostile environments."}]},"Sf036fO-ZUk":{"wy":"Pointed to as an impressive technical N64 homebrew demo showing unexpectedly high-res textures.","q":{"x":"James is like Kaze","i":39747189,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":47554666,"author":"cubefox","month":"2026-03","story":"I Built an Open-World Engine for the N64 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Texture streaming on N64 with surprisingly high-res textures.","quote":"The textures look uncharacteristically high res","text":"The same guy, James Lambert, also implemented texture streaming (which would not be invented until two console generations later) in an N64 demo. The textures look uncharacteristically high res: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39747189,"author":"echelon","month":"2024-03","story":"VR Powered by N64 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Incredible N64 demos and technical videos by James Lambert.","quote":"James is like Kaze [3] and builds incredible N64 demos [4] and technical videos about them.","text":"This is what James Lambert does as a follow up when Valve shuts down [1] his \"Portal in N64\" project [2]. James is like Kaze [3] and builds incredible N64 demos [4] and technical videos about them. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38442679,"author":"kmeisthax","month":"2023-11","story":"RPG Engine for the Nintendo 64","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Prerendered cutscenes ate up the lion's share of the disc. If you sacrificed on those and used lots of compression, you absolutely could build an RPG on the N64 that would stand up to whatever you could do on PlayStation. However, nobody wanted to do that. The only RPGs we got on the N64 were Quest 64 and Paper Mario. The reason why requires diving into the development history of what would have been a third RPG for the system: MOTHER 3. To put it bluntly, they ran out of storage space, and pivoted to the N64 DD to get more. The custom floppy disks the N64 DD took could hold 64MB[0]. Even then, this wasn't enough, so the game was then broken up into two parts: an N64 cartridge plus a DD expansion pack, which would give them 96MB in total to play with. MOTHER 3 would then continue in development hell before being canned and reborn as a Game Boy Advance title[1]. Fire Emblem was also supposed to release on the N64. However, unlike Itoi biting off more than he could chew with the N64, FE64 failed because the head designer, Shouzou Kaga, didn't even want to touch the hardware. He'd stalled for time by working on Satellaview FE games[2] on the SNES, and then left Nintendo entirely right before Thracia 776 came out. He'd then release a totally-not-Fire-Emblem clone on PlayStation and get sued into oblivion for... basically telling all the games press that he was making Fire Emblem on PlayStation. So that's a 50% success rate for RPGs on the N64 - not a good look. Could homebrewers "},{"id":37550421,"author":"JohnBooty","month":"2023-09","story":"Demo of megatextures running on n64 hardware","stance":"reference","why":"Videos of the megatextures project in action.","quote":"Appear to be videos of this project in action","text":"Appear to be videos of this project in action: \"How I implemented MegaTextures on real Nintendo 64 hardware\" - [VIDEO] \"Megatextures Tech Demo - N64brew Summer Game Jam 2023 Submission\" - [LINK]"}],"r":[["NQFqDKRAROI",2],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["kya_LXa_y1E",1],["GVT3WUa-48Y",1]]},"TMbl_ofF3AM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fascinating history of Soviet staged-combustion engines and why they stayed in use in American rockets.","q":{"x":"There's a cool documentary called \"The Engines That Came In From The Cold\" (available on youtube[1])","i":27566302,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27566302,"author":"cartoonworld","month":"2021-06","story":"U.S. senators propose 25% tax credit for semiconductor manufacturing","stance":"rec","why":"Cool documentary about Soviet engines that remain unsurpassed.","quote":"There's a cool documentary called \"The Engines That Came In From The Cold\" (available on youtube[1])","text":"Its interesting to me that while in the US, we were spearheading the computer revolution with our cutting edge semiconductor industry, Soviet rocket designs remain pretty much unsurpassed to this day.[0] There's a cool documentary called \"The Engines That Came In From The Cold\" (available on youtube[1]) and if you have a Netflix subscription, I recommend the documentary \"Cosmodrome\" (2008)[2] which is about the history of the Soviet space program and these engines. I'm sure you're aware that the US Space program has been reliant on ex-Soviet heavy lift vehicles for decades more or less. Especially, since the Space Shuttle program was canned, if it weren't for SpaceX, the Russian lift would be the only bus on the route, aside from NRO launches and other um..stuff Indeed, the Soviets had a completely parallel semiconductor and electronics industry as well. I don't know for certain but it was undoubtedly lagging western developments by a large margin. Nonetheless, the Soviets had some very interesting developments of their own, such as the Kola superdeep borehole. It is all terribly fascinating. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8524298,"author":"ianstallings","month":"2014-10","story":"Orbital Sciences Antares rocket explodes after liftoff","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the NK-33 engines and how they reached the US.","quote":"I watched it on netflix myself. Fascinating stuff.","text":"These engines were talked about in the documentary *The Engines That Came In From The Cold\" [VIDEO] I watched it on netflix myself. Fascinating stuff."},{"id":24064438,"author":"jcun4128","month":"2020-08","story":"SpaceX test flight of Starship SN-5 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary about finding hidden Russian rocket engines.","quote":"","text":"yeah that's possible those are the ones, I just remember seeing some documentary about finding these Russian rocket engines that were hidden away to protect them from being destroyed I think edit: oh it's NK33/RD180 [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8529791,"author":"hindsightbias","month":"2014-10","story":"Antares rocket explosion: The question of using decades-old Soviet engines","stance":"reference","why":"Video on Russian and Ukrainian rocket engines.","quote":"Video on Russian/Ukranian engines","text":"The NK-33 may be old, but it offers equal or superior performance to anything made in the US. Another engine, the RD-180 is used in the Atlas. US industries have the rights to manufacture, but want $800M and 4 years to build it or create a new engine. That tells you the state of US tech. [LINK] Video on Russian/Ukranian engines: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["oYp5XuGYqqY",1],["PGv5BqNL164",1]]},"e3GFG0sXIig":{"wy":"HN commenters link it in voting-reform discussions as a concise explanation of range voting versus other systems.","q":{"x":"The following video covers 'Range Voting' in relation to Arrow's Theorem","i":13705719,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18507505,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2018-11","story":"The Second Half of Watergate Was Bigger, Worse, and Forgotten by the Public","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant to changing how we vote.","quote":"Relevant videos (IMO)","text":"Elections are supposed to be bloodless revolutions. If you can't effect change through the electoral system, then the non violent solution is to revolutionize how we vote. Relevant videos (IMO) First Past The Post Voting - [LINK] Range Voting - [VIDEO] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [LINK] Bonus video This video will make you angry - [LINK]"},{"id":13697545,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-02","story":"Robots Aren’t Killing the American Dream","stance":"rec","why":"Voting system videos.","quote":"I'll post some videos from people smarter then me","text":"Elections were (IMO) meant to be bloodless revolutions. its just the 1% has captured both primary political parties. Also, the flawed first past the post electoral system discourages competition (IMO to keep it cheaper for the 1% to own the whole process). I'll post some videos from people smarter then me: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":13705719,"author":"redahs","month":"2017-02","story":"Kenneth Arrow Has Died","stance":"rec","why":"Covers range voting in relation to Arrow's Theorem.","quote":"The following video covers 'Range Voting' in relation to Arrow's Theorem","text":"You might like 'undefined behavior'. The following video covers 'Range Voting' in relation to Arrow's Theorem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19147100,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2019-02","story":"A tiny Swiss company thinks it can help stop climate change","stance":"reference","why":"Alternatives to current voting.","quote":"","text":"Reform the electoral system to achieve the representation we all deserve. The electoral system we use now: [LINK] Alternatives: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] We can start introducing these to everyone at the state and local level to build momentum for electoral reform at the federal level. A good old fashioned general strike until we get what we need from our \"representatives\"."},{"id":19212613,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2019-02","story":"U.S. Supreme Court Puts Limits on Police Power to Seize Private Property","stance":"reference","why":"Explains range voting alongside other systems.","quote":"","text":"How important is this privilege if I can maliciously control how you vote? Now imagine the good we can do with real electoral reform (Democrats idea of repealing citizens United is a meager start, but weak lip service to real reform) What we have now - First Past The Post Voting - [LINK] Range Voting - [VIDEO] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [LINK] Electoral reform is just step 1, something we can all come together for. Something no one could possibly be against. How about if I control who can be voted for? Of course it matters - [LINK] bonus video: This video will make you angry - [LINK]"},{"id":18240473,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2018-10","story":"China May Have $5.8T in Hidden Debt with ‘Titanic’ Risks","stance":"reference","why":"Explains range voting alongside other systems.","quote":"","text":"The real problem is both main stream political parties don't represent around 45% of the country. First Past The Post Voting- [LINK] Range Voting- [VIDEO] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [LINK]"},{"id":18058156,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2018-09","story":"Decline of Global Extreme Poverty Continues but Has Slowed","stance":"reference","why":"Explains range voting alongside other systems.","quote":"","text":"Some tweaks I'd make: Corporate structure: [LINK] Electoral system: What we have now - [LINK] Range voting - [VIDEO] Alternative vote - [LINK] Single transferrable vote - [LINK] Marx wrote about the theory of societal evolution; That there are certain phases civilization must pass through. The communism he wrote about would have to be very far down the road, 100+ years (IMO). This does not mean people sit back and do nothing, we still would have to work towards it. (Most) Capitalists profit from the status quo, and work to keep in place systems to maintain their spot in the hierarchy. Thus the antiquated electoral system in the USA, and the top heavy power/pay structures of business. TL;DR: give the average person more of a say/choice in the world they live in (politics/workplace), and they will find their way forward. (IMO)"},{"id":15678286,"author":"fny","month":"2017-11","story":"Arrow's Theorem (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Range voting satisfies Arrow's criteria.","quote":"Range voting (i.e. 0-5 rating system) satisfies all the criteria.","text":"Arrows theorem is misleading because it only applies to rank order systems. Range voting (i.e. 0-5 rating system) satisfies all the criteria. [0] So yes, democracy is theoretically possible. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15759186,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-11","story":"Join the Battle for Net Neutrality","stance":"reference","why":"Explains range voting alongside other systems.","quote":"","text":"\"bad electoral system.\" First Past The Post Voting - [LINK] Range Voting - [VIDEO] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK]"},{"id":15778043,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-11","story":"More Than a Million Pro-Repeal Net Neutrality Comments Were Likely Faked","stance":"reference","why":"Explains range voting alongside other systems.","quote":"","text":"First Past the Post voting: [LINK] Range Voting: [VIDEO] Single Transferable vote: [LINK] Do away with voting booths, attach voting to everyone's tax forms. Give everyone a $5 tax credit for voting. People who don't do taxes can go to a public library and just pull the voting portion off of the tax forms there and mail it in. Break up corporations in every sector of the economy and either resell the chunks back to the market or turn them all into 100% worker owned and operated co-ops."},{"id":14439046,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-05","story":"The nearly 5M US Census Blocks with zero population (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Perhaps because powerful people don't want to find a solution; The powerful is exactly who is represented in our flawed electoral system: http//i.imgur.com/Uc6kX8G.jpg [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14020114,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-04","story":"Men Without Work","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"For example my desire as a buyer would be to purchase an awesome steak for about a dollar but the failings of our society have again let me down.\" \"Lobby\" congress to right these wrongs perhaps? [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["l8XOZJkozfI",2],["QT0I-sdoSXU",2]]},"eDNX65d-FBY":{"wy":"HN people link it as an old-timey explainer for nuclear thermal propulsion, and as evidence these engines were already built and tested.","q":{"x":"This video is worth checking out if you haven’t seen it yet.","i":17427770,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":24891380,"author":"cwojno","month":"2020-10","story":"New nuclear engine concept could help realize 3-month trips to Mars","stance":"rec","why":"Old-timey explainer on nuclear rocket operation.","quote":"Old timey video explaining it","text":"This article didn't explain it at all, so you're right to ask. tl;dr: Gaseous propellant (I'm guessing hydrogen) is heated with fission then pointed in the opposite direction of intended travel. The uranium in this design is not a propellant, but a heat source. Aside: you can use photons/heat as a propellant, but it's thrust is very low [LINK] . Ideal propellants typically have a high exit velocity and low mass. That gives you the longest amount of \"burn\" time, and the greatest amount of control for the weight. [LINK] Back in the day when the US was building more of these nuclear rockets, the propellant of choice was typically hydrogen [LINK] . Old timey video explaining it [VIDEO] . I'm assuming this proposed design would also use hydrogen, but I couldn't find any sources on the propellant for their design. Liquid hydrogen served to keep the reactor cool as it transitioned from liquid to gas as that phase change absorbs energy. The gas is the directed through the reactor core where the gas heats up. As gases heat up, they absorb energy, their average particle velocities increase. Eventually, the hydrogen molecules (mostly H2 or H-H gaseous hydrogen), makes it to the nozzle and is ejected. The high-velocity hydrogen is what actually provides the bulk of the thrust to the spacecraft. Compare this to Project Orion ( [LINK] ) which intended to detonate nuclear warheads and the craft essentially rode the shock wave into the stars. I would classify this method of propulsion, not s"},{"id":17427770,"author":"wizardforhire","month":"2018-06","story":"The Tyranny of the Rocket Equation (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of nuclear rocket propulsion and limits.","quote":"This video is worth checking out if you haven’t seen it yet.","text":"aerospikes There’s a lot of reason why they haven’t been used yet, but I think most the compelling argument against them is that they won’t be used until fuel costs become the competitive factor in launch prices. [LINK] [LINK] Nuclear rockets are quite the concept but have their obvious limitations. This video is worth checking out if you haven’t seen it yet. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23601355,"author":"stqism","month":"2020-06","story":"The US military is getting serious about nuclear thermal propulsion","stance":"reference","why":"Old video on weight reduction without chemical fuel.","quote":"according to this old video we’d see a reduction of weight due to the absence of heavy chemical fuel","text":"I don’t know if any real rockets were built, but according to this old video we’d see a reduction of weight due to the absence of heavy chemical fuel. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21093899,"author":"inamberclad","month":"2019-09","story":"NASA Wants to Send Nuclear Rockets to the Moon and Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows nuclear rocket engines have already been run.","quote":"These types of engines have already been run","text":"It's much more likely to look like NERVA than Orion. [LINK] These types of engines have already been run: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["pxBQLFLei70",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["OL6-x0modwY",1]]},"hZ1OgQL9_Cw":{"wy":"HN points to it as an impressive example of ML-based video restoration, and as the test case in the debate over whether upscaling historical footage is respectful or misleading.","q":{"x":"Having it in color with sound made the video seem much more real and relatable.","i":24656098,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24656098,"author":"ijidak","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTubers are upscaling the past to 4K, but historians want them to stop","stance":"rec","why":"Color and smoother frames make the era feel real and relatable.","quote":"Having it in color with sound made the video seem much more real and relatable.","text":"As an African-American it was actually useful to watch some of these colorized videos and get a better since for how African-Americans integrated into the society at that time. Having it in color with sound made the video seem much more real and relatable. For me this just ads another facet to develop a better-rounded grasp of the era. Inserting the extra frames seems to slow the footage down and allow more time for digestion. I find it often difficult to track what's going on in old videos because of the off-speed of the recordings. But for the historians who spend a lot of time with these artifacts, I'm sure this feels like a bastardization of history. But as a lay person, it makes me want to devour more of these videos to get a fascinating and easier-to-digest look at life back in those times. Btw, this is the video I watched: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24098301,"author":"rbinv","month":"2020-08","story":"The German front in rare color photos, 1914-1918","stance":"rec","why":"ML-upscaled 4K 60fps footage of 1911 New York.","quote":"there have been some impressive ML-powered video remasterings recently","text":"Somewhat related, there have been some impressive ML-powered video remasterings recently (colorized and upscaled to 4K at 60 fps): - Footage Of The First Flying Machines 1890-1910: [LINK] - A Trip Through New York City in 1911: [VIDEO] - Views of Tokyo, Japan, 1913-1915: [LINK]"},{"id":41818350,"author":"7373737373","month":"2024-10","story":"How do you choose where to be?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is something I often wonder when I see historical video footage - why do the people there were where they were? [VIDEO] In retrospect they may have missed out on many things, if they were in another place at that time, life might have turned out much differently for them, for the better or worse. But life is oftentimes happenstance or habit, instead of willed intentional (inter)action. Habitual or even changes of perspective can be rare, and choosing to explore without any direction can be very difficult."},{"id":33263769,"author":"waltbosz","month":"2022-10","story":"Show HN: I made a new AI colorizer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Very cool. Your results look much better than the washed out AI colorization that I've seen in the past. I think you could charge money for this service. A suggestion for something fun to do / marketing tool: recolorize this video frame-by-frame [VIDEO] Feature request: colorize B&W comic books. I really want to create a full color book of Calvin & Hobbes comics. (Not for publication)"},{"id":24655073,"author":"jchw","month":"2020-10","story":"YouTubers are upscaling the past to 4K, but historians want them to stop","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example comparing upscaled footage to original.","quote":"Please consider doing this before commenting, because in my opinion it really illuminates how silly this all is.","text":"I just tried watching one of the upscaled and interpolated videos, and then comparing it to the source. Please consider doing this before commenting, because in my opinion it really illuminates how silly this all is. 4K: [VIDEO] Original: [LINK] The colorization, of course, is lies. The upscaling and interpolation, however, is harder to argue about. Sure, if you were to freeze a frame and zoom in, you are looking at data that may not directly correspond to what was recorded. But it didn’t come out of thin air either - its upscaled and interpolated. I have read the arguments and some of the comments on this page and I still cannot figure out how this isn’t just rationalizing elitism. Of course we need to keep the source material, but nobody is suggesting to not do that."}]},"lBVvnDfW2Xo":{"wy":"HN people point to it as the full explanation of how gas furnaces work, mostly in threads on heating, power outages and gas appliances.","q":{"x":"For a full on description of how a good portion of furnaces, tech connections made a video not long ago","i":26162363,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31415399,"author":"8note","month":"2022-05","story":"SEC charges Nvidia with inadequate disclosures about impact of cryptomining","stance":"rec","why":"Covers what the asker is looking for about furnaces.","quote":"Tech connections has what you're looking for","text":"Tech connections has what you're looking for: [VIDEO] Efficiency wise, the comparison between a heat pump and a furnace depends on the outside temperature. Heat pumps lose their efficiency for high ∆T, so for winter in a Russia, a furnace is likely the better option"},{"id":26162363,"author":"8note","month":"2021-02","story":"What went wrong with the Texas power grid?","stance":"rec","why":"Full description of how furnaces work.","quote":"For a full on description of how a good portion of furnaces, tech connections made a video not long ago","text":"For a full on description of how a good portion of furnaces, tech connections made a video not long ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26174277,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-02","story":"Analysis: Texas power outage leaves officials working political angles","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of forced-air furnaces needing electricity.","quote":"","text":"Yes, they do need need electricity. Source: I live in Ontario, Canada, and am currently being kept warm by one. Most (gas) furnaces are forced air, and how exactly would the fan run without electricity? How do you think the thermostat runs its thermistor (and probably LCD display) without a power? The Technology Connections YT channel has a recent good overview, \"Forced-air Furnaces: The What, Why, and How\": * [VIDEO] As for efficiency, you can get units that are 99% efficient (AFUE): * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":49232179,"author":"fhdkweig","month":"2026-08","story":"Switching to electric stoves can dramatically cut indoor air pollution","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the flame is contained and never mixes with indoor air.","quote":"Technology Connections did a video (because of course he did) on how gas furnaces work.","text":"Technology Connections did a video (because of course he did) on how gas furnaces work. The short of it is that the flame is contained inside of metal tubes and never mixes with indoor air. No exhaust gases can leak into the breathable air. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34648901,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-02","story":"I Bought a CO2 Monitor, and It Broke Me","stance":"reference","why":"Shows condensing furnaces are sealed off from the house.","quote":"","text":"> It's not supposed to be sealed, it's just supposed to have draft. It depends on the model. High (>90%) efficiency, condensing furnaces are completely sealed off from the house: they take in air from the outside (via PVC pipe), combust, extract the heat, and exhaust the results (via PVC pipes). * [VIDEO] Older lower efficiency (<85-90%) furnaces suck in air from the house, exhaust into metal pipes (PVC would melt because not as much heat is extracted from the fumes), can be back-drafted and you have to worry about CO. Basically: if the furnace has vents in which you can see a pilot light, it's the older style."},{"id":31612635,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2022-06","story":"What a gas stove ban means for restaurants","stance":"reference","why":"Shows modern furnaces use outside air for combustion.","quote":"","text":"Modern gas furnaces use the outside atmosphere for combustion. It helps get them high (for gas) efficiency levels! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1],["NRoUC9P1PmA",1]]},"nBiJB8YuDBQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it in discussions of overcriminalization and prosecutorial overreach, notably after Aaron Swartz's death.","q":{"x":"I ask that you all watch; titled Illegal Everything","i":5053318,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5053318,"author":"hellome","month":"2013-01","story":"Aaron Swartz commits suicide","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on overcriminalization, suggested after Aaron Swartz's death.","quote":"I ask that you all watch; titled Illegal Everything","text":"I want to offer my condolences for your loss, Im very empathetic to Arron's situation. I can only imagine someone that graduated and took the right path in life to be potential labeled as a felon If in fact he what I assumed cared greatly about his reputation I ask that you all watch; titled Illegal Everything [VIDEO] its my goal that maybe this tragedy may help the next Aaron????"},{"id":3763540,"author":"nextparadigms","month":"2012-03","story":"Don't Talk to Cops","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-doc on how easily Americans are criminalized.","quote":"There's also this mini-doc that's related to this","text":"Any chance the US law will ever be fixed so that people can't be easily arrested over anything, really? There's also this mini-doc that's related to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49053457,"author":"attila-lendvai","month":"2026-07","story":"The growing vigilante movement to knock out Flock surveillance cameras","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among sources on excessive laws.","quote":"John Stossel's Illegal Everything","text":"the problem with pitching Flock as \"the triumph of technology over criminality\" is that because of all the bogus edicts, an average american commits three felonies every single day. and whoever picks who gets prosecuted for what, is the owner running the plantation. Three Felonies a Day [LINK] In 2006, the Code of Laws of the United States of America: 200000 pages ( [LINK] ) with 500 pages/week it's about 10 years just to read it, i.e. to have a chance not breaking it (but this wrongly assumes that it's not growing meanwhile). In 2012: Federal Code: 75000 pages, Tax Code: 72000 pages. The Criminalization of Everyday Life [LINK] [LINK] John Stossel's Illegal Everything [VIDEO] John Stossel - Technically, That's Illegal [LINK] “There’s no way to rule innocent men. The only power any government has is the power to crack down on criminals. Well, when there aren’t enough criminals, one makes them. One declares so many things to be a crime that it becomes impossible for me to live without breaking laws. Who wants a nation of law-abiding citizens? What’s there in that for anyone? But just pass the kind of laws that can neither be observed or enforced nor objectively interpreted – and you create a nation of law-breakers – and then you cash in on guilt.” — Ayn Rand (1905–1982), 'Atlas Shrugged' (1957) Everything I Want To Do Is Illegal: War Stories from the Local Food Front by Joel Salatin [LINK]"}],"r":[["Ik1AK56FtVc",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["wq5D43qAsVg",1],["LfascZSTU4o",1],["7Q25-S7jzgs",1]]},"pNlq-EVld70":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as the authoritative source for why the port is faster and why Go was chosen.","q":{"x":"The relationship of their performance gains to functional programming ideas is explained beginning at 8:14","i":43335868,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44888555,"author":"yomismoaqui","month":"2025-08","story":"Show HN: Doom port to pure Go – Gore","stance":"rec","why":"Hejlsberg's reasoning for choosing Go for the TypeScript compiler.","quote":"You can check his arguments here","text":"After hearing the explanation about why he picked Go for the Typescript compiler I can only be more of a fan of Anders Hejlsber. He said that Go was chosen due to its suitability for the workload, offering low-level control, optimized native code, automatic memory management, and concurrency. And this coming from the same guy that created C#, Typescript, Delphi & Turbo Pascal shows how a real engineer takes a decision of this magnitude. Is Go the best decision for every SW project? No, but in this case the arguments (and the tests they did during their evaluation) check out. And no, picking F# simply because you love the language is not a mature decision. You can check his arguments here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43335868,"author":"odyssey7","month":"2025-03","story":"A 10x Faster TypeScript","stance":"rec","why":"Explains performance gains via immutable data and parallelizable problems from 8:14.","quote":"The relationship of their performance gains to functional programming ideas is explained beginning at 8:14","text":"The key: > immutable data structures --> \"we are fully concurrent, because these are what I often call embarrassingly parallelizable problems\" The relationship of their performance gains to functional programming ideas is explained beginning at 8:14 [VIDEO]"},{"id":44131228,"author":"kristianp","month":"2025-05","story":"Show HN: I rewrote my Mac Electron app in Rust","stance":"reference","why":"Hejlsberg breaks down speedup: native code versus multithreading.","quote":"Hejlsberg also says in this video, about 3.3x performance is from going native and the other 2-3x is by using multithreading.","text":"Here's the FAQs, where they explain the decision to go with Go and not, say, rust. [LINK] Hejlsberg also says in this video, about 3.3x performance is from going native and the other 2-3x is by using multithreading. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43333557,"author":"dcre","month":"2025-03","story":"A 10x Faster TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Shows code side by side with many if statements.","quote":"they show some of the code side by side and it is just a ton of if statements","text":"In the embedded video they show some of the code side by side and it is just a ton of if statements. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43333574,"author":"dataviz1000","month":"2025-03","story":"A 10x Faster TypeScript","stance":"reference","why":"Demo compiling VS Code explains where the 10x figure comes from.","quote":"the video linked in the post might answer your question?","text":"Not sure if it does but the video linked in the post might answer your question? I think he is compiling vscode which includes Monaco editor which is where they are getting 10x faster stat. (I might be wrong here.) [0] [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"A-4dIImaodQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as an entertaining, fact-based explainer of gerrymandering tactics, though one commenter criticizes it as inconsistent.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend the John Oliver explanation","i":17454023,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17454023,"author":"tajen","month":"2018-07","story":"Cities don't have to offer huge subsidies to companies like Apple and Amazon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humorous, fact-based explanation of gerrymandering tactics.","quote":"I highly recommend the John Oliver explanation","text":"For those unfamiliarwith gerrymandering, I highly recommend the John Oliver explanation, a humorist of actual facts, showing maps and tactics of politicians that are borderline criminal. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14138881,"author":"cjlarose","month":"2017-04","story":"Gerrymandering Is Illegal, but Only Mathematicians Can Prove It","stance":"reference","why":"Episode featuring Illinois earmuff district.","quote":"This district in particular was featured in [a recent LastWeekTonight episode][2].","text":"[Illinoi's 4th congressional district][1] was called the \"earmuff\" district because of its usual shape. It joined two predominately latino communities with a narrow strip of the I-294. But not all weirdly shaped districts are bad. Between the two latino communities in that area, there is a predominately African-American community. By joining two latino communities together into a single district, it ensured that they had a representative serving their interests. This district in particular was featured in [a recent LastWeekTonight episode][2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24958525,"author":"aaron695","month":"2020-11","story":"Let the Incarcerated Vote","stance":"critical","why":"Accuses the segment of inconsistency on gerrymandering.","quote":"John Oliver did an anti-gerrymandering special, until he got to a seat where they split apart Blacks and Latinos.","text":"John Oliver did an anti-gerrymandering special, until he got to a seat where they split apart Blacks and Latinos. Then that was ok -> [VIDEO]"}]},"BN-34JfUrHY":{"wy":"HN people value its authenticity: no cuts, no cheating, and real tool use done entirely with materials found on site.","q":{"x":"This entire channel is fascinating and strives to be very “authentic” about doing everything without turning off the camera and cheating.","i":34175981,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34175981,"author":"Waterluvian","month":"2022-12","story":"Hafting an Axe: DIY","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Making a stone axe with low tech methods, authentically and without cuts.","quote":"This entire channel is fascinating and strives to be very “authentic” about doing everything without turning off the camera and cheating.","text":"If this fascinates you, be sure to check out this video on making a Celtic axe using low tech methods. [VIDEO] This entire channel is fascinating and strives to be very “authentic” about doing everything without turning off the camera and cheating."},{"id":35692717,"author":"elevation","month":"2023-04","story":"My Search for the Origins of Clothing","stance":"reference","why":"Shows hafting a stone axe head by friction fit instead of twine.","quote":"Jon Plant secures a stone tool head to a shaft by building a stone axe called a \"celt\" with more primitive methods [0].","text":"I haven't read Postrel, but the idea of twine being necessary to secure a stone tool to a wooden shaft seems wrong to me. Jon Plant secures a stone tool head to a shaft by building a stone axe called a \"celt\" with more primitive methods [0]. The friction fit he achieves is likely more robust than a twine lashing because it helps deliver a higher impulse to the work-piece than a mount made of cordage. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21002195,"author":"dooglius","month":"2019-09","story":"Emergent Tool Use from Multi-Agent Interaction","stance":"reference","why":"Example of real tool use with enormous state space.","quote":"something more like [0] where the state space is enormous and it takes a good deal of reasoning and planning","text":"The state space here looks pretty small, it seems to me that with so much training it's just a case of brute-force search. When I think of \"tool use\" in regards to the intelligence of early humans, I imagine something more like [0] where the state space is enormous and it takes a good deal of reasoning and planning to get to a desired result. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"G77ev9pks4I":{"wy":"Recommended as a sharp piece of media criticism by a well-liked music YouTuber, and cited for the concept of reification.","q":{"x":"His video Stock Music & Reality TV - How to Misrepresent the World [1] is also really good.","i":27173083,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27173039,"author":"andensande","month":"2021-05","story":"Audacity: Actions we propose to take on PR 835","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tantacrul's video on corporate music, from a favorite music YouTuber","quote":"one of the best music youtubers around.","text":"Not related as such, but worth pointing out. The author of this discussion - and the project manager of Audacity - is Tantacrul, who is, in my opinion, one of the best music youtubers around. His video[0] on corporate music was fantastic, and his video on Shostakovich[1] made me totally reconsider his music. Plus, he's funny! So there's that. Highly recommend! [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":27173083,"author":"jraph","month":"2021-05","story":"Audacity: Actions we propose to take on PR 835","stance":"rec","why":"How stock music misrepresents the world in reality TV","quote":"His video Stock Music & Reality TV - How to Misrepresent the World [1] is also really good.","text":"His reviews of the various scorewriters (Sibelius, Musescore, Dorico) are also fantastic, even if you are not into music. His review of Musescore lead him to become the project manager of Musescore, and probably of Audacity as a consequence. His video Stock Music & Reality TV - How to Misrepresent the World [1] is also really good. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34091868,"author":"lozenge","month":"2022-12","story":"Netflix: 30% bitrate saving using Film Grain Synthesis","stance":"reference","why":"Explains reification of cultural meaning","quote":"Your comment reminds me of the idea of reification explained here","text":"Your comment reminds me of the idea of reification explained here - [VIDEO] Film grain doesn't mean anything, except what the public have been conditioned for it to mean back when it was a natural chemical process."},{"id":31355936,"author":"Chris2048","month":"2022-05","story":"Please don’t let anyone Americanise it (1992)","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates over-optimization of mass-market products","quote":"","text":"The problem is it's a race to the bottom with these kinds of things, comparable to the over-salt-&-sugar-ing of American foods: [VIDEO] \"..until eventually, they’re shipping hamburger buns with exactly three sesame seeds artfully arranged in a triangle, and nobody buys their hamburgers any more.\" -- [LINK]"}],"r":[["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["PaKIZ7gJlRU",1]]},"-BeTq99LqUo":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as an example of the absurdly powerful lasers sold cheaply online, and a nod to the channel's DIY laser builds.","q":{"x":"builds insanely strong lasers with basically salvaged junk electronics.","i":32938909,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32938909,"author":"adamsmith143","month":"2022-09","story":"Neutralisation of cockroaches with laser automated by machine vision","stance":"rec","why":"Builds extremely strong lasers from salvaged electronics","quote":"builds insanely strong lasers with basically salvaged junk electronics.","text":"You've obviously never heard of styropyro who builds insanely strong lasers with basically salvaged junk electronics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22375632,"author":"darken","month":"2020-02","story":"Tip: Don't record laser tattoo removal (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Cheap tattoo removal laser kit demonstrated","quote":"Tattoo removal tends to use ~1MW pulsed lasers (yes, megawatt).","text":"Tattoo removal tends to use ~1MW pulsed lasers (yes, megawatt). The pulse is extremely short (nanoseconds), but at that power/instant-brightness it's enough to fry sensitive imaging electronics. Can also do fun things like blast holes through CDs. I'd learned about the topic the other day here (\"plays\" with a cheap tattoo removal kit off the web): [VIDEO]"},{"id":21946219,"author":"scottlocklin","month":"2020-01","story":"Particle accelerator fits on the head of a pin","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dunno man you can buy some really wack lasers off of ebay","text":"Dunno man you can buy some really wack lasers off of ebay: [VIDEO] They were talking about this kind of particle accelerator in the 90s back when I used to hang out with accelerator physicists. Though back then it was going to be terahertz waves rather than lasers. Still got to power it with something. IMO best mad scientist weapon is something like this: [LINK]"},{"id":22275897,"author":"ben_w","month":"2020-02","story":"Lasersaur – open-source laser cutter","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"whose included safety goggles are more than a little suspicious","text":"More worryingly, it’s possible to buy pre-built laser etchers online that don’t come with safety glass: [LINK] And Q-switched pulse lasers for hair removal (i.e. to be aimed at your face) whose included safety goggles are more than a little suspicious: [VIDEO]"}]},"1wVho3I0NtU":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best commentary of the goal, in Spanish, usually after Maradona's death or in discussions of remarkable goals.","q":{"x":"Best commentary of that goal is in Spanish","i":25211833,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25211833,"author":"lordnacho","month":"2020-11","story":"Diego Maradona has died","stance":"rec","why":"Best commentary of the goal, in Spanish.","quote":"Best commentary of that goal is in Spanish","text":"Best commentary of that goal is in Spanish: [VIDEO] The ease with which he played, and lived, was the stuff of legend. There's something tragic in it as well, since it probably contributed to his early passing."},{"id":34177862,"author":"wslh","month":"2022-12","story":"Pelé, the Global Face of Soccer, Dies at 82","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a rare goal.","quote":"A goal like this will be difficult to see in the future","text":"It is impossible to predict but we can talk about the past: Messi is Argentinian and Maradona too. A goal like this will be difficult to see in the future: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25211852,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2020-11","story":"Diego Maradona has died","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of the second goal against England in 1986.","quote":"In case someone has not seen the second goal against England in 1986","text":"In case someone has not seen the second goal against England in 1986 Original footage: [VIDEO] An interview many years later: [LINK]"},{"id":16028232,"author":"Cyph0n","month":"2017-12","story":"Concussion doctor says he found CTE in living NFL player","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as an impressive sporting feat.","quote":"Maradona's goal of the century","text":"I'm not very familiar with football either, but I can say that OP is heavily exaggerating and probably not familiar with sports history at all. Heck, I'm not huge sports fan, I can come up with half a dozen more impressive feats in sport off the top of my head. * Beamon's long jump record in 1968 Olympics: [LINK] * Maradona's goal of the century: [VIDEO] * Zidane CL final, won Real Madrid its 9th CL title: [LINK] * O'Sullivan, fastest 147[1] in history (snooker = 4D chess in my opinion): [LINK] * Sotomayor's high jump world record in 1988 Olympics (his lowest time and still 0.01 m higher than anyone else since then!): [LINK] * Usain Bolt [1] 147 is the maximum number of points you can get in a game of snooker. The only way to do this is to alternate pocketing a red ball then a black ball until all red balls are off the table; this nets you 120 points. Once this is done, you need to pocket the remaining colored balls, ending with the black ball, for an additional 27 points. All this must be done without conceding a single point to your opponent (i.e., no mistakes) . Start watching from 1:10 for the action."}]},"4AivEQmfPpk":{"wy":"Cited in threads on borders, Taiwan's status and how many countries exist, as a quick explanation of why recognition is political.","q":{"x":"You might find this video clip interesting","i":17602405,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17602405,"author":"dwheeler","month":"2018-07","story":"Why No HTTPS?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the naming game around Taiwan.","quote":"You might find this video clip interesting","text":"Officially, many countries and businesses agree that Taiwan isn't a country, while continuing in practice to do everything that only makes sense if Taiwan is in fact a country. This is an old game that fools no one. I wouldn't fuss about the name, we all know what's really going on, and the name game probably saves millions of lives. You might find this video clip interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24671782,"author":"judge2020","month":"2020-10","story":"Modi government to set up app store alternative to Google Play, Apple App Store","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote source for count of 193 countries.","quote":"There are 193 countries (footnote[0])","text":"There are 193 countries (footnote[0]), so that might mean 193 app stores, but then some countries might delegate the power to require their own app store to their own regions/\"states\". 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23352009,"author":"dwheeler","month":"2020-05","story":"HashiCorp forbids its software being used in China","stance":"reference","why":"Explains reasons for not admitting Taiwan as a country.","quote":"In all practical senses Taiwan is a separate country, but there are reasons not to admit it","text":"It says it only applies to the People's Republic of China, and in the notes above it's only because of the laws of the People's Republic of China. In all practical senses Taiwan is a separate country, but there are reasons not to admit it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10869081,"author":"jack9","month":"2016-01","story":"The Great Republican Land Heist","stance":"reference","why":"Borders depend on whether land can be defended.","quote":"country borders are based on if land can be defended from other claimants","text":"> The government owns all land until it is transferred/sold to a private owner. That's not a new thing: that precedent goes back to Day 1 of the United States, and maybe further I think you are talking about the fluidity of borders and lands worldwide. In reality, country borders are based on if land can be defended from other claimants ( [VIDEO] ). This is the final arbitration, even after exploring existing treaties (which arent always followed, [LINK] ). The idea that the US federal government owns land until it's disseminated is one of those american myths that the US Govt tries REALLY hard to let alone, despite most federal lands being originally acquired by fiat and in the last 200 years by eminent domain (sometimes a front for private parties like the railway barons). Almost any private vs federal case can raise issues that can be elevated to the supreme court where the USG has a lot more to lose, regardless of the outcome. So they make lots of small steps, wisely. When they run into a case, even if they lose, the USG will simply work around the small setback."}],"r":[["LrObZ_HZZUc",1]]},"De0Am_QcZiQ":{"wy":"Cited in discussions of software getting slower and of efforts to reduce friction in programming.","m":[{"id":22862506,"author":"oreally","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How to rediscover the joy of programming?","stance":"rec","why":"Linked as effort to make programming less frustrating.","quote":"You could also try to contribute to people also trying to make programming less friction-ish.","text":"Have you ever considered what's making you not enjoy programming anymore? Journalling your day-to-day might help. Here's one example that I found that really screws with people not enjoying programming. If you have a career, chances are you're going through rules/bullshit(ie. code standards, needlessly large codebases) made up by other programmers within your organization and that's making you not enjoy programming as much - in contrast to when you were younger and only needed to get things working. On the bright side, there are ways to handle it. While I don't recommend going YOLO and willfully breaking all the rules or even quitting without a plan, I do recommend trying to find some other outlet/hobby that you enjoy after work hours. You could also try to contribute to people also trying to make programming less friction-ish. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21836840,"author":"ttctciyf","month":"2019-12","story":"Almost everything on computers is perceptually slower than it was in 1983 (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Blow's talk on software getting slower, with Photoshop startup example.","quote":"","text":"> Suppose during my regular game development everyday life I've installed Photoshop recently and I want a look at a screenshot of the game - someone reported a problem or something. > So I double-click on the thing ... one ... two ... three ... four ... five ... six ... it's about seven seconds before I can actually see the image. [...] > So that's really slow and I'm going to talk about that for a bit, but there's an element of severe irony to this which is that as soon as I double click this thing within one second it draws an image and it's a pretty high resolution interesting image. It's just not the image that I care about. > So obviously it's not hard to start a process and draw an image on the screen in much less than seven seconds. They just don't manage that. > Now. I gave a speech a year ago that started this same way. That was 2016. It's now 2017 and of course a new version of Photoshop has come out and of course what will align directly with my point in the next few slides: they've made it worse. And the great way in which they've made it worse is: say there's some operation that you want to do maybe once in a while like create a new image... So I'm going to go to file, new ... Urghh. And that menu takes - it probably takes about a second to come up. > And you might think \"Oh, well, you know it was just all these assets were cold or something.. Maybe they come off the hard drive. It'll be faster next time.\" And it's like: \"Well, let's test that out. Nope.\" Like ev"},{"id":19276699,"author":"muthdra","month":"2019-03","story":"Windows 10: New study shows Home edition users are baffled by updates","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I'm not sure most programmers even know what makes things slow anymore","text":"Our computers (meaning the whole of software and hardware) are extremely primitive, their culture soaked in insanity and in general they are perfectly hateable. It's not my choice to have a temper tantrum about it because they were already shitty when I got here. But what's on my mind isn't how hateable computers are; it's how loveable something shitty can be as long as it's shinier and bigger than last year. \"I'm not sure most programmers even know what makes things slow anymore\" - [VIDEO]"}]},"NiTsduRreug":{"wy":"Cited as the source for comprehensible input, the principle behind immersion-based language learning methods and apps.","q":{"x":"Their method is based on Stephen Krashen's \"language acquisition through comprehensible input\"[1] and... it just works.","i":24027686,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":18549446,"author":"risos","month":"2018-11","story":"How to Almost Learn Italian","stance":"rec","why":"Krashen's input hypothesis on acquisition versus learning","quote":"Krashen's input hypothesis seems to be an important part of the process.","text":"I think Duolingo is great for forming habits. Ultimately, if you want to learn something new you need to work on that skill over a decent period of time, and this becomes hard to do without a regular habit. Duolingo is also great at exposing yourself to the language gradually, but unless your goal is to simply be able to hold a conversation or say a few phrases, I don't think it will get you anywhere in the long run. As I'm getting more into language learning (specifically Japanese), Krashen's input hypothesis seems to be an important part of the process. [VIDEO] Essentially he makes the distinction between \"learning\" that word A in language X has some meaning Y, and acquiring an unconscious understanding that word A means Y. His main point being that this unconscious acquisition (that is, being able to hear a words/phrase and not having to put conscious effort into to translating it in your head) only comes from comprehensible input, that is, listening to the language and understanding messages (I'm not so convinced that it has to be entirely 100% comprehensible, rather from experience think language can be acquired even from something like 20% comprehensible material). Communities have been rising up around Krashen's theory, one being AJATT (All japanese all the time). alljapaneseallthetime.com The creator of the site advocated listening to native Japanese material 24/7 (or at least as much as you possibly can during the day) as well as making i+1 sentence (linking back to "},{"id":44091575,"author":"jkoff","month":"2025-05","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (May 2025)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains comprehensible input principle behind commenter's app","quote":"It's based on the principle of language acquisition through comprehensible input, as described here","text":"Link: [LINK] I'm building Infinite Pod, a web app that generates language learning podcasts tuned to your individual learning goals and level. It's based on the principle of language acquisition through comprehensible input, as described here: [VIDEO] It's still a bit rough, but feels magical in my own testing so I wanted to make it available to others."},{"id":24027686,"author":"wst_","month":"2020-08","story":"Show HN: Japanese Language Learning Forum","stance":"reference","why":"Krashen's comprehensible input theory underlies the immersion method","quote":"Their method is based on Stephen Krashen's \"language acquisition through comprehensible input\"[1] and... it just works.","text":"I don't want to discourage the author but, to be honest, what I see is nowhere close to what I figure would help me to learn Japanese. Questions I see on home page have nothing to do with learning - some about traveling around the country, some about translating \"good morning\", etc. True - I haven't registered. TBH though I don't see why should I. When it comes to learning any language, but Japanese in particular, I can't thank enough to the guys who created Mass Immersion Approach web page[0]. Their method is based on Stephen Krashen's \"language acquisition through comprehensible input\"[1] and... it just works. I've been learning Japanese in the classroom for around 2 years. I've been living in Japan for 5 years and my Japanese was rather mediocre. I've left Japan for a year and forgot a lot. Grammar is gone now, most sentence patterns are gone, mose kanji (and I've never been big on kanji) are gone. Now, after 4 months of MIA I am better then I've ever been. If you truly want to learn the language - read the page, watch their YT videos[2], just do yourself a favor and stop wasting your time. PS. I am not related. Just a happy learner. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":20391280,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2019-07","story":"How I Taught My Kid to Read","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to rebut learning sounds first as acquisition","quote":"That’s not how language acquisition works.","text":"> We seem to just pick up our native language so easily as we're growing up, but it seems difficult to pick up a new language with the same ease later on. Easily? It takes many thousands of hours to learn our native language in the first few years of life. > I started with learning the sounds of the language first (and how to pronounce the written language) which set up the framework for learning words and phrases later on. That’s not how language acquisition works. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"OhnjMdzGusc":{"wy":"HN brings it up in electric vehicle threads as proof that electric cars date back over a century, with Leno an entertaining guide.","q":{"x":"Jay Leno has an entertaining segment driving around LA in his restored Baker, he can tell you all about it, fascinating vehicle","i":30564604,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30564604,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2022-03","story":"The cold hard truth about electric vehicles in winter","stance":"rec","why":"Leno drives his restored Baker around LA","quote":"Jay Leno has an entertaining segment driving around LA in his restored Baker, he can tell you all about it, fascinating vehicle","text":"In 1909, there were thousands of Baker Electric cars cruising the streets of NYC quietly and at 35mph. You didn’t need to go much faster inside the city. 100 miles to a charge, good old fully recyclable glass lead acid batteries. Gasoline vehicles eventually outcompeted them by reaching 60mph and having more range (sound familiar?) but the parent isn’t wrong about Rockefeller, cheap and abundant fuel came 2 years later: > …growing electrification of lighting produced a drop in kerosene demand, creating a supply problem. It appeared that the burgeoning oil industry would be trapped into over-producing kerosene and under-producing gasoline since simple distillation could not alter the ratio of the two products from any given crude. The solution appeared in 1911 when the development of the Burton process allowed thermal cracking of crude oils, which increased the percent yield of gasoline from the heavier hydrocarbons. [LINK] Jay Leno has an entertaining segment driving around LA in his restored Baker, he can tell you all about it, fascinating vehicle: (starts at 3:20) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24451583,"author":"Bayart","month":"2020-09","story":"Ami, a tiny cube on wheels that French 14-year-olds can drive","stance":"reference","why":"Early Baker electric cars resembled small modern EVs","quote":"first wave of electric cars, over a hundred years ago, such as the Baker [1]","text":"I find it surprisingly, and interestingly, similar to the first wave of electric cars, over a hundred years ago, such as the Baker [1]. They were small, didn't require much mechanical insight (as EVs do) and particularly suited for small drives to the grocery store. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16212868,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2018-01","story":"Worked for 52 Years, Still Running: 1912 C-T 4WD Electric Truck","stance":"reference","why":"Leno recounts driving an old electric car to a hotel","quote":"","text":"Jay Leno (tv host and car enthusiast) has a few very old electric cars in his collection. In one video (link at the end) he talks about how he and his wife drove one of the electric cars down to a fancy hotel. They expected to be a surprising and exotic sight, but one of the bellhops asked them if they knew Mrs X, who had been driving to the hotel for 5 or six decades until 2001 in her similar electric car. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_DTpQ4Kk2wA",1],["EH6vE97qIP4",1],["B1J2RMorJXM",1],["sj8Sg8qnjOg",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["g2tMcMQqSbA",1],["jEbRVNxL44c",1]]},"aFdpBqmDpzM":{"wy":"HN cites it as the primary source for Amazon's Working Backwards method whenever product-definition approaches come up.","q":{"x":"A nice video","i":32955704,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32955704,"author":"kylewatson","month":"2022-09","story":"I accidentally started a movement – Policing the Police by scraping court data","stance":"rec","why":"A nice video on Amazon's well-defined process.","quote":"A nice video","text":"Sounds good. Thank you for taking this the right way - I found it hard to articulate the criticism in a constructive way with few words, so it was verbose and I hoped for the best. Amazon, for all its faults, has this process well defined. These links may be helpful to you. Good Luck [LINK] [LINK] A nice video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39892683,"author":"atombender","month":"2024-04","story":"Headline Driven Development","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Amazon's Working Backwards methodology and fake press release.","quote":"This is just Amazon's \"Working Backwards\" methodology [1] expressed simplistically.","text":"This is just Amazon's \"Working Backwards\" methodology [1] expressed simplistically. There's also a book about written by key Amazon people. [2] A core part of the Working Backwards idea is to start out by writing a fake press release, even for internal projects. This helps crystallize exactly what is important and what isn't, as it forces you to express your goal precisely and succinctly as well as define what it brings to the table. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":26180074,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2021-02","story":"There’s no such thing as “a startup within a big company”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Amazon's working backwards innovation process.","quote":"","text":"With all due respect to the author, I think the article kind of romanticizes start-up founders and their perils. Amazon famously dubs itself with being the biggest startup in the world. There's a company that espouses Clay Christensen's philosophy of building enduring businesses. Over the years, a lot has been said and written about how Amazon manages to do it, I mean, this is better demonstrated by Jeff, its founder, whose wealth went from $10B in 1997 to $1B in 2002/3, but still bet big on AWS (2003), Prime (2005), and Kindle (2007) in an unprecedented run of series of innovations that'd could have killed the company. -- \"working backwards\" I want to draw some parallels to the YC application process with how Amazon operates, from what I've read and what I've experienced as an ex-employee: A lot of product development is funded at Amazon after review of what's called a PR/FAQ doc [0] (this is the \"working backwards from the customer\" part). The PR needs to clearly articulate in a headline or two what the product is about; whilst the first paragraph must present a complete summary of what the product would be in its v1 form at launch. The next few paragraphs detail the current problem and the proposed solution interlaced by imaginary quotes from would-be customers; and the concluding paragraph has a clear call-to-action on exactly how customers can make use of the proposed solution. If you've ever filled out a YC form, you'd find yourself going through a similar exercise. And"}]},"pBiWtJJN5zk":{"wy":"Shared as a recent, detailed comparison of the two early GUIs and where their conventions differ.","q":{"x":"this video comparing the Apple Lisa to the Xerox Star highlights the differences","i":45701077,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":35769426,"author":"GeekyBear","month":"2023-05","story":"Xerox Star 8010 Interfaces, high quality polaroids (1981)","stance":"rec","why":"Recent look comparing the Xerox Star 8010 and Apple Lisa.","quote":"a more recent look at both the Star 8010 and the Apple Lisa.","text":"Here's video of Xerox personel demonstrating the Star 8010 UI back in the day. [LINK] Also, a more recent look at both the Star 8010 and the Apple Lisa. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47766828,"author":"OhMeadhbh","month":"2026-04","story":"Macs Since 1983","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Meh. the Lisa was absolutely, positively, explicitly NOT a Mac. If you don't get that, then you misunderstand the business of computing in the late 70s / early 80s. The author also ignored Jef Raskin's work on the original Mac project (I wrote a serial driver for him in the early 80s for a 6809 prototype he was working on.) If you're going to make one of these Mac hagiographies, it's useful to point out the Canon Cat (which was more in line with Jef's original idea for the Mac) and then point out how different it was from main-stream Macs. And then point out WHY the Lisa was not a Mac. So... Why do people think the Lisa was a prototype Mac? It's easy to understand the confusion. It has a 68k processor, a mouse and a bitmap screen. It was even used as a development platform for the original Macs. But it is not a Mac. The Lisa didn't have \"apps\" and \"files\" in the same way we think about them. It had \"tools\" and \"documents.\" The Lisa was strongly influenced by the Xerox Star. And while it's not exactly true to say the Lisa was Apple's version of the Star, there was definitely a strong resemblance. The Lisa (and the Star) came with all the functionality it would ever have built into the system. (This restriction changed later, but when launched, it was considered a complete system.) It had \"apps\" in the sense there were tool icons you could (double?) click to create a blank document of a specific type. But it took Apple over a year to decide they wanted to give third-parties the"},{"id":45701077,"author":"linguae","month":"2025-10","story":"Carmack on Operating Systems (1997)","stance":"reference","why":"Highlights differences between Lisa and Xerox Star GUI conventions.","quote":"this video comparing the Apple Lisa to the Xerox Star highlights the differences","text":"I remember trying out Plan 9 a few years ago. I like its underpinnings; I like how the designers pushed the Unix notion of \"everything is a file\" to its limits, with various services being presented to the operating system as file servers implemented using a protocol named 9P. It's a cool research operating system with a lot of very interesting ideas, but ultimately it's not my daily driver due to a lack of hardware and software support, though I am working on a research project (but for conventional *nix operating systems) that takes advantage of 9P. Carmack's description of the rio GUI is apt; rio is heavily based on Xerox PARC's Cedar environment from the mid-1980s ( [LINK] ). I had a hard time getting used to rio, though eventually I learned it. I even purchased an old Sun three-button mouse (with no click-wheel) since Plan 9 didn't like my click-wheel mouse. rio does not adopt a lot of the conventions that were introduced by the Apple Lisa, Apple Macintosh, and Microsoft Windows, resulting in a completely foreign experience for most new Plan 9 users. There is a misconception that Apple and Microsoft simply stole the idea of GUIs from Xerox PARC without further innovating on it. This misconception is false; the Apple Lisa and the Apple Macintosh introduced many GUI conventions that were not present in Xerox PARC's GUIs (this video comparing the Apple Lisa to the Xerox Star highlights the differences: [VIDEO] ), and Windows introduced additional innovations. Back to Plan 9"}],"r":[["vwCdKU9uYnE",1],["Gk-9Fd2mEnI",1],["EQs5VxNPhzk",1],["43CMV8KIs3E",1],["s0ukXDnWlTY",1],["VO_1AdYRGW8",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["29RkHH-psrY",1],["JRcXUuQYR90",1],["rHP-OPXK2ig",1]]},"rnLWSC5p1XE":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an easy-to-grasp introduction to Chomsky's ideas on universal grammar and the language faculty.","q":{"x":"a pretty good, easy to grasp intro to his ideas on universal grammar and the language faculty","i":219131,"y":"2008"},"m":[{"id":219131,"author":"wheels","month":"2008-06","story":"Spoken language affects thought as programming languages affect algorithms?","stance":"rec","why":"Easy-to-grasp intro to Chomsky's universal grammar and language faculty","quote":"a pretty good, easy to grasp intro to his ideas on universal grammar and the language faculty","text":"Pinker seems to be on the other end of the academic spectrum from the SWH. For those who don't know linguistics, Chomsky was the driving force in modern linguistics. Last week I stumbled across this video, the first 15 minutes or so of such are a pretty good, easy to grasp intro to his ideas on universal grammar and the language faculty: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17749456,"author":"uRreading2slow","month":"2018-08","story":"Google's censored Chinese search engine: a catalogue of ethical violations?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Maybe we should be, though? [..] Great. I'm not sure that should top your list of injustices, though. It doesn't top mine, because it's not one thats happening in my back yard. So do you think we should care, or shouldn't we? If your point ultimately is no, we should not care, then why not be honest and say \"but we shouldn't care, and here's why\". And how can you claim \"nobody\" cares? Are you sure there aren't people in many countries who wish companies wouldn't just deal with their butcherers, but give the oppressed a voice? If you don't care, just say it. It's not about where it happens, it's about whether my \"friends\" are involved in it. If Google didn't use this site as a PR platform, too, there'd be no point in talking about it. But since many people here use their products, because they claim a \"Hi fellow Kids\" kind of humanness, it is relevant. When some total stranger acts horrible to others, but they can handle it themselves, I probably wouldn't bother getting involved. But if that guy was chatting to me 5 minutes prior to that, and considers himself someone on good terms with me, I would absolutely at least say something. It doesn't matter what's on \"top\" of the \"list of priorites\", it only matters that it matters, and that I have a position on it I must make known or be complicit by silence. It's really simple, we understand that intuitively in all sorts of situations, it doesn't get more complicated because the behaviour to have a position on gets worse. Totalit"},{"id":2240917,"author":"espeed","month":"2011-02","story":"A rare look into North Korea's famed Propaganda School","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky's recent talk at Google","quote":"And here is his recent talk at Google","text":"Noam Chomsky @ Harvard - Propaganda and Control of The Public Mind [LINK] And here is his recent talk at Google... Google Talk: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["HbUnatPfSgg",1]]},"wymmCdLdPvM":{"wy":"Linked in threads on sums of cubes and Diophantine equations as an accessible introduction.","q":{"x":"Or watch videos about it","i":37806648,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37806648,"author":"db48x","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Fermat's Last Theorem with More Terms: Why Does It Stop at 3?","stance":"rec","why":"Videos on Diophantine equations and sums of cubes.","quote":"Or watch videos about it","text":"It's an open question that has been studied for thousands of years. This type of equation (a polynomial with integer coefficients) is called a Diophantine equation; you can look it up on Wikipedia. Or watch videos about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20896122,"author":"jerf","month":"2019-09","story":"42 is found to be the sum of three cubes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I only recently heard what I think is a pretty solid answer to this question [1] that put it this way: We've pretty much got addition nailed down. We're pretty solid on multiplication. However, we are surprisingly weak mathematically when we try to put the two together; you can write down some extremely small math statements that baffled mathematicians for centuries, and for such statements that we have solutions for, they often get into very, very complicated math. So you have things like Fermat's Last Theorum, or if you prefer, the Question: For a^n + b^n = c^n, are there any solutions with n > 2 for integers? Note how it's just multiplication and addition on the integers. There are simpler questions, but they don't get much simpler. There's no real analysis, transcendental numbers, imaginaries or higher-dimensional numbers, infinities, and so on, at least not in the question itself; it's literally so simple you can reasonably explain it to a middle schooler. It took mathematicians centuries, and the simplest version of the current proof is still PhD-level work to understand. (I suspect even a Master's student in math would have to carefully craft their entire post-doc education for the purpose of understanding this exact proof to be able to say they fully understood it, with no lemmas taken on faith, and I'm still not sure they could make it without a lot of independent study.) You have things like \"Can all of the numbers of a certain form be created via 'a^3 + b^3 + c^3'?"},{"id":19492407,"author":"heinrichf","month":"2019-03","story":"Sum-of-Three-Cubes Problem Solved for ‘Stubborn’ Number 33","stance":"reference","why":"2015 episode from when 33 was unsolved.","quote":"a followup from the 2015 episode when the 33 problem was unsolved","text":"Numberphile video on the topic with Andrew Booker: [LINK] which is a followup from the 2015 episode [VIDEO] when the 33 problem was unsolved. The announcement elicited this Mathoverflow discussion about \"mic drop\" moments in mathematics: [LINK]"}],"r":[["YE8MVNMzpbo",1],["aeWyp2vXxqA",1],["FlQYU3m1e80",1],["c33AZBnRHks",1],["6JwEYamjXpA",1]]},"0n_Ty_72Qds":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference whenever software or algorithms rigidly deny people with no recourse.","m":[{"id":38300835,"author":"blitzar","month":"2023-11","story":"UnitedHealth uses AI model with 90% error rate to deny care, lawsuit alleges","stance":"reference","why":"Origin of the catchphrase from Little Britain.","quote":"a catchphrase first used in the British sketch comedy television programme Little Britain in 2004","text":"\"Computer says no\" is a catchphrase first used in the British sketch comedy television programme Little Britain in 2004. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38942718,"author":"pixl97","month":"2024-01","story":"Your last name contains invalid characters (2010)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":">A human can think outside the box in a way a `strcmp` can't. Computer says no [VIDEO] --- A human can do many things, but never underestimate the power of the system (Hello Moloch) to remove said creative abilities from humans. Also never underestimate how creatively stupid humans can be."},{"id":39030728,"author":"pixl97","month":"2024-01","story":"AI poisoning could turn open models into destructive \"sleeper agents\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Computer says no. [VIDEO] Computers don't argue. [LINK] ----- The problem here is you want to make a Moloch problem an individual problem... This doesn't always work, people will defer to the system and not take self responsibility, especially when the incentives align to defer."},{"id":32777960,"author":"andsoitis","month":"2022-09","story":"AirPods Pro Service Program for Sound Issues","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"My experience of the Service Program has been very poor. One of my AirPods Pro has an annoying tick. It's like a metronome (regular) and orthogonal to the music I'm listening to. I've taken it to the Apple Store, but instead of the service rep putting it in their ear to confirm the issue, they use some machine that checks and, in my case, \"computer says no!\" to my complaint. [VIDEO] The rubber around the ear insert is changeable, so it wouldn't be unhygienic for them to listen directly. Their refusal at an exchange has caused me to eschew trusting them for the new AirPods, and instead I've moved on to another brand."},{"id":28044500,"author":"romwell","month":"2021-08","story":"Sentenced by Algorithm","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Something resembling fair\", i.e. unfair? We already have that. Not that the idea of codifying fairness is new; a certain guy named Hammurabi started the trend a while ago. The result of humanity's collective attempt to create a Transparent Fairness Flowchart still leaves much to be desired. Replacing codex with code, will, however, streamline the process by entirely removing (the already meager) accountability for bad decisions, as well making it effectively impossible to correct them: \"Computer says no\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26229377,"author":"bondolo","month":"2021-02","story":"Whistleblowers: Software keeping inmates in Arizona prisons beyond release dates","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"has always been a great illustration of this point","text":"Little Britain's recurring bit Computer says \"No\", has always been a great illustration of this point. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24219565,"author":"lvturner","month":"2020-08","story":"Ever wonder what your dog is thinking?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's certainly a situation I've ran in to in several banks (globally).","text":"People, presently seem much less willing to challenge what a computer says however. I'm wondering if Little Britain's \"computer says no\"[1] sketch is somewhat foreshadowing here. It's certainly a situation I've ran in to in several banks (globally). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16093382,"author":"jjawssd","month":"2018-01","story":"Can Washington Be Automated?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Automation can reduce transparency, by moving open discussions and reasoning into obscure, sometimes proprietary computer code. [VIDEO]"}]},"1seNMSamtxM":{"wy":"HN cites it as a quick introduction to why Intel ME is a trust problem for anyone worried about hardware-level access.","q":{"x":"This embedded computer system has access to all your memory devices.","i":36670979,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38413662,"author":"LabMechanic","month":"2023-11","story":"Tell HN: Your account can be deleted anytime, for any reason","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as reason not to trust computers.","quote":"Don't trust computers, see: Intel ME explained (intel management engine) from insider","text":"I use KeePassXC to manage passwords. Save the encrypted file (Password.kdbx, 3 kB) wherever you want. Extra points: For emails, buy a domain like mydomain.com for about $15 a year. This lets you have a stable email address (e.g., johndoe@mydomain.com) no matter which email service you use. Consider [LINK] or something similar. I also use Portmaster when I am on Windows. Forget defending against the government, they have state authority (violence, jail time), you don't. See: [LINK] One-time pad (OTP) or \"true randomness\" (uniform probability distribution) is your only safe option (as long as they cannot time travel), but do it offline. Don't trust computers, see: Intel ME explained (intel management engine) from insider [VIDEO] Physics is your only safe haven. Seemingly, everything/everybody has to abide by the law of physics. Even your \"almighty\" state."},{"id":37577252,"author":"d-161","month":"2023-09","story":"Snowden leak: Cavium networking hardware may contain NSA backdoor","stance":"reference","why":"Insider on remote management hardware behind Intel ME.","quote":"","text":"The Intel Management Engine always runs as long as the motherboard is receiving power, even when the computer is turned off. This issue can be mitigated with deployment of a hardware device, which is able to disconnect mains power. Intel's main competitor AMD has incorporated the equivalent AMD Secure Technology (formally called Platform Security Processor) in virtually all of its post-2013 CPUs. [LINK] Ylian Saint-Hilaire, principal Engineer working on remote management software including hardware manageability: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36670979,"author":"EternalCarnage","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: Problems for the Next Decade","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the hidden embedded computer inside Intel machines.","quote":"This embedded computer system has access to all your memory devices.","text":"The problem of having a \"secret\" computer (with an operating system) within your computer. [VIDEO] This embedded computer system has access to all your memory devices. Disabling it? Not convenient."}]},"988jPjs1gao":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of early academic FM synthesis composition and the Golden Mean in music.","q":{"x":"work Stria ( ) unifies timbre and scale structure about the Golden Mean as a musical interval.","i":41459397,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41459397,"author":"waffletower","month":"2024-09","story":"Generalized Carlos Scales","stance":"reference","why":"Chowning's Stria unifies timbre and scale around the Golden Mean","quote":"work Stria ( ) unifies timbre and scale structure about the Golden Mean as a musical interval.","text":"John Chowning's ( [LINK] ) work Stria ( [VIDEO] ) unifies timbre and scale structure about the Golden Mean as a musical interval. It's completion predates this work by Carlos by nearly a decade (1977) and the composition process apparently began as early as 1972 ( [LINK] ). There are many academic papers available which analyze this work: [LINK]"},{"id":29515619,"author":"TheOtherHobbes","month":"2021-12","story":"Yamaha DX7 chip reverse-engineering, part 4: how algorithms are implemented","stance":"reference","why":"Early FM piece made with expensive mainframe time","quote":"The early FM pieces weren't very pop.","text":"The early FM pieces weren't very pop. [VIDEO] You may think it sounds crude, but pieces like these took hours of expensive mainframe (PDP-10) time. There wasn't a lot of opportunity for careful sculpting of fine details. You could easily synthesize something like this in real time now. But not many people do, which I think is a shame. If you can read French, there's more background here: [LINK]"},{"id":16594166,"author":"diydsp","month":"2018-03","story":"Making music using new sounds generated with machine learning","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Chowning's academic FM synthesis compositions","quote":"I dare you to look up compositions by Chowning himself!","text":"There is a tendency to judge and predict the commercial success of this (and every) music project. As a long-time player and designer of digital music instruments in academic laboratory and underground contexts, i can say it's almost impossible to produce a hit directly from a laboratory. It has to go through a commercial layer first. Then an artistic layer. That's not a dig at anyone or anything. Music is a projection of life's experiences into sound... A lab can inject some tech into the music world, but we won't know for approximately 1-2 decades whether any instrument or technique is a hit or it. FM Synthesis, formalized by Chowning at Stanford ,comes to mind. His initial implementations were idiosyncratic. His math was an organization of community knowledge. It took Yamaha's need to make sound cards and keyboards to direct all this effort those sounds into the chips and instruments we ended up buying and loving in the 80s. And it took artists and producers to bring out the best of the techniques. Also, you'll find when the tech passes through the artistic layer, there are many more mundane variables than the technology, such as re-sale value and ability to get them re-paired. For example, if you think these sounds are non-compelling, I dare you to look up compositions by Chowning himself! [1] They are nothing like Samantha Fox's \"Touch Me,\" [2] a famous example of the Yamaha DX-7 FM synthesizer. The point of research like this is not really to inspire music fans so much "}]},"Akm7ik-H_7U":{"wy":"HN links it as a concise explanation of why free parking is not really free and how parking rules shape cities.","q":{"x":"IMHO free parking isn't actually free","i":15716614,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":17711280,"author":"cbhl","month":"2018-08","story":"Shortage of charging stations to become a bottleneck to mass adoption of EVs?","stance":"reference","why":"History of mandatory parking minimums","quote":"See the history around mandatory parking minimums, like from this video from Vox","text":"Government hasn't historically been the one building parking spots. See the history around mandatory parking minimums, like from this video from Vox: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15716614,"author":"chadgeidel","month":"2017-11","story":"Denver Radically Expanded Its Transit, So Why Are More People Driving Cars?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why free parking has hidden costs","quote":"IMHO free parking isn't actually free","text":"Even reducing the number of free parking spots would help. IMHO free parking isn't actually free: [VIDEO] . The company I work for is building a large new parking structure in my office because people have complained about parking for so long. Do they offer transit options? No. It seems shortsighted. EDIT: I see you are being downvoted. I assume that's because of your \"charge tolls\" recommendation. Most folks I talk to think they \"pay their fare share\" with taxes and roads. They are surprised that road and gas taxes only pay for about half: [LINK]"},{"id":15159554,"author":"GuiA","month":"2017-09","story":"As Bike-Sharing Brings Out Bad Manners, China Asks, What’s Wrong with Us?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the long-term hidden costs of free parking","quote":"","text":"In some cases it does not impede public right of way; but it has a much deeper, longer term, insidious cost. [VIDEO]"}]},"B9MShtCg4fk":{"wy":"Used as a counter-example in music theory discussions to show how mode changes a melody's mood.","q":{"x":"The canonical video on the subject","i":41092863,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41092863,"author":"Kye","month":"2024-07","story":"Tritone Substitutions","stance":"reference","why":"Imperial March in major mode changes mood to triumphant","quote":"It changes the mood from \"this is the bad guys!\" to something more positive and triumphant.","text":"The canonical video on the subject: [LINK] Some people will naively say \"All music has pentatonic! Universal!\" but ignore vital cultural context. One example in the video is ragas: they can look a lot like Western scales and modes, but they have a time and context. Western music also has a time and context, but it doesn't stand out as much to us unless someone goes full ham breaking that context like with the Imperial March in a Major mode: [VIDEO] It changes the mood from \"this is the bad guys!\" to something more positive and triumphant."},{"id":22414593,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2020-02","story":"Musicians algorithmically generate melodies, release them to public domain","stance":"reference","why":"Counter-example of Imperial March in a major key","quote":"Here's the Imperial Death March played in a Major-scale, instead of a minor scale.","text":"Though you asked for an example... but maybe its best to show a counter-example instead? Here's the Imperial Death March played in a Major-scale, instead of a minor scale. [VIDEO] If everything was in an 8-note (no accidental, transpositions only) measure, they'd sound like \"Vader's redemption\". To sound sad, you MUST shift to a minor key. A minor key has sharps and flats to create tension. Any \"sad sounding\" song cannot be properly played in a major key."},{"id":24677821,"author":"082349872349872","month":"2020-10","story":"Falling in love again with the haunting sounds of interwar Polish tango","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those who suspect HN may have been infested by Rebel shills and their fake news: [VIDEO] Anyone know where I can find the drawing of the stormtrooper in a locker room, complete with I M P E R I A L knuckle tats? I'm doing my part!"}]},"DrRFzwPE0d4":{"wy":"Mentioned mostly as a joke or as an example of real stunt work with no wires or tricks.","m":[{"id":34436267,"author":"gregmac","month":"2023-01","story":"Boston Dynamics’ bipedal robot Atlas can now grab and toss, like humans","stance":"reference","why":"Real stunt with no wires or tricks","quote":"the stunt you saw was real","text":"> A reality check, a \"what you've seen today was staged\", a \"don't think this was real, and then make poor life choices\". I liked the integrity of that. I think it shows something different from that: \"the stunt you saw was real\". As in: there were no wires, camera tricks, editing, or visual or special effects. Just someone extremely disciplined and good at what they do, trying many times to get it perfect. I don't think the only thing preventing people who watch his movies thinking they could fight a gang of people using a 10'step ladder [1] is the blooper real.. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7991396,"author":"phlo","month":"2014-07","story":"Your high IQ might kill your startup (2010)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The point was to emphasize how ladders are clearly superior to knives, or even sticks.","text":"The point was to emphasize how ladders are clearly superior to knives, or even sticks. As has been demonstrated in literature[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28583923,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2021-09","story":"How Marvel took over cinema and TV","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"But most fight scenes were fake, even before CGI. In fact, almost nobody ever gets hurt on set... and when people do get hurt, its in stunts (not the fight scenes). The number of \"realistic fight scenes\" are incredibly slim. Chinese martial arts are mostly Wuxia: a branch of martial arts that was developed for stage performance (flips, spins, etc. etc.) that has very little practical relevance. \"Kung Fu\" films (ie: Bruce Lee, Jackie Chan) reduce the number of filps... but even then, Jackie Chan's use of hilarious stage props is anything but realistic. I mean: just imagine \"Die Hard\", no one can sustain that many injuries realistically and keep going. Its fake action, yes with practical effects / non-CGI (blood packs and stunts), but its still fantasy. And \"Princess Bride\" duel is fully ridiculous: with more flips and gymnastics than actual fighting (arguably the point: it was a comedy film making fun of tropes). Anyone who has actually been in a fighting-sport (themselves mostly \"fake\", as duels took place centuries ago), will instantly recognize the bad form most of these older fight scenes had. Enemies charge the protagonist one at a time (yes: even in \"practical\" Kung Fu movies with few special effects) ---------- \"Fake\" is fine. But there's a lack of progression or understanding going on in a lot of the CGI-fight scenes. They're willing to spend tons of money on big explosions or special effects, but they don't always hit home with me. The famous \"Jackie Chan Ladder Fight"}]},"FtutLA63Cp8":{"wy":"HN links it as the original video whenever someone renders Bad Apple on unusual hardware or displays.","m":[{"id":41812272,"author":"pzmarzly","month":"2024-10","story":"“Bad Apple” in Minecraft","stance":"reference","why":"Scene analysis: Eirin reaching for the moon","quote":"the picture that the author called \"The sun\" is actually Eirin [0] looking at the moon.","text":"I learned way more about computer graphics here than I expected. Kudos to the author. One nit: the picture that the author called \"The sun\" is actually Eirin [0] looking at the moon. In that scene [1] she's reaching for the moon, where she was exiled from, only to hesitate and retract the hand. In the next scene, Kaguya [2] also reaches for the moon, but does not hesitate. I'm not sure what the symbolism here was supposed to mean, as according to Touhou wiki it was Eirin's plan to steal the moon. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":40791772,"author":"sphars","month":"2024-06","story":"Show HN: I built a JavaScript-powered flipdisc display","stance":"reference","why":"The original Bad Apple video","quote":"For the unawares, the original video","text":"This is what I was hoping to see, seems like the logical next step in showing off this awesome project! For the unawares, the original video: [VIDEO] My favorite version, also using real-life objects: [LINK] (HN discussion: [LINK] )"},{"id":26590574,"author":"dwheeler","month":"2021-03","story":"Bad Apple on an Apple //e: playing video on a stock 1 MHz Apple II [video]","stance":"reference","why":"The original video from 2009","quote":"The video's been around since 2009.","text":"The video's been around since 2009. Video: [VIDEO] Wikipedia article: [LINK] !! I recommend watching this video, \"Bad Apple explained:History and Analysis\": [LINK] It's fascinating. I think this video is a fabulous example of community creation."}]},"Hs-rgvkRfwc":{"wy":"Dropped into threads about dialects and mutual intelligibility as a comedic illustration.","m":[{"id":45850387,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2025-11","story":"Rockstar employee shares account of the company's union-busting efforts","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Right the fact you may not be able to understand some Scottish people because of their accent doesn't mean they're not competent English speakers, it just means the accent is difficult for you to understand, which isn't relevant when writing. There are a few famous movie scenes where somebody deliberately uses perfectly reasonable English sentences but with such a thick accent that most English users cannot understand it, but once you know what they said you can play that sound back and yeah, that's what they said, you just couldn't understand the accent e.g.. [VIDEO] Indeed the joke is that people keep repeating what the hard-to-understand bloke said even when it's perfectly obvious what he said, because if you can understand it then you can't tell whether it was hard to understand. That's not even Scottish, the bloke in that scene is from Somerset, which is the far side of the country but exactly like Scotland most people in Somerset don't talk like that most of the time, but some of them do, some of the time and to them it's normal, that's just how you say words."},{"id":43333737,"author":"VyseofArcadia","month":"2025-03","story":"The US island that speaks Elizabethan English","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You say that, but what comes to mind is the scene from Hot Fuzz where Simon Pegg's character needs a translator to understand a thick West Country accent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40860147,"author":"wongarsu","month":"2024-07","story":"Why is Chile so long?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You don't even have to go all the way to China. The English countryside has multiple so called \"accents\" that are basically unintelligible to a speaker of London English, with plenty of famous examples in popular media (e.g. [1][2]). Similarly, Germany has plenty of mutually unintelligible dialects. They are all related to each other and any two geographically adjacent dialects are mutually intelligible, but as distance grows it becomes harder to bridge the gap (which is why everyone learns Standard German nowadays). Luxembourgish meanwhile is in every sense a dialect of German with French influences, but due to having an army is considered its own language. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":47104851,"author":"NooneAtAll3","month":"2026-02","story":"How far back in time can you understand English?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> older man approached me and spoke to me in English and I couldn’t understand a word he said like this [VIDEO] ?"}]},"LCIsIfEOEI8":{"wy":"Mentioned as a quirky example of demoscene creativity and related overhead-projector techniques.","m":[{"id":25455750,"author":"chromanoid","month":"2020-12","story":"Creative Code-Generated Art","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a wild demo category entry.","quote":"","text":"There are and were all kinds of competitions. The wild category is probably the most obvious one regarding no size limitations, but the generic \"combined demo\" category which is very often present at competitions is also usually without restrictions (but it must run on the competition machine). See [LINK] for wild demos (e.g. [VIDEO] :)) or [LINK] for \"combined demos\""},{"id":8857713,"author":"theandrewbailey","month":"2015-01","story":"Scroll Slow. Have Fun","stance":"reference","why":"Similar technique with transparencies on an overhead projector.","quote":"I've seen this technique used with transparencies on an overhead projector before.","text":"I've seen this technique used with transparencies on an overhead projector before. Things get interesting when the fields aren't aligned exactly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41420577,"author":"IshKebab","month":"2024-09","story":"Using the moiré effect to show different arrows to each observer (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Some of the others were hilarious and embarrassing","text":"That's equal parts hilarious and impressive. At least the first one. Some of the others were hilarious and embarrassing: [VIDEO]"}]},"Pubd-spHN-0":{"wy":"HN uses it purely as a joke reference when something is being forced or a pun on 'fetch' fits.","m":[{"id":33073715,"author":"btown","month":"2022-10","story":"Raft Is So Fetch: The Raft Consensus Algorithm Explained Through Mean Girls","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"But the entire premise of the scene [VIDEO] is that the act of telling someone to \"stop trying to make fetch happen\" is toxic and mean-spirited, and if someone actually does want to make \"fetch\" happen, they should be encouraged to do so. And if your \"fetch\" is the normalization of Mean Girls as a valid lede for didactic technical discourse, then you go Glen Coco."},{"id":24087028,"author":"vosper","month":"2020-08","story":"P2 powers internal collaboration at WordPress.com, and is now free","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory \"Mean Girls\" ;)","text":"\"a8c\" is Automattic (makers of Wordpress) for anyone who was confused. Obligatory \"Mean Girls\" ;) [VIDEO]"},{"id":14901638,"author":"Balgair","month":"2017-08","story":"Hacking Voting Machines at Defcon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I dunno. The constant barrage of: \"BlockChains, but for Cancer!\" just seems ... derivative now. Like the 'Uber, but for dogs' was this time last year. Everyone knows these hacker-coins are a scam, the last one with them looses their shirt. Yeah, the ideas are kinda cool, but, nah man. Like, from Mean Girls : \"Gretchen, stop trying to make 'Fetch' happen, it's not gonna happen\" [VIDEO]"}]},"QrGrOK8oZG8":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a joke or pop-culture reference, and as a famous YouTube oddity with its own CSS selector.","m":[{"id":39392158,"author":"015a","month":"2024-02","story":"YouTube Oddities","stance":"reference","why":"Only video with a dedicated CSS selector on YouTube.","quote":"The video: Adult Swim's \"Too Many Cooks\" skit","text":"My favorite YouTube oddity: If you hunt over the CSS the site downloads on, IIRC, every page; you'll eventually come across this selector: [VIDEO] h1.ytd-watch-metadata { color:#e6d140; font-family:Georgia,serif; font-size:250%; font-variant:small-caps; font-weight:normal; transform:skew(-5deg,0deg); text-shadow:1px 1px #000,1px 1px .1px #000 } A selector that appears to be referencing a very specific video ID; as far as I can tell, the only video in the billions of videos on YouTube to receive this treatment (at least, in terms of a \"css selector with video-id in it\"). The video: Adult Swim's \"Too Many Cooks\" skit ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":31781347,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-06","story":"Gnome has no thumbnails in the file picker and my toilets are blocked (2021)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Everybody wants to make it into the \"Too Many Cooks\" credits.","text":"Everybody wants to make it into the \"Too Many Cooks\" credits. [VIDEO] (Is that RMS with the machete?)"},{"id":25455063,"author":"yowlingcat","month":"2020-12","story":"Flanderization","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the perennial Adult Swim sketch which satirizes these tropes, Too Many Cooks","text":"I know they're annoying, but sometimes, a fun thing to do with these tropes is to enjoy them for how campy and trashy they are, along the lines of B-movies and the perennial Adult Swim sketch which satirizes these tropes, Too Many Cooks [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"TWFzWRoVNnE":{"wy":"HN cites it as a source for Intel's market-segmentation tactics on its high-end desktop platform.","q":{"x":"Linus Tech Tips has a rant about how Intel is trying to segment the market by only offering more PCI-E lanes on the most-expensive CPUs","i":14526943,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":20599245,"author":"chx","month":"2019-08","story":"AMD Ryzen 7 3700X is such a hit it almost outsold Intel’s entire CPU range","stance":"reference","why":"Background on Intel's HEDT platform strategy.","quote":"","text":"> Are the recent AMD CPUs generally better for their price than Intel ones, and are there any downsides? My personal context is compiling programs, Oh boy, are you in for a nice surprise. [LINK] the Ryzen 7 3700X (323 USD) stands its ground against the i9 9900K (485 USD). The top end is not even funny, there is no consumer Intel CPU that can stand against the Ryzen 9 3900X (499 USD), the LGA 2066 workstation chips that can are double, triple the price. Or, to put it another way: at the same price the 3900X vs 9900k it is 32% faster when compiling the kernel. Passmark [LINK] shows no less than fifty percent . Right now the 3900X is in such a short supply it sells for 700-ish on eBay. This will resolve in a few months -- there's a 3950X scheduled for September with 16 cores instead of 12 cores, slightly lower base clock (3.8 vs 3.5) but slightly higher turbo clock (4.7 vs 4.6) tightening the screw on Intel. Of course, it'll have a \"halo\" price at 750 USD but will compete, again, with Intel workstation chips double the price (and much higher TDP as well). Intel is in deep... and they have nothing to counter with, Comet Lake-S is only scheduled for next year, this roadmap was posted just a few weeks ago: [LINK] and these are just words, there is no guarantee whatsoever they will be able to ship 10nm in mass market quantity ever . Ps. Intel's workstation (HEDT) strategy is a mess anyways -- [LINK] they essentially have nothing to offer between 485 USD and 950 USD which of course r"},{"id":14774004,"author":"redtuesday","month":"2017-07","story":"AMD Threadripper prices undercut Intel's Core i9 by as much as $1,000","stance":"reference","why":"Source on Intel's paid VROC RAID key on X299.","quote":"","text":"Like I have written in the other thread already: add to that the ECC support of Threadripper [0], 20 more PCIe lanes and no raid key shenanigans (on Intels x299 you have to pay 100$ extra for RAID 1 and 300$ for RAID 5 support with their VROC feature [1]). Unless you really need AVX 512 and better single thread performance (and 2 more cores in case of the not available 18 core part), why would you buy Intels new offering? Because you can reuse the cooler since it's compatible with x99? [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14489759,"author":"mtgx","month":"2017-06","story":"Apple Teases Upcoming Space Gray iMac Pro, \"the most powerful Mac ever\"","stance":"reference","why":"Source on Intel's upcoming 18-core chip.","quote":"","text":"I just can't believe they chose the 18-core 2018 Intel vaporware chip [1] over this year's 16-core AMD Threadripper. Very strange decision on Apple's part. Not to mention they could've sold the iMac Pro for like $1,000 less. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14526943,"author":"seanp2k2","month":"2017-06","story":"PCIe 4.0 will be twice as fast as today's slots","stance":"reference","why":"Rant on Intel limiting PCIe lanes on cheaper X299 CPUs.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips has a rant about how Intel is trying to segment the market by only offering more PCI-E lanes on the most-expensive CPUs","text":"Fairly recent benchmark with cards dropping nothing from 16x to 8x : [LINK] Linus Tech Tips has a rant about how Intel is trying to segment the market by only offering more PCI-E lanes on the most-expensive CPUs despite mobo support with their new X299 platform (they've done this in the past as well): [VIDEO] Basically, if you want the full 44 lanes, you have to get the top-spec processor for $1k ($1k for the cpu itself). The i9s above the 7900x don't even have details yet, since Intel is working on adapting their Xeons to their new HEDT platform to keep up with AMD. Here's the breakdown: [LINK] I've also heard speculation that they're crippling the lanes on cheaper chips because they're so worried about cannibalizing their server market. Don't expect ECC support on these either. Honestly, if any Xeon BIOSes and CPUs supported unlocked multipliers, they'd be a better deal, but I get that overclocking and max stability don't really mix. OR they could totally wow everyone and come out with some 5.0Ghz (all-core) 16-core chip that isn't overclockable any further but can run ECC. Sell THAT for $2k to workstation users and rich gamers. Make it 2P capable as well in case you need 32 cores. Maybe AMD will do it with ThreadRipper, which BTW has 64 PCI-E 3.0 lanes ( [LINK] )."},{"id":14491166,"author":"tutanchamun","month":"2017-06","story":"Apple Teases Upcoming Space Gray iMac Pro, \"the most powerful Mac ever\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"relevant for people who don't know: [VIDEO] the funnier version: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] EDIT: by mistake I originally linked this video I had in my clipboard: [LINK]"}]},"XIaovi1JWyY":{"wy":"Cited in threads about evacuation rules, mostly as an example of a certification test that commenters call unrealistic.","m":[{"id":18206176,"author":"Animats","month":"2018-10","story":"Trump Just Signed a Law That Changes Life Aboard Airlines","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Nah. It's just a requirement to study the issue from a safety perspective.[1] \"SEC. 577. Minimum dimensions for passenger seats. (a) In general.—Not later than 1 year after the date of enactment of this Act, and after providing notice and an opportunity for comment, the Administrator of the Federal Aviation Administration shall issue regulations that establish minimum dimensions for passenger seats on aircraft operated by air carriers in interstate air transportation or intrastate air transportation, including minimums for seat pitch, width, and length, and that are necessary for the safety of passengers.\" That just means an aircraft with a tight seat pitch has to pass the aircraft evacuation time test.[1] Some of those tests may need to be rerun. Which is risky; people are injured in evacuation tests. The standards need to be re-evaluated, though, because people are fatter. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12231036,"author":"FabHK","month":"2016-08","story":"Crashing, Burning Planes Don’t Stop Passengers from Grabbing Their Luggage","stance":"reference","why":"Certification evacuation test of the A380.","quote":"","text":"It is a certification requirement for airliners that they can be evacuated in 90 seconds, with half the emergency exits closed/inoperational (see [1], [2]). This is tested with volunteers before certification, see e.g. [3] for a video of the certification test for the Airbus A380. The details [4] are quite interesting: a third of \"pax\" must be over 50, three life-size infant dolls must be carried along, etc. Given those certification requirements, a slow passenger is very much a problem. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":25191836,"author":"throwaheyy","month":"2020-11","story":"What it’s like inside your plane’s hidden ‘crew rest’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"I have the same impression from videos like [1] but I suppose that is all that is needed to certify the aircraft. It may not happen in the \"real world\", you never know how everyone in a plane of hundreds passengers will react, but the design of the plane has to allow it to be \"possible\". [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21092755,"author":"elliekelly","month":"2019-09","story":"Plane Tests Must Use Average Pilots, NTSB Says After 737 Max Crashes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the airlines \"comply\" with the 90 second requirement by conducting an evacuation test in completely unrealistic circumstances.","text":"This makes a lot of sense. They need similar requirements for evacuation testing. Regulations say all passengers in a plane need to be able to evacuate within 90 seconds. As Americans get bigger and airplane seats get smaller the airlines \"comply\" with the 90 second requirement by conducting an evacuation test in completely unrealistic circumstances.[1] The test participants are all handpicked, fit, airline employees who prepare and practice for the drill. The test \"starts\" with everyone in their seat (seat backs & try tables up) and with their life jacket already on. And in recent years the FAA hasn't required these tests as frequently. Why? Because even with all the preparation that goes into it, the mad-rush to the exits in a staged emergency, tends to be quite dangerous for the participants. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19875888,"author":"avar","month":"2019-05","story":"Deadly stupidity during Aeroflot flight 1492 emergency evacuation","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"These tests have been widely critiqued as completely unrealistic","text":"Those passengers aren't to blame, regulators and aircraft designers are to blame. People are idiots in the aggregate, and reliably exhibit herd behavior counterproductive to their survival. Aircraft design must account for this. There's something to the famous Feynman quote if we apply it in this context: \"For a successful technology, reality must take precedence over public relations, for Nature cannot be fooled. -- Richard P. Feynman In order to be certified aircraft must be completely evacuated during a test in 90 seconds. Here's such a test of the A380, Boeing etc. conduct tests in the same manner: [VIDEO] These tests have been widely critiqued as completely unrealistic, e.g. here: [LINK] Numerous real-world examples of emergency evacuations \"in the wild\" have shown that evacuations proceed nothing like what's shown in these tests. If aircraft manufacturers and regulators faced up to this we wouldn't have this problem. You'd get actual safety instead. E.g. design changes to aircraft mitigating the aisle blockages that do happen in these evacuations, but aren't accounted for in tests, more emergency exits etc. This is a recurring topic, e.g. in 2015 a BA flight in Las Vegas took 5 minutes to evacuate: [LINK]"}]},"YbYWhdLO43Q":{"wy":"HN mostly shares it as a joke and as an example of surprisingly good marketing.","q":{"x":"It’s hilarious. Great, great marketing.","i":18598227,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19119733,"author":"tareqak","month":"2019-02","story":"Facebook adds 5 divs, 9 spans and 30 CSS classes to every post in the timeline","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There are some kinds of ads that I do like for their entertainment value","text":"I'm surprised that companies don't create packaging materials that are actually also ads and have them be stuffed in all the packages that say Amazon sends. I'm resentful of advertising in general, so the comment above is a morbid expression of \"how low can they go\". There are some kinds of ads that I do like for their entertainment value e.g. the Squatty Potty ads by the Harmon Brothers or the Apple Cookie Monster ad [0][1][2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":34925441,"author":"boulos","month":"2023-02","story":"It’s time for Alphabet to spin off YouTube","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's definitely bizarre.","text":"Ahh, you mean the original Squatty Potty ads: [VIDEO] It's definitely bizarre. I assume they were going for memorable, but your report sounds like it failed at the basic goal: remembering the product."},{"id":28102334,"author":"aasasd","month":"2021-08","story":"Squatting or Kneeling May Be Beneficial for Health","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Squatting may also be beneficial in other contexts, as explained in the seminal ‘kink/unkink’ video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24211125,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: Why is Reddit on mobile so obsessed with making me use their app?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"For those wondering what \"Squatty Potty\" is, the company that makes it has a video that explains it well, featuring a pooping unicorn: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18598227,"author":"baxtr","month":"2018-12","story":"The popularity of the Squatty Potty","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It’s hilarious. Great, great marketing.","text":"If you don’t know the video, enjoy it here: [VIDEO] It’s hilarious. Great, great marketing. On a more serious note, my experience is that any other small chair will do the same trick."},{"id":16471775,"author":"jey","month":"2018-02","story":"To solve problems caused by sitting, learn to squat (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here's an amusing explainer video","text":"Yup, it's more biomechanically natural. There's a footstool product that makes Western toilets more comfortable, giving you the best of both worlds: [LINK] Here's an amusing explainer video: [VIDEO]"}]},"YcjlhFVTY50":{"wy":"HN cites it as period footage showing how Apple presented the II line and how the company leaned on its education market to survive.","m":[{"id":48360016,"author":"ndiddy","month":"2026-06","story":"\"The Apple Boogie\" 1987 Mac Promo Album Cassette Tape [video]","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Apple promotional music videos.","quote":"They did music videos for a few of these too","text":"They did music videos for a few of these too: Blue Busters: [LINK] Apple II Forever: [VIDEO] Leading the Way: [LINK] I guess stuff like this is what happens when your marketing department has too little to do."},{"id":48157145,"author":"flomo","month":"2026-05","story":"Steve Jobs Next Computer: His Forgotten Exile Years","stance":"reference","why":"Apple II Forever promo as evidence Apple survived on its education market.","quote":"Apple survived by chanting","text":"> \"the Macintosh itself was not a commercial success\" which is another strange claim Macintosh was in fact not successful for many years, and Apple survived by chanting \"Apple II Forever!\" at their legacy edu market. [VIDEO] Also the fail Apple III was Jobs' machine. Scully and Gasse made the Macintosh II line successful by marketing expensive workstations to creative professionals. That was against Jobs' \"vision\" so of course he discounts it. One thing which has never changed: Apple won't lift their finger unless they get a 30% margin."},{"id":47608960,"author":"joezydeco","month":"2026-04","story":"Apple at 50","stance":"reference","why":"Apple II promo show originally presented with computer-controlled slide projectors.","quote":"originally presented to the audience with a massive bank of computer-controlled slide projectors","text":"There's also \"Apple ][ Forever\" [VIDEO] These videos have a certain look, and it comes from how they were originally presented to the audience with a massive bank of computer-controlled slide projectors that could cross-fade between images."}]},"Z3dSkkEr-wk":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete example of YouTube and Google search failing to find content by exact title.","q":{"x":"Here's the video for anyone curious","i":39464771,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41974776,"author":"jerlam","month":"2024-10","story":"Cannot Search for Joe Rogan Trump Interview on YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Sounds similar to what happened with ProZD's video which wasn't political or controversial","text":"Sounds similar to what happened with ProZD's video which wasn't political or controversial: [VIDEO] follow up - [LINK] It was flagged internally as high-risk and removed from the search rankings."},{"id":39464771,"author":"johnfernow","month":"2024-02","story":"YouTube dominates TV streaming in US, per Nielsen's latest report","stance":"reference","why":"Shows YouTube search failing to surface a famous video by exact title.","quote":"Here's the video for anyone curious","text":"Here's the video for anyone curious: [VIDEO] tl;dw: First, he searches the exact name of the video on YouTube: it shows a fan-made animated skit of it, the wrong videos but from the right channel, and several re-uploads with only a few hundred views. Second, he adds the channel name before the name of the video in the search bar. Same results. Third, he tries searching for a famous line from the video: still no luck. Finally, he sorts by view count and it shows up, but still not as the first result. Is this some obscure video from some obscure channel? Nope, the video has 15 million views from a channel with 3.91 million subscribers that uploads videos regularly. He has a verified account which was created in 2006. I just tried doing the same search as he did in an incognito window, and it also didn't show up for me until I sorted by view count. I ran it on archive.today, and while it has slightly different results than what I got, nowhere in the search results does it ever show the original video with 15 million views. [LINK]"},{"id":40847789,"author":"ForHackernews","month":"2024-07","story":"Google dropping continuous scroll in search results","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Google fails at this (deliberately, I can only assume)","text":"What? Where do you come up with this? Expecting to see the original video by the creator when you search for the exact title of that video , verbatim, is not a self-nullifying mysterious goal. Google fails at this (deliberately, I can only assume): [VIDEO]"}]},"cyN-CRNrb3E":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an example of a hard, chaotic control problem that fast feedback and good control design can solve.","q":{"x":"Triple!!! inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much much harder task","i":12476108,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":28306768,"author":"dragontamer","month":"2021-08","story":"The Myth of a Superhuman AI","stance":"reference","why":"Example of superhuman machine control of an unstable system.","quote":"Just the assumption of super-human control systems is baked into modern products.","text":"> We don't know how to make AI that will learn to play even simple game like montezuma's revenge in some reasonable play time like 30 min. Explain to me why a Tool-assisted Speedrun doesn't count as \"AI\". Its certainly not a human who is playing that ( [LINK] ). > Something that could in one package: I'm not sure if that sort of thing is worth the investment. But if we have \"Fly the F35 airframe\" (which is incredibly unstable), then we suddenly reach \"only computers can fly this thing\" levels. Just the assumption of super-human control systems is baked into modern products. [VIDEO] Our cell phones are largely manufactured by machines these days. A few tidbits here and there remain in human hands for cheapness... but a lot of today's stuff is basically an AI. [LINK]"},{"id":14014338,"author":"obstinate","month":"2017-04","story":"Cancer Isn’t a Logic Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a triple pendulum stabilized, cited as a solved hard problem.","quote":"Double and triple pendulums have been solved","text":"Double and triple pendulums have been solved: [VIDEO] I have little doubt cancer will be someday as well. It might not -- probably won't -- happen in my lifetime. But if humanity hasn't collapsed, I find it very unlikely that we're still dealing with cancer in a thousand years."},{"id":13368091,"author":"phinnaeus","month":"2017-01","story":"Let’s Talk About Self-Driving Cars","stance":"reference","why":"Fast-sampling feedback control stabilizes a chaotic triple pendulum.","quote":"you don't necessarily have to be able to predict the future if you are simply monitoring the present state at a high enough sampling rate","text":"When you have mutliple vehicles, each potentially with their own variant of control software, then each could be internally deterministic but \"foreign\" to the other vehicle. Kind of like a double pendulum. Edit: But also like that cool double/triple pendulum thing ( [VIDEO] ) you don't necessarily have to be able to predict the future if you are simply monitoring the present state at a high enough sampling rate. Note that this thing is sampling at 1ms. Not sure what kind of sampling would be necessary for cars, certainly a function of velocity and braking power."},{"id":12476108,"author":"imaginenore","month":"2016-09","story":"Neuromorphic computing implementation of pole balancing","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of balancing a triple inverted pendulum on a physical cart.","quote":"Triple!!! inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much much harder task","text":"Two-pole balancing (2009): [LINK] Double inverted pendulum balancing (2015), a much harder task: [LINK] Double inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much harder task: [LINK] Triple!!! inverted pendulum balancing with a physical cart (2011), a much much much harder task: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12477301,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-09","story":"Neuromorphic computing implementation of pole balancing","stance":"reference","why":"Triple inverted pendulum balanced using feedforward control.","quote":"Here's the triple inverted pendulum balancing problem, solved using feedforward control.[2]","text":"The single inverted pendulum balancing problem has been solved using neural nets, fuzzy logic, and nonlinear control theory. It's a standard problem in controls classes. Here's a system learning how to do this.[1] Takes about 200 trials. Here's the triple inverted pendulum balancing problem, solved using feedforward control.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8007595,"author":"fjarlq","month":"2014-07","story":"Double Pendulum in fewer than 100 lines of JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Triple pendulum balanced on a cart.","quote":"balancing a triple pendulum","text":"Cool. Feature request: mount the pivot on a programmable cart. It'd be fun to write software to balance the double pendulum. e.g. [LINK] and, balancing a triple pendulum: [VIDEO]"}]},"iKcWu0tsiZM":{"wy":"It is cited as a comedic parody of diversity and inclusion initiatives in threads on those topics.","q":{"x":"A short-film/parody on how an extreme obsession with inclusive speech makes it impossible to communicate clearly without consequences.","i":31126180,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47905413,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2026-04","story":"The Self-Defeating Condescension of an Anti-Racist Education","stance":"reference","why":"Seven-minute satirical short on equity education","quote":"this wonderful 7-minutes short satirical movie aptly named \"Modern Educayshun","text":"> In a 2019 blog post, I expressed concern that training teachers to recognize “worship of the written word” as “white supremacy culture,” as many equity trainers urged, could alienate students of color from school. 2019... By then some people had already noticed the problem since years and years. Here's Neel Kolhatkar 's take on it in 2016, in this wonderful 7-minutes short satirical movie aptly named \"Modern Educayshun\" (the realisator plays the main character): [VIDEO] I don't know if there's much else to do than laugh about this entire madness of a movement. What disgust me really the most is that those very same who've been trying to portrait heterosexual white men in the west as \"patriarchal\" and responsible for all evils (vs women, vs men of colors, etc.) are the very same who are turning a complete blind eye on the actual patriarchal societies that do the most horrible things one can imagine. For a reality check, for example: there are 230 million woman that have undergone forced female genital mutilation. And if rounded about 0% of those have been done by white men from the west. And because people have nothing to answer to that, they'll downvote. So yeah: my advice would be... Do like me: pick your fights."},{"id":41969042,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2024-10","story":"Could you pass this 8th grade test from 1912?","stance":"reference","why":"An Indian-Australian comedian's take on modern education","quote":"An indian-australian's semi-recent take on modern \"educayshun","text":"I'm pretty sure proper grammar and spelling would already gain you quite some points. An indian-australian's semi-recent take on modern \"educayshun\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":31126180,"author":"MrBlueIncognito","month":"2022-04","story":"Google’s AI-powered ‘inclusive warnings’ feature is very broken","stance":"reference","why":"Short film parodying obsession with inclusive speech","quote":"A short-film/parody on how an extreme obsession with inclusive speech makes it impossible to communicate clearly without consequences.","text":"[VIDEO] A short-film/parody on how an extreme obsession with inclusive speech makes it impossible to communicate clearly without consequences."},{"id":15948785,"author":"calvinbhai","month":"2017-12","story":"I don’t want to be the pink elephant in the room","stance":"reference","why":"Comedic short making a similar point about diversity efforts","quote":"Feels like the video makes a similar point.","text":"Dear Mallika, Many thanks for writing this article! Totally echoes my thoughts. fwiw, as an Indian male engineer in US, I initially was very enthusiastically thinking \"lets go out of our way to support the pink elephants\" and later was surprised when some of my peers (female engineers from India in US or of Indian origin) showed utter disinterest in being part of many of these women centric meetups/conferences. They were more enthusiastic to go to conferences that successfully enforced a 'No Asshole Rule' on attendees, followed by attending conferences without 'all male' speakers, than conferences of any other type. (I thought the feedback I received was an outlier, but it is good to know I'm not alone). Hope such articles bring positive changes to improve diversity organically and address the pink elephant syndrome. This article reminded me of this video \"Modern Educayshun\" (by an Aussie Comedian of Indian origin) [VIDEO] . Feels like the video makes a similar point."},{"id":11630041,"author":"eva1984","month":"2016-05","story":"How my company is lowering the bar to increase diversity","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Feels like a real-world version of this video","text":"I think I probably guessed which company this is. But this doesn't smell right. Feels like a real-world version of this video: [VIDEO]"}]},"lAtVOK04XvA":{"wy":"Cited as a concrete example that humans can learn echolocation, in threads about animal and human sonar.","q":{"x":"a story of a man who figured it out when he was a child, and eventually learned to ride a bicycle, and started teaching it to others.","i":27405854,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37387288,"author":"twobitshifter","month":"2023-09","story":"Can We Talk to Whales?","stance":"reference","why":"Blind people can use echolocation.","quote":"People without sight can also use echolocation.","text":"People without sight can also use echolocation. With the right head location sensors and a set of headphones you could describe objects to them with sound. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27405854,"author":"mikewarot","month":"2021-06","story":"Humans Can Learn How to 'Echolocate' in 10 Weeks, Experiment Shows","stance":"reference","why":"Man who learned echolocation as a child and rides a bicycle.","quote":"a story of a man who figured it out when he was a child, and eventually learned to ride a bicycle, and started teaching it to others.","text":"I remember long ago, when I used to watch TV, a story of a man who figured it out when he was a child, and eventually learned to ride a bicycle, and started teaching it to others. Thanks to search engines, and video on demand... here it is [VIDEO]"},{"id":25859554,"author":"teleforce","month":"2021-01","story":"How far does sound travel in the ocean?","stance":"reference","why":"Blind human echolocation as similar to bat mechanism.","quote":"The latter is similar to the mechanism being used by bats or blind human [3].","text":"Blue Whale is the world largest (30m/100ft) and also the second loudest (189 underwater dB) animal, has been reported to communicate for about 1600 miles or 2600 km away. This distance is approximately the distance of Puerto Rico to the shores of Newfoundland [1]. The loudest animal goes to Sperm Whale with 230 underwater dB, and it's louder than the sound of jet engine at take-off. According to experts, whale's consciousness and sense of self is based on sound, not sight. They communicate using sound types namely repeated songs (each song quantum duration of 15-20 seconds) and D calls (down-swept short pulse) [2]. I think that the longer repeated songs are for communication while the short D calls are for echo-location for determining sea depth, icebergs, islands and troupe of shrimps (their staple foods). The latter is similar to the mechanism being used by bats or blind human [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"}]},"pBdGOrcUEg8":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke or warning shorthand, the 'Stringer Bell warning', about leaving written evidence of illegal plans.","q":{"x":"This is what’s known as a Stringer Bell warning[0]","i":28295821,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28295821,"author":"CPLX","month":"2021-08","story":"Googlespeak – How Google limits thought about antitrust","stance":"reference","why":"Stringer Bell warning about written conspiracy evidence.","quote":"This is what’s known as a Stringer Bell warning[0]","text":"This is what’s known as a Stringer Bell warning[0] and it doesn’t reflect well on the organization who has to make it this aggressively. Yes, it stands to reason that if you’re engaged in a potentially unlawful conspiracy you need to be careful what you put in writing. However if this is coming up constantly and prevents you from using common sense words for your regular business operations then it’s a pretty clear red flag that your actions may be subjecting you to legal liability. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9642382,"author":"grugq","month":"2015-06","story":"Operational PGP","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a criminal keeping an incriminating diary.","quote":"creating a detailed diary of you criminal activities is a terrible idea","text":"To elaborate, the security precautions you take are entirely dependent on the threat you face (or believe you face). If you're Dread Pirate Roberts, creating a detailed diary of you criminal activities is a terrible idea [1]. If you're a 13 yr old girl, creating a detailed day by day diary is probably not a life threatening decision. You should take the precautions appropriate for your threat model and risk appetite. I suggest deleting correspondence after a set period of time. I know people that do a yearly purge of all their sensitive mail for the previous year in January. If you are encrypting for the sake of encrypting, as some people do (see: cover traffic), then you can keep archives of your mail because you face no negative outcome if your archive is compromised. There is no downside, so go for it. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16109432,"author":"wpietri","month":"2018-01","story":"CEO of porn cryptocurrency disappears with investor money","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It of course brings to mind this classic bit from the Wire","text":"This is such a great/terrible example of the absolute arrogance in the tech industry. I really do try to defend tech in general, but then something like this comes along. Your claim is that you can enter into an industry literally thousands of years old and use a little algorithmic magic to prevent sex trafficking where the world's collective police departments have consistently failed. And it's all done by an anonymous bunch of dudes. Pretending that they have an \"extrajurisdictional company\". Which, yes, is a criminal conspiracy. Except conducted on the web, and funded by an illegal securities sale. It of course brings to mind this classic bit from the Wire: [VIDEO]"}]},"rkuhWA9GdCo":{"wy":"Cited after the 2022 invasion as evidence Zeihan predicted the Ukraine war years earlier.","q":{"x":"Here is a three minute snippet from one of his talks from five years ago","i":33621524,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36224932,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2023-06","story":"Russia’s population nightmare is going to get even worse","stance":"reference","why":"Zeihan predicted Ukraine war years earlier","quote":"he predicted the Ukraine war 5 years before it happened","text":"Peter Zeihan had an interesting take on this, that the Russians moved on Ukraine because their demographic decline means that if they don't invade now, they will forever lose the ability to wage war and hence defend their homeland. The Soviet-era population is rapidly aging out of military service age, and the post-Soviet generation is tiny, and so they will not be able to field a meaningful army if they wait. Basically Ukraine is a last-ditch attempt to save the Russian state. [LINK] Interestingly, he predicted the Ukraine war 5 years before it happened: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33621524,"author":"recuter","month":"2022-11","story":"Are We Prisoners of Geography?","stance":"reference","why":"Short snippet of a Zeihan talk from years ago","quote":"Here is a three minute snippet from one of his talks from five years ago","text":"> It isn't the Stratfor origin, or the blazenly pro-American optimism per se that concern me Are you sure about that? Because all you've written is that Zeihan, who focuses on practical and immediate concerns of demographics, energy and agricultural markets - is obfuscating realities and monopolizing ears. Eh? As opposed to what or whom? Here is a three minute snippet from one of his talks from five years ago : [VIDEO] What brilliant insights are being crowded out? What specifically is he wrong about?"},{"id":31969137,"author":"maxvu","month":"2022-07","story":"Asset-Level Transition Risk in the Global Coal, Oil, and Gas Supply Chains","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"An older summary","text":"Reiterating from memory of his lectures on YouTube: - The Russians consider some nine-odd geographic boundaries of the former union to be crucial to defense (e.g. between mountains and deserts, certain ports). Without fortifying there, a lot of the Rus land area is open and hard to defend. Interestingly, Ukraine contains only one in Crimea (which they already had) but is on the way to ones in Romania, Poland, etc. - With the fall of the union, there was not only a baby bust but also the collapse of technical training programs. The \"demographic cliff\" means that not only will the army half in size but, e.g. the rail lines and electric are also in danger. - While Ukrainian resolve and EU unity were a surprise to even the US, he also sees a leadership failing not unlike the Chinese one - authoritarian that drives out opposition too harshly also drives out the people in the room that push back on bad ideas. FSB had intelligence that overwhelmingly, UA would accept occupation? An older summary: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31840213,"author":"MR4D","month":"2022-06","story":"The end of the world is just beginning for shipping","stance":"reference","why":"Zeihan mentions Ukraine invasion prediction in 2017","quote":"He predicted the invasion of Ukraine back in this video from 2017","text":"Here’s one for you: He predicted the invasion of Ukraine back in this video from 2017 (actually earlier, but this was the easiest source for me to cite): [VIDEO] The whole video is pretty short, but he mentions Ukraine about 2 1/2 minutes in."}]},"tLK_i-TQ3kQ":{"wy":"Referenced in discussions of GPU virtualization and VDI as a demo of licensing-free GPU sharing.","q":{"x":"he had 31 virtual desktop instances all running with full GPU acceleration, with RDP as the VDI client software","i":40002311,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43386792,"author":"yvdriess","month":"2025-03","story":"Raytracing on Intel's Arc B580","stance":"reference","why":"Shows datacenter Intel GPUs supporting SR-IOV","quote":"Wendell's video on Flex 170 GPU from Intel - Subscription Free GPU Accelerated VDI on Proxmox 8.1","text":"Some counter points. On the die size argument, which I see being echoed a lot online: Why would a customer care or factor that into their purchasing decisions? Saying that these dGPUs with large dies are what is going to put Intel out of business is ludicrous and the Xe cores are shared amongst many of Intel's most lucrative products. You can afford larger dies on N4 compared to when the 40-series were launched. It's no longer the leading edge node and yields have likely improved. dGPUs have pretty expensive GDDR modules, I do not have data on the exact proportion but I would bet that the memory modules is the more important line item. BoM matters less on lower volume (compared to mobile SoCs) dGPU units. Masks masks, R&D and validation are big fixed up-front costs. Recurring software support is also independent of how many units get sold. Xe cores are shared by many Intel products (client & server CPUs, datacenter GPUs and gaming GPUs). B580 is widely popular for gamers, Intel cannot keep up the demand at the moment. I doubt they need to unlock SRIOV on the gaming segment dGPUs to get rid of stock, as you seem to suggest. Their datacenter GPUs [1] offer support for SRIOV, as you probably already know, so I assume you are bemoaning market segmentation. [1] [VIDEO] -- Wendell's video on Flex 170 GPU from Intel - Subscription Free GPU Accelerated VDI on Proxmox 8.1"},{"id":40304128,"author":"paulmd","month":"2024-05","story":"Apple introduces M4 chip","stance":"reference","why":"Enthusiast interest in GPU virtualization without subscriptions","quote":"","text":"gpu passthrough for VMs is not supported on apple silicon period afaik. there may be some \"native\" renderer built on top of metal but apple doesn't support SR-IOV or \"headless passthrough\". [LINK] otoh no, it is not \"more or less [automatic]\" in other hardware either, SR-IOV has been on the enthusiast wishlist for a ridiculously long time now because basically nobody implements it (or, they restrict it to the most datacenter-y of products). intel iGPUs from the HD/UHD Intel Graphics Technology era have a concept called GVT-g which isn't quite SR-IOV but generally does the thing. Newer Xe-based iGPUs do not support this, nor do the discrete graphics cards. AMD's iGPUs do not have anything at all afaik. Their dGPUs don't even implement reset properly, which is becoming a big problem with people trying to set up GPU clouds for AI stuff - a lot of times the AMD machines will need a hard power reset to come back. NVIDIA GPUs do work properly, and do implement SR-IOV properly... but they only started letting you do passthrough recently, and only 1 VM instance per card (so, 1 real + 1 virtual). Curious what you're using (I'm guessing intel iGPU or nvidia dGPU) but generally this is still something that gets Wendell Level1techs hot and bothered about the mere possibility of this feature being in something without a five-figure subscription attached. [VIDEO] It does suck that Apple refuses to implement vulkan support (or sign graphics drivers), I think that's de-facto how people inter"},{"id":40368111,"author":"justinclift","month":"2024-05","story":"VMware Fusion Pro: Now available free for personal use","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a newer VDI option on Proxmox","quote":"There are new VDI contenders still coming up though.","text":"There are new VDI contenders still coming up though. This caught my attention recently (due to trialling Proxmox for another purpose): [VIDEO] There's clearly still demand for VDI solutions too. A recent example: [LINK]"},{"id":40002311,"author":"belthesar","month":"2024-04","story":"Why is remote desktop slow when host monitor is off unless HDMI cable is used?","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of many VDI instances with GPU acceleration over RDP","quote":"he had 31 virtual desktop instances all running with full GPU acceleration, with RDP as the VDI client software","text":"Like most thing in Windows, the sauce is buried under layers of Registry tweaks and/or Group Policies, but enabling RemoteFX (released with Windows Server 2008 R2 SP1) for RDP enables both h.264/h.265 stream compression, and with additional settings changes to enforce usage of the GPU on the desktop session over RDP, you can get solid 3D acceleration. In this video from Wendell @ Level1Techs, he had 31 virtual desktop instances all running with full GPU acceleration, with RDP as the VDI client software. [VIDEO] . As another data point, I had game developers remoting into their workstations during Quarantine, using RDP with full graphics acceleration using the same tweaks. What the shinier VDI/Remote Desktop solutions like Sunshine+Moonlight, Parsec, Horizon, Citrix do for you is make all of this easier to set up and use. But as far as capability is concerned, RDP is quite powerful and quite capable, assuming you jump through the hoops to set it up right."}]},"wKDdLWAdcbM":{"wy":"HN posts it as a joke about bureaucrats and institutional conformity.","m":[{"id":48628280,"author":"Npovview","month":"2026-06","story":"Did my old job only exist because of fraud?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sir Humphrey explains the role of a civil servant","text":"Sir Humphrey explains the role of a civil servant [VIDEO] \"Bernard, I have served eleven governments in the past thirty years. If I had believed in all their policies, I would have been passionately committed to keeping out of the Common Market, and passionately committed to going into it. I would have been utterly convinced of the rightness of nationalising steel. And of denationalising it and renationalising it. On capital punishment, I'd have been a fervent retentionist and an ardent abolitionist. I would've been a Keynesian and a Friedmanite, a grammar school preserver and destroyer, a nationalisation freak and a privatisation maniac; but above all, I would have been a stark, staring, raving schizophrenic.\""},{"id":46957775,"author":"pwatsonwailes","month":"2026-02","story":"Frontier AI agents violate ethical constraints 30–50% of time, pressured by KPIs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's instructive though, despite the flaws, and at this point has been replicated enough in different ways that we know it's got some basis in reality. There's a whole bunch of constructivist research around interactionism, that shows that whilst it's not just the person's default ways of behaving or just the situation that matters, the situational context definitely influences what people are likely to do in any given scenario. Reicher & Haslam's research around engaged followership gives a pretty good insight into why Zimbardo got the results he did, because he wasn't just observing what went on. That gets into all sorts of things around good study design, constructivist vs positivist analysis etc, but that's a whole different thing. I suspect, particularly with regards to different levels, there's an element of selection bias going on (if for no other reason that what we see in terms of levels of psychopathy in higher levels of management), but I'd guess (and it's a guess), that culture convincing people that achieving the KPI is the moral good is more of a factor. That gets into a whole separate thing around what happens in more cultlike corporations and the dynamics with the VC world (WeWork is an obvious example) as to why organisations can end up with workforces which will do things of questionable purpose, because the organisation has a visible a fearless leader who has to be pleased/obeyed etc (Musk, Jobs etc), or more insidiously, a valuable goal that must be pursue"},{"id":27649130,"author":"shadilay","month":"2021-06","story":"Key witness in Assange case admits to lies in indictment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Very funny.","text":"Very funny. In reality politicians have very little impact on the operation of these agencies who exist in the permanent bureaucracy. [VIDEO]"}]},"xAxtxPAUcwQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as a nostalgic example of the promised any-media-on-demand future that never fully arrived.","q":{"x":"we never got the 21st century we were promised, which was this - any movie ever made","i":33079928,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34242598,"author":"mansilladev","month":"2023-01","story":"AT&T's predictions of the future (1993)","stance":"reference","why":"Reminiscent 1990s telecom ad.","quote":"These also remind me of the Qwest commercials.","text":"These also remind me of the Qwest commercials. (\"Every movie.. every language.. any time\") - [VIDEO] The tone and cadence of the rhetorical questions in the AT&T commercials also reminds me of comedian Tom Papa (\"Have you ever..? .. I have\") - [LINK]"},{"id":33079928,"author":"dublin","month":"2022-10","story":"The deception of “buying” digital movies","stance":"reference","why":"Ad used as example of the promised any-movie future.","quote":"we never got the 21st century we were promised, which was this - any movie ever made","text":"Yeah, but that's a real problem for those of us who don't watch a lot of movies. Many people aren't capable of figuring out \"where to look\" at all anyway. I am, to some degree, but it's just not worth spending that much of my time to hunt them down on the rare occasions I really want to watch something. Also, I'll just note that we never got the 21st century we were promised, which was this - any movie ever made: [VIDEO] The fundamental problem is that the people who own movie IP rights are truly evil."},{"id":19463525,"author":"snowwrestler","month":"2019-03","story":"A brief history of why artists are no longer making a living making music","stance":"reference","why":"Example ad showing tech companies promising free media.","quote":"","text":"I think it's incomplete to characterize the state of the industry today without discussing how those consumer perceptions of value were intentionally created. If you worked in the music industry for 10 years then your experience almost completely post-dates these efforts. Starting even well before Napster, investors and technology companies saw the opportunity to change the allocation of value from artists and producers, to distribution. To be clear, that was not the allocation 30+ years ago. Record stores and radio stations did not make immensely more money than the labels and artists. Tech companies and investors worked aggressively to \"commoditize their complement\"--that is, depress the value of art on their platforms, so that they could reallocate consumer spending to their products and services. \"Buy a computer and Internet connection and you can have all the music and movies you want for free\" was explicitly advertised by technology companies--see for example: [VIDEO] I think this historical perspective is crucial for understanding how we got to today. Music files are small, so the music industry was attacked first. The large size of video files kept them safe for long enough for visual artists and producers to understand what was happening and counteract. That is why you see producer-owned distribution channels for video today (Hulu, HBO Go, Disney+, etc), but not label-owned distribution channels for music. This is why Netflix, Amazon, and now Apple have had to get in"},{"id":20751656,"author":"cardiffspaceman","month":"2019-08","story":"Neil Young’s Lonely Quest to Save Music From Low Quality Streaming","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Remember this Qwest commercial [1]?","text":"I'm not an audiophile but I have a pair of speakers that is probably 70 or so laptops of volume and something nice to hook them to. I stream lots of music through the YouTube app in my smart TV, and last night some of that was written by Grieg[0]. Remember this Qwest commercial [1]? Streaming music has its pleasures and undistorted sound should be one of them. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"ywaTX-nLm6Y":{"wy":"Cited in battery and electrical safety threads as a vivid demonstration of what very high-current battery banks can do.","q":{"x":"His entire rig at one point delivered >80k amps, but he's fine.","i":41987037,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41987037,"author":"hobs","month":"2024-10","story":"The electrostatic world of insects","stance":"reference","why":"Styropyro lies across car batteries and shorts them, delivering over 80k amps.","quote":"His entire rig at one point delivered >80k amps, but he's fine.","text":"As shown in StyroPyro's video of him laying across dozens of car batteries (and then shorting them through various things) [VIDEO] His entire rig at one point delivered >80k amps, but he's fine."},{"id":40880530,"author":"h0l0cube","month":"2024-07","story":"Batteries: How cheap can they get?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows high-current battery shorting causing melting and fire.","quote":"","text":"> One wrong poke with a screwdriver I'm not sure where you're jamming the screwdriver, but certainly any wrenches/tools you use should be insulated if you're dealing with very high current and/or high voltage. Enclosures, insulated wires, conduits, terminal covers should be used to avoid short circuits. Also proper earthing and circuit isolation with RCBOs to protect from electric shock and overcurrents frying the wires/you, all which should be switched to the off position when you're poking your screwdriver, eh? ;) > And they’re big enough, no portable fire extinguisher is going to make a dent either. If you aren't doing basic safety things and somehow manage thermal runaway on LiFePo4 (pretty hard), you're probably going to melt some copper. Probably best not to put your battery assembly near flammable things, unless you want to see the world burn like this guy (though at low voltage/high current) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41100461,"author":"foobarian","month":"2024-07","story":"How to debug your battery design","stance":"reference","why":"Shows what happens when batteries are abused.","quote":"thanks to our industrious Youtuber friends who took the risk so we don't have to!","text":"You would probably enjoy seeing what happens, thanks to our industrious Youtuber friends who took the risk so we don't have to! [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38565164,"author":"elif","month":"2023-12","story":"Tesla shares 48V architecture with other automakers to move the industry","stance":"reference","why":"Styropyro touches high-current 12V battery bank safely.","quote":"here[1] is styropyro touching 2 contacts which have more current than the largest bolts of lightning","text":"The reason to not keep going higher is purely practicality because stepping down bigger voltage gaps for lower voltage applications begins to become less efficient/more waste heat. If you design 100v computers, monitor, headlights, seat adjustment motors, window/windshield motors, pumps, etc. you could cut these 48v wires in half again but if you end up stepping everything down anyway it loses its utility. The safety rules about voltage and the human body are often poorly overstated, as is the addage that 'its the amps that kill you, not the volts.' in reality it takes a whole lot of both [0]. As further research, here[1] is styropyro touching 2 contacts which have more current than the largest bolts of lightning (enough to almost instantly vaporize a crowbar) with his bare hands, but because it is 12V it is not able to pass through him. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"zdKCQKBvH-A":{"wy":"Cited in self-driving threads as a documented case of a robotaxi failing at traffic cones, with some comments also noting the support team response.","q":{"x":"At 30:18 there's a very interesting tidbit.","i":27170711,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30857728,"author":"Honest_Carrot","month":"2022-03","story":"Waymo begins driverless rides in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"Shows passenger display rendering in real time.","quote":"you can see the render is real-time.","text":"IIRC correctly in their Waymo videos you can see inside the cabin. Each passenger gets a video feed of that video, so I think it is real-time. Let me find a link. EDIT: This was posted above for different reasons, but you can see the render is real-time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29080746,"author":"drawkbox","month":"2021-11","story":"Walmart delivery powered by Cruise – Self-driving deliveries are here","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Waymo's support team reaching a stuck car.","quote":"Waymo does have a team that follows cars in trouble,","text":"Yeah Cruise announced this Walmart delivery with self-driving cars in Nov 2020 to launch in 2021 [1] and it is now available at the first location in Arizona. Cruise has been testing here but probably more on proving grounds like Apple has. Apple just bought a proving ground this year that they have been leasing since 2017 [2]. Testing picking up as well as competition. I am sure Cruise will expand it in Arizona. Most of the self-driving operations are in the East Valley (Chandler, Gilbert, Tempe) as the roads are pretty simple and square. The Scottsdale area this is in also pretty simple and by the reservation. Self-driving cars are everywhere with Waymo in Arizona as well, many entirely unattended. If you drive anywhere you see them. For Waymo samples, this JJRicks Studios channel [3] did a series on it for a while to show rides with no driver. Waymo does have a team that follows cars in trouble, on the infamous \"cone\" one you can see it [4]. For the most part the operation is solid, very few incidents like the \"cone\" incident where the cone was just in between LiDAR and CV detection and probably caused a covering of the line that confused the decision process. These types of issues are best to resolve in a baseline test with little change before the more challenging ones. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28291107,"author":"OJFord","month":"2021-08","story":"Waymo starts offering autonomous rides in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"A passenger's ride in a driverless Waymo in Arizona.","quote":"blogger taking one for a ride (alone, in the back seat) in Arizona","text":"I had no idea it was available anywhere to just anyone, and without a backup driver in the driver seat. It still isn't to me in the UK, but really cool to see, it's a lot further along (especially in terms of regulation/being allowed) than I realised. Couple of articles deep from the submission, blogger taking one for a ride (alone, in the back seat) in Arizona: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27170711,"author":"22c","month":"2021-05","story":"Waymo AV Freaked Out by Traffic Cones, Blocks Traffic, Evades Support Vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Reveals how route planning hides closed lanes from the car.","quote":"At 30:18 there's a very interesting tidbit.","text":"At 30:18 there's a very interesting tidbit. [VIDEO] They way I understand that is the route planner, which I assume is ran completely separately to the self-driving car, removes un-navigable spaces from the route planning. So in this situation, the roadside assistance driver is surprised that the entire lane has not been removed from the route planner. That's pretty damning and it makes me wonder if there is a huge gap in autonomy here that is being papered over by clever route planning (ie. avoid that lane altogether and the AI won't have to worry about the cones). The AI should be able to figure out on its own that the right lane was closed, it shouldn't need route planning to do that for it."},{"id":33214159,"author":"deng","month":"2022-10","story":"Even after $100B, self-driving cars are going nowhere","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"like a hilarious Black Mirror episode","text":"I must say, the linked video in the article to the rogue Waymo car is like a hilarious Black Mirror episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40140412,"author":"botanical","month":"2024-04","story":"Waymo illegal overtake into wrong-way driving","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Google's AI is just as bad as the rest.","text":"Google's AI is just as bad as the rest. I remember when it couldn't get past a traffic cone after not being in an accident for \"countless hours\": [VIDEO] These companies should be fined hefty amounts and barred from beta testing out in public and putting people in danger. Governments should carry out comprehensive tests on a self-driving car's capabilities, same as cars without proven passenger safety (Euro NCAP) aren't allowed to be on roads carrying passengers. And now this great AI company is also providing AI services to pariah states like Apartheid Israel to be used on people. That's scary."},{"id":38646918,"author":"cma","month":"2023-12","story":"Weak-to-Strong Generalization","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Waymo had a big mistake with cones where a lane was blocked off for construction","text":"> I also don't recall traffic cones being a very difficult problem beyond Cruise + cones on windshield in SF. I have no love for Cruise. But its straightforward to pause if there's a large object on the windshield. Waymo had a big mistake with cones where a lane was blocked off for construction and they thought it was the inverse and started driving down the blocked off side: [LINK] full video: [VIDEO] In the statement before that timestamp they say some of it was caused by remote operator error. But it seems to have enough problems with cones to need an operator in the first place, and you can see the planner is wrong. I think Cruise would be using a human or at least summoning one to oversee in any situation with unexpected cones, based on what they have said about how often they use remote assistance too."},{"id":38008473,"author":"botanical","month":"2023-10","story":"California suspends GM Cruise's autonomous vehicle deployment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it failed to proceed past a traffic cone on a sunny day","text":"I've said this previously when a self-driving Uber killed a woman, and I'll say it again: I don't understand why authorities allow self-driving cars to beta test out in public. I thought Waymo was the best of the lot with something like millions of hours on the road and no incidents, but when they started accepting passengers, it failed to proceed past a traffic cone on a sunny day: [VIDEO] The government should initiate a comprehensive test on a self-driving car's capabilities. In their current form, I wouldn't trust any of the others like Tesla and Uber that think self-driving is as easy putting it out on the road. And that too with Tesla and their cost-saving no-LIDAR nonsense. These companies should be fined hefty amounts and barred from beta testing out in public."},{"id":37046122,"author":"botanical","month":"2023-08","story":"A self-driving Uber killed a woman. The backup driver pleaded guilty","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it failed to proceed past a traffic cone","text":"I don't understand why authorities allow self-driving cars to beta test out in public. I thought waymo was the best with something like millions of hours on the road but when they started accepting passengers, it failed to proceed past a traffic cone: [VIDEO] I wouldn't trust any of the others like Tesla and Uber that think self-driving is easy. And Tesla with their cost-saving no LIDAR nonsense. These companies should be fined hefty amounts and barred from testing out in public."},{"id":37185127,"author":"botanical","month":"2023-08","story":"Cruise told by CA DMV to reduce robotaxi fleet 50% following crash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it failed to proceed past a traffic cone","text":"I've said it before when a self-driving Uber killed a woman, and I'll say it again: I don't understand why authorities allow self-driving cars to beta test out in public. I thought Waymo was the best with something like millions of hours on the road but when they started accepting passengers, it failed to proceed past a traffic cone: [VIDEO] I wouldn't trust any of the others like Tesla and Uber that think self-driving is as easy putting it out on the road. And Tesla with their cost-saving no LIDAR nonsense. These companies should be fined hefty amounts and barred from testing out in public."},{"id":33277495,"author":"patrick451","month":"2022-10","story":"Next Stop for Waymo One: Los Angeles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You can easily find videos of waymo cars doing crazy stuff","text":"You can easily find videos of waymo cars doing crazy stuff that stretches the definition of \"working\" pretty far. E.g., [VIDEO] Nobody has time for shenanigans like that."},{"id":28239772,"author":"rhacker","month":"2021-08","story":"How we’ve built the World’s Most Experienced Urban Driver","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"here's the video I watched that made me not want ANY Waymos near where I live","text":"It took a while, here's the video I watched that made me not want ANY Waymos near where I live: [VIDEO] I think the thing that made me so furiously mad is that there's all these situations in the video that people might laugh at, but there are situations where I've been stuck in traffic in an emergency and if I found out it's because Google was trying to make it's next pentillionth dollar I would blow my top. Can you imagine one of these during the Campfire evacuation?"},{"id":27954951,"author":"kjksf","month":"2021-07","story":"Tesla AutoPilot – I give up, it's downright lethal","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Around min. 17 it gets confused by a cone and stops in the middle of two lane road, hugging both lanes.","text":"[VIDEO] Here's a video of Waymo car in Phoenix. Around min. 17 it gets confused by a cone and stops in the middle of two lane road, hugging both lanes. Then google sends support but just when they arrive, the car drives away from them. They were lucky it didn't lead to an accident and as long as they keep the fleet to 600 cars then yeah, the accident rate will be much lower than Tesla's AutoPilot, shipped in more than a million cars. My point is not to rag on Waymo just to inject some reality. We don't have a choice between \"safe and unsafe\" way of developing self-driving software. We have a choice between \"test software we know can't handle all situation on real roads and make it better based on that testing\" or \"we'll never have self-driving software\". Except the other option will rather be: \"U.S. doesn't allow testing of self-driving software on real roads and a Chinese company will develop it and will capture a trillion-dollar market in U.S.\""}]}});
